{"record_id":"D41-B01-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architectural decision framing","concept":"Architecture trade-off analysis","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an architecture trade-off be evaluated?","answer":"Evaluate candidate architectures against explicit requirements, constraints, operational conditions, risks, and lifecycle consequences rather than declaring one architecture universally superior.","explanation":"Architecture decisions are contextual optimization problems. A candidate can improve one dimension while worsening another. The decision record should make those dimensions explicit and preserve unresolved uncertainty.","principle":"Architecture quality is fitness for the stated context, not universal architectural superiority.","rule":"Do not select an architecture before identifying the decision drivers and constraints.","objective":"Select or retain an architecture based on traceable requirements and explicit trade-offs.","requirements":["Identify decision drivers","Identify hard constraints","Define candidate architectures","Evaluate consequences","Record risks and uncertainty"],"constraints":["No universal architectural winner","Evidence must be distinguished from assumptions"],"preconditions":["A concrete architectural decision exists","Relevant requirements are available or explicitly marked unknown"],"expected_behavior":"The resulting decision identifies candidates, decision drivers, trade-offs, risks, rationale, and verification needs.","alternative":"Use a lightweight decision record when the architectural decision is small and reversible.","tradeoff":"More analysis improves traceability but increases decision cost.","edge_case":"When requirements conflict or are incomplete, preserve the conflict rather than inventing a resolution.","verification_method":"Review each selected decision driver against the originating requirement or evidence.","verification_guidance":"A decision is traceable when each major architectural choice can be connected to an explicit requirement, constraint, or evidence-backed operational concern.","language":"language-neutral","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B01-R002","D41-B01-R003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established software architecture decision-analysis practice; synthesized record; no execution claim."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B01-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Decision drivers","concept":"Hard constraints versus preferences","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must hard constraints be separated from preferences in architecture decisions?","answer":"A hard constraint can eliminate an otherwise attractive architecture, while a preference only changes relative desirability among feasible candidates.","explanation":"Mixing mandatory requirements with preferences can cause an architecture to be selected even though it violates a non-negotiable condition. Decision records should explicitly distinguish must-have constraints from optimization preferences.","principle":"Feasibility should be evaluated before optimization.","rule":"Reject or redesign candidates that violate verified hard constraints before comparing softer qualities.","objective":"Prevent infeasible architectures from winning through favorable secondary characteristics.","requirements":["Classify constraints","Validate constraint applicability","Evaluate feasibility before ranking trade-offs"],"constraints":["Some constraints may themselves be uncertain"],"preconditions":["Candidate architectures have been identified"],"expected_behavior":"Candidates violating confirmed hard constraints are marked incompatible rather than merely receiving a lower preference score.","failure_mode":"Treating every requirement as a weighted preference can hide mandatory incompatibilities.","root_cause":"Failure to separate feasibility constraints from optimization objectives.","solution":"Maintain separate constraint and preference fields in the architecture decision model.","alternative":"Use a feasibility matrix before a multi-criteria comparison.","tradeoff":"Strict feasibility filtering can exclude unconventional solutions if the constraint was incorrectly classified.","edge_case":"A constraint may be temporary and should then be represented with lifecycle scope rather than as a permanent architectural law.","verification_method":"Trace each hard constraint to a requirement, policy, contract, or observed operational condition.","verification_guidance":"Unverified assumptions must not be promoted to hard constraints.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B01-R001","D41-B01-R003"],"depends_on":["D41-B01-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established engineering decision-analysis principle; synthesized record; no empirical benchmark claimed."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B01-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Decision drivers","concept":"Reversibility of architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should reversibility affect an architecture decision?","answer":"Irreversible or expensive-to-reverse decisions generally require stronger evidence and more deliberate validation than inexpensive reversible decisions.","explanation":"Architecture decisions differ in cost of reversal. Selecting a database model, service boundary, or externally exposed contract may create long-lived coupling. A reversible decision can tolerate more experimentation.","principle":"Required decision rigor should reflect the cost and difficulty of reversal.","rule":"Increase validation depth as irreversible commitment and migration cost increase.","objective":"Reduce long-term architectural risk without unnecessarily slowing reversible decisions.","requirements":["Identify reversal cost","Identify migration dependencies","Determine appropriate validation depth"],"constraints":["Reversal cost is context-dependent"],"expected_behavior":"Decision records explicitly identify reversibility and consequences of later change.","tradeoff":"More up-front validation increases time spent before implementation but can reduce costly migration risk.","edge_case":"A technically reversible decision may be practically irreversible because of data volume, contractual commitments, or organizational dependencies.","verification_method":"Review the proposed reversal path and identify concrete dependencies that would have to change.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B01-R001","D41-B02-R001"],"depends_on":["D41-B01-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture decision practice; synthesized record; no execution claim."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B01-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Decision drivers","concept":"Quality attribute interaction","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should quality attributes be evaluated together?","answer":"Quality attributes interact: improving latency, availability, isolation, simplicity, consistency, cost, or scalability can impose consequences on other attributes.","explanation":"Architecture cannot reliably be optimized by examining a single quality attribute in isolation. For example, stronger isolation can introduce communication overhead, while stronger consistency can increase coordination requirements.","principle":"Architecture qualities are coupled through mechanisms and constraints.","rule":"Record both the targeted quality improvement and the principal qualities that may be negatively affected.","objective":"Make second-order architectural consequences visible.","tradeoff":"Multi-dimensional analysis requires more information than single-attribute optimization.","edge_case":"Some attributes may be independent in a specific architecture and should not be assumed to conflict without evidence.","verification_method":"For each major proposed optimization, identify affected quality attributes and the mechanism causing the interaction.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B01-R001","D41-B02-R003"],"depends_on":["D41-B01-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established software architecture quality-attribute reasoning; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B01-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Decision drivers","concept":"Architecture decision record","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What information should an architecture decision record preserve?","answer":"A useful architecture decision record preserves context, problem, constraints, candidates, decision, rationale, consequences, risks, alternatives, evidence, and verification requirements.","explanation":"The record should preserve why the decision exists, not only what was selected. This allows later engineers and automated systems to reconstruct the decision context and identify whether changed conditions invalidate it.","principle":"Architecture decisions should be traceable to their context and consequences.","objective":"Create machine-retrievable architectural reasoning rather than an unexplained final choice.","requirements":["Context","Decision drivers","Candidates","Rationale","Consequences","Evidence","Risks"],"expected_behavior":"A future reasoning system can determine why a candidate was selected and under which assumptions it remains applicable.","verification_method":"Check whether the decision can be reconstructed without relying on undocumented historical context.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B01-R001","D41-B01-R002"],"depends_on":["D41-B01-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture documentation practice; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B02-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Application structure","concept":"Monolith versus modular monolith","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the architectural trade-off between a traditional monolith and a modular monolith?","answer":"A traditional monolith may permit simpler internal development but can accumulate weak boundaries; a modular monolith deliberately enforces internal module boundaries while retaining a single deployable unit.","explanation":"Both approaches can use one deployment artifact. The important distinction is the strength and enforceability of internal boundaries, not merely deployment topology.","principle":"Deployment unity and internal modularity are separate architectural dimensions.","rule":"Do not infer poor modularity solely from a monolithic deployment model.","objective":"Choose the appropriate internal structure without prematurely introducing distributed deployment.","requirements":["Define module boundaries","Define dependency direction","Define allowed inter-module interactions"],"constraints":["A modular monolith still shares deployment and often process-level resources"],"tradeoff":"A modular monolith can preserve simpler deployment while requiring disciplined boundaries and dependency management.","alternative":"Distributed services can provide stronger deployment and failure isolation at the cost of network and operational complexity.","edge_case":"A monolith with strong boundaries may be an appropriate intermediate architecture before service extraction.","verification_method":"Inspect dependency boundaries and verify that prohibited module dependencies cannot occur or are detected.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B02-R002","D41-B03-R001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["D41-B03-R001"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established modular architecture principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B02-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Application structure","concept":"Monolith decomposition boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When is a module boundary useful inside a monolith?","answer":"A module boundary is useful when it isolates a cohesive responsibility, controls dependencies, and provides a meaningful change or ownership boundary.","explanation":"A boundary that exists only by naming convention but permits unrestricted access does not provide the same architectural value as an enforced boundary.","principle":"A useful boundary limits coupling and clarifies responsibility.","rule":"Define module interfaces and restrict direct access to internal implementation details where practical.","objective":"Reduce accidental coupling before considering distributed decomposition.","constraints":["Boundary enforcement mechanisms differ by language and architecture"],"edge_case":"Very small systems may incur unnecessary complexity from excessive module fragmentation.","security_implication":"Clear boundaries can reduce unintended access to sensitive functionality but do not substitute for authorization.","performance_implication":"In-process module calls generally avoid network serialization and network latency associated with separate services.","verification_method":"Static dependency analysis, architecture tests, or code review can verify allowed dependency directions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B02-R001","D41-B02-R003"],"depends_on":["D41-B02-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established modular design practice; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B02-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Application structure","concept":"Monolith operational simplicity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What operational advantages can a monolithic deployment provide?","answer":"A single deployable unit can reduce the number of independently deployed components, service-to-service network interactions, and operational coordination points.","explanation":"These advantages concern operational topology, not universal application quality. A monolith can still be internally complex and difficult to maintain.","principle":"Reducing distributed components can reduce certain classes of operational complexity.","tradeoff":"Fewer deployment units can simplify deployment and local debugging, while increasing the blast radius of some failures and reducing independent scaling options.","constraints":["Actual operational complexity depends on deployment tooling, application size, and organizational structure"],"edge_case":"A large monolith may become operationally complex despite having one deployable artifact.","verification_method":"Measure actual deployment steps, dependency count, failure domains, and operational ownership rather than assuming simplicity from topology alone.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B02-R001","D41-B02-R004"],"depends_on":["D41-B02-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["D41-B02-R004"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed-systems trade-off reasoning; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B02-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Application structure","concept":"Independent service deployment","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What architectural capability does independently deployable services provide?","answer":"Independent services can allow components to be deployed, scaled, and operated separately when their boundaries and operational dependencies support that independence.","explanation":"Independent deployment can reduce coordination for some changes but introduces distributed-system concerns such as network failures, observability requirements, service contracts, and operational overhead.","principle":"Deployment independence is valuable only when the system can support the resulting distributed coordination costs.","tradeoff":"Independent deployment improves release isolation but increases distributed operational complexity.","edge_case":"Services that must always change together may gain little from independent deployment and can create unnecessary coordination overhead.","failure_mode":"Creating service boundaries without operational independence can produce a distributed monolith.","root_cause":"Strong coupling between supposedly independent services.","solution":"Reassess boundaries and dependency direction before splitting further.","verification_method":"Examine deployment dependencies, API contracts, runtime communication, and coordinated release requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B02-R003","D41-B02-R005"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["D41-B02-R003"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed architecture principles; synthesized record."],"