{"record_id":"D41-B04-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Persistence architecture","concept":"Relational modeling and relationships","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"When does relational modeling provide architectural value?","answer":"Relational modeling is particularly useful when entities have structured relationships, integrity constraints, transactional requirements, and query patterns that benefit from relational operations.","explanation":"Relational databases provide mechanisms for representing relationships and enforcing integrity. Whether those mechanisms are necessary depends on application semantics rather than database fashion.","objective":"Identify workloads where relational semantics materially support correctness and maintainability.","requirements":["Entity relationships","Integrity constraints where required","Transaction semantics where required"],"tradeoff":"Strong relational structure can improve integrity and query expressiveness while requiring deliberate schema evolution and modeling.","edge_case":"A relational database can also store semi-structured data, so relational versus document capability should not be reduced to rigid versus flexible schemas.","verification_method":"Map required integrity constraints and representative queries to supported relational capabilities.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DATABASE_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B04-R001","D41-B04-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B04-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established relational database principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B04-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Persistence architecture","concept":"Document-oriented modeling","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"When can document-oriented persistence be useful?","answer":"Document-oriented persistence can be useful when application data is naturally accessed as aggregate documents, schema flexibility is valuable, or query patterns align with document boundaries.","explanation":"Document modeling can reduce joins for access patterns centered on complete aggregates, but it can introduce duplication or update complexity when the same data must be maintained across many documents.","objective":"Determine whether document boundaries align with application access and ownership boundaries.","tradeoff":"Embedding related data can simplify reads but can increase duplication and update complexity.","edge_case":"Highly interconnected data may fit poorly with a document-centric access model unless access patterns are deliberately designed.","performance_implication":"Read performance depends on document size, indexing, access patterns, storage engine, and workload; no universal advantage should be claimed.","verification_method":"Map representative read and write operations to the proposed document structure and identify duplication/update consequences.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DATABASE_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B04-R001","D41-B04-R002"],"depends_on":["D41-B04-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["D41-B04-R002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established document-database modeling principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B04-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Persistence architecture","concept":"Database bottleneck independence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why does changing database technology not automatically solve scaling problems?","answer":"Database technology is only one part of a persistence architecture; schema design, access patterns, indexing, transactions, connection management, storage, network topology, and workload distribution can remain bottlenecks.","explanation":"Replacing one database technology with another without identifying the actual bottleneck can change complexity without solving the underlying constraint.","principle":"Architecture changes should target demonstrated bottlenecks or requirements.","rule":"Identify the bottleneck mechanism before selecting a replacement persistence technology.","objective":"Avoid technology substitution without causal justification.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require workload-specific measurement or carefully bounded analysis.","verification_method":"Profile representative workloads and identify the resource or operation limiting the system before changing persistence architecture.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B04-R001","D41-B04-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B04-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established performance diagnosis principle; synthesized record; no benchmark claim."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B04-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Persistence architecture","concept":"Polyglot persistence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the architectural trade-off of using multiple persistence technologies?","answer":"Polyglot persistence can align different workloads with different storage models, but it increases operational, consistency, data-movement, tooling, and engineering complexity.","explanation":"Using multiple persistence technologies can be justified when distinct requirements cannot be served effectively by one technology. It should not be introduced solely for technological variety.","objective":"Determine whether multiple persistence models provide sufficient architectural value to justify additional complexity.","requirements":["Distinct workload requirements","Clear ownership boundaries","Operational capability"],"constraints":["Each technology introduces its own lifecycle, backup, monitoring, security, and migration concerns"],"tradeoff":"Workload-specific fit versus increased operational and cognitive complexity.","edge_case":"A small system with limited operational resources may be harmed by unnecessary persistence diversity.","security_implication":"Each persistence technology creates an additional security configuration and access-control surface.","verification_method":"Document why each persistence technology exists and identify the data ownership and integration boundaries between them.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_TRADEOFF","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B04-R001","D41-B04-R002","D41-B04-R003"],"depends_on":["D41-B04-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established polyglot-persistence trade-off analysis; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B05-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Communication architecture","concept":"Synchronous versus asynchronous communication","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the core architectural trade-off between synchronous and asynchronous communication?","answer":"Synchronous communication couples a caller to timely availability and response from the callee, while asynchronous communication can decouple timing but requires explicit handling of delivery, ordering, retries, duplication, and eventual processing.","explanation":"The distinction is fundamentally about temporal coupling and coordination semantics. Asynchronous communication does not automatically improve reliability or performance; it changes the failure and state-management model.","principle":"Communication style should match required timing, consistency, failure, and workflow semantics.","objective":"Select communication semantics that match business and system requirements.","tradeoff":"Synchronous interaction can simplify immediate request-response semantics while increasing temporal coupling; asynchronous interaction can reduce temporal coupling while increasing coordination complexity.","edge_case":"A system may combine synchronous and asynchronous communication in different workflow stages.","verification_method":"Identify whether each operation requires an immediate response and define failure, retry, ordering, and duplicate-processing semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B05-R002","D41-B05-R003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed communication principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B05-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Communication architecture","concept":"Synchronous request dependency","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What risk is introduced by a synchronous dependency chain?","answer":"A synchronous dependency chain can propagate latency and failures across components because an upstream operation may remain dependent on downstream availability and response time.","explanation":"If A waits for B and B waits for C, a delay or failure in C can affect B and then A. Timeouts limit waiting but do not eliminate the dependency or necessarily prevent partial work.","failure_mode":"Latency amplification or cascading request failure.","root_cause":"Runtime dependency on downstream synchronous responses.","solution":"Use explicit timeouts, bounded retries where appropriate, failure isolation, or asynchronous processing when the workflow semantics permit it.","security_implication":"Untrusted or compromised dependencies can potentially consume resources through prolonged or repeated requests.","performance_implication":"End-to-end latency may accumulate across sequential synchronous calls.","edge_case":"A short synchronous dependency can be appropriate when immediate response semantics are essential and dependency reliability is acceptable.","verification_method":"Map synchronous call graphs and test downstream delay and failure scenarios.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_FAILURE_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B05-R001","D41-B05-R004"],"depends_on":["D41-B05-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed failure-propagation principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026