{"record_id":"D41-B05-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Communication architecture","concept":"Asynchronous workflow semantics","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What must an asynchronous architecture define explicitly?","answer":"It should define message durability expectations, delivery semantics, idempotency, retry behavior, ordering requirements, failure handling, acknowledgement semantics, and eventual state behavior where relevant.","explanation":"Removing synchronous waiting does not remove coordination. The architecture must instead define how work is represented and what happens when messages are delayed, duplicated, lost, reordered, or repeatedly failed.","principle":"Asynchronous systems require explicit temporal and delivery semantics.","rule":"Do not introduce asynchronous messaging without defining failure and duplicate-processing behavior.","objective":"Make asynchronous behavior deterministic enough for the required business semantics.","edge_case":"Some workflows tolerate duplicate or reordered events while others require strict ordering or idempotent processing.","security_implication":"Message authorization, integrity, confidentiality, replay protection, and tenant isolation may be required depending on the system.","verification_method":"Test duplicate, delayed, reordered, failed, and retried messages against the declared processing semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B05-R001","D41-B05-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B05-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established asynchronous-system engineering principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B05-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Communication architecture","concept":"Timeout as failure containment","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why are timeouts important in distributed communication?","answer":"Timeouts bound how long a component waits for an unavailable or slow dependency, preventing unbounded waiting and allowing the caller to enter an explicit failure path.","explanation":"A timeout is a control mechanism, not proof that the underlying operation failed. The remote operation may continue after the caller stops waiting unless the protocol supports cancellation.","principle":"Distributed waiting should have explicit temporal bounds.","rule":"Every synchronous remote dependency should have an intentional timeout policy appropriate to its operation.","failure_mode":"Unbounded waiting can consume threads, connections, memory, or other resources.","root_cause":"Missing or inappropriate timeout configuration.","solution":"Define bounded timeout behavior and explicit handling of timeout outcomes.","edge_case":"An overly short timeout can create false failures when legitimate operations exceed the selected bound.","performance_implication":"Timeout values affect resource retention and perceived latency; they should be based on workload and service objectives rather than arbitrary constants.","verification_method":"Inject delayed dependency responses and verify that timeout behavior, cleanup, retry policy, and error propagation match the design.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B05-R002","D41-B05-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B05-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed timeout and resource-management principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B05-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Communication architecture","concept":"Retry amplification","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can retries worsen a distributed failure?","answer":"Retries can amplify load on an already failing dependency, especially when multiple upstream components retry independently or without backoff and attempt limits.","explanation":"A retry mechanism changes traffic volume during failure. Without bounded attempts, appropriate backoff, and failure isolation, retries can consume resources and delay recovery.","failure_mode":"Retry storm or cascading overload.","root_cause":"Unbounded or synchronized retries against a failing dependency.","solution":"Use bounded retry policies, suitable backoff, jitter where appropriate, and explicit failure handling.","tradeoff":"Retries can improve recovery from transient failures but increase load and latency when failures are persistent.","edge_case":"Some operations are unsafe to retry unless idempotency or duplicate-operation semantics are guaranteed.","security_implication":"Attackers may exploit expensive retry paths to amplify resource consumption.","performance_implication":"Retries increase total request volume and can increase tail latency.","verification_method":"Test persistent and intermittent dependency failures while observing retry count, load, resource consumption, and recovery behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_FAILURE_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B05-R002","D41-B05-R004"],"depends_on":["D41-B05-R004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed retry and failure-amplification principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B06-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API architecture","concept":"REST versus RPC decision framing","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should REST and RPC be compared architecturally?","answer":"Compare them according to resource-oriented versus operation-oriented interaction models, contract requirements, client needs, transport characteristics, interoperability requirements, and system boundaries.","explanation":"REST and RPC are architectural communication approaches with different modeling tendencies. Neither is universally appropriate for every API.","principle":"API style should match the semantics and consumers of the interaction.","objective":"Select an API interaction model that represents the domain operations and integration requirements clearly.","requirements":["Define API consumers","Define interaction semantics","Define contract requirements","Define interoperability needs"],"tradeoff":"Resource-oriented interfaces can provide standardized interaction semantics, while RPC-style interfaces can model explicit operations directly; actual suitability depends on the domain and protocol.","edge_case":"A system can expose different API styles at different boundaries when their consumers and semantics differ.","verification_method":"Model representative operations in both styles and compare clarity, contract stability, tooling, and consumer requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"API_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B06-R002","D41-B06-R003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established API architecture principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2