{"record_id":"D41-B06-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API architecture","concept":"Resource-oriented API modeling","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What characterizes resource-oriented API modeling?","answer":"Resource-oriented modeling represents domain entities or resources and defines standardized operations over their representations and relationships.","explanation":"The model can make common CRUD-like interactions predictable, but complex domain operations may require explicit action semantics or additional resource modeling.","principle":"API structure should expose meaningful domain concepts rather than mirror arbitrary internal implementation details.","objective":"Provide a stable conceptual interface for resource-oriented consumers.","tradeoff":"Standardized resource interactions can improve uniformity while complex workflows may require additional modeling.","edge_case":"Long-running operations may need explicit job resources or other asynchronous representations.","security_implication":"Authorization should be evaluated against the requested resource and operation rather than inferred solely from URL structure.","verification_method":"Review whether representative domain operations map clearly to resources, representations, relationships, and allowed operations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"API_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B06-R001","D41-B06-R004"],"depends_on":["D41-B06-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["D41-B06-R003"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established resource-oriented API design principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B06-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API architecture","concept":"Operation-oriented API modeling","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can operation-oriented API modeling be useful?","answer":"Operation-oriented modeling can be useful when the domain contains explicit commands or procedures that do not map naturally to simple resource CRUD semantics.","explanation":"Operations such as calculate, approve, execute, or initiate may have complex semantics and can sometimes be represented more directly as explicit operations.","principle":"API modeling should represent domain behavior accurately rather than forcing every interaction into a simplistic resource abstraction.","objective":"Represent command-oriented workflows clearly and explicitly.","tradeoff":"Explicit operations can improve semantic clarity for complex commands while providing less uniformity than standardized resource interactions.","edge_case":"A command may change several resources and therefore require carefully defined transactional and failure semantics.","security_implication":"Authorization should cover the specific operation and affected resources.","verification_method":"Review complex domain workflows and determine whether resource operations or explicit commands provide a clearer stable contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"API_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B06-R001","D41-B06-R002"],"depends_on":["D41-B06-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":["D41-B06-R002"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established API modeling principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B06-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API architecture","concept":"API contract stability","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should API contract stability influence architecture selection?","answer":"An API used by independent consumers becomes a compatibility boundary, so architectural changes must account for versioning, evolution, backward compatibility, and migration requirements.","explanation":"An internally convenient API can become expensive to change after external or independently deployed consumers depend on it.","principle":"An interface becomes an architectural constraint when other components depend on it.","rule":"Evaluate contract evolution before exposing an interface as an independent boundary.","objective":"Prevent accidental long-term coupling through unstable interfaces.","edge_case":"An internal API with tightly controlled consumers may tolerate more coordinated evolution than a public API.","security_implication":"Contract changes can alter authorization, validation, and data-exposure behavior.","verification_method":"Identify consumers, compatibility requirements, versioning strategy, and migration paths for proposed API contracts.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"API_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B06-R001","D41-B06-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B06-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established API compatibility principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B06-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API architecture","concept":"API gateway versus direct service exposure","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the architectural trade-off of placing a gateway between clients and services?","answer":"A gateway can centralize cross-cutting concerns such as routing, authentication integration, rate limiting, or aggregation, but it can also become an additional dependency and potential bottleneck or failure domain.","explanation":"The value of a gateway depends on which concerns genuinely benefit from centralized handling and whether the gateway itself is resilient and appropriately scoped.","objective":"Determine whether centralized boundary concerns justify the additional architectural component.","tradeoff":"Centralization can simplify client interaction and policy enforcement while adding infrastructure complexity and another runtime dependency.","security_implication":"A gateway can provide a controlled ingress boundary but must not become the sole assumption for downstream authorization.","performance_implication":"Gateway processing and additional network hops may affect latency and throughput; actual impact requires workload-specific measurement.","edge_case":"Internal systems may use direct service communication while external traffic uses a gateway.","verification_method":"Identify gateway responsibilities, failure behavior, scaling model, and whether downstream services independently enforce required security policies.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_TRADEOFF","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B06-R004","D41-B07-R001"],"depends_on":["D41-B06-R004"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"prove