{"record_id":"D41-B07-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment topology","concept":"Local versus distributed execution","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the architectural trade-off between local and distributed execution?","answer":"Local execution can reduce network coordination and simplify state access, while distributed execution can provide isolation, independent scaling, geographic placement, or organizational separation at the cost of distributed-system complexity.","explanation":"Distribution changes the failure model because components communicate through potentially unreliable boundaries. It should be introduced for a concrete requirement rather than treated as an automatic architectural improvement.","principle":"Distribution is a capability with associated coordination and failure costs.","objective":"Determine whether distribution provides sufficient value for the system's requirements.","tradeoff":"Local execution generally reduces communication complexity; distributed execution can improve isolation and independence but adds network and operational concerns.","edge_case":"A system can be logically distributed while some components remain co-located for latency or reliability reasons.","verification_method":"List the concrete requirement motivating distribution and validate whether the benefit depends on actual physical separation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B07-R002","D41-B07-R003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed architecture principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B07-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment topology","concept":"Network boundary failure model","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What changes when a local dependency becomes a distributed dependency?","answer":"The dependency becomes subject to network latency, connection failure, partial failure, serialization, timeout, retry, and availability concerns that may not exist in the same form for local calls.","explanation":"Moving a component across a process or network boundary is an architectural change, not merely a deployment change. The communication contract must explicitly handle the new failure model.","principle":"A distributed boundary introduces independent failure and timing behavior.","rule":"Reevaluate error handling, timeouts, retries, serialization, and observability whenever a local dependency becomes remote.","objective":"Prevent local assumptions from surviving an architectural boundary change.","failure_mode":"Code assumes local-call reliability and fails under network conditions.","root_cause":"Distribution introduced without adapting interaction semantics.","solution":"Define explicit remote-call contracts and failure handling.","verification_method":"Inject network delay, connection failure, malformed responses, and partial availability into the distributed dependency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B07-R001","D41-B07-R004"],"depends_on":["D41-B07-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed failure-model principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B07-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment topology","concept":"Co-location for latency-sensitive components","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When can co-locating components be architecturally useful?","answer":"Co-location can be useful when components have latency-sensitive interactions, high communication volume, or shared local-resource requirements that make a network boundary undesirable.","explanation":"Co-location can reduce network communication but may increase shared failure scope and reduce independent deployment or scaling options.","objective":"Determine whether communication characteristics justify co-location.","tradeoff":"Lower communication overhead versus reduced physical isolation and deployment independence.","edge_case":"Co-location may be useful temporarily while boundaries are stabilized and later distribution is introduced deliberately.","performance_implication":"Potential latency and serialization savings must be validated against actual workload characteristics rather than assumed.","verification_method":"Measure representative communication patterns and determine whether network separation materially affects required latency or throughput.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_TRADEOFF","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B07-R001","D41-B07-R002"],"depends_on":["D41-B07-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established deployment-topology trade-off principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B07-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment topology","concept":"Failure-domain separation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can distributed deployment improve failure isolation?","answer":"Separating components into distinct failure domains can prevent certain failures from directly consuming the same process or host resources, provided dependencies and shared infrastructure do not recreate the same failure path.","explanation":"Physical or process separation can reduce some blast-radius paths but does not guarantee isolation. Shared databases, networks, control planes, or orchestration infrastructure can still create common failure domains.","principle":"Failure isolation depends on actual shared dependencies, not merely process boundaries.","objective":"Evaluate whether distribution provides meaningful failure containment.","constraints":["Shared infrastructure may preserve common failure modes"],"failure_mode":"Assuming independent services are isolated when they share a critical dependency.","root_cause":"Incomplete failure-domain analysis.","solution":"Map shared resources and model dependency failure propagation.","verification_method":"Construct a dependency and failure-domain graph and test representative component failures.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"RELIABILITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B07-R002","D41-B07-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B07-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established reliability and failure-domain analysis principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B07-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment topology","concept":"Distributed operational burden","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What additional operational concerns can distribution introduce?","answer":"Distribution can introduce service discovery, network observability, distributed tracing, certificate management, deployment coordination, configuration management, capacity management, and failure diagnosis requirements.","explanation":"The exact burden depends on topology and tooling. These concerns should be evaluated before introducing additional distributed components.","objective":"Include operational complexity in architecture cost analysis.","tradeoff":"Operational complexity increases as more independently communicating components and infrastructure boundaries are introduced.","edge_case":"Strong automation and platform capabilities can reduce operational burden but do not eliminate distributed failure semantics.","verification_method":"Inventory new operational responsibilities created by each additional runtime boundary and verify ownership for each responsibility.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"OPERATIONS_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B07-R001","D41-B08-R001"],"depends_on":["D41-B07-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed operations principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B08-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Event-driven architecture","concept":"Event-driven versus request-driven architecture","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the core trade-off between event-driven and request-driven architecture?","answer":"Request-driven architecture emphasizes explicit requester-to-provider interactions, while event-driven architecture emphasizes publication of state or business events consumed by interested components.","explanation":"Event-driven designs can reduce direct coupling between producers and consumers but introduce temporal, delivery, ordering, and observability complexity.","principle":"Interaction style should match whether the system primarily needs direct responses or propagation of facts/events to multiple consumers.","objective":"Select communication semantics appropriate to workflow and coupling requirements.","tradeoff":"Event-driven communication can reduce direct producer-consumer coupling while increasing asynchronous coordination complexity.","edge_case":"A system may use request-driven APIs for commands and event-driven propagation for resulting facts.","verification_method":"Classify representative interactions as commands, queries, or events and evaluate required response timing and coupling.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B08-R002","D41-B08-R003"],"depends_on":["D41-B05-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established event-driven architecture principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B08-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Event-driven architecture","concept":"Event producer-consumer decoupling","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How can events reduce direct coupling?","answer":"A producer can publish an event without directly invoking every consumer, allowing consumers to subscribe independently when the event contract and delivery infrastructure support that model.","explanation":"The producer still depends on the event schema and delivery mechanism. Event-driven architecture therefore shifts some coupling from direct runtime invocation to contracts and event infrastructure.","principle":"Removing direct calls does not eliminate coupling; it changes its form.","objective":"Understand the actual coupling characteristics of event-driven systems.","tradeoff":"Reduced direct dependency versus increased dependency on event contracts, delivery semantics, and observability.","edge_case":"A producer may still become tightly coupled if consumers require unstable or producer-specific event formats.","verification_method":"Map producer dependencies to event contracts and identify whether consumers can evolve independently.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"EVENT_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B08-R001","D41-B08-R004"],"depends_on":["D41-B08-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established event-driven coupling principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B08-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Event-driven architecture","concept":"Eventual processing delay","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What architectural consequence follows from asynchronous event processing?","answer":"A consumer may process an event later than the producer emitted it, so dependent state may temporarily differ across components.","explanation":"Asynchronous processing introduces temporal separation. Systems must define whether temporary divergence is acceptable and how users or downstream processes observe processing state.","principle":"Asynchronous propagation implies a time interval during which derived state may not yet reflect the source event.","failure_mode":"Consumers observe stale or temporarily inconsistent derived state.","root_cause":"Expected asynchronous processing delay is treated as impossible or unspecified.","solution":"Define freshness expectations, processing states, reconciliation behavior, and user-visible semantics.","edge_case":"Some workflows require immediate consistency and therefore may not tolerate asynchronous propagation at a particular boundary.","verification_method":"Inject processing delay and verify that system behavior remains within declared freshness and consistency requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B08-R001","D41-B08-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B08-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established asynchronous consistency principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B08-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Event-driven architecture","concept":"Event contract evolution","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must event schemas be treated as contracts?","answer":"Consumers can depend on event fields and semantics, so changes to event structure or meaning can break independently evolving consumers.","explanation":"An event can become a durable integration boundary. Contract evolution therefore requires compatibility analysis and explicit migration strategy.","principle":"Published events are interfaces when independent consumers rely on them.","rule":"Treat externally consumed event schemas and semantics as versioned contracts where compatibility matters.","objective":"Prevent silent breakage across asynchronous consumers.","edge_case":"An internal event with a single controlled consumer may have different evolution requirements.","security_implication":"Event changes must be reviewed for newly exposed data or changed authorization assumptions.","verification_method":"Identify consumers and validate schema changes against compatibility requirements and consumer tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"EVENT_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B08-R002","D41-B08-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B08-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established event-contract evolution principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B08-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Event-driven architecture","concept":"Command versus event distinction","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should commands and events be distinguished?","answer":"A command expresses requested action, while an event represents something that has occurred; confusing them can create incorrect ownership and processing semantics.","explanation":"Commands generally have an intended recipient and represent an instruction. Events represent facts that may have multiple interested consumers. Their failure, ownership, and retry semantics can therefore differ.","principle":"Interaction semantics should reflect whether the message requests behavior or records a fact.","rule":"Do not model an instruction as a historical fact merely to reuse an event mechanism.","objective":"Preserve clear intent and ownership in message-driven architecture.","edge_case":"A system may use command messages asynchronously, but their semantic distinction from events should remain explicit.","verification_method":"Inspect message definitions and determine whether each represents an imperative request or an already-observed fact.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"EVENT_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B08-R001","D41-B08-R004"],"depends_on":["D41-B08-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established event-driven domain modeling principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B09-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency architecture","concept":"Strong versus eventual consistency","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should strong and eventual consistency be compared?","answer":"Strong consistency prioritizes coordinated visibility of updates according to the selected consistency model, while eventual consistency permits temporary divergence with an expectation that replicas or derived state converge under specified conditions.","explanation":"The terms describe consistency behavior, not a universal quality ranking. The appropriate model depends on correctness requirements, latency, availability, partition behavior, and workflow semantics.","principle":"Consistency requirements should be derived from business correctness rather than chosen as a default ideology.","objective":"Select consistency semantics that preserve required correctness while permitting acceptable system behavior.","requirements":["Define stale-read tolerance","Define conflict behavior","Define convergence expectations","Define correctness invariants"],"tradeoff":"Stronger coordination can simplify some correctness guarantees but may increase coordination cost or reduce availability under certain failure conditions; eventual consistency can improve decoupling or availability characteristics while requiring reconciliation and stale-state handling.","edge_case":"Different entities or operations in the same system may legitimately require different consistency semantics.","verification_method":"Define invariants and test reads, writes, concurrent updates, and failure scenarios against the selected consistency model.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_DATA_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B09-R002","D41-B09-R003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed consistency principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B09-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency architecture","concept":"Business invariant identification","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should consistency requirements be derived from business requirements?","answer":"Identify invariants that must remain true, determine which operations depend on those invariants, and then select consistency and transaction mechanisms capable of preserving them under expected concurrency and failure conditions.","explanation":"Consistency should be defined in terms of observable correctness. A system does not need identical visibility timing for every piece of data if the required business invariants remain protected.","principle":"Consistency is a means of preserving correctness properties.","objective":"Avoid over-constraining architecture with unnecessary consistency requirements.","requirements":["Identify invariants","Identify concurrent operations","Identify acceptable stale-state windows","Define failure behavior"],"edge_case":"An invariant may cross service or database boundaries and therefore require architectural coordination.","verification_method":"Create concurrent and failure scenarios and verify that declared invariants remain true.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_REASONING","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B09-R001","D41-B09-R004"],"depends_on":["D41-B09-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established consistency-analysis methodology; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B09-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency architecture","concept":"Stale-read tolerance","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why is stale-read tolerance an important architecture decision driver?","answer":"If a consumer can safely operate on data that is not the newest version, eventual or asynchronously derived state may be acceptable; if stale data can cause incorrect or unsafe behavior, stronger coordination may be required.","explanation":"Staleness is not inherently a defect. Its acceptability depends on the semantics of the operation and the consequences of using older information.","principle":"The acceptable consistency window is domain-specific.","objective":"Make consistency requirements explicit instead of assuming every read requires the latest state.","edge_case":"The same data may permit stale reads for display but require stronger guarantees for an authorization or financial decision.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive decisions should not rely on stale authorization state unless the security model explicitly permits that behavior.","verification_method":"Classify each critical read by maximum acceptable staleness and test behavior near and beyond that boundary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"CONSISTENCY_REQUIREMENT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B09-R001","D41-B09-R002"],"depends_on":["D41-B09-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established consistency and correctness analysis; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B09-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency architecture","concept":"Conflict resolution","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why does eventual consistency require conflict handling?","answer":"Independent updates can temporarily diverge and later converge in ways that produce conflicting values or state transitions, so the system needs an explicit conflict policy when conflicts are possible.","explanation":"Conflict resolution may use domain rules, versioning, causal information, last-write policies, merges, manual review, or rejection. The correct mechanism depends on the semantics of the data.","failure_mode":"Conflicting updates cannot be reconciled correctly.","root_cause":"Eventual convergence was designed without a domain-specific conflict model.","solution":"Define conflict detection and resolution semantics before adopting a consistency model that permits conflicting concurrent updates.","tradeoff":"Automated conflict resolution can improve availability and throughput but may encode irreversible or incorrect domain decisions if the rule is inappropriate.","edge_case":"Some domains make conflicts impossible through partitioning or ownership constraints.","security_implication":"Conflict resolution must not permit unauthorized state to overwrite authoritative state.","verification_method":"Generate concurrent conflicting updates and verify that the resulting state follows the declared domain policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_DATA_FAILURE_ANALYSIS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B09-R001","D41-B09-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B09-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established distributed-data conflict-resolution principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B09-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency architecture","concept":"Consistency boundary selection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can consistency requirements be scoped instead of applied globally?","answer":"Define consistency boundaries around entities, operations, or workflows according to their correctness requirements rather than imposing one consistency level on every subsystem.","explanation":"Different operations can have different tolerance for stale state and coordination. Explicit boundaries allow the architecture to spend coordination effort where correctness requires it.","principle":"Consistency should be scoped to the smallest boundary that preserves required invariants.","objective":"Avoid unnecessary global coordination while protecting critical correctness properties.","tradeoff":"Fine-grained consistency boundaries can improve efficiency but increase architectural complexity and require precise documentation.","edge_case":"Cross-boundary invariants may still require coordination or compensating mechanisms.","verification_method":"Map invariants to consistency boundaries and test cross-boundary failure scenarios.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B09-R002","D41-B10-R001"],"depends_on":["D41-B09-R002"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established consistency-boundary design principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B10-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Cross-cutting architecture selection","concept":"Architecture decision matrix","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How can an architecture decision matrix support deterministic reasoning?","answer":"Represent candidates against explicit decision criteria, hard constraints, evidence, risks, and consequences so a reasoning system can compare candidates without relying on undocumented preferences.","explanation":"A matrix is useful when the criteria are explicit and the values are evidence-backed or clearly marked as assumptions. It should not hide uncertainty behind arbitrary scores.","principle":"Structured criteria improve traceability when their semantics are explicit.","objective":"Make architecture comparisons machine-readable and reproducible.","requirements":["Candidate list","Criteria","Constraint status","Evidence","Trade-offs","Uncertainty"],"constraints":["Numerical scores should not be treated as objective truth","Criteria weights require explicit justification"],"tradeoff":"Structured comparison improves consistency but can create false precision if uncertain judgments are represented as exact numbers.","verification_method":"Audit each criterion for definition, evidence, applicability, and uncertainty before using it in a decision.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B01-R001","D41-B10-R002"],"depends_on":["D41-B01-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture decision-analysis practice; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B10-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Cross-cutting architecture selection","concept":"Avoiding false precision in architecture scoring","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should architecture scores not be treated as objective truth?","answer":"Architecture criteria often contain context-dependent estimates and uncertain judgments, so a numerical score can create an appearance of precision that exceeds the underlying evidence.","explanation":"A score can be useful as a transparent decision aid when its definitions and evidence are explicit, but it should not conceal uncertainty or substitute for engineering reasoning.","principle":"Representation precision must not exceed evidence precision.","rule":"Record evidence and uncertainty alongside any comparative scoring mechanism.","objective":"Prevent deterministic reasoning systems from treating subjective estimates as verified facts.","edge_case":"Measured operational criteria can support quantitative comparison when measurement conditions are explicitly preserved.","verification_method":"Trace each quantitative value to measured evidence or explicitly classified estimation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"DECISION_ANALYSIS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B10-R001","D41-B10-R003"],"depends_on":["D41-B10-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established evidence-quality and decision-analysis principle; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B10-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Cross-cutting architecture selection","concept":"Architecture fitness under changing requirements","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can an architecture decision become invalid over time?","answer":"Architecture fitness depends on requirements, workload, dependencies, organizational constraints, technology lifecycle, and operational conditions, all of which can change.","explanation":"An architecture should therefore retain the assumptions that justified it. When important assumptions change, the decision should be reevaluated rather than treated as permanent truth.","principle":"Architectural validity is conditional on its decision context.","objective":"Enable future reassessment without losing historical rationale.","requirements":["Record assumptions","Record decision drivers","Record lifecycle conditions"],"edge_case":"A decision may remain valid even after some assumptions change if the affected assumptions were not material to the decision.","verification_method":"Periodically compare recorded decision assumptions against current requirements and operating conditions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B01-R003","D41-B10-R004"],"depends_on":["D41-B01-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture lifecycle principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B10-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Cross-cutting architecture selection","concept":"Architecture migration trigger","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should trigger architectural reevaluation?","answer":"Reevaluation should be triggered by material changes in requirements, workload, reliability needs, security boundaries, regulatory constraints, technology lifecycle, operating cost, or previously invalidated assumptions.","explanation":"Architecture should not be changed continuously without cause, but significant changes in decision drivers can invalidate the original trade-off analysis.","objective":"Provide explicit conditions for reassessing an existing architecture.","requirements":["Define material change criteria","Track relevant assumptions","Preserve prior decision evidence"],"edge_case":"Minor changes that do not affect decision drivers should not automatically trigger architectural migration.","tradeoff":"Frequent reevaluation improves responsiveness but can cause unnecessary churn if triggers are poorly defined.","verification_method":"Compare current system conditions with the assumptions and constraints recorded in the original decision.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_LIFECYCLE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B10-R003","D41-B10-R005"],"depends_on":["D41-B10-R003"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established architecture lifecycle and change-management principles; synthesized record."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D41-B10-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Cross-cutting architecture selection","concept":"Trade-off evidence ledger","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should an evidence ledger preserve for an architecture trade-off?","answer":"An evidence ledger should connect each important architectural claim to its evidence type, source or measurement, applicability, uncertainty, and decision consequence.","explanation":"This prevents assumptions, estimates, benchmarks, observations, and established facts from being silently treated as equivalent evidence.","principle":"Architectural reasoning should preserve the provenance and epistemic status of its inputs.","objective":"Enable the Brain to distinguish verified evidence from inference during architecture reasoning.","requirements":["Claim","Evidence","Evidence type","Applicability","Uncertainty","Decision consequence"],"constraints":["No fabricated provenance","No fabricated measurements"],"edge_case":"Some architectural claims may remain unresolved and should be represented as UNKNOWN rather than forced into a conclusion.","verification_method":"Audit whether every high-impact claim in an architecture decision has an identifiable evidence status and limitation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"EVIDENCE_ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D41-B10-R001","D41-B10-R002","D41-B10-R003"],"depends_on":["D41-B10-R001"],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Established evidence-traceability principles; synthesized record; no fabricated empirical evidence."],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D41-B11-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Service Decomposition","concept":"Bounded-context-aligned decomposition","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"When should a system boundary be aligned with a business or domain boundary?","answer":"A service or module boundary should usually align with a cohesive domain responsibility when that boundary has clear ownership, relatively stable interfaces, and limited need for synchronous coordination with unrelated responsibilities.","principle":"Architectural boundaries should minimize unnecessary coupling while preserving cohesive ownership.","requirements":["clear responsibility","explicit interface","manageable coupling"],"constraints":["domain boundaries may evolve","some workflows legitimately cross boundaries"],"alternative":"Technical-layer decomposition can be used when domain boundaries are immature or the system is primarily infrastructure-oriented.","tradeoff":"Domain-aligned boundaries improve ownership and change isolation but may require explicit coordination across domains.","edge_case":"A domain that appears independent may still require frequent transactional coordination with another domain.","verification_method":"Inspect dependency graph, change coupling, ownership boundaries, and cross-boundary workflow frequency.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B01-R001","D41-B02-R004"],"depends_on":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Microservices architecture literature","Eric Evans, Domain-Driven Design"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B11-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Service Decomposition","concept":"Fine-grained versus coarse-grained services","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the architectural trade-off between fine-grained and coarse-grained services?","answer":"Fine-grained services can improve independent ownership and deployment but increase network calls, operational overhead, distributed failure modes, and coordination complexity. Coarse-grained services reduce distributed complexity but can increase internal coupling and deployment scope.","requirements":["identify ownership","measure communication patterns","identify operational constraints"],"constraints":["network boundaries introduce failure","service count alone does not determine architecture quality"],"alternative":"A modular monolith can provide strong internal boundaries without immediately introducing network boundaries.","tradeoff":"Fine granularity favors independent evolution; coarse granularity favors simpler coordination and operation.","security_implication":"More service boundaries create more network trust boundaries and credential/configuration surfaces.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained decomposition may increase latency through additional network hops.","verification_method":"Compare dependency graph, deployment independence, communication frequency, latency, failure propagation, and team ownership.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B01-R002","D41-B02-R003"],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Microservices architecture literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B11-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Service Decomposition","concept":"Shared database versus service-owned persistence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What should be considered when choosing shared database access versus service-owned persistence?","answer":"Shared persistence can simplify transactions and reporting but increases coupling through schemas and shared operational dependencies. Service-owned persistence improves ownership and isolation but requires explicit APIs, events, synchronization, or distributed consistency mechanisms.","requirements":["identify ownership","identify transaction boundaries","identify reporting requirements"],"constraints":["cross-service transactions become harder with independent persistence","shared schemas can become hidden contracts"],"tradeoff":"Shared persistence favors immediate consistency and simpler cross-domain queries; independent persistence favors autonomy and change isolation.","security_implication":"Shared database credentials and broad data access can enlarge the blast radius of compromise.","performance_implication":"Independent persistence may require additional network or replication operations.","verification_method":"Map data ownership, write paths, schema dependencies, transaction requirements, and access privileges.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B04-R001","D41-B09-R002"],"provenance":["Martin Fowler, Microservices architecture literature","Eric Evans, Domain-Driven Design"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B11-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Service Decomposition","concept":"Shared library versus duplicated implementation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When is a shared library preferable to duplicating small amounts of implementation across components?","answer":"A shared library is valuable when behavior must remain consistent, the abstraction is stable, and independent versioning is not essential. Duplication may be preferable when coupling through a shared release cycle would cost more than maintaining small independent implementations.","requirements":["assess abstraction stability","assess change frequency","assess ownership"],"constraints":["shared libraries create dependency coupling","duplication can cause behavioral drift"],"alternative":"Generate or maintain small adapters around a stable shared primitive.","tradeoff":"Reuse reduces duplication but increases dependency coupling; duplication increases local autonomy but may increase maintenance cost.","verification_method":"Compare change frequency, dependency graph, release coupling, semantic stability, and duplicated defect rates.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B02-R005"],"provenance":["Software engineering modularity and reuse literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B11-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Service Decomposition","concept":"Architecture boundary decision evidence","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What evidence should support a new architectural boundary?","answer":"A boundary decision should be supported by responsibility ownership, dependency structure, change patterns, communication requirements, transaction requirements, security boundaries, deployment needs, and operational constraints.","requirements":["document decision drivers","identify rejected alternatives","record assumptions"],"constraints":["early architectural evidence may be incomplete"],"expected_behavior":"A boundary decision should be explainable and revisable when new evidence appears.","verification_method":"Review decision record against dependency, ownership, runtime, security, and operational evidence.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B01-R001","D41-B10-R005"],"provenance":["Architecture decision record practices","Software architecture decision-making literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B12-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Boundary Design","concept":"API gateway versus direct service access","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the architectural trade-offs between an API gateway and direct client-to-service communication?","answer":"An API gateway can centralize routing, authentication integration, rate limiting, and aggregation, while direct access can reduce intermediary complexity and latency. A gateway also becomes an additional availability and operational dependency if poorly designed.","requirements":["identify client types","identify cross-cutting concerns","define failure behavior"],"constraints":["gateway must not become an uncontrolled business-logic monolith"],"security_implication":"A gateway can centralize security controls but also becomes a high-value attack surface.","performance_implication":"Additional hops and aggregation can affect latency.","verification_method":"Evaluate routing, security, failure isolation, latency, scaling, and ownership requirements.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B06-R001","D41-B07-R002"],"provenance":["Microservices architecture literature","OWASP API Security guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B12-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Boundary Design","concept":"Synchronous API versus asynchronous messaging","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should an architecture prefer synchronous request-response over asynchronous messaging?","answer":"Synchronous communication is appropriate when the caller requires an immediate response and the dependency is part of the request's critical path. Asynchronous messaging is appropriate when work can be decoupled, delayed, retried, buffered, or processed independently.","requirements":["define response requirement","define latency tolerance","define failure semantics"],"constraints":["asynchronous systems require explicit delivery and consistency handling"],"tradeoff":"Synchronous communication simplifies immediate response semantics but increases temporal coupling; asynchronous communication improves decoupling but introduces delivery, ordering, and eventual-consistency concerns.","verification_method":"Classify each interaction by response necessity, latency tolerance, delivery semantics, and dependency criticality.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B05-R001","D41-B08-R002"],"provenance":["Distributed systems engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B12-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Boundary Design","concept":"API composition versus client-side composition","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the trade-offs between composing multiple backend responses in a gateway and composing them in the client?","answer":"Backend composition can centralize aggregation and reduce client complexity but increases gateway responsibility and potentially server-side latency. Client composition distributes responsibility but can increase network calls and expose internal service topology.","requirements":["identify client capabilities","define ownership of composition","measure request patterns"],"constraints":["client must not require unstable internal APIs"],"tradeoff":"Backend composition favors centralized control; client composition favors simpler backend aggregation responsibility.","security_implication":"Client-side composition can expose more APIs and authorization surfaces.","performance_implication":"Composition location changes network round trips and payload sizes.","verification_method":"Measure request count, latency, authorization complexity, failure handling, and client diversity.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B12-R001"],"provenance":["API architecture literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B12-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Boundary Design","concept":"Versioned API versus backward-compatible evolution","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should every API change create a new version?","answer":"Not necessarily. Backward-compatible changes can often evolve an API without creating a new version. A new version is justified when compatibility cannot be preserved or when semantic changes require clients to adopt different behavior.","requirements":["identify consumers","classify compatibility","define migration path"],"constraints":["version proliferation increases maintenance burden"],"tradeoff":"Explicit versions isolate breaking changes but create parallel contracts; compatibility-first evolution reduces version proliferation but constrains change.","verification_method":"Evaluate schema compatibility, semantic compatibility, consumer behavior, and migration requirements.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B10-R003"],"provenance":["API compatibility engineering practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B12-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Boundary Design","concept":"Contract-first versus implementation-first API design","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the difference between contract-first and implementation-first API development?","answer":"Contract-first development defines externally observable behavior before implementation, enabling independent validation and consumer coordination. Implementation-first development can move quickly for exploratory work but may allow the API contract to emerge from internal structure.","requirements":["define external behavior","identify consumers","establish compatibility rules"],"constraints":["contract-first design requires sufficient understanding of requirements"],"tradeoff":"Contract-first favors interoperability and explicit boundaries; implementation-first favors rapid exploration.","verification_method":"Compare declared contract with implementation behavior and consumer expectations.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B10-R001"],"provenance":["API design and contract engineering practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B13-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Architecture","concept":"CQRS applicability","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"When can CQRS provide meaningful architectural value?","answer":"CQRS can be useful when command and query workloads, models, consistency requirements, or scaling characteristics differ substantially. It introduces additional models and synchronization complexity and should not be used solely because separate read and write models appear conceptually clean.","requirements":["distinct read/write characteristics","clear consistency requirements","operational capability"],"constraints":["additional synchronization complexity","eventual consistency may be introduced"],"tradeoff":"CQRS can optimize divergent workloads and models but increases architectural complexity and data synchronization requirements.","performance_implication":"Independent read models can be optimized for query workloads.","verification_method":"Determine whether read/write requirements materially differ and whether added synchronization complexity is justified.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B09-R001","D41-B08-R003"],"provenance":["Greg Young, CQRS architecture literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B13-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Architecture","concept":"Event sourcing applicability","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What factors determine whether event sourcing is appropriate?","answer":"Event sourcing is appropriate when the sequence of domain events is itself valuable, historical reconstruction matters, and the organization can operate append-oriented event storage and projections. It is inappropriate when historical event semantics provide little value relative to the added operational and modeling complexity.","requirements":["event semantics must be meaningful","history requirements must justify complexity","projection strategy must be defined"],"constraints":["schema evolution is required","event correction requires explicit strategies"],"tradeoff":"Event sourcing provides durable event history and reconstruction capabilities at the cost of modeling, storage, projection, and evolution complexity.","verification_method":"Assess audit requirements, reconstruction needs, event semantics, projection requirements, and operational maturity.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B13-R001"],"provenance":["Domain-Driven Design and Event Sourcing literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B13-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Architecture","concept":"Normalized versus denormalized read models","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why might an architecture deliberately denormalize data?","answer":"Denormalization can reduce expensive joins and optimize read-oriented workloads by storing data in a form closer to query requirements. The cost is increased duplication, synchronization responsibility, and possible stale data.","requirements":["identify read workload","define synchronization strategy","define consistency tolerance"],"constraints":["duplicated data must have an ownership source","staleness must be acceptable"],"tradeoff":"Normalization favors integrity and reduced duplication; denormalization favors read efficiency and query simplicity.","performance_implication":"Denormalization can reduce query-time joins but increases write and synchronization work.","verification_method":"Compare measured query workload, update frequency, consistency requirements, and storage cost.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B04-R003"],"provenance":["Database systems literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B13-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Architecture","concept":"Database-per-service versus shared schema","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What architectural consequence follows from giving each service independent persistence?","answer":"Independent persistence establishes stronger data ownership and service autonomy but removes the ability to rely on simple cross-service database transactions and joins. Cross-boundary workflows require explicit integration mechanisms.","requirements":["explicit ownership","integration contracts","consistency strategy"],"constraints":["distributed transactions may be unavailable or undesirable"],"tradeoff":"Independent persistence favors autonomy; shared schemas favor simple relational coordination.","security_implication":"Independent stores can narrow data-access privileges when ownership is correctly enforced.","verification_method":"Inspect data ownership, cross-boundary writes, transaction requirements, and access privileges.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B11-R003"],"provenance":["Microservices architecture literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B13-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Architecture","concept":"Polyglot persistence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When is using multiple persistence technologies architecturally justified?","answer":"Polyglot persistence is justified when different workloads have materially different storage requirements that cannot be served adequately by one technology. It increases operational, backup, observability, migration, and skill complexity.","requirements":["demonstrable workload mismatch","operational ownership","backup and recovery strategy"],"constraints":["each additional technology creates operational overhead"],"tradeoff":"Multiple specialized stores can improve workload fit while increasing platform complexity.","performance_implication":"Specialized stores may better match access patterns but introduce integration overhead.","verification_method":"Document workload requirements and compare them against the capabilities and operational costs of candidate technologies.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B04-R004"],"provenance":["Database architecture literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B14-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Distributed Coordination","concept":"Saga versus distributed transaction","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why might a saga be selected instead of a distributed transaction?","answer":"A saga coordinates a multi-step business operation through local transactions and compensating actions, avoiding some distributed transaction coupling. It introduces explicit workflow state, compensation logic, and temporary inconsistency.","requirements":["business operation can be decomposed","compensation semantics exist","workflow state can be tracked"],"constraints":["compensation is not equivalent to rollback in every domain"],"tradeoff":"Sagas favor service autonomy and availability patterns but require explicit compensation and failure handling.","security_implication":"Authorization must remain valid across each workflow step.","verification_method":"Verify each step's local transaction, compensation behavior, failure path, and final-state invariants.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B05-R004"],"provenance":["Microservices distributed transaction and saga literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B14-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Distributed Coordination","concept":"Orchestration versus choreography","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the trade-off between orchestration and choreography in distributed workflows?","answer":"Orchestration centralizes workflow coordination and makes process flow explicit, while choreography distributes coordination through events and participant reactions. Orchestration can simplify reasoning about workflow state; choreography can reduce central coordination but make global behavior harder to trace.","requirements":["define workflow ownership","define failure handling","define observability"],"constraints":["distributed workflows require traceable state regardless of coordination style"],"tradeoff":"Orchestration centralizes process knowledge; choreography distributes it.","verification_method":"Evaluate workflow visibility, coupling, failure handling, evolution, and observability.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B08-R002"],"provenance":["Distributed workflow architecture literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B14-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Distributed Coordination","concept":"Idempotency as an architectural boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should idempotency be considered during distributed architecture design?","answer":"Retries, duplicate delivery, client repetition, and partial failures can cause the same operation to be requested more than once. Idempotent operation design reduces the risk that repeated requests create unintended duplicate effects.","requirements":["identify repeatable operations","define idempotency key or equivalent mechanism when required","define duplicate behavior"],"constraints":["not every operation can be made naturally idempotent","idempotency state may require durable storage"],"security_implication":"Idempotency mechanisms must prevent unauthorized reuse of another request's identity or key.","verification_method":"Execute or simulate repeated requests and verify the resulting business state against the intended invariant.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B05-R003"],"provenance":["Distributed systems reliability literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B14-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Distributed Coordination","concept":"At-least-once versus exactly-once claims","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why should architecture decisions distinguish delivery semantics from business-effect semantics?","answer":"A messaging system's delivery guarantee does not automatically prove that a business operation occurs exactly once. At-least-once delivery may produce duplicates that require idempotent consumers, while stronger end-to-end semantics require analysis of the complete processing path.","requirements":["define delivery semantics","define processing semantics","define business-effect invariant"],"constraints":["transport guarantees do not automatically extend across application boundaries"],"verification_method":"Trace duplicate, retry, crash, and acknowledgement scenarios through the full processing path.","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B14-R003"],"provenance":["Distributed systems literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B14-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Distributed Coordination","concept":"Timeout boundary placement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should timeout ownership be explicit in distributed architectures?","answer":"Without explicit timeout boundaries, a failed or slow dependency can consume resources across multiple layers. Each boundary should define an appropriate timeout, cancellation behavior, and failure propagation strategy.","requirements":["define caller timeout","define downstream timeout","define cancellation behavior"],"constraints":["timeouts must reflect realistic dependency behavior","arbitrary short timeouts can create false failures"],"tradeoff":"Tighter timeouts improve failure detection but can reject valid slow operations; longer timeouts preserve patience but consume resources longer.","performance_implication":"Timeout configuration directly affects resource occupancy and tail latency.","verification_method":"Test slow dependency, timeout, cancellation, and retry scenarios while observing resource release.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B07-R003"],"provenance":["Distributed systems reliability engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B15-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resilience Architecture","concept":"Retry versus fail-fast","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When can retries improve resilience and when can they make failures worse?","answer":"Retries can recover from transient failures, but repeated retries against an overloaded or permanently failing dependency can amplify load and delay recovery. Retry policy must consider error classification, backoff, attempt limits, and idempotency.","requirements":["classify transient versus permanent failures","define retry limit","define backoff","verify idempotency"],"constraints":["retries consume resources","not all operations are safe to repeat"],"security_implication":"Retry behavior must not accidentally repeat sensitive operations without proper authorization and idempotency controls.","performance_implication":"Retries increase total work and can increase tail latency.","verification_method":"Test transient failure, persistent failure, overload, duplicate request, and recovery scenarios.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B07-R004","D41-B14-R003"],"provenance":["Google SRE literature","Distributed systems reliability literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B15-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resilience Architecture","concept":"Circuit breaker versus continuous retry","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What architectural problem does a circuit breaker address?","answer":"A circuit breaker limits repeated calls to a failing dependency and allows the caller to fail or degrade locally while the dependency recovers. It is complementary to retry logic rather than a universal replacement for it.","requirements":["define failure threshold","define open-state behavior","define recovery probing"],"constraints":["poor thresholds can create premature or delayed recovery behavior"],"tradeoff":"Circuit breaking protects resources and dependencies but introduces state and tuning requirements.","verification_method":"Simulate dependency failure, sustained failure, recovery, and intermittent failure.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B15-R001"],"provenance":["Release It!, Michael T. Nygard","Resilience engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B15-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resilience Architecture","concept":"Bulkhead isolation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why isolate resource pools between workloads?","answer":"Bulkhead isolation prevents one workload from consuming all shared resources and thereby reducing the availability of unrelated workloads. It is useful when workloads have different failure or traffic characteristics.","requirements":["identify resource pools","define capacity limits","define degradation behavior"],"constraints":["fragmented capacity can reduce overall utilization if limits are poorly chosen"],"tradeoff":"Isolation improves failure containment but can reduce sharing efficiency.","performance_implication":"Resource partitioning may protect latency for critical workloads at the cost of unused capacity in less active partitions.","verification_method":"Inject overload into one workload and verify unrelated workloads retain their defined resource capacity.","evidence_level":"RESILIENCE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B07-R005"],"provenance":["Release It!, Michael T. Nygard"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B15-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resilience Architecture","concept":"Graceful degradation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"When should a system intentionally provide reduced functionality instead of failing completely?","answer":"Graceful degradation is appropriate when some functionality can be safely omitted while preserving a useful and correct core experience. The degraded behavior must be explicitly defined rather than silently producing incorrect results.","requirements":["identify critical functionality","define safe degraded state","define user-visible semantics"],"constraints":["degraded output must not be mistaken for complete output"],"tradeoff":"Degradation improves availability but may reduce functionality and complicate state semantics.","verification_method":"Disable noncritical dependency or capability and verify that critical invariants remain intact.","evidence_level":"RESILIENCE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B15-R002"],"provenance":["Resilience engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B15-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resilience Architecture","concept":"Fail-open versus fail-closed","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should an architecture choose between fail-open and fail-closed behavior?","answer":"The choice depends on the consequence of allowing an operation during uncertainty versus blocking it. Security authorization commonly favors fail-closed behavior, while some availability-oriented non-security features may safely degrade or fail open under explicitly defined conditions.","requirements":["identify failure consequence","classify security impact","define safe default"],"constraints":["the correct choice is domain-specific"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive authorization failures generally require conservative denial rather than implicit permission.","verification_method":"Simulate dependency failure and verify the system enters the explicitly defined safe state.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B15-R004"],"provenance":["Security architecture principles","OWASP guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B16-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment Architecture","concept":"Single deployment versus independent deployment","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the trade-off between a single deployable system and independently deployable components?","answer":"A single deployment simplifies coordination, testing, and operational management but increases deployment scope. Independent deployment enables localized release and scaling but requires stronger compatibility, observability, and operational discipline.","requirements":["assess release coupling","assess deployment automation","assess compatibility"],"constraints":["independent deployment does not eliminate integration complexity"],"tradeoff":"Single deployment favors operational simplicity; independent deployment favors release autonomy.","verification_method":"Measure release dependencies, rollback scope, compatibility requirements, and operational overhead.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B02-R002"],"provenance":["Software architecture and deployment engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B16-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment Architecture","concept":"Immutable infrastructure versus in-place mutation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why can immutable deployment reduce operational uncertainty?","answer":"Immutable deployment replaces deployed artifacts rather than modifying them in place, making the deployed state easier to identify and reproduce. It can increase infrastructure replacement cost and requires appropriate artifact and configuration management.","requirements":["reproducible artifacts","versioned configuration","replacement strategy"],"constraints":["stateful systems may require additional architecture for persistent data"],"tradeoff":"Immutability improves reproducibility and rollback clarity but can require stronger automation and state separation.","verification_method":"Compare deployed artifact identity, reproducibility, rollback process, and configuration drift.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B07-R001"],"provenance":["Infrastructure engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B16-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment Architecture","concept":"Blue-green versus rolling deployment","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What architectural trade-offs distinguish blue-green and rolling deployments?","answer":"Blue-green deployment maintains two deployment environments and shifts traffic between them, enabling a relatively clear cutover and rollback. Rolling deployment replaces instances incrementally, reducing duplicate capacity requirements but creating a period where versions coexist.","requirements":["define traffic switching","define compatibility","define rollback"],"constraints":["coexisting versions require compatibility during rolling deployment"],"tradeoff":"Blue-green favors clean environment switching; rolling favors incremental resource usage.","performance_implication":"Blue-green can temporarily require additional capacity for the parallel environment.","verification_method":"Test mixed-version compatibility, health checks, traffic switching, and rollback behavior.","evidence_level":"DEPLOYMENT_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B16-R001"],"provenance":["Deployment engineering practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B16-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment Architecture","concept":"Feature flags versus release branching","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How do feature flags change deployment architecture?","answer":"Feature flags separate deployment from feature activation, allowing code to be deployed before it becomes visible. They add runtime configuration, state management, testing combinations, and lifecycle cleanup requirements.","requirements":["define flag ownership","define default state","define removal plan"],"constraints":["stale flags create configuration complexity","flag combinations expand test space"],"tradeoff":"Feature flags improve controlled rollout but increase runtime decision complexity.","security_implication":"Flags controlling privileged behavior require authorization and secure configuration management.","verification_method":"Test enabled, disabled, partial rollout, invalid configuration, and flag removal states.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B16-R001"],"provenance":["Continuous delivery engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B16-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Deployment Architecture","concept":"Configuration externalization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should environment-specific configuration normally be separated from application artifacts?","answer":"Separating configuration from artifacts allows the same software artifact to operate under different environments and reduces the need to rebuild code for environment changes. Secrets require additional secure handling and should not simply be externalized into publicly accessible configuration.","requirements":["version configuration policy","separate secrets","define precedence"],"constraints":["configuration itself can be security-sensitive"],"security_implication":"External configuration must enforce access control and secret protection.","verification_method":"Deploy the same artifact under distinct configurations and verify intended behavior without rebuilding the artifact.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B07-R001"],"provenance":["Twelve-Factor App methodology","Secure configuration engineering practices"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B17-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Scalability Architecture","concept":"Vertical versus horizontal scaling","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What distinguishes vertical and horizontal scaling?","answer":"Vertical scaling increases resources available to an existing instance, while horizontal scaling adds instances or processing nodes. Horizontal scaling can improve capacity and fault distribution but requires appropriate statelessness, coordination, or partitioning.","requirements":["identify bottleneck","identify state ownership","define scaling unit"],"constraints":["not every workload scales horizontally"],"tradeoff":"Vertical scaling can preserve architectural simplicity; horizontal scaling can provide broader capacity and failure distribution at the cost of coordination complexity.","performance_implication":"Scaling strategy must match the actual resource bottleneck rather than assuming more instances improve every workload.","verification_method":"Identify measured bottleneck and evaluate capacity behavior under the chosen scaling dimension.","evidence_level":"SCALABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B07-R005"],"provenance":["Distributed systems and performance engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B17-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Scalability Architecture","concept":"Stateless versus stateful service instances","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why does statelessness simplify horizontal scaling?","answer":"Stateless instances do not require local session state to survive on a particular instance, allowing requests to move between instances more freely. Stateful instances can still be appropriate when local state provides meaningful performance or coordination benefits, but state placement becomes part of scaling design.","requirements":["identify state","define ownership","define recovery behavior"],"constraints":["externalizing state can introduce additional latency or dependencies"],"tradeoff":"Statelessness improves placement flexibility; local state can improve locality but complicates scaling and failover.","performance_implication":"External state access can add network latency.","verification_method":"Test instance replacement, request redistribution, and state recovery behavior.","evidence_level":"SCALABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B17-R001"],"provenance":["Distributed systems architecture literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B17-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Scalability Architecture","concept":"Partitioning strategy","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What must be considered when choosing a partitioning key?","answer":"A partitioning key should distribute workload sufficiently while preserving required locality and avoiding pathological hotspots. The key must also support the access patterns and correctness requirements of the system.","requirements":["understand access patterns","estimate key distribution","identify hotspot risk"],"constraints":["changing partition keys later can be operationally expensive"],"performance_implication":"Poor partitioning can create hotspots even when aggregate capacity appears sufficient.","verification_method":"Analyze key distribution, access locality, hotspot behavior, and rebalancing requirements.","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B04-R005"],"provenance":["Distributed database and partitioning literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B17-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Scalability Architecture","concept":"Load balancing strategy","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can load-balancing strategy affect correctness as well as performance?","answer":"Some workloads depend on locality, session affinity, ordering, or state placement. A load-balancing policy that distributes requests evenly may still violate those requirements if state or ordering assumptions are ignored.","requirements":["identify state affinity","identify ordering requirements","identify health criteria"],"constraints":["equal request distribution does not imply equal resource distribution"],"performance_implication":"Balancing strategy should account for actual resource cost rather than request count alone.","verification_method":"Test distribution under representative workloads and verify state, ordering, health, and capacity requirements.","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B07-R002"],"provenance":["Distributed systems and load-balancing engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B17-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Scalability Architecture","concept":"Caching versus direct computation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When can caching be an architectural optimization rather than merely a performance optimization?","answer":"Caching changes the system's data-access architecture by introducing another state representation, freshness policy, invalidation strategy, and failure mode. It is justified when repeated access cost is significant and acceptable staleness or explicit invalidation can be defined.","requirements":["identify expensive operation","define freshness requirement","define invalidation strategy"],"constraints":["cache correctness must be explicitly defined"],"tradeoff":"Caching can reduce latency and load but introduces stale data, invalidation complexity, and additional operational state.","verification_method":"Test hit, miss, stale, invalidation, eviction, and cache-unavailable scenarios.","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B04-R004"],"provenance":["Performance engineering and distributed systems literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B18-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Centralized versus decentralized observability","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the trade-offs between centralized observability infrastructure and locally managed observability?","answer":"Centralized observability can provide consistent collection, correlation, and governance, while decentralized approaches may reduce platform coupling and allow specialized tooling. Distributed systems generally require common identifiers and compatible telemetry semantics regardless of storage location.","requirements":["define telemetry schema","define correlation identifiers","define retention requirements"],"constraints":["centralized systems can become critical dependencies"],"tradeoff":"Centralization improves consistency; decentralization can improve autonomy and reduce single-platform coupling.","verification_method":"Trace a representative transaction across components and verify that required evidence remains reconstructable.","evidence_level":"OBSERVABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B10-R004"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry architecture guidance","Observability engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B18-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Logs versus metrics versus traces","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why are logs, metrics, and traces complementary rather than interchangeable?","answer":"Metrics summarize measurable system behavior, logs preserve discrete contextual events, and traces connect work across distributed boundaries. A mature architecture uses each according to the question it is intended to answer.","requirements":["define diagnostic questions","define correlation strategy","avoid unnecessary telemetry"],"constraints":["telemetry volume and cost must be controlled"],"tradeoff":"Metrics are efficient for aggregate trends, logs provide detailed events, and traces provide distributed request context.","performance_implication":"Excessive telemetry can consume CPU, storage, network, and processing resources.","verification_method":"Map representative incident questions to the telemetry type required to answer them.","evidence_level":"OBSERVABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B18-R001"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry documentation","Observability engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B18-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Synchronous versus asynchronous telemetry export","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the architectural trade-off between synchronous and asynchronous telemetry export?","answer":"Synchronous export can make telemetry delivery more immediate but couples application latency to the telemetry destination. Asynchronous export decouples application execution but requires buffering, backpressure, and loss-handling strategies.","requirements":["define telemetry durability requirement","define buffering capacity","define acceptable loss"],"constraints":["telemetry must not destabilize the workload it observes"],"tradeoff":"Synchronous export favors immediacy; asynchronous export favors application isolation.","performance_implication":"Synchronous export can directly add latency to application operations.","verification_method":"Test exporter unavailability, queue saturation, application shutdown, and telemetry loss behavior.","evidence_level":"OBSERVABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B15-R003"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry architecture guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B18-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Telemetry sampling","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why might an architecture sample traces instead of retaining every trace?","answer":"Sampling reduces telemetry volume and storage cost while retaining representative diagnostic information. The sampling strategy must preserve important failure, latency, or security-relevant traces when possible.","requirements":["define sampling policy","identify high-value traces","define retention requirements"],"constraints":["random sampling can miss rare important events"],"tradeoff":"Higher sampling reduces cost but can reduce diagnostic coverage.","performance_implication":"Sampling reduces telemetry processing and storage overhead.","verification_method":"Evaluate whether sampled telemetry retains required diagnostic evidence for normal and abnormal scenarios.","evidence_level":"OBSERVABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B18-R002"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B18-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Observability as architecture evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should observability influence architecture decisions?","answer":"Architectures should expose sufficient evidence to distinguish expected behavior from failure, measure relevant service objectives, trace important workflows, and diagnose boundary failures. Observability should be treated as an architectural requirement rather than a post-deployment decoration.","requirements":["define diagnostic requirements","define service indicators","define correlation mechanism"],"constraints":["not every internal implementation detail requires telemetry"],"verification_method":"For each critical workflow, identify whether an operator can reconstruct request path, failure point, relevant state, and outcome from available evidence.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B18-R001","D41-B18-R002"],"provenance":["Site Reliability Engineering literature","OpenTelemetry guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B19-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"Centralized versus distributed authorization","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the trade-offs between centralized and distributed authorization decisions?","answer":"Centralized authorization can provide consistent policy evaluation and governance but may create dependency and latency concerns. Distributed authorization can reduce central dependency and support local enforcement but increases policy distribution and consistency challenges.","requirements":["define policy ownership","define enforcement points","define failure behavior"],"constraints":["authorization must remain explicit at trust boundaries"],"security_implication":"Distributed policy copies must not silently diverge in ways that create unauthorized access.","verification_method":"Evaluate policy consistency, enforcement coverage, dependency failure, and authorization latency.","evidence_level":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B06-R003"],"provenance":["OWASP authorization guidance","Security architecture literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B19-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"Perimeter security versus defense in depth","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is perimeter-only security insufficient for distributed systems?","answer":"Modern architectures contain multiple services, identities, data stores, and trust boundaries. Security controls should therefore exist at relevant layers so compromise or failure of one boundary does not automatically provide unrestricted access to the rest of the system.","requirements":["identify trust boundaries","apply least privilege","protect sensitive resources"],"constraints":["additional controls create configuration and operational overhead"],"security_implication":"Defense in depth limits blast radius when one control is bypassed.","verification_method":"Map trust boundaries and verify independent controls for authentication, authorization, input handling, and data access.","evidence_level":"SECURITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B15-R005"],"provenance":["NIST security architecture guidance","OWASP guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B19-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"Zero trust boundary placement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"How does trust-boundary placement affect architecture?","answer":"A trust boundary identifies where assumptions about identity, authorization, data integrity, or execution environment must be re-established. Boundaries should be placed where risk and ownership changes justify independent validation rather than merely following network topology.","requirements":["identify actors","identify resources","identify trust transitions"],"constraints":["excessive boundaries can increase complexity","network location alone does not establish trust"],"security_implication":"Each relevant boundary should enforce the controls required by the threat model.","verification_method":"Review data and request flows and identify where identity, authorization, validation, and integrity assumptions change.","evidence_level":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B19-R001","D41-B19-R002"],"provenance":["NIST Zero Trust Architecture, SP 800-207"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B19-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"Secrets centralization versus local configuration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should secret management be separated from ordinary application configuration?","answer":"Secrets require stronger access controls, rotation, auditing, and exposure prevention than ordinary configuration. Central secret management can improve governance but introduces a dependency that requires availability and recovery planning.","requirements":["protect secret values","define rotation","define access policy","define recovery"],"constraints":["secret stores become security-critical infrastructure"],"security_implication":"Secrets should not be embedded in source code or broadly accessible configuration.","verification_method":"Inspect source, build artifacts, configuration, runtime environment, access policies, and rotation process for secret exposure.","evidence_level":"SECURITY_ENGINEERING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B16-R005"],"provenance":["NIST security guidance","OWASP Secrets Management guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B19-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"Security control placement trade-off","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should a security control exist only at the outer boundary or also near the protected resource?","answer":"Controls should be placed according to the threat model and trust boundaries. Outer controls can reduce unwanted traffic, while resource-level controls protect against internal compromise, misrouting, or bypass of upstream controls.","requirements":["identify threat paths","identify protected assets","define enforcement ownership"],"constraints":["duplicate controls increase operational complexity"],"tradeoff":"Multiple enforcement points improve containment but increase policy and configuration management requirements.","verification_method":"Model bypass paths and verify that critical resources remain protected if an upstream control is compromised or misconfigured.","evidence_level":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B19-R002","D41-B19-R003"],"provenance":["NIST security architecture guidance","OWASP guidance"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B20-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Analysis","concept":"Reversibility of architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"DECISION_RULE","question":"Why should architecture decisions be classified by reversibility?","answer":"Reversible decisions can often be tested and changed later, while difficult-to-reverse decisions deserve stronger evidence and analysis before commitment. This classification helps allocate architectural effort according to the cost of being wrong.","requirements":["identify reversal cost","identify migration path","identify lock-in"],"constraints":["some apparently reversible decisions become expensive after adoption"],"tradeoff":"Investing more analysis in irreversible decisions can reduce future migration cost while avoiding unnecessary analysis for easily reversible choices.","verification_method":"Estimate migration effort, compatibility constraints, data conversion cost, and operational disruption.","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B01-R001","D41-B10-R005"],"provenance":["Architecture decision-making literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B20-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Analysis","concept":"Cost of complexity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should architectural complexity be treated as a cost?","answer":"Every architectural mechanism introduces cognitive, operational, testing, failure, and maintenance costs. Complexity is justified when the capability it provides addresses a meaningful requirement or risk.","requirements":["identify complexity source","identify benefit","identify operational burden"],"constraints":["complexity is context-dependent","simple-looking systems can hide complexity"],"tradeoff":"Additional architectural mechanisms should earn their complexity through measurable or clearly justified benefits.","verification_method":"List new components, protocols, states, dependencies, failure modes, and operational responsibilities introduced by the decision.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B01-R002"],"provenance":["Software architecture and complexity literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B20-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Analysis","concept":"Architecture fitness criteria","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should architecture alternatives be compared without reducing the decision to a single generic score?","answer":"Define decision-specific fitness criteria such as correctness, latency, availability, security, operability, scalability, cost, changeability, and team constraints. Evaluate each alternative against the criteria using explicit evidence and record uncertainty rather than hiding it in an unexplained aggregate score.","requirements":["define decision criteria","define evidence sources","record uncertainty"],"constraints":["criteria may conflict","qualitative evidence may be necessary"],"tradeoff":"Explicit multi-criteria analysis preserves decision context better than an unexplained single ranking.","verification_method":"Check that every selected criterion is relevant, evidence-backed, and traceable to the architectural requirement.","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B10-R001","D41-B10-R002"],"provenance":["Architecture decision analysis literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B20-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Analysis","concept":"Architecture experiment before commitment","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"When should an architecture alternative be prototyped or experimentally evaluated?","answer":"An alternative should be experimentally evaluated when an important decision depends on uncertain behavior that cannot be established reliably from existing evidence, especially for performance, compatibility, failure behavior, or operational characteristics.","requirements":["identify uncertainty","define experiment hypothesis","define measurable acceptance criteria"],"constraints":["prototype results may not represent production behavior","experiment scope must be controlled"],"tradeoff":"Experiments consume time but can reduce uncertainty before an expensive architectural commitment.","performance_implication":"Performance experiments should use representative workloads and clearly label measurements as experimental rather than universal.","verification_method":"Define hypothesis, controlled conditions, observed evidence, limitations, and decision impact.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B01-R003"],"provenance":["Software architecture evaluation and experimental engineering literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}
{"record_id":"D41-B20-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Analysis","concept":"Architecture decision closure","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence is required before an architecture decision can be considered ready for implementation?","answer":"The decision should identify the requirement, constraints, alternatives considered, selected approach, rejected alternatives, assumptions, trade-offs, risks, dependencies, verification plan, and unresolved uncertainties. A decision is not validated merely because an architecture document exists.","requirements":["explicit requirement linkage","alternative analysis","risk analysis","verification plan"],"constraints":["some uncertainties may remain and must be recorded"],"expected_behavior":"The implementation team should be able to derive concrete architectural constraints and verification activities from the decision.","verification_method":"Trace requirement to decision, decision to implementation constraints, and constraints to tests or operational verification evidence.","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","related_records":["D41-B01-R001","D41-B10-R005","D41-B20-R001"],"provenance":["Architecture Decision Record practices","Software architecture governance literature"],"created_at":"2026-09-21","updated_at":"2026-09-21"}

{"record_id":"D41-B21-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Stateless Services vs Stateful Services","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a system keep application services stateless versus retaining operational state inside service instances?","answer":"Prefer stateless service instances when horizontal scaling, replacement, load balancing, and failure recovery are important and state can be externalized without unacceptable complexity. Stateful instances can be justified when locality, latency, specialized in-memory state, or coordination semantics provide material benefits and the architecture explicitly manages ownership, persistence, recovery, and placement.","principle":"Service-local state should be retained only when its architectural value exceeds the operational complexity introduced by instance affinity, recovery, replication, and rescheduling.","requirements":["Define which state is authoritative.","Define persistence and recovery behavior for stateful components.","Ensure routing and placement assumptions are explicit.","Define failure and restart semantics."],"constraints":["Externalizing state can add network latency and infrastructure dependencies.","Stateful placement can complicate scaling and failover.","Some workloads inherently benefit from locality."],"alternatives":["Stateless service with external state store","Stateful service with durable local state","Hybrid architecture with ephemeral local cache and external source of truth"],"tradeoff":{"benefits_of_stateless":["simpler horizontal scaling","simpler replacement","simpler load balancing"],"costs_of_stateless":["additional state-store dependency","possible network overhead"],"benefits_of_stateful":["locality","lower repeated state-fetch cost","specialized stateful processing"],"costs_of_stateful":["harder recovery","placement constraints","state replication complexity"]},"decision_factors":["state size","state durability requirement","latency sensitivity","failure recovery objective","scaling model","data ownership"],"failure_modes":["lost local state","stale replicated state","incorrect instance affinity","recovery without sufficient state","hidden state dependency"],"security_implication":"Stateful components may retain sensitive data beyond a request lifetime; retention, encryption, isolation, and destruction requirements must therefore be explicit.","performance_implication":"Local state can reduce network access but may increase memory pressure and reduce scheduling flexibility.","testing_guidance":["restart instances while state is active","move workload between instances","validate recovery from durable state","verify behavior after cache/state loss"],"verification_method":"Verify that every required state category has an explicit authority, durability, recovery, and lifecycle definition.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Sam Newman, Building Microservices"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B12-R001","contrasts_with:D41-B07-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B21-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Synchronous Compute vs Asynchronous Job Execution","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a request perform work synchronously versus enqueueing work for asynchronous execution?","answer":"Use synchronous execution when the caller needs an immediate result and the operation fits within the required latency and reliability envelope. Use asynchronous execution when work is long-running, bursty, independently retryable, resource-intensive, or does not require an immediate response. The boundary should explicitly define job state, idempotency, failure reporting, and completion semantics.","principle":"Execution mode should follow the caller's response contract and the workload's duration, reliability, and resource characteristics rather than implementation convenience.","requirements":["Define response semantics.","Define job identity for asynchronous work.","Define retry and duplicate-execution behavior.","Define completion and failure reporting."],"constraints":["Asynchronous execution introduces state and eventual completion complexity.","Synchronous execution couples request lifetime to computation lifetime.","Retries can create duplicate effects if operations are not idempotent."],"alternatives":["Synchronous request/response","Asynchronous queue-backed job","Hybrid immediate acknowledgement plus asynchronous completion"],"tradeoff":{"synchronous":["simpler caller semantics","immediate result","tighter coupling"],"asynchronous":["better workload isolation","independent scaling","more complex lifecycle"]},"decision_factors":["operation duration","caller latency requirement","burstiness","retryability","resource intensity","user interaction model"],"failure_modes":["request timeout","orphaned job","duplicate execution","lost completion notification","unbounded queue growth"],"security_implication":"Asynchronous job identifiers and authorization context must not permit unauthorized users to retrieve or control another user's jobs.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous execution can smooth bursts and improve resource utilization but adds queueing and scheduling latency.","testing_guidance":["inject queue delay","terminate workers during jobs","retry duplicate deliveries","verify caller authorization after job creation"],"verification_method":"Verify that the selected execution model satisfies the declared response, durability, retry, and completion contract.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B10-R003","related_to:D41-B16-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B21-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Serverless Functions vs Long-Lived Services","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What architectural factors determine whether serverless functions or long-lived application services are more appropriate?","answer":"Serverless functions are suitable for independently deployable event or request handlers with variable demand and limited execution characteristics. Long-lived services are often preferable when workloads require sustained processing, specialized runtime control, predictable resource residency, persistent connections, or execution characteristics poorly matched to function limits. The decision must account for operational model, scaling behavior, startup characteristics, observability, and platform constraints.","principle":"Choose the compute abstraction whose lifecycle and resource model naturally match the workload instead of forcing workload behavior into platform limits.","requirements":["Identify execution-duration requirements.","Identify concurrency and scaling characteristics.","Identify runtime and dependency requirements.","Define operational ownership and observability."],"constraints":["Serverless platforms impose provider-specific execution and resource constraints.","Long-lived services require more explicit capacity and lifecycle management.","Provider-specific integrations can increase portability costs."],"alternatives":["Serverless functions","Containerized long-lived services","Managed application platform","Batch compute"],"tradeoff":{"serverless_benefits":["automatic elasticity","reduced server lifecycle management","event-oriented deployment"],"serverless_costs":["platform constraints","possible startup latency","provider coupling"],"service_benefits":["runtime control","persistent resources","predictable process model"],"service_costs":["capacity management","patching and lifecycle responsibility"]},"decision_factors":["execution duration","traffic variability","runtime requirements","startup sensitivity","persistent connections","portability","operational capability"],"failure_modes":["function timeout","cold-start-sensitive path","unexpected concurrency amplification","capacity exhaustion","resource leak in long-lived process"],"security_implication":"The selected platform must provide adequate isolation, identity handling, secret management, and least-privilege execution.","performance_implication":"Performance depends on workload characteristics and platform behavior; no universal latency or cost superiority should be assumed without measurement.","testing_guidance":["test cold and warm execution where applicable","test concurrency limits","test timeout behavior","test long-lived resource cleanup"],"verification_method":"Verify that documented workload characteristics fit the execution limits and lifecycle model of the selected platform.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Lambda Developer Guide"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center"}],"relationships":["alternative_to:D41-B21-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B21-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Dedicated Compute vs Shared Compute Pools","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should workloads receive dedicated compute capacity instead of sharing a common compute pool?","answer":"Dedicated capacity is justified when workload isolation, predictable resource availability, compliance boundaries, noisy-neighbor avoidance, or specialized hardware requirements materially affect system objectives. Shared pools are preferable when workloads are compatible and resource multiplexing improves utilization. The architecture should define capacity guarantees and isolation boundaries rather than treating physical or logical dedication as inherently superior.","principle":"Compute isolation should be proportional to the workload's availability, security, performance, and compliance requirements.","requirements":["Define isolation objectives.","Define resource guarantees.","Define workload compatibility.","Define capacity and failure behavior."],"constraints":["Dedicated capacity can reduce utilization and increase management overhead.","Shared pools can introduce contention.","Isolation mechanisms vary by infrastructure platform."],"alternatives":["Dedicated hosts","Dedicated clusters","Shared pools with quotas","Shared pools with workload classes"],"tradeoff":{"dedicated":["stronger isolation","predictable capacity","higher infrastructure cost"],"shared":["higher multiplexing potential","simpler fleet management","contention risk"]},"decision_factors":["resource contention","security isolation","compliance","hardware specialization","capacity predictability","utilization"],"failure_modes":["noisy neighbor","quota starvation","underutilized dedicated capacity","incorrect isolation assumptions"],"security_implication":"Dedicated capacity may simplify isolation but does not replace application-level authorization or secure configuration.","performance_implication":"Isolation can improve predictability, but actual performance gains must be established from workload measurements rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":["introduce competing workloads","validate quotas","measure tail latency under contention","test isolation boundaries"],"verification_method":"Verify resource guarantees and isolation behavior under representative contention and failure conditions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Kubernetes Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B07-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B21-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Batch Processing vs Stream Processing","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a workload use batch processing instead of continuous stream processing?","answer":"Batch processing fits workloads where bounded processing windows, high throughput over accumulated data, or simpler recomputation are acceptable. Stream processing fits workloads requiring low-latency reaction to continuously arriving events. The decision depends on freshness requirements, event ordering, state management, replay requirements, processing cost, and operational complexity.","principle":"Processing architecture should match the required freshness and event-state semantics rather than assuming continuous processing is always more responsive or more efficient.","requirements":["Define freshness objective.","Define event-time and processing-time semantics where relevant.","Define replay and recovery requirements.","Define state management strategy."],"constraints":["Streaming introduces continuous operational state and failure-handling complexity.","Batch introduces processing delay.","Hybrid systems require explicit reconciliation semantics."],"alternatives":["Scheduled batch","Micro-batch","Continuous stream processing","Hybrid batch-plus-stream architecture"],"tradeoff":{"batch":["simpler recomputation","efficient bulk processing","higher freshness delay"],"stream":["lower potential latency","continuous processing","greater state and operational complexity"]},"decision_factors":["freshness","event volume","replay needs","statefulness","processing cost","operational maturity"],"failure_modes":["late events","duplicate events","batch backlog","stream state corruption","incorrect event-time handling"],"security_implication":"Both models require controlled access to source data, processing state, and retained event history.","performance_implication":"Throughput and latency depend on workload and implementation; architecture selection should be supported by measured workload characteristics.","testing_guidance":["inject late events","replay historical data","simulate processing interruption","compare batch recomputation with streaming state"],"verification_method":"Verify that freshness, replay, ordering, and recovery requirements are explicitly satisfied by the selected processing model.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Apache Kafka Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B16-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B22-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Object Storage vs Block Storage vs File Storage","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should an architecture choose among object, block, and file storage?","answer":"Object storage is suited to durable, independently addressed objects and large unstructured datasets; block storage is suited to systems requiring block-level volumes and filesystem or database control; file storage is suited to shared hierarchical filesystem semantics. The choice should follow access semantics, consistency expectations, latency requirements, sharing model, durability, and operational constraints.","principle":"Storage abstraction should be selected from required access semantics and workload behavior, not from storage technology popularity.","requirements":["Define access pattern.","Define sharing requirements.","Define durability and recovery requirements.","Define latency and throughput requirements."],"constraints":["Object storage does not provide ordinary block-device semantics.","Block storage generally requires filesystem or application management.","Shared file storage introduces coordination and metadata considerations."],"alternatives":["Object storage","Block volumes","Shared filesystem","Local ephemeral storage"],"tradeoff":{"object":["highly scalable object model","good fit for unstructured data","different access semantics"],"block":["fine-grained control","database-friendly","more lifecycle management"],"file":["shared hierarchical namespace","familiar filesystem semantics","coordination and scaling considerations"]},"decision_factors":["object size","access frequency","random vs sequential access","shared access","metadata requirements","durability"],"failure_modes":["wrong consistency assumptions","unexpected latency","metadata bottleneck","capacity exhaustion","incorrect durability assumptions"],"security_implication":"Storage authorization must be enforced at the appropriate object, volume, filesystem, and application boundaries.","performance_implication":"Storage performance is workload and platform dependent; architecture decisions should use measured characteristics when performance is critical.","testing_guidance":["test concurrent access","measure representative read/write patterns","test recovery","validate permissions"],"verification_method":"Verify that storage semantics match application assumptions for addressing, consistency, sharing, durability, and recovery.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Storage Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B04-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B22-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Local Storage vs Remote Durable Storage","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When is local storage appropriate compared with remote durable storage?","answer":"Local storage is appropriate for temporary data, caches, scratch space, or workloads where locality provides value and loss is acceptable. Remote durable storage is appropriate for authoritative data that must survive instance replacement or be shared across compute nodes. If local storage contains authoritative state, the architecture must explicitly provide durability, recovery, and placement guarantees.","principle":"Durability requirements determine whether local storage can be authoritative; locality alone must not be mistaken for persistence.","requirements":["Classify data as authoritative or ephemeral.","Define recovery behavior.","Define lifecycle and deletion semantics.","Define sharing requirements."],"constraints":["Local storage may disappear with instance loss.","Remote storage introduces network dependency.","Replication and durability guarantees vary by implementation."],"alternatives":["Ephemeral local disk","Persistent local volume","Remote object storage","Remote database/storage service"],"tradeoff":{"local":["low-latency locality","simple access","failure/lifecycle risk"],"remote":["durability and sharing","network dependency","potential latency overhead"]},"decision_factors":["data criticality","durability","latency","sharing","failure domain","recovery objective"],"failure_modes":["data loss after restart","stale local copy","split-brain local state","remote storage outage"],"security_implication":"Sensitive data stored locally can bypass centralized storage controls unless endpoint encryption, access control, and lifecycle management are explicitly implemented.","performance_implication":"Local access can reduce network overhead, while remote storage may provide stronger durability and scalability; actual characteristics require workload measurement.","testing_guidance":["terminate compute instances","restore from durable storage","simulate remote storage unavailability","verify cache invalidation"],"verification_method":"Verify that loss of local storage cannot violate the declared durability contract for authoritative data.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Kubernetes Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B21-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B22-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Relational Database vs Document Database","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What architectural trade-offs distinguish relational and document-oriented persistence?","answer":"Relational databases provide structured schemas, relational constraints, joins, and mature transactional semantics suited to strongly structured relationships. Document databases can represent aggregate-shaped data with flexible schemas and can simplify access patterns that naturally retrieve an entire document. Neither model is universally preferable; the choice depends on data relationships, consistency requirements, query patterns, schema evolution, and operational capabilities.","principle":"Choose persistence based on domain access patterns and integrity requirements rather than schema flexibility alone.","requirements":["Identify aggregate boundaries.","Identify relationship and join requirements.","Define consistency and transaction requirements.","Define schema evolution strategy."],"constraints":["Document models can complicate cross-document integrity.","Relational joins can become costly at scale depending on workload and topology.","Both models require deliberate indexing and data modeling."],"alternatives":["Relational database","Document database","Polyglot persistence","Relational core with document-like columns/features"],"tradeoff":{"relational":["strong relational integrity","rich query semantics","structured schema"],"document":["aggregate-oriented representation","schema flexibility","cross-document integrity may require application logic"]},"decision_factors":["relationships","transaction boundaries","query patterns","schema evolution","data integrity","scale characteristics"],"failure_modes":["mis-modeled aggregates","application-enforced integrity gaps","unbounded document growth","excessive joins"],"security_implication":"Security controls must account for database-level permissions as well as application authorization; flexible schemas do not remove the need for data classification.","performance_implication":"Performance is workload dependent and must be evaluated against representative queries, indexes, and data distributions.","testing_guidance":["test integrity constraints","test schema evolution","measure representative query plans","test concurrent updates"],"verification_method":"Verify that the selected model can express required integrity constraints and dominant query patterns without undocumented assumptions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center"}],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B04-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B22-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Normalized Data Model vs Denormalized Read Model","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture normalize data versus maintain deliberately denormalized representations?","answer":"Normalization is useful for reducing update anomalies and maintaining a coherent source of truth. Denormalization can simplify or accelerate read-heavy access patterns when duplicated data can be updated and reconciled reliably. Denormalized representations should normally have explicit ownership, refresh, invalidation, and consistency semantics rather than becoming unmanaged duplicate sources of truth.","principle":"Denormalization is an optimization or access-model decision only when duplication has explicit lifecycle and consistency rules.","requirements":["Identify authoritative source.","Define synchronization mechanism.","Define acceptable staleness.","Define rebuild and reconciliation procedures."],"constraints":["Denormalization increases consistency-management complexity.","Normalization can require more joins or query processing.","Materialized data can become stale."],"alternatives":["Fully normalized model","Controlled denormalized model","Materialized views","Read-optimized projection"],"tradeoff":{"normalized":["stronger single-source integrity","less duplicated data","potentially more complex reads"],"denormalized":["simpler/faster target reads in suitable workloads","higher synchronization complexity"]},"decision_factors":["read/write ratio","query complexity","consistency requirement","refresh latency","rebuild capability"],"failure_modes":["stale projection","partial synchronization","duplicate authority","failed rebuild"],"security_implication":"Duplicated data expands the security surface and must inherit appropriate access-control and retention requirements.","performance_implication":"Denormalization can reduce read-time work but may increase write and synchronization costs; measured workload data should guide critical decisions.","testing_guidance":["simulate update propagation failure","rebuild projections","validate staleness limits","compare representative read/write workloads"],"verification_method":"Verify that every duplicated field has a defined authority and deterministic reconciliation path.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B12-R005","related_to:D41-B13-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B22-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Single Storage Engine vs Polyglot Persistence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When is using multiple storage technologies justified instead of standardizing on one primary storage engine?","answer":"Polyglot persistence is justified when materially different workloads require storage semantics that one engine cannot provide efficiently or safely. A single primary engine is preferable when additional technologies would mainly add operational complexity without satisfying a distinct requirement. Each additional storage technology should have a documented workload-specific reason, ownership model, backup strategy, and recovery procedure.","principle":"Storage diversity should be requirement-driven rather than technology-driven.","requirements":["Identify distinct storage semantics required.","Document ownership for each technology.","Define backup and recovery per technology.","Define cross-store consistency expectations."],"constraints":["Multiple engines increase operational and knowledge burden.","Cross-store transactions are more complex.","Each technology adds security, monitoring, and lifecycle responsibilities."],"alternatives":["Single primary database","Primary database plus specialized store","Polyglot persistence"],"tradeoff":{"single":["lower operational complexity","standardized tooling","possible workload mismatch"],"polyglot":["workload-specific capabilities","higher operational complexity","cross-store consistency challenges"]},"decision_factors":["workload diversity","query semantics","scale","operational maturity","team expertise","recovery complexity"],"failure_modes":["technology sprawl","orphaned datastore","inconsistent backups","cross-store divergence"],"security_implication":"Every storage technology introduces its own credentials, permissions, encryption, network exposure, and patching requirements.","performance_implication":"Specialized storage can improve suitability for a specific workload, but gains must be demonstrated against operational cost.","testing_guidance":["test each datastore recovery path","test cross-store failure","validate backup completeness","test authorization independently"],"verification_method":"Verify that every storage technology maps to a distinct requirement and has complete lifecycle ownership.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B22-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B23-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency Architecture","concept":"Strong Consistency vs Eventual Consistency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should an architecture choose between strong and eventual consistency for distributed data?","answer":"Strong consistency is appropriate when consumers must observe updates according to a defined consistency contract before proceeding. Eventual consistency is appropriate when temporary divergence is acceptable and availability, latency, partition tolerance, or independent scaling provide sufficient value. The decision must be made per data interaction rather than applied indiscriminately to an entire system.","principle":"Consistency strength should match the business consequence of stale or conflicting observations.","requirements":["Define the consistency contract.","Identify authoritative state.","Define acceptable staleness.","Define conflict handling where multiple writers exist."],"constraints":["Stronger consistency can increase coordination or latency.","Eventual consistency requires consumers to tolerate stale data.","Network partitions constrain distributed coordination."],"alternatives":["Strong consistency","Eventual consistency","Bounded-staleness consistency","Session/read-your-writes semantics"],"tradeoff":{"strong":["clearer read semantics","more coordination requirements"],"eventual":["greater decoupling and availability potential","stale-read and reconciliation complexity"]},"decision_factors":["business criticality","staleness tolerance","write concurrency","network topology","latency requirements"],"failure_modes":["stale decision","lost update","conflict","incorrect assumption of immediate propagation"],"security_implication":"Authorization decisions must not rely on stale security-critical state unless the resulting risk is explicitly accepted and controlled.","performance_implication":"Consistency mechanisms can introduce coordination and latency costs; exact effects depend on topology and implementation.","testing_guidance":["delay replication","reorder updates","simulate partitions","test stale reads","test conflict resolution"],"verification_method":"Verify each cross-component read/write path against an explicit consistency contract and stale-data tolerance.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Jepsen distributed systems research"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B09-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B23-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency Architecture","concept":"Quorum Reads and Writes vs Single-Replica Access","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a distributed datastore use quorum-style read/write coordination instead of single-replica access?","answer":"Quorum-style coordination can provide stronger consistency or fault tolerance properties when the datastore and replication model support them. Single-replica access can reduce coordination overhead but may expose stale data or reduce tolerance to replica failure. The exact guarantees depend on the datastore's replication protocol and configuration; quorum terminology must not be treated as a universal consistency guarantee.","principle":"Replica coordination should be selected from the actual datastore consistency model rather than from generic quorum terminology.","requirements":["Document replication semantics.","Define read and write quorum behavior.","Define failure tolerance.","Validate datastore-specific guarantees."],"constraints":["Quorum reads/writes can increase latency.","Replica failures can reduce availability when quorum cannot be met.","Datastore implementations differ materially."],"alternatives":["Single-replica read/write","Quorum-based access","Leader-based access","Datastore-specific consistency levels"],"tradeoff":{"quorum":["stronger coordination potential","greater failure/latency sensitivity"],"single_replica":["lower coordination cost","potentially weaker freshness and fault tolerance"]},"decision_factors":["consistency requirement","replica count","failure model","latency","availability"],"failure_modes":["quorum unavailable","stale replica read","misconfigured consistency level","false assumption of linearizability"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive reads must use a consistency level compatible with the authorization model.","performance_implication":"Additional replica coordination can increase latency; performance must be validated on the actual datastore.","testing_guidance":["kill replicas","inject network partitions","test configured consistency levels","validate stale-read behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify documented datastore semantics under replica failure and partition conditions rather than inferring guarantees from configuration names.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Apache Cassandra Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B23-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B23-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency Architecture","concept":"Optimistic Concurrency vs Pessimistic Locking","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture use optimistic concurrency control versus pessimistic locking?","answer":"Optimistic concurrency is suitable when conflicts are relatively infrequent and transactions can detect and retry conflicting updates. Pessimistic locking is suitable when conflicts are sufficiently costly or likely that reserving access during a critical section is preferable. Lock duration, contention, deadlocks, retries, and failure recovery must be included in the architectural decision.","principle":"Concurrency control should reflect conflict frequency and conflict cost rather than defaulting to either locking or retries.","requirements":["Define conflict semantics.","Define retry behavior.","Define lock scope and duration when locking is used.","Define transaction boundaries."],"constraints":["Optimistic retries can amplify workload under contention.","Pessimistic locks can reduce concurrency and create deadlocks or blocking.","Correctness depends on transaction isolation and implementation."],"alternatives":["Optimistic version checks","Pessimistic row/resource locks","Compare-and-swap semantics","Serialized command processing"],"tradeoff":{"optimistic":["high concurrency under low conflict","retry complexity under contention"],"pessimistic":["explicit exclusion","blocking and deadlock risk"]},"decision_factors":["conflict rate","conflict cost","transaction duration","contention","retry tolerance"],"failure_modes":["lost update","retry storm","deadlock","lock starvation","stale version"],"security_implication":"Authorization and ownership checks must occur within an appropriate consistency boundary so stale permissions cannot be used to modify protected resources.","performance_implication":"Optimistic control can perform well under low contention; pessimistic control can become costly under high contention. Actual behavior requires measurement.","testing_guidance":["generate concurrent updates","inject transaction delays","test deadlock handling","test retry limits"],"verification_method":"Verify that concurrent conflicting operations cannot violate the defined invariants under the chosen control mechanism.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"PostgreSQL Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Designing Data-Intensive Applications"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B09-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B23-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency Architecture","concept":"Read Repair vs Background Reconciliation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should consistency repair occur during reads versus through background reconciliation?","answer":"Read-time repair can reduce the lifetime of detected divergence on frequently accessed data but adds latency and write activity to user requests. Background reconciliation can isolate repair from request paths and systematically scan data, but divergence may persist until reconciliation runs. The appropriate architecture depends on freshness requirements, repair cost, access frequency, and correctness consequences.","principle":"Repair placement should balance freshness, request-path complexity, and the cost of detecting and correcting divergence.","requirements":["Define divergence detection mechanism.","Define repair authority.","Define maximum acceptable divergence duration.","Define repair retry and audit behavior."],"constraints":["Read repair can increase tail latency.","Background repair can leave stale data visible.","Repair itself can conflict with concurrent writes."],"alternatives":["Read-time repair","Periodic reconciliation","Continuous reconciliation","Hybrid repair"],"tradeoff":{"read_repair":["faster correction on accessed records","request-path overhead"],"background":["isolated request path","delayed correction"]},"decision_factors":["freshness requirement","access frequency","repair cost","dataset size","auditability"],"failure_modes":["repair loop","repairing stale state over newer state","unbounded divergence","repair backlog"],"security_implication":"Repair mechanisms must preserve authorization and provenance; automatic correction must not bypass normal ownership rules.","performance_implication":"Repair frequency and placement affect request latency and background resource consumption.","testing_guidance":["inject divergent replicas","test concurrent writes during repair","measure repair backlog","verify idempotent repair"],"verification_method":"Verify that divergence is detected, corrected, audited, and bounded within the declared consistency objective.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B22-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B23-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Consistency Architecture","concept":"Linearizable Semantics vs Application-Level Reconciliation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a system require strong linearizable semantics instead of accepting application-level reconciliation?","answer":"Linearizable semantics are appropriate when operations must appear to occur atomically in a single real-time order consistent with the system's defined contract. Application-level reconciliation can be appropriate when temporary divergence is acceptable and conflicts can be resolved deterministically without violating business invariants. The architecture must identify which invariants cannot tolerate reconciliation delay.","principle":"Do not replace a required consistency guarantee with reconciliation unless the business invariant remains safe during divergence.","requirements":["Identify non-negotiable invariants.","Define allowed divergence.","Define deterministic conflict resolution.","Define audit and recovery behavior."],"constraints":["Strong consistency may require coordination and reduce availability in some distributed designs.","Reconciliation cannot repair every invariant after the fact.","Incorrect conflict resolution can permanently corrupt business state."],"alternatives":["Linearizable coordination","Optimistic reconciliation","Conflict-free data structures where applicable","Single-writer authority"],"tradeoff":{"strong_semantics":["clearer invariant enforcement","coordination cost"],"reconciliation":["greater decoupling","complex conflict semantics and temporary inconsistency"]},"decision_factors":["business invariant","conflict reversibility","staleness tolerance","coordination cost","failure model"],"failure_modes":["irreversible conflicting writes","incorrect reconciliation","duplicate authority","invariant violation during divergence"],"security_implication":"Access-control state and security policy changes may require stronger consistency than ordinary informational data.","performance_implication":"Strong coordination may add latency or reduce availability depending on architecture; claims must be implementation-specific.","testing_guidance":["simulate simultaneous conflicting writes","delay propagation","verify invariant preservation","test reconciliation correctness"],"verification_method":"Verify invariants under concurrency, delay, duplication, and partition scenarios.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Jepsen distributed systems research"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Designing Data-Intensive Applications"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B23-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B24-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Placement","concept":"Edge Compute vs Centralized Compute","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should computation move toward users or devices instead of remaining in centralized infrastructure?","answer":"Edge computation is justified when locality materially reduces latency, bandwidth usage, disconnected-operation requirements, or data-transfer constraints. Centralized computation is preferable when shared state, centralized governance, specialized resources, or simpler operations dominate. Edge placement increases deployment, update, observability, and security complexity.","principle":"Move computation toward the edge only when locality provides a measurable architectural benefit that outweighs distributed operational complexity.","requirements":["Define latency or locality objective.","Define synchronization strategy.","Define edge lifecycle management.","Define security and update model."],"constraints":["Edge nodes may have weaker connectivity and resources.","Distributed deployments increase fleet-management complexity.","Sensitive logic and data may be exposed across more execution locations."],"alternatives":["Centralized compute","Regional compute","Edge compute","Hybrid edge-central architecture"],"tradeoff":{"edge":["lower locality-related latency","reduced central bandwidth in suitable workloads","greater fleet complexity"],"central":["simpler operations","centralized resources and state","potential network latency"]},"decision_factors":["latency","bandwidth","connectivity","data locality","hardware constraints","operational maturity"],"failure_modes":["edge node divergence","stale software","offline state conflicts","partial fleet failure"],"security_implication":"Every edge node becomes an additional trust boundary requiring secure boot/runtime controls, credential management, update mechanisms, and data protection.","performance_implication":"Edge placement may improve locality but can reduce available compute resources; actual gains require workload-specific measurement.","testing_guidance":["simulate disconnected operation","test remote updates","test partial fleet failure","measure edge-to-central synchronization"],"verification_method":"Verify that edge deployment provides the required locality benefit and that offline, update, and recovery semantics are deterministic.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Cloudflare architecture documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B07-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B24-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Placement","concept":"Centralized Policy Decision vs Local Policy Decision","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should policy decisions be evaluated centrally versus locally at the enforcement point?","answer":"Centralized policy decisions simplify policy management and provide a common decision source, while local decisions can reduce dependency on network reachability and improve enforcement latency. A hybrid design can distribute policy data while retaining centralized policy authority. Security-critical local evaluation requires explicit policy freshness and fail-safe behavior.","principle":"Separate policy authority from enforcement placement when necessary, and define exactly how stale policy is handled.","requirements":["Define policy authority.","Define policy distribution and freshness.","Define enforcement behavior during policy-service failure.","Define audit requirements."],"constraints":["Centralized decisions introduce availability and latency dependencies.","Local decisions risk stale policy if updates lag.","Distributed policy increases synchronization complexity."],"alternatives":["Centralized decision point","Local embedded policy engine","Hybrid centrally managed/local enforcement"],"tradeoff":{"central":["consistent policy authority","network dependency"],"local":["low dependency and latency","policy synchronization complexity"]},"decision_factors":["security criticality","latency","connectivity","policy update frequency","auditability"],"failure_modes":["stale authorization","policy-service outage","inconsistent policy versions","fail-open enforcement"],"security_implication":"Authorization should fail according to explicit security policy when current policy cannot be obtained or verified.","performance_implication":"Local evaluation can reduce network overhead but may increase memory and update complexity.","testing_guidance":["disconnect policy authority","inject stale policy","roll policy versions","verify fail-open/fail-closed behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify policy version, freshness, authorization result, and failure behavior at each enforcement point.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B11-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B24-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Placement","concept":"Database-Centric Logic vs Application-Centric Logic","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should business or data logic reside inside the database versus the application layer?","answer":"Database-centric logic can enforce invariants close to persistent data and reduce duplicated enforcement across clients, while application-centric logic can provide broader domain orchestration, portability, and integration control. Critical invariants that must hold regardless of caller may benefit from database enforcement, whereas workflow and external-service orchestration usually belong above the persistence layer.","principle":"Place logic at the lowest layer capable of enforcing the invariant without creating unacceptable coupling or duplication.","requirements":["Classify the invariant or behavior.","Identify all possible data writers.","Define transaction boundaries.","Define portability requirements."],"constraints":["Database logic can increase vendor coupling.","Application logic may allow multiple writers to bypass shared invariants.","Cross-service workflows generally cannot be fully enforced by one database."],"alternatives":["Database constraints/triggers/procedures","Application services","Hybrid enforcement"],"tradeoff":{"database":["strong proximity to data integrity","centralized enforcement","potential coupling"],"application":["broader orchestration","greater portability","risk of duplicated enforcement"]},"decision_factors":["invariant criticality","number of writers","transaction scope","vendor portability","workflow complexity"],"failure_modes":["bypassed application validation","database vendor lock-in","duplicated inconsistent rules","cross-service partial enforcement"],"security_implication":"Security invariants should not rely solely on client-side logic; authorization must be enforced at trusted boundaries.","performance_implication":"Moving logic into the database can reduce round trips for some operations but may increase database CPU contention.","testing_guidance":["test all write paths","bypass application layer where possible","test concurrent transactions","validate migration behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify that every invariant is enforced at a trusted boundary reachable by all relevant mutation paths.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"PostgreSQL Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B22-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B24-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Placement","concept":"Precompute vs Compute-on-Read","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should derived information be precomputed rather than calculated when requested?","answer":"Precomputation is useful when a derived result is expensive to calculate repeatedly, is read frequently, and can tolerate controlled refresh or synchronization complexity. Compute-on-read is preferable when data changes frequently, derived logic changes often, or stale materialization would be unacceptable. The architecture should define invalidation and rebuild behavior for precomputed data.","principle":"Precompute when repeated computation cost exceeds the consistency and storage cost of maintaining derived state.","requirements":["Define source-of-truth data.","Define refresh trigger or schedule.","Define acceptable staleness.","Define rebuild path."],"constraints":["Precomputed results can become stale.","Storage and refresh workloads increase.","Changing derivation logic may require full recomputation."],"alternatives":["Compute on demand","Scheduled materialization","Event-driven materialization","Hybrid cache/materialization"],"tradeoff":{"precompute":["lower repeated read computation","higher synchronization/storage complexity"],"read_compute":["simpler freshness model","higher repeated computation cost"]},"decision_factors":["read frequency","computation cost","data volatility","freshness","rebuild cost"],"failure_modes":["stale result","partial refresh","failed rebuild","incorrect derivation version"],"security_implication":"Derived data may inherit or aggregate sensitive source information and requires appropriate access controls.","performance_implication":"Precomputation can improve request latency but consumes resources during generation and refresh.","testing_guidance":["change source data","interrupt refresh","rebuild from source","compare derived output with reference computation"],"verification_method":"Verify that derived output is reproducible from authoritative inputs and that freshness metadata is correct.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B22-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B24-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Placement","concept":"Client-Side Computation vs Server-Side Computation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should computation be performed on clients rather than on servers?","answer":"Client-side computation can reduce server work, improve responsiveness for suitable interactive operations, and support offline behavior. Server-side computation is necessary when logic requires protected data, authoritative state, centralized policy, or trusted execution. Security-sensitive decisions must not rely solely on client-controlled computation.","principle":"Place computation according to trust, data locality, offline requirements, and resource constraints rather than assuming either client or server is universally preferable.","requirements":["Classify trusted versus untrusted computation.","Define authoritative server state.","Define offline behavior.","Define synchronization semantics."],"constraints":["Client environments are heterogeneous and potentially untrusted.","Server execution increases centralized resource demand.","Duplicated logic can drift between client and server."],"alternatives":["Client-only","Server-only","Shared client/server implementation with authoritative server validation"],"tradeoff":{"client":["local responsiveness","reduced server work","untrusted execution environment"],"server":["centralized authority","consistent environment","network dependency"]},"decision_factors":["trust boundary","offline needs","data sensitivity","compute intensity","device capability","latency"],"failure_modes":["client tampering","logic divergence","offline conflict","excessive server load"],"security_implication":"Client-generated decisions must be treated as untrusted input whenever they affect authorization, financial state, or other protected invariants.","performance_implication":"Client execution can reduce server workload but depends on device capabilities and data-transfer requirements.","testing_guidance":["tamper with client results","test offline synchronization","test heterogeneous devices","verify server-side invariant enforcement"],"verification_method":"Verify that all security and business invariants remain enforced independently of client computation.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST Secure Software Development Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B24-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B25-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Interface Architecture","concept":"Public API Contract vs Internal API Contract","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should architecture distinguish public-facing API contracts from internal service interfaces?","answer":"Public contracts generally require stronger backward-compatibility, documentation, versioning, and consumer-impact controls because external consumers cannot be coordinated as easily. Internal contracts can evolve with coordinated deployments but still require explicit ownership and compatibility rules. Treating every interface as a public contract can increase unnecessary governance, while treating public interfaces as informal internal APIs creates compatibility risk.","principle":"Contract governance should reflect the ability to coordinate consumers and the cost of breaking compatibility.","requirements":["Identify consumer ownership.","Define compatibility policy.","Define versioning/deprecation policy.","Define contract testing where appropriate."],"constraints":["External consumers may remain on old versions.","Internal teams may still have independent deployment schedules.","Breaking changes can propagate through dependency graphs."],"alternatives":["Strict versioned public API","Backward-compatible evolving API","Internal contract with coordinated releases"],"tradeoff":{"strict_public":["consumer stability","higher evolution cost"],"internal":["faster coordinated evolution","dependency coordination requirement"]},"decision_factors":["consumer control","release independence","compatibility cost","API lifespan","documentation requirements"],"failure_modes":["breaking external clients","undeclared dependency","version proliferation","unclear ownership"],"security_implication":"Public interfaces require explicit authentication, authorization, input validation, abuse controls, and data minimization.","performance_implication":"Compatibility layers and version translation can add processing overhead but may reduce migration disruption.","testing_guidance":["run contract compatibility tests","test old clients against new server","validate deprecation paths","test unauthorized consumers"],"verification_method":"Verify that interface behavior remains compatible with every declared supported consumer version.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"RFC 9110 HTTP Semantics"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google API Design Guide"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B11-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B25-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Interface Architecture","concept":"Backward Compatibility vs Clean Break","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture preserve backward compatibility instead of introducing a breaking interface change?","answer":"Backward compatibility is generally appropriate when consumers cannot be migrated atomically or when compatibility cost is lower than coordinated migration risk. A clean break can be justified when preserving the old contract creates unacceptable security, correctness, or architectural constraints and migration can be controlled. The decision should include an explicit migration and deprecation plan.","principle":"Breaking changes are architectural migrations, not merely implementation changes, and must be evaluated against consumer coordination and invariant preservation.","requirements":["Inventory consumers.","Define migration path.","Define compatibility window.","Define retirement criteria."],"constraints":["Compatibility layers increase maintenance burden.","Clean breaks can strand consumers.","Security fixes may require incompatible behavior changes."],"alternatives":["Backward-compatible extension","Versioned API","Translation layer","Controlled breaking migration"],"tradeoff":{"compatibility":["lower immediate disruption","longer maintenance"],"clean_break":["simpler target contract","higher migration coordination"]},"decision_factors":["consumer count","consumer control","security impact","migration duration","maintenance cost"],"failure_modes":["unknown consumers","partial migration","permanent legacy path","security regression from compatibility"],"security_implication":"Security-critical compatibility behavior must not preserve insecure semantics merely for convenience.","performance_implication":"Translation and compatibility layers can add overhead; removal after migration can simplify execution paths.","testing_guidance":["test mixed-version operation","test migration rollback","test unknown/legacy clients","verify security behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify that every supported contract version has an explicit lifecycle and that retirement does not leave unmanaged consumers.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google API Design Guide"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Microsoft REST API Guidelines"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B25-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B25-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Interface Architecture","concept":"Schema Evolution via Optional Fields vs Versioned Schemas","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an interface evolve through compatible optional fields versus explicit schema versions?","answer":"Optional compatible fields are useful when old consumers can safely ignore additions and new consumers can tolerate missing fields. Explicit versioning is more appropriate when semantics, required fields, validation rules, or representation meaning change incompatibly. Versioning should represent meaningful contract differences rather than every minor extension.","principle":"Use compatible evolution for additive semantic changes and explicit versions for incompatible contract meaning.","requirements":["Define unknown-field behavior.","Define required versus optional semantics.","Define compatibility policy.","Define version retirement process."],"constraints":["Optional fields can accumulate ambiguity.","Version proliferation increases maintenance.","Some consumers reject unknown fields."],"alternatives":["Additive optional fields","Explicit schema version","Content negotiation","Translation layer"],"tradeoff":{"optional":["fewer versions","simpler deployment","potential semantic ambiguity"],"versioned":["explicit contract","higher maintenance and migration burden"]},"decision_factors":["consumer behavior","semantic compatibility","validation rules","version lifespan","migration control"],"failure_modes":["consumer rejection of new fields","ambiguous defaults","silent semantic change","version fragmentation"],"security_implication":"New fields must not unintentionally expose sensitive data to clients that were never authorized to receive them.","performance_implication":"Supporting multiple schema versions can add serialization and translation overhead.","testing_guidance":["test old and new schemas","test unknown fields","test missing optional fields","test authorization for newly exposed data"],"verification_method":"Verify wire compatibility and semantic compatibility independently; syntactic compatibility alone is insufficient.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"JSON Schema Specification"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google API Design Guide"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B25-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B25-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Interface Architecture","concept":"Generated Client SDKs vs Handwritten Clients","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should API consumers use generated clients rather than handwritten client implementations?","answer":"Generated clients are useful when an API has a sufficiently precise machine-readable contract and many clients must remain synchronized with that contract. Handwritten clients may be preferable when client behavior requires specialized abstraction, protocol features not captured by the schema, or fine-grained control. Generated code must still be reviewed for compatibility, security, error handling, and operational behavior.","principle":"Code generation reduces repetitive contract mapping but does not eliminate the need for architectural and security review.","requirements":["Maintain authoritative API schema.","Define generator version.","Define generated-code ownership.","Define customization boundaries."],"constraints":["Generated clients may reflect generator limitations.","Generator upgrades can cause broad changes.","Custom modifications can be overwritten."],"alternatives":["Generated SDK","Handwritten client","Generated low-level transport plus handwritten domain wrapper"],"tradeoff":{"generated":["consistency","reduced repetitive implementation","toolchain dependency"],"handwritten":["control","custom behavior","higher maintenance effort"]},"decision_factors":["API complexity","client count","schema quality","generator maturity","customization needs"],"failure_modes":["stale generated code","incompatible generator upgrade","unsafe generated defaults","manual edits overwritten"],"security_implication":"Generated clients must be checked for authentication handling, TLS validation, secret exposure, unsafe deserialization, and error-data leakage.","performance_implication":"Generated abstractions can introduce overhead depending on implementation; critical paths should be measured rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":["regenerate from schema","compare generated changes","run contract tests","test security-sensitive generated paths"],"verification_method":"Verify generated artifacts against the authoritative contract and execute client integration tests against supported server versions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OpenAPI Specification"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OpenAPI Generator Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B25-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B25-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Interface Architecture","concept":"RPC Interface vs Resource-Oriented HTTP Interface","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What architectural factors distinguish RPC-style interfaces from resource-oriented HTTP interfaces?","answer":"RPC interfaces model operations or procedures explicitly and can closely match domain commands, while resource-oriented HTTP interfaces emphasize resources, representations, standard HTTP semantics, and generic intermediaries. RPC can be appropriate for action-heavy or tightly controlled service interactions; resource-oriented interfaces can provide standardized semantics and broad interoperability. The choice should follow domain interaction patterns and consumer requirements.","principle":"Interface style should reflect the domain's interaction model and interoperability needs rather than stylistic preference.","requirements":["Define operation semantics.","Define error semantics.","Define idempotency where relevant.","Define compatibility and observability conventions."],"constraints":["RPC can become procedure-specific and tightly coupled.","Resource-oriented designs can awkwardly represent complex commands if applied rigidly.","Both styles require authentication and authorization."],"alternatives":["RPC","REST/resource-oriented HTTP","Graph-oriented API","Event interface"],"tradeoff":{"rpc":["explicit commands","strong operation alignment","potential coupling"],"resource_http":["standard HTTP semantics","interoperability","resource modeling complexity"]},"decision_factors":["domain interaction","interoperability","operation semantics","tooling","consumer diversity"],"failure_modes":["action tunneling","ambiguous resource semantics","inconsistent error models","hidden coupling"],"security_implication":"The interface style does not provide authorization automatically; each operation or resource must have explicit access-control semantics.","performance_implication":"Protocol and serialization overhead depend on implementation and payload patterns; interface style alone does not establish performance superiority.","testing_guidance":["test idempotency","test error mapping","test authorization per operation/resource","test compatibility"],"verification_method":"Verify that every interface operation has explicit semantics for identity, authorization, input validation, errors, and retries.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"RFC 9110 HTTP Semantics"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"gRPC Documentation"}],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B03-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B26-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Thread-Based Concurrency vs Async Event-Loop Concurrency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should an architecture choose between thread-based and asynchronous event-loop concurrency?","answer":"Thread-based concurrency can provide a straightforward model for blocking operations and leverage existing synchronous libraries, while asynchronous event loops can efficiently coordinate many I/O-bound operations when the entire execution path supports non-blocking behavior. Async designs can become ineffective or unsafe when blocking operations are introduced without isolation. CPU-bound workloads generally require a different execution strategy such as processes or specialized compute.","principle":"Concurrency architecture must match the blocking behavior and resource profile of the workload and its libraries.","requirements":["Classify workload as I/O-bound or CPU-bound.","Identify blocking dependencies.","Define concurrency limits.","Define cancellation and timeout semantics."],"constraints":["Threads have scheduling and synchronization complexity.","Async event loops can be blocked by synchronous operations.","CPU-bound work may require process or native parallelism."],"alternatives":["Thread pool","Async event loop","Process pool","Hybrid concurrency"],"tradeoff":{"threads":["simple blocking integration","thread synchronization/resource overhead"],"async":["efficient I/O multiplexing","requires non-blocking discipline"]},"decision_factors":["I/O intensity","blocking libraries","CPU intensity","concurrency level","cancellation needs"],"failure_modes":["event-loop blocking","thread exhaustion","deadlock","unbounded task creation"],"security_implication":"Concurrency bugs can create authorization races, shared-state corruption, and request-context leakage.","performance_implication":"Performance depends on workload and runtime; concurrency model should be selected using representative measurements rather than generic throughput assumptions.","testing_guidance":["inject blocking calls","stress concurrency","test cancellation","test shared-state races","measure tail latency"],"verification_method":"Verify that concurrency limits, cancellation, and shared-state synchronization preserve correctness under load.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Python asyncio Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Java Concurrency Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B21-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B26-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Shared Mutable State vs Message Passing","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should concurrent components share mutable state versus communicate through messages?","answer":"Shared mutable state can provide direct access and low coordination overhead within a tightly controlled process but requires synchronization and careful ownership. Message passing can isolate components and make ownership explicit, at the cost of serialization, delivery semantics, and additional lifecycle complexity. The appropriate model depends on contention, isolation, consistency, and communication requirements.","principle":"Prefer the concurrency model that makes ownership and synchronization semantics explicit at the required scale.","requirements":["Define state ownership.","Define synchronization or message delivery semantics.","Define failure behavior.","Define ordering and duplication rules where messages are used."],"constraints":["Shared state creates synchronization coupling.","Message passing adds communication and serialization overhead.","Both models can suffer from incorrect lifecycle management."],"alternatives":["Shared mutable state","Immutable shared state","Message passing","Actor-style ownership"],"tradeoff":{"shared":["low direct-access overhead","race-condition and synchronization risk"],"message":["explicit ownership","serialization and delivery complexity"]},"decision_factors":["contention","state size","failure isolation","communication frequency","ordering requirements"],"failure_modes":["data race","deadlock","lost message","duplicate processing","stale state"],"security_implication":"Shared state can unintentionally expose data across security contexts; message channels require authenticated and authorized producers/consumers.","performance_implication":"Shared memory can reduce communication overhead while message passing can improve isolation; actual performance is implementation dependent.","testing_guidance":["stress concurrent mutation","inject message duplication","reorder messages","test consumer failure"],"verification_method":"Verify that each mutable state item has a single clear ownership model and that communication semantics are explicit.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Erlang/OTP Design Principles"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Java Concurrency Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B16-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B26-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Parallelism vs Serial Execution","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should independent work be executed in parallel instead of serially?","answer":"Parallel execution is appropriate when tasks are sufficiently independent, the available resources can support concurrency, and the reduction in elapsed time justifies coordination overhead. Serial execution can be preferable when tasks share mutable state, ordering is required, work units are too small to amortize scheduling overhead, or resource contention would negate the benefit.","principle":"Parallelism should be introduced only where dependency independence and resource capacity make concurrency beneficial and correct.","requirements":["Identify dependency graph.","Define ordering constraints.","Define resource limits.","Define aggregation semantics."],"constraints":["Parallel work can increase memory and CPU pressure.","Ordering may become nondeterministic.","Coordination overhead can exceed useful work for small tasks."],"alternatives":["Serial execution","Bounded parallelism","Fully parallel execution","Pipeline parallelism"],"tradeoff":{"parallel":["potentially lower elapsed time","higher resource and coordination cost"],"serial":["simpler determinism","potentially longer elapsed time"]},"decision_factors":["dependency graph","task size","resource capacity","ordering","contention"],"failure_modes":["race conditions","resource exhaustion","nondeterministic aggregation","work duplication"],"security_implication":"Parallel tasks must preserve isolation of security context and avoid shared credential or request-state corruption.","performance_implication":"Parallelism does not guarantee speedup; contention, synchronization, and hardware limits can reduce or eliminate gains.","testing_guidance":["vary concurrency","stress resource limits","test deterministic aggregation","inject task failure"],"verification_method":"Verify dependency constraints and bounded resource usage before enabling parallel execution.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Python concurrent.futures Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B26-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B26-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Bounded Concurrency vs Unbounded Concurrency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should concurrent work usually be bounded rather than allowed to grow without a defined limit?","answer":"Bounded concurrency protects CPU, memory, connections, queues, and downstream dependencies from uncontrolled demand. Unbounded concurrency can appear responsive under low load but may amplify overload and cause cascading failure. Limits should be selected from resource capacity and dependency constraints and should include backpressure or admission control.","principle":"Concurrency is a resource allocation decision and must have explicit upper bounds appropriate to system capacity.","requirements":["Define concurrency limits.","Define admission/backpressure behavior.","Define queue capacity.","Define overload response."],"constraints":["Too-low limits can reduce utilization.","Too-high limits can overload dependencies.","Limits must account for multiple resource dimensions."],"alternatives":["Fixed worker pool","Adaptive concurrency limit","Queue with bounded consumers","Unbounded task creation"],"tradeoff":{"bounded":["resource protection","possible queueing"],"unbounded":["simple submission model","resource exhaustion risk"]},"decision_factors":["CPU","memory","connection limits","downstream capacity","latency objective"],"failure_modes":["task explosion","memory exhaustion","connection exhaustion","cascading overload"],"security_implication":"Concurrency limits can act as resource-abuse controls but should not substitute for authentication or application-level abuse protection.","performance_implication":"Optimal concurrency is workload dependent and should be measured; increasing concurrency can increase throughput only until a bottleneck is reached.","testing_guidance":["load beyond capacity","test queue saturation","measure latency under different limits","verify recovery after overload"],"verification_method":"Verify that overload remains bounded and that the system degrades according to a declared policy when capacity is exceeded.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B17-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B26-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Work Stealing vs Fixed Worker Assignment","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should concurrent workloads use dynamic work stealing instead of fixed worker assignment?","answer":"Work stealing is useful when task durations vary and balanced utilization is important because idle workers can acquire available work. Fixed assignment can provide predictable locality and simpler ownership when workloads are stable or state affinity is required. Dynamic scheduling can complicate ordering, debugging, and locality assumptions.","principle":"Dynamic scheduling is valuable when workload imbalance dominates; fixed ownership is valuable when locality and deterministic placement dominate.","requirements":["Define task independence.","Define worker ownership and locality.","Define scheduling fairness.","Define task retry semantics."],"constraints":["Work stealing can move work away from useful local state.","Fixed assignment can leave resources idle.","Scheduling overhead must be considered for small tasks."],"alternatives":["Fixed partitioning","Work stealing","Central queue","Partitioned queues with rebalancing"],"tradeoff":{"work_stealing":["better load balancing potential","more dynamic scheduling complexity"],"fixed":["predictable placement","imbalance risk"]},"decision_factors":["task-duration variance","state locality","fairness","ordering","scheduling overhead"],"failure_modes":["hot partition","worker starvation","duplicate task ownership","locality loss"],"security_implication":"Task reassignment must preserve tenant and authorization isolation; work movement must not cross trust boundaries unintentionally.","performance_implication":"Dynamic balancing can improve utilization for heterogeneous task durations but introduces scheduling overhead.","testing_guidance":["vary task durations","kill workers","measure queue imbalance","test tenant isolation after reassignment"],"verification_method":"Verify task ownership, reassignment, retry, and isolation invariants under worker failure and uneven workloads.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Java ForkJoinPool Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Operating Systems and Concurrent Programming literature"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B26-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B27-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Dependency Architecture","concept":"Synchronous Dependency Call vs Local Replica","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a service call an authoritative dependency directly versus maintaining a local replica of required data?","answer":"Direct calls preserve a clear source of truth and current data semantics but add network dependency and latency. Local replicas can improve availability and read latency but introduce replication, staleness, storage, and reconciliation complexity. Replication should be used when those costs are justified by explicit availability or performance requirements.","principle":"Replicate data only when reduced dependency coupling or improved locality provides sufficient value to justify consistency management.","requirements":["Identify authoritative source.","Define replication mechanism.","Define acceptable staleness.","Define replica rebuild process."],"constraints":["Local replicas can become stale.","Direct calls can create dependency chains.","Replica maintenance increases operational complexity."],"alternatives":["Direct authoritative read","Local cache","Materialized replica","Periodic snapshot"],"tradeoff":{"direct":["fresh source semantics","network dependency"],"replica":["lower dependency/read latency","staleness and synchronization complexity"]},"decision_factors":["freshness","dependency availability","read volume","latency","data size"],"failure_modes":["stale authorization data","replication lag","replica divergence","dependency cascade"],"security_implication":"Replicated security-sensitive data must preserve access-control and revocation semantics; stale replicas can create security exposure.","performance_implication":"Local replicas can reduce network dependency but consume storage and update resources.","testing_guidance":["delay replication","disable authoritative source","test replica rebuild","validate revocation propagation"],"verification_method":"Verify that replica staleness cannot violate declared correctness or security invariants.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B22-R004","related_to:D41-B23-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B27-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Dependency Architecture","concept":"Library Dependency vs Remote Service Dependency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should functionality be implemented as an in-process library instead of a remote service?","answer":"An in-process library is appropriate when functionality is stable, computationally local, and benefits from low-latency invocation and simple deployment. A remote service is appropriate when independent ownership, scaling, isolation, centralized data, or independent lifecycle management provide material value. A remote service introduces network failure, versioning, and operational dependencies that should be justified.","principle":"Create a service boundary when independent lifecycle or trust/resource isolation provides meaningful value, not merely to distribute code.","requirements":["Define ownership boundary.","Define deployment independence.","Define data ownership.","Define network failure semantics."],"constraints":["Libraries couple release lifecycles.","Services introduce network and operational complexity.","Duplicated library copies can diverge."],"alternatives":["In-process library","Remote service","Shared library plus optional service boundary"],"tradeoff":{"library":["low invocation overhead","simple local execution","release coupling"],"service":["independent deployment and scaling","network and operational complexity"]},"decision_factors":["ownership","release cadence","scaling","data locality","security isolation","reuse"],"failure_modes":["hidden distributed dependency","library version drift","network outage","service over-fragmentation"],"security_implication":"Remote boundaries can provide stronger isolation but require explicit authentication, authorization, and secure transport.","performance_implication":"In-process calls avoid network overhead; service calls add latency and serialization but can isolate resource-intensive workloads.","testing_guidance":["compare integration boundaries","test service outage","test library version compatibility","measure representative invocation paths"],"verification_method":"Verify that the selected boundary provides a concrete lifecycle, ownership, scaling, or isolation benefit.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Sam Newman, Building Microservices"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B03-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B27-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Dependency Architecture","concept":"Direct Dependency Graph vs Dependency Inversion","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should architecture invert dependencies through abstractions instead of allowing direct component dependencies?","answer":"Dependency inversion is useful when higher-level policies should remain independent of replaceable infrastructure or implementations, enabling substitution and isolated testing. Direct dependencies can be simpler when the implementation is stable, internal, and unlikely to require substitution. Excessive abstraction can obscure actual relationships and create unnecessary indirection.","principle":"Introduce dependency inversion when it protects a meaningful architectural boundary; avoid abstraction that has no independent reason to exist.","requirements":["Identify stable policy versus replaceable implementation.","Define abstraction ownership.","Define substitution contract.","Keep dependency direction explicit."],"constraints":["Abstractions add cognitive and maintenance cost.","Direct dependencies can create tight coupling.","Incorrect abstraction boundaries can freeze the wrong design."],"alternatives":["Direct dependency","Interface-based dependency inversion","Dependency injection","Adapter boundary"],"tradeoff":{"inversion":["testability and replaceability","additional abstraction"],"direct":["simplicity","tighter coupling"]},"decision_factors":["change frequency","replaceability","testing isolation","ownership","architectural boundary"],"failure_modes":["leaky abstraction","interface over-generalization","hidden coupling","abstraction churn"],"security_implication":"Dependency boundaries should prevent untrusted implementations from bypassing security policy or validation contracts.","performance_implication":"Abstraction overhead is usually context dependent; critical paths should be measured rather than assumed to be free.","testing_guidance":["substitute implementations","test contract behavior","verify dependency direction","detect bypass paths"],"verification_method":"Verify dependency direction against intended architecture and ensure implementations satisfy the declared abstraction contract.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Robert C. Martin, Clean Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Dependency Injection"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B20-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B27-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Dependency Architecture","concept":"Strong Compile-Time Dependency vs Dynamic Plugin Dependency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a system use statically integrated components versus dynamically loaded plugins?","answer":"Static integration is appropriate when component compatibility, deployment simplicity, and predictable startup behavior are more important than runtime extensibility. Dynamic plugins are useful when independently developed extensions, optional capabilities, or third-party integration are core requirements. Dynamic loading requires stronger interface contracts, compatibility checks, isolation, and supply-chain controls.","principle":"Runtime extensibility should be treated as an architectural requirement with explicit trust and compatibility boundaries, not as a free form of modularity.","requirements":["Define plugin contract.","Define compatibility/version rules.","Define trust and isolation model.","Define installation and update lifecycle."],"constraints":["Plugins expand the executable trust surface.","Dynamic compatibility is harder to verify.","Static integration can increase deployment coupling."],"alternatives":["Static linking/integration","Dynamic plugins","Sandboxed plugin runtime"],"tradeoff":{"static":["predictability","simpler lifecycle","less runtime extensibility"],"dynamic":["extensibility","higher trust and compatibility complexity"]},"decision_factors":["extensibility","trust model","deployment model","third-party ecosystem","compatibility"],"failure_modes":["malicious plugin","incompatible plugin","runtime crash","dependency collision"],"security_implication":"Plugins execute potentially privileged code and require strong provenance, integrity validation, least privilege, isolation, and update controls.","performance_implication":"Dynamic discovery/loading can add startup overhead and indirection; runtime impact depends on implementation.","testing_guidance":["load incompatible plugin","test malicious input/plugin","verify signature/integrity where applicable","test isolation"],"verification_method":"Verify plugin identity, compatibility, permissions, and isolation before activation.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OWASP Software Component Verification Standard"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Python importlib Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B27-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B27-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Dependency Architecture","concept":"Vendor-Specific Integration vs Portable Abstraction","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should architecture use provider-specific capabilities instead of a portable abstraction layer?","answer":"Provider-specific features are justified when they materially satisfy requirements that a portable abstraction cannot meet efficiently or correctly. Portable abstractions are useful when multiple providers are genuinely expected or migration flexibility has high value. Building a lowest-common-denominator abstraction solely for hypothetical portability can remove useful capabilities and create its own maintenance cost.","principle":"Portability is valuable when it is an actual requirement; provider coupling should be accepted deliberately when it provides material benefits.","requirements":["Identify real portability requirement.","Document provider-specific dependencies.","Define migration assumptions.","Define abstraction ownership."],"constraints":["Provider-specific features increase switching cost.","Portable abstractions can limit functionality.","Abstraction layers may become expensive to maintain."],"alternatives":["Provider-specific implementation","Portable abstraction","Adapter architecture preserving provider capabilities"],"tradeoff":{"specific":["feature depth","provider coupling"],"portable":["migration flexibility","possible capability loss and abstraction complexity"]},"decision_factors":["number of providers","migration likelihood","feature requirements","cost of switching","team expertise"],"failure_modes":["false portability","abstraction leakage","vendor lock-in discovered late","unsupported feature gap"],"security_implication":"Provider-specific controls may provide security capabilities unavailable in a generic abstraction; portability must not remove required protections.","performance_implication":"Provider-native features may exploit platform optimizations, but portability layers can add overhead depending on implementation.","testing_guidance":["test abstraction against each supported provider","identify unsupported semantics","perform migration exercise if portability is contractual"],"verification_method":"Verify that claimed portability covers actual required semantics rather than only API shape.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B26-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B28-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Configuration in Code vs External Configuration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should configuration be embedded in code versus supplied externally at deployment or runtime?","answer":"Stable behavior that defines the software's contract can belong in code, while environment-specific values, operational parameters, endpoints, feature settings, and secrets generally benefit from external configuration. External configuration should have explicit schema, validation, precedence, versioning, and secure handling rules.","principle":"Separate software behavior from environment-specific configuration while keeping configuration typed, validated, and auditable.","requirements":["Define configuration schema.","Define precedence rules.","Validate configuration before use.","Protect secrets separately from ordinary configuration."],"constraints":["External configuration can drift from code.","Too much configuration can make behavior difficult to reason about.","Configuration errors can become runtime failures."],"alternatives":["Compile-time constants","Environment configuration","Configuration service","Typed configuration files"],"tradeoff":{"code":["strong version control","less runtime flexibility"],"external":["environment flexibility","drift and validation risk"]},"decision_factors":["environment variation","change frequency","security sensitivity","auditability","deployment model"],"failure_modes":["invalid configuration","secret leakage","configuration drift","ambiguous precedence"],"security_implication":"Secrets should not be embedded in source code or ordinary configuration repositories; access and rotation must be controlled.","performance_implication":"Configuration lookup strategy can affect startup or request performance; frequently accessed configuration may need local caching with explicit refresh semantics.","testing_guidance":["test missing configuration","test invalid values","test precedence","test secret redaction"],"verification_method":"Verify that all configuration values have defined types, defaults where appropriate, validation rules, and provenance.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"The Twelve-Factor App"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST Secure Software Development Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B27-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B28-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Feature Flags vs Deployment-Based Feature Control","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should feature flags be used instead of deployment-time or code-level feature selection?","answer":"Feature flags are useful when features need controlled rollout, rapid disablement, experimentation, or independent activation from deployment. They introduce runtime decision complexity, state management, testing combinations, and cleanup obligations. Deployment-based selection is simpler when activation changes are infrequent and coordinated deployment is acceptable.","principle":"Use feature flags for controlled variability that has operational value, and retire flags when their decision period ends.","requirements":["Define flag owner.","Define targeting semantics.","Define default and failure behavior.","Define retirement date or condition."],"constraints":["Flag combinations increase test space.","Flag state can drift.","Incorrect targeting can expose unfinished functionality."],"alternatives":["Compile-time configuration","Deployment-time configuration","Runtime feature flags"],"tradeoff":{"flags":["controlled rollout","runtime complexity"],"deployment":["simpler runtime behavior","less operational flexibility"]},"decision_factors":["rollout control","rollback need","experimentation","test complexity","flag lifespan"],"failure_modes":["stale flag","flag combination explosion","wrong audience","insecure default"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive feature flags require authorization-aware evaluation and fail-safe defaults; flags must not become hidden access-control substitutes.","performance_implication":"Runtime flag evaluation can add overhead; local evaluation or caching may reduce dependency cost when appropriate.","testing_guidance":["test every supported flag state","test flag-service failure","test targeting boundaries","verify cleanup"],"verification_method":"Verify flag ownership, default behavior, targeting rules, and retirement criteria.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Feature Toggles"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B19-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B28-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Static Configuration Snapshot vs Dynamic Configuration Service","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should configuration be loaded once at startup versus updated dynamically during runtime?","answer":"Static startup configuration is simpler, deterministic, and easier to reason about, while dynamic configuration can support operational changes without redeployment. Dynamic updates require versioning, validation, propagation, rollback, and behavior during configuration-service failure. Critical behavior should define whether an update applies immediately, at request boundaries, or only after restart.","principle":"Dynamic configuration is an operational capability that requires consistency and rollback semantics, not merely a remote key-value lookup.","requirements":["Define configuration version.","Define update propagation semantics.","Define rollback mechanism.","Define behavior when configuration source is unavailable."],"constraints":["Dynamic updates can change behavior without code deployment.","Stale or partially propagated configuration can create inconsistent behavior.","Configuration changes can be security-sensitive."],"alternatives":["Startup-only configuration","Periodic refresh","Push-based configuration","Hybrid static/dynamic configuration"],"tradeoff":{"static":["determinism","requires deployment/restart for changes"],"dynamic":["operational flexibility","runtime consistency and rollback complexity"]},"decision_factors":["change frequency","criticality","restart cost","consistency needs","operational controls"],"failure_modes":["partial rollout","invalid update","stale configuration","rollback failure"],"security_implication":"Dynamic security configuration must be authenticated, authorized, integrity-protected, audited, and fail-safe.","performance_implication":"Frequent remote configuration checks can add network and CPU overhead; caching changes freshness semantics.","testing_guidance":["inject invalid configuration","simulate configuration-service outage","test rollback","test mixed-version propagation"],"verification_method":"Verify configuration changes are versioned, validated, observable, and reversible before activation.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Kubernetes Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST Secure Software Development Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B24-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B28-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Environment-Specific Configuration vs Immutable Application Artifact","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should architecture separate environment differences from application artifacts?","answer":"Immutable application artifacts reduce ambiguity by keeping the deployed software identical across environments while supplying environment-specific configuration externally. Building different artifacts for each environment can simplify some compile-time differences but increases the risk that tested and deployed artifacts diverge. The preferred boundary depends on runtime requirements and build tooling.","principle":"Where practical, promote artifact immutability and move environment variation into explicitly controlled configuration or infrastructure.","requirements":["Define artifact identity.","Define environment configuration.","Track configuration version.","Ensure build reproducibility."],"constraints":["Some platforms require environment-specific compilation.","External configuration can still drift.","Immutable artifacts do not guarantee immutable infrastructure."],"alternatives":["One artifact plus external configuration","Environment-specific builds","Parameterized deployment package"],"tradeoff":{"immutable_artifact":["stronger promotion consistency","requires configuration discipline"],"environment_builds":["flexibility","artifact divergence risk"]},"decision_factors":["build reproducibility","environment differences","deployment process","runtime configurability"],"failure_modes":["works-in-test-only","artifact mismatch","configuration drift","unreproducible build"],"security_implication":"Environment secrets must remain outside artifacts when possible and be injected through controlled secret-management mechanisms.","performance_implication":"Artifact strategy generally has little direct runtime impact; build and deployment behavior are the main trade-off dimensions.","testing_guidance":["promote identical artifact across environments","compare artifact digests","test configuration validation","reproduce build"],"verification_method":"Verify that the production artifact identity is traceable to a tested artifact and that environment differences are explicit and controlled.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"The Twelve-Factor App"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"SLSA Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B19-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B28-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Configuration Schema Strictness vs Flexible Configuration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should configuration schemas reject unknown or invalid fields instead of tolerating them?","answer":"Strict configuration validation is appropriate when configuration errors can cause unsafe behavior, silent misconfiguration, or difficult diagnosis. Flexible configuration can support gradual schema evolution but risks silently ignored or misspelled settings. A mature architecture can allow controlled compatibility while still rejecting values that affect correctness or security.","principle":"Configuration validation should fail early when ambiguity can produce incorrect or unsafe system behavior.","requirements":["Define valid fields and types.","Define unknown-field policy.","Define defaults explicitly.","Produce actionable validation errors."],"constraints":["Strict schemas can complicate backward-compatible rollout.","Flexible schemas can hide operator mistakes.","Defaults can conceal missing required intent."],"alternatives":["Strict schema","Backward-compatible schema","Strict core plus extensible metadata"],"tradeoff":{"strict":["early failure","strong correctness","migration friction"],"flexible":["easier extension","silent error risk"]},"decision_factors":["safety impact","configuration ownership","evolution rate","operator expertise","compatibility needs"],"failure_modes":["misspelled option silently ignored","unsafe default","invalid type accepted","partial configuration rollout"],"security_implication":"Security configuration should generally reject ambiguous or unknown security-critical settings rather than silently ignoring them.","performance_implication":"Validation is usually a startup/control-plane cost; runtime configuration parsing should be minimized when not required.","testing_guidance":["inject unknown fields","test invalid types","test missing required values","test compatibility transitions"],"verification_method":"Verify that invalid security- or correctness-critical configuration cannot silently activate unintended behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"JSON Schema Specification"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST Secure Software Development Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B28-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B29-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Modularity Architecture","concept":"Fine-Grained Modules vs Coarse-Grained Modules","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should an architecture choose the granularity of modules within a codebase?","answer":"Fine-grained modules can isolate responsibilities and enable focused reuse, but excessive fragmentation increases dependency navigation and interface overhead. Coarse-grained modules simplify structure but can accumulate unrelated responsibilities and make change isolation harder. Module boundaries should follow cohesive responsibilities and stable dependency relationships.","principle":"Module granularity should maximize cohesion and minimize unnecessary coupling rather than maximize module count.","requirements":["Define responsibility boundaries.","Identify dependency direction.","Measure change coupling where possible.","Define public versus internal module interfaces."],"constraints":["Too many modules increase cognitive overhead.","Too few modules create large change surfaces.","Premature modularization can freeze unstable boundaries."],"alternatives":["Fine-grained modules","Coarse-grained modules","Layered modular architecture"],"tradeoff":{"fine":["isolation","reuse","navigation overhead"],"coarse":["simplicity","larger change and dependency surfaces"]},"decision_factors":["cohesion","change coupling","team ownership","reuse","dependency complexity"],"failure_modes":["cyclic dependencies","god module","abstraction leakage","fragmentation"],"security_implication":"Module boundaries can support privilege and trust separation but must not be mistaken for runtime security isolation unless enforced by the platform.","performance_implication":"Excessive in-process abstraction can add indirection, but module count alone does not establish runtime performance impact.","testing_guidance":["analyze dependency cycles","test module contracts","measure change impact","verify ownership boundaries"],"verification_method":"Verify that module dependencies form the intended direction and that each module has a cohesive responsibility.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Robert C. Martin, Clean Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Refactoring"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B27-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B29-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Modularity Architecture","concept":"Layered Architecture vs Feature-Oriented Architecture","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should code organization follow technical layers versus end-to-end business features?","answer":"Layered organization can make shared technical concerns and dependency direction explicit, while feature-oriented organization can keep business behavior and related components together, reducing cross-layer navigation for changes. Either can become problematic when boundaries do not reflect actual dependencies. The architecture should choose the organization that makes ownership, change impact, and dependency direction easiest to enforce.","principle":"Code organization should make the system's dominant change and dependency boundaries visible.","requirements":["Define dependency direction.","Define ownership boundaries.","Define cross-cutting concern handling.","Prevent uncontrolled layer bypass."],"constraints":["Layers can scatter one feature across many directories or packages.","Feature-oriented structures can duplicate shared infrastructure concepts.","Both models can degrade without architectural enforcement."],"alternatives":["Layered","Feature-oriented","Hexagonal/ports-and-adapters","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":{"layered":["clear technical separation","feature changes cross many layers"],"feature":["strong feature locality","shared concerns require deliberate design"]},"decision_factors":["change patterns","team ownership","dependency direction","cross-cutting concerns","reuse"],"failure_modes":["layer bypass","feature leakage","duplicated infrastructure","cyclic dependency"],"security_implication":"Security boundaries should remain explicit regardless of source-code organization.","performance_implication":"Source organization generally has limited direct runtime effect; architectural indirection can matter when it changes execution paths.","testing_guidance":["trace feature changes","check dependency rules","test cross-cutting policies","detect forbidden imports"],"verification_method":"Verify dependency rules and representative feature change paths against the selected organization model.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Robert C. Martin, Clean Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B29-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B29-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Modularity Architecture","concept":"Compile-Time Modularity vs Runtime Modularity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should modularity be enforced at build time versus discovered and composed at runtime?","answer":"Compile-time modularity provides stronger dependency and compatibility guarantees before deployment and is appropriate for stable components. Runtime modularity supports optional capabilities and dynamic extension but requires runtime discovery, compatibility validation, isolation, and failure handling. The decision should reflect whether extensibility is a core requirement or incidental convenience.","principle":"Runtime composition should be introduced only when dynamic extensibility provides value that static composition cannot provide acceptably.","requirements":["Define module contract.","Define discovery mechanism.","Define compatibility validation.","Define failure and isolation behavior."],"constraints":["Runtime discovery increases failure modes.","Compile-time composition reduces dynamic flexibility.","Dynamic components increase supply-chain and trust concerns."],"alternatives":["Static modules","Dynamic plugins","Hybrid static core plus dynamic extensions"],"tradeoff":{"compile_time":["early validation","less runtime flexibility"],"runtime":["extensibility","higher runtime complexity and trust surface"]},"decision_factors":["extensibility","compatibility","trust","deployment model","failure tolerance"],"failure_modes":["runtime incompatibility","missing module","malicious extension","partial initialization"],"security_implication":"Dynamic module loading must enforce provenance, integrity, least privilege, and isolation appropriate to the extension's authority.","performance_implication":"Runtime discovery/loading adds startup or activation overhead; steady-state impact depends on implementation.","testing_guidance":["load missing modules","load incompatible modules","test malicious module behavior","test partial initialization"],"verification_method":"Verify module identity, contract compatibility, permissions, and lifecycle before activation.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OWASP Software Component Verification Standard"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Python importlib Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B27-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B29-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Modularity Architecture","concept":"Shared Utility Module vs Domain-Owned Utility","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should reusable logic live in a shared utility module versus remain owned by the domain component that uses it?","answer":"Shared utilities are appropriate for genuinely generic behavior with stable semantics and multiple independent consumers. Domain-owned helpers are preferable when behavior depends on domain rules or is likely to evolve with one bounded context. Prematurely extracting domain logic into shared utilities can create coupling and prevent independent evolution.","principle":"Share only behavior whose semantics are independently stable and generic.","requirements":["Identify semantic ownership.","Assess reuse across independent consumers.","Define API stability expectations.","Avoid sharing mutable domain state."],"constraints":["Shared utilities create dependency coupling.","Duplicated generic logic can drift.","Domain semantics hidden in utilities become difficult to locate."],"alternatives":["Shared utility library","Domain-local helper","Core platform utility"],"tradeoff":{"shared":["reuse and consistency","coupling and change coordination"],"domain_owned":["locality and independent evolution","possible duplication"]},"decision_factors":["semantic stability","number of independent consumers","change coupling","ownership"],"failure_modes":["utility becomes dumping ground","breaking shared consumers","hidden domain dependency"],"security_implication":"Security utilities should be centrally maintained when consistency is critical, while domain-specific authorization should remain explicit and context-aware.","performance_implication":"Shared utility placement generally has limited runtime impact unless it changes algorithmic or dependency behavior.","testing_guidance":["test utility contract independently","trace consumers","test version compatibility","detect domain leakage"],"verification_method":"Verify that shared logic has semantics that remain valid independently of any single domain consumer.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Refactoring"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Robert C. Martin, Clean Architecture"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B29-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B29-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Modularity Architecture","concept":"Stable Abstraction Boundary vs Direct Concrete Dependency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a module expose an abstraction boundary instead of directly exposing concrete implementation details?","answer":"An abstraction boundary is valuable when consumers should depend on stable behavior while implementations may change independently. Direct concrete dependencies are simpler when implementation and contract are intentionally identical and change together. Abstractions should encode meaningful stable semantics rather than merely mirror every implementation method.","principle":"An abstraction is justified when it protects a stable contract from implementation change or isolates a meaningful architectural boundary.","requirements":["Define stable semantics.","Define implementation-independent contract.","Identify expected variation.","Prevent leakage of implementation details."],"constraints":["Abstractions can become artificial and difficult to evolve.","Concrete exposure can create accidental coupling.","Poorly designed interfaces force consumers to depend on irrelevant behavior."],"alternatives":["Concrete dependency","Minimal interface","Adapter boundary","Port-and-adapter abstraction"],"tradeoff":{"abstraction":["replaceability and decoupling","interface maintenance"],"concrete":["simplicity","implementation coupling"]},"decision_factors":["expected change","consumer count","implementation variability","testing needs","ownership"],"failure_modes":["leaky abstraction","fat interface","premature abstraction","implementation lock-in"],"security_implication":"Abstraction boundaries should prevent consumers from obtaining capabilities beyond the intended contract.","performance_implication":"Indirection overhead is context dependent; architectural abstraction should not be rejected or accepted based on assumed micro-costs without evidence.","testing_guidance":["substitute implementation","test contract only","check interface leakage","verify forbidden concrete dependencies"],"verification_method":"Verify that consumers depend only on behavior declared as part of the stable contract.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Robert C. Martin, Clean Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Refactoring"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B27-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B30-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Selection","concept":"Architecture Evaluation by Quality Attribute Scenarios","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should architecture alternatives be evaluated against quality attributes rather than vague preferences?","answer":"Translate important quality goals into concrete scenarios containing stimulus, context, expected response, and measurable response criteria. Evaluate each architecture against those scenarios and record evidence, assumptions, trade-offs, and unresolved uncertainty. This produces a decision trace that can be revisited when requirements or evidence change.","principle":"Architecture decisions should be evaluated against explicit quality-attribute scenarios rather than generic labels such as scalable or maintainable.","requirements":["Identify quality attributes.","Define concrete scenarios.","Define response measures.","Record evidence and assumptions."],"constraints":["Some quality attributes are difficult to quantify early.","Scenario weights can reflect subjective business priorities.","Architecture evaluation is sensitive to workload assumptions."],"alternatives":["Informal architecture preference","Quality-attribute scenario analysis","Architecture trade-study"],"tradeoff":{"scenario_based":["traceable evaluation","requires explicit requirements and evidence"],"informal":["fast initial choice","weak reproducibility and auditability"]},"decision_factors":["business objectives","risk","quality attributes","evidence quality","uncertainty"],"failure_modes":["vague criteria","unmeasured assumptions","criteria drift","false precision"],"security_implication":"Security scenarios should include concrete threat conditions, protected assets, trust boundaries, and required responses.","performance_implication":"Performance scenarios should specify workload, environment, metric, and measurement method; unsupported numeric claims remain unknown.","testing_guidance":["convert scenarios into tests where feasible","validate assumptions","compare alternatives under representative workload"],"verification_method":"Verify that every major architecture decision maps to explicit scenarios and evidence or clearly labeled assumptions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"SEI Quality Attribute Workshop literature"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B01-R001","related_to:D41-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B30-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Selection","concept":"Architecture Risk Reduction Through Spikes vs Full Implementation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an uncertain architecture be investigated with a technical spike before committing to full implementation?","answer":"A technical spike is appropriate when an unresolved technical uncertainty could materially affect architecture selection and can be investigated with a bounded experiment. The spike should test a specific hypothesis rather than become an unstructured prototype. Results should record environment, assumptions, measurements if any, limitations, and whether the result changes the architectural decision.","principle":"Resolve high-impact uncertainty with the smallest credible experiment before committing irreversible architectural cost.","requirements":["State the uncertainty explicitly.","Define experiment success criteria.","Bound the experiment scope.","Record evidence and limitations."],"constraints":["Spikes can produce misleading results if the environment differs from production.","Prototype code can be mistaken for production-ready implementation.","Experiments consume engineering time."],"alternatives":["Proceed without experiment","Technical spike","Proof of concept","Production pilot"],"tradeoff":{"spike":["uncertainty reduction","short-term engineering cost"],"full_commitment":["faster initial progress","higher risk of late architectural discovery"]},"decision_factors":["uncertainty magnitude","reversibility","experiment cost","production similarity","decision deadline"],"failure_modes":["prototype bias","unrepresentative benchmark","scope creep","false confidence"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive experiments should use isolated environments and synthetic or appropriately protected data.","performance_implication":"Spike measurements are valid only within their tested environment and workload; they must not be generalized without evidence.","testing_guidance":["define hypothesis before experiment","record environment","repeat measurements when relevant","document limitations"],"verification_method":"Verify that the spike actually tests the architectural uncertainty and that conclusions are bounded by observed evidence.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"SEI Architecture Tradeoff Analysis Method"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B01-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B30-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Selection","concept":"Reversible vs Irreversible Architecture Decisions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should architecture distinguish decisions that are cheap to reverse from decisions that create durable coupling?","answer":"Reversible decisions can be deferred or tested with lower commitment when requirements are uncertain, while irreversible decisions deserve stronger evidence, broader review, and explicit documentation before adoption. Examples include persistent data models, public contracts, infrastructure coupling, and organizational ownership boundaries. Reversibility should be evaluated in terms of actual migration cost, not merely theoretical replaceability.","principle":"Apply greater decision discipline to choices whose reversal requires data migration, consumer coordination, or structural reorganization.","requirements":["Estimate reversal cost.","Identify affected consumers and data.","Document assumptions.","Define migration path for high-risk decisions."],"constraints":["Reversibility estimates are uncertain.","A decision that appears local may create hidden dependency.","Delay can itself have cost."],"alternatives":["Immediate commitment","Time-boxed deferral","Experimental implementation","Explicit architectural commitment"],"tradeoff":{"reversible":["lower commitment risk","may defer useful certainty"],"irreversible":["can provide strong foundation","higher migration cost if wrong"]},"decision_factors":["migration cost","consumer coupling","data persistence","uncertainty","time pressure"],"failure_modes":["hidden irreversible dependency","premature commitment","migration under load","data lock-in"],"security_implication":"Irreversible security architecture decisions should be reviewed for long-term trust-boundary and credential-management consequences.","performance_implication":"Some irreversible choices constrain future scaling options; performance assumptions should be documented and validated before hard-to-change commitments.","testing_guidance":["identify migration path","prototype irreversible boundaries","test compatibility assumptions","review dependency graph"],"verification_method":"Verify that high-reversal-cost decisions have explicit evidence, owners, assumptions, and migration considerations.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"SEI Architecture Tradeoff Analysis Method"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Evolutionary Architecture"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B10-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B30-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Selection","concept":"Architecture Decision Record vs Living Architecture Model","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should architecture decisions be captured as decision records versus maintained through a continuously updated architecture model?","answer":"Decision records preserve why a significant choice was made, including alternatives, constraints, and evidence at a point in time. A living architecture model represents the current structural state. They serve different purposes and are complementary: records preserve decision history while the current model supports present-state reasoning. Maintaining only one can lose either historical rationale or current structure.","principle":"Separate architectural decision history from current architecture state while maintaining links between them.","requirements":["Record decision context.","Record alternatives and rationale.","Maintain current architecture model.","Link decisions to affected components."],"constraints":["Decision records become stale if not updated or superseded.","Living models can lose historical rationale.","Both require ownership and maintenance."],"alternatives":["ADRs only","Architecture model only","ADRs plus living model"],"tradeoff":{"records":["historical rationale","can diverge from current state"],"living_model":["current structure","historical reasoning may disappear"]},"decision_factors":["system complexity","change frequency","governance requirements","auditability","team size"],"failure_modes":["decision without current implementation","architecture drift","orphaned ADR","undocumented reversal"],"security_implication":"Security architecture decisions should remain traceable to current trust boundaries and controls.","performance_implication":"Architecture models should distinguish documented intent from measured runtime behavior.","testing_guidance":["detect drift between model and implementation","verify decision links","review superseded decisions","validate current architecture"],"verification_method":"Verify that significant decisions are traceable from rationale to current architecture and implementation status.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Architecture Decision Records community practice"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B30-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Selection","concept":"Architecture Conformance Tests vs Manual Architecture Review","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should architectural rules be enforced automatically through tests instead of relying only on manual review?","answer":"Automated architecture conformance tests are valuable for rules that can be expressed deterministically, such as dependency direction, forbidden imports, layer boundaries, API compatibility, or required security controls. Manual review remains necessary for qualitative trade-offs, ambiguous requirements, and decisions requiring contextual judgment. Combining both reduces repeated manual checking while preserving human architectural reasoning.","principle":"Automate deterministic architectural constraints and reserve human review for context-dependent decisions.","requirements":["Define enforceable architecture rules.","Define test scope.","Define acceptable exceptions.","Integrate checks into validation workflow."],"constraints":["Not all architecture qualities are mechanically testable.","Poorly designed rules can block legitimate evolution.","Tests can validate structure without proving business suitability."],"alternatives":["Manual review","Architecture conformance tests","Hybrid automated plus manual governance"],"tradeoff":{"automated":["repeatability","early detection","limited semantic coverage"],"manual":["contextual reasoning","higher recurring effort","review inconsistency risk"]},"decision_factors":["rule determinism","change frequency","failure cost","tooling capability","governance needs"],"failure_modes":["false positives","rule bypass","tests encoding obsolete architecture","structural compliance without behavioral correctness"],"security_implication":"Automated checks can enforce mandatory security boundaries but should complement, not replace, threat modeling and security testing.","performance_implication":"Architecture tests should focus on structural properties and avoid being mistaken for runtime performance validation.","testing_guidance":["run conformance checks in CI","test allowed exceptions","review rule coverage","detect obsolete rules"],"verification_method":"Verify that each automated architecture rule has a clear invariant, deterministic failure condition, and owner for maintenance.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Thoughtworks, Evolutionary Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"ArchUnit Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B20-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B31-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Multi-Tenancy Architecture","concept":"Shared Application with Shared Data vs Tenant-Isolated Data Stores","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a multi-tenant system share data infrastructure versus isolate tenant data into separate stores?","answer":"Shared data infrastructure can improve resource utilization and simplify fleet management, while tenant-isolated stores can strengthen isolation, simplify tenant-specific recovery, or satisfy regulatory and contractual requirements. The decision should be based on isolation requirements, scale, operational cost, noisy-neighbor risk, recovery requirements, and tenant-specific data residency needs.","principle":"Tenant isolation should match the required security, compliance, recovery, and operational boundary rather than assuming physical separation is always necessary.","requirements":["Define tenant isolation level.","Define tenant data ownership.","Define cross-tenant access rules.","Define backup and recovery scope.","Define tenant lifecycle operations."],"constraints":["Shared stores require strong tenant-aware authorization and data partitioning.","Isolated stores increase infrastructure and operational overhead.","Cross-tenant analytics can become more complex with physical isolation."],"alternatives":["Shared database with tenant partitioning","Schema-per-tenant","Database-per-tenant","Dedicated infrastructure per tenant"],"tradeoff":{"shared":["higher resource utilization potential","simpler fleet management","stronger dependence on logical isolation"],"isolated":["stronger physical/logical boundary","higher operational cost and management complexity"]},"decision_factors":["security isolation","compliance","tenant count","tenant size variance","data residency","recovery requirements","cost"],"failure_modes":["cross-tenant data leakage","tenant identifier omission","noisy neighbor","incorrect backup scope","tenant deletion affecting other tenants"],"security_implication":"Shared tenancy requires authorization and data-partitioning controls on every relevant access path; tenant identifiers must not be trusted solely because they originate from client input.","performance_implication":"Shared infrastructure can improve utilization but may create contention; isolated infrastructure can improve predictability while increasing idle capacity.","testing_guidance":["attempt cross-tenant reads and writes","test missing tenant context","simulate noisy tenants","restore individual tenant data","test tenant deletion"],"verification_method":"Verify that every tenant-scoped data operation derives and enforces trusted tenant context and that recovery boundaries match the declared isolation model.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B07-R002","related_to:D41-B24-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B31-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Multi-Tenancy Architecture","concept":"Tenant-Aware Application vs Separate Application Instances","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should tenants share application instances versus receive separately deployed application instances?","answer":"Shared application instances are appropriate when tenants have compatible runtime requirements and strong logical isolation is sufficient. Separate instances can be justified by regulatory isolation, customized runtime requirements, high-value tenant protection, or materially different scaling characteristics. Separate instances increase deployment, patching, monitoring, and configuration complexity.","principle":"Application-instance isolation should be proportional to tenant-specific security, compliance, customization, and reliability requirements.","requirements":["Define isolation boundary.","Define deployment ownership.","Define tenant-specific configuration.","Define patch and upgrade process.","Define resource quotas."],"constraints":["Separate instances increase operational surface area.","Shared instances require rigorous tenant context enforcement.","Customization can create divergent versions."],"alternatives":["Shared multi-tenant application","Tenant groups with isolated instances","Dedicated tenant deployments"],"tradeoff":{"shared":["lower operational overhead","higher logical-isolation dependence"],"separate":["stronger runtime isolation","higher lifecycle complexity"]},"decision_factors":["tenant isolation","customization","compliance","scale variance","operational capacity"],"failure_modes":["configuration leakage","cross-tenant resource contention","version drift","inconsistent security patches"],"security_implication":"Separate application instances can reduce blast radius but do not eliminate the need for identity, authorization, and secure configuration controls.","performance_implication":"Dedicated instances can improve resource predictability while potentially lowering aggregate utilization.","testing_guidance":["test tenant routing","test instance isolation","test upgrade consistency","simulate compromised tenant workload"],"verification_method":"Verify that the chosen deployment boundary corresponds to the required tenant trust and operational isolation boundary.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS SaaS Factory architecture guidance"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B31-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B31-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Multi-Tenancy Architecture","concept":"Shared Resource Pool vs Tenant Resource Quotas","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a multi-tenant architecture impose explicit per-tenant resource quotas?","answer":"Explicit quotas are useful when tenant workloads can compete for finite resources or when predictable service levels are required. A fully shared pool without quotas can maximize utilization but allows one tenant to consume disproportionate capacity. Quotas should be paired with observable usage, clear rejection or throttling behavior, and appropriate administrative controls.","principle":"Resource sharing requires explicit fairness and overload boundaries when tenant behavior can materially affect other tenants.","requirements":["Define resource dimensions.","Define quota scope.","Define enforcement behavior.","Define administrative override policy.","Define usage observability."],"constraints":["Rigid quotas can underutilize capacity.","Quota calculation can be difficult for bursty workloads.","Different resources require different controls."],"alternatives":["Unbounded shared pool","Fixed quotas","Adaptive quotas","Quota plus burst allowance"],"tradeoff":{"shared":["high utilization potential","noisy-neighbor risk"],"quota_controlled":["predictable resource allocation","possible underutilization or rejection"]},"decision_factors":["tenant workload variability","SLO requirements","resource scarcity","fairness","business tiering"],"failure_modes":["quota bypass","resource starvation","incorrect quota accounting","burst rejection"],"security_implication":"Quota controls can reduce resource-abuse risk but must not allow one tenant to manipulate accounting for another.","performance_implication":"Quotas can protect tail behavior under contention; exact limits should be established from measured capacity.","testing_guidance":["generate competing tenant workloads","test quota exhaustion","test burst allowance","verify accounting accuracy"],"verification_method":"Verify that tenant resource consumption cannot exceed declared limits except through explicitly authorized and auditable mechanisms.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Kubernetes Resource Management Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B21-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B31-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Multi-Tenancy Architecture","concept":"Tenant-Specific Customization vs Configuration-Driven Product Model","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should tenant differences be represented through configuration instead of custom code paths?","answer":"Configuration-driven customization is preferable when differences can be expressed through stable, validated parameters without changing core behavior. Tenant-specific code is justified only when behavior is genuinely different and cannot be represented safely through configuration. Excessive custom code creates version divergence, testing complexity, and upgrade friction.","principle":"Prefer configuration for bounded variation and code only for materially distinct behavior.","requirements":["Define supported customization dimensions.","Validate configuration.","Define compatibility guarantees.","Define upgrade behavior."],"constraints":["Configuration can become an implicit programming language.","Custom code increases maintenance and security surface.","Overly generic configuration can obscure business rules."],"alternatives":["Configuration-driven variation","Feature flags","Tenant-specific extensions","Separate application versions"],"tradeoff":{"configuration":["centralized evolution","validation and complexity burden"],"custom_code":["maximum flexibility","versioning and security complexity"]},"decision_factors":["variation complexity","number of tenants","upgrade frequency","security risk","support burden"],"failure_modes":["configuration explosion","tenant-specific fork","inconsistent behavior","unbounded customization"],"security_implication":"Tenant configuration must not grant capabilities beyond the tenant's authorization boundary or permit arbitrary code execution unless explicitly designed and isolated.","performance_implication":"Configuration evaluation can add runtime branching; excessive dynamic configuration can complicate optimization and caching.","testing_guidance":["test configuration combinations","test invalid tenant configuration","upgrade customized tenants","verify capability boundaries"],"verification_method":"Verify that each supported customization is represented by a bounded schema and cannot alter protected architectural invariants.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"The Twelve-Factor App"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS SaaS Factory architecture guidance"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B28-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B31-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B31","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Multi-Tenancy Architecture","concept":"Global Tenant Control Plane vs Tenant-Local Control Plane","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should tenant lifecycle and configuration management be centralized versus distributed into tenant-local control planes?","answer":"A global control plane simplifies fleet-wide policy, lifecycle, and inventory management, while tenant-local control planes can provide stronger autonomy, regional independence, or regulatory separation. A distributed control plane requires explicit reconciliation and authority semantics. The architecture should distinguish control-plane authority from tenant workload execution.","principle":"Centralize control where uniform governance is required and decentralize only when autonomy or locality provides a concrete requirement-driven benefit.","requirements":["Define control-plane authority.","Define tenant autonomy.","Define reconciliation protocol.","Define control-plane failure behavior."],"constraints":["Centralization can become a management dependency.","Distributed control planes create synchronization complexity.","Control-plane compromise can affect many tenants."],"alternatives":["Global control plane","Regional control planes","Tenant-local control planes","Hierarchical control plane"],"tradeoff":{"global":["consistent governance","central dependency and blast radius"],"distributed":["local autonomy","higher synchronization and governance complexity"]},"decision_factors":["regulatory boundaries","regional independence","tenant customization","fleet size","control-plane availability"],"failure_modes":["control-plane outage","configuration divergence","split authority","partial rollout"],"security_implication":"Control-plane privileges require strong authentication, authorization, auditing, and blast-radius controls because they can affect many tenants.","performance_implication":"Local control can reduce management latency but introduces additional state and reconciliation overhead.","testing_guidance":["disconnect control planes","simulate conflicting updates","test rollback","verify authority boundaries"],"verification_method":"Verify that every control-plane action has one authoritative owner and deterministic behavior during partial connectivity.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS SaaS Factory architecture guidance"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B24-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B32-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Geographic Architecture","concept":"Single-Region Deployment vs Multi-Region Deployment","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a system deploy across multiple geographic regions instead of operating from one primary region?","answer":"Multi-region deployment is justified when geographic failure tolerance, latency requirements, data residency, or regional availability materially affect system objectives. A single-region architecture is simpler and can still achieve strong availability within its region. Multi-region architecture introduces replication, routing, deployment, data-consistency, and operational complexity.","principle":"Geographic distribution should be driven by explicit regional availability, latency, residency, or disaster-recovery requirements.","requirements":["Define regional failure objective.","Define data residency requirements.","Define traffic-routing behavior.","Define cross-region data semantics.","Define recovery procedures."],"constraints":["Cross-region coordination introduces latency.","Data residency may restrict replication.","Operating multiple regions increases cost and operational complexity."],"alternatives":["Single region","Primary plus disaster-recovery region","Active-active multi-region","Regional independent deployments"],"tradeoff":{"single_region":["simpler operations","larger geographic failure boundary"],"multi_region":["geographic resilience and locality potential","replication and operational complexity"]},"decision_factors":["RTO/RPO","latency","regulation","traffic distribution","operational maturity"],"failure_modes":["regional split-brain","replication lag","routing failure","inconsistent regional versions"],"security_implication":"Cross-region replication expands the geographic security and compliance boundary and must preserve encryption, access controls, and data residency constraints.","performance_implication":"Regional locality can reduce network distance while cross-region synchronization can add latency and bandwidth consumption.","testing_guidance":["simulate region loss","test routing failover","test replication lag","validate residency constraints"],"verification_method":"Verify that regional failure behavior, data replication, routing, and recovery satisfy documented RTO/RPO and residency requirements.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google Cloud Architecture Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B07-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B32-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Geographic Architecture","concept":"Global Data Replication vs Regional Data Ownership","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should data be globally replicated versus owned within a specific geographic region?","answer":"Global replication can improve locality and availability for globally distributed consumers but expands consistency and compliance complexity. Regional ownership can simplify authority and residency but may require remote access or regional aggregation. The architecture should identify which data requires global visibility and which data must remain regionally authoritative.","principle":"Data geography should follow authority, regulatory, latency, and recovery requirements rather than compute topology alone.","requirements":["Classify data residency requirements.","Define authoritative region.","Define replication scope.","Define cross-region access policy."],"constraints":["Global replication may violate residency requirements.","Regional ownership can increase cross-region access latency.","Cross-region aggregation can create duplicate data."],"alternatives":["Global replication","Regional primary with read replicas","Region-local ownership","Selective field-level replication"],"tradeoff":{"global":["local access potential","higher compliance and consistency complexity"],"regional":["clear authority and residency","cross-region access complexity"]},"decision_factors":["residency","latency","availability","consistency","data sensitivity"],"failure_modes":["unauthorized replication","stale global copy","conflicting regional writes","incorrect residency classification"],"security_implication":"Data classification must precede replication decisions; sensitive data should not be copied across regions without explicit authorization and legal/compliance basis.","performance_implication":"Replication improves locality for some reads but consumes network bandwidth and can increase write latency depending on topology.","testing_guidance":["verify replication filters","test region outage","test residency enforcement","inject replication delay"],"verification_method":"Verify every replicated dataset has documented origin, permitted regions, freshness semantics, and access policy.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST Privacy Framework"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B23-R001","related_to:D41-B22-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B32-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Geographic Architecture","concept":"Global Load Balancing vs Regional Routing","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should traffic be routed globally based on broad health and proximity versus explicitly directed to a regional endpoint?","answer":"Global load balancing can distribute traffic according to health, geography, and capacity while providing a unified entry point. Explicit regional routing can provide deterministic locality, regulatory control, or simpler debugging. Global routing introduces dependency on routing policy and health detection, while regional routing can require clients or upstream systems to understand geographic placement.","principle":"Traffic routing should encode the actual availability, locality, and compliance requirements of the workload.","requirements":["Define routing authority.","Define health signals.","Define failover behavior.","Define geographic policy.","Define DNS or routing cache effects where applicable."],"constraints":["Global routing decisions can be delayed by cached routing state.","Health checks may not capture all application failures.","Regional routing can create uneven utilization."],"alternatives":["Global traffic manager","Geo-aware DNS","Client-directed region","Static regional endpoint"],"tradeoff":{"global":["automatic distribution/failover","routing complexity"],"regional":["deterministic locality","manual or client routing burden"]},"decision_factors":["latency","regional health","residency","capacity","failover objectives"],"failure_modes":["routing to unhealthy region","stale routing information","regional overload","routing-policy conflict"],"security_implication":"Routing must not circumvent regional access controls or data residency requirements.","performance_implication":"Routing locality can reduce network distance, but routing decisions and failover propagation introduce their own latency characteristics.","testing_guidance":["simulate regional health failure","test stale routing","test geographic policy","verify failover behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify that routing decisions respect health, geographic, authorization, and residency constraints under normal and failure conditions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Route 53 Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google Cloud Global Load Balancing Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B32-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B32-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Geographic Architecture","concept":"Active-Active Regions vs Active-Standby Regions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should multiple regions actively serve traffic instead of maintaining a standby region?","answer":"Active-active regions can use capacity in multiple regions and provide geographic locality, but require stronger cross-region consistency, traffic management, deployment coordination, and failure handling. Active-standby simplifies some state and operational concerns but leaves standby capacity less utilized and requires reliable failover procedures.","principle":"Active-active architecture is justified when concurrent regional capacity or locality provides enough value to offset the complexity of multi-writer or multi-region operation.","requirements":["Define traffic ownership.","Define data write semantics.","Define failover procedure.","Define standby activation or active balancing behavior."],"constraints":["Active-active can create multi-writer conflicts.","Active-standby requires tested failover.","Both models require regional health detection."],"alternatives":["Active-active","Active-standby","Primary plus warm standby","Independent regional cells"],"tradeoff":{"active_active":["capacity utilization and locality","higher consistency and routing complexity"],"active_standby":["simpler primary authority","standby utilization and failover complexity"]},"decision_factors":["RTO","RPO","regional traffic","write topology","operational maturity","cost"],"failure_modes":["split-brain","double writers","failed failover","stale standby","uneven regional load"],"security_implication":"Failover must preserve identity, authorization, secrets, and audit controls across regions.","performance_implication":"Active-active can reduce geographic latency while requiring additional synchronization; no performance advantage should be assumed without measurement.","testing_guidance":["exercise regional failure","test concurrent writes","test failback","validate audit continuity"],"verification_method":"Verify regional ownership and write authority remain unambiguous during failure and recovery transitions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B32-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B32-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B32","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Geographic Architecture","concept":"Global Control Plane vs Regional Control Planes","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should operational control be centralized globally versus partitioned by geographic region?","answer":"A global control plane simplifies unified governance and fleet-wide policy, while regional control planes can preserve regional autonomy and continue operating during global control-plane outages. Regional control planes increase coordination and configuration-divergence complexity. The architecture should define which controls require global consistency and which can safely be regional.","principle":"Control-plane topology should follow the required consistency and failure domain of operational decisions.","requirements":["Classify global versus regional policies.","Define authority hierarchy.","Define synchronization.","Define operation during global control-plane failure."],"constraints":["Global control can become a broad dependency.","Regional control can produce policy divergence.","Cross-region governance may be complex."],"alternatives":["Global control plane","Regional control planes","Hierarchical global-regional control"],"tradeoff":{"global":["central governance","larger blast radius and dependency"],"regional":["fault isolation and autonomy","synchronization complexity"]},"decision_factors":["policy consistency","regional independence","failure isolation","regulation","operational scale"],"failure_modes":["policy divergence","split authority","global control outage","partial configuration rollout"],"security_implication":"Security policies that require global consistency need explicit versioning and propagation guarantees; regional autonomy must not bypass mandatory controls.","performance_implication":"Regional control can reduce control-plane latency but increases synchronization and management overhead.","testing_guidance":["disconnect global control","introduce conflicting regional policy","test reconciliation","verify mandatory policy inheritance"],"verification_method":"Verify that mandatory controls have a single authoritative source and that regional policies cannot violate global invariants.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B31-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B33-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Lifecycle Architecture","concept":"Hot Data vs Cold Data Storage","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should data move from hot storage to colder archival storage?","answer":"Hot storage is appropriate for frequently accessed data requiring low-latency access, while cold storage is appropriate for data with low access frequency but ongoing retention requirements. Tiering should account for retrieval latency, retention, legal hold, restoration procedures, metadata availability, and total lifecycle cost.","principle":"Storage tier should follow access frequency and retention requirements while preserving required retrieval and compliance semantics.","requirements":["Define retention policy.","Define access classes.","Define retrieval objective.","Define lifecycle transition rules.","Define deletion and legal-hold behavior."],"constraints":["Cold storage may have longer retrieval times.","Tier transitions can complicate application behavior.","Archived data still requires security and integrity controls."],"alternatives":["Single hot tier","Hot/cold tiering","Archive storage","Delete after retention"],"tradeoff":{"hot":["fast access","higher ongoing resource cost"],"cold":["lower access-oriented resource cost","retrieval delay and lifecycle complexity"]},"decision_factors":["access frequency","retention","retrieval objective","data volume","compliance"],"failure_modes":["premature archival","unretrievable archive","retention violation","archive corruption"],"security_implication":"Archived data remains subject to access control, encryption, retention, and secure deletion requirements.","performance_implication":"Cold tiers trade access latency for storage economics; exact retrieval performance is provider and implementation dependent.","testing_guidance":["restore archived records","test lifecycle transitions","test legal holds","verify deletion behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify that lifecycle transitions preserve retention, integrity, authorization, and required retrieval guarantees.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Storage Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-88 Guidelines for Media Sanitization"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B22-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B33-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Lifecycle Architecture","concept":"Retention by Time vs Retention by Business State","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should data retention be determined by elapsed time versus business lifecycle state?","answer":"Time-based retention is appropriate when regulatory, operational, or storage requirements map directly to elapsed time. State-based retention is appropriate when records must remain available until a business condition, case closure, legal event, or lifecycle milestone occurs. Complex systems may combine both with legal holds or explicit overrides.","principle":"Retention rules should represent the actual obligation that determines when data may be deleted.","requirements":["Define retention authority.","Define deletion eligibility.","Define legal hold behavior.","Define lifecycle state transitions.","Audit retention decisions."],"constraints":["State-based retention can be harder to automate.","Time-based deletion can conflict with unresolved business obligations.","Legal holds can override ordinary retention."],"alternatives":["Fixed duration","Business-state retention","Hybrid state plus maximum duration"],"tradeoff":{"time_based":["simple automation","may not reflect business lifecycle"],"state_based":["business alignment","more complex state tracking"]},"decision_factors":["regulation","business lifecycle","legal requirements","data volume","automation maturity"],"failure_modes":["premature deletion","indefinite retention","state transition bug","legal hold bypass"],"security_implication":"Retention and deletion are security controls; excessive retention increases exposure while premature deletion can violate obligations.","performance_implication":"Retention policy affects storage volume and lifecycle processing workload rather than directly determining application latency.","testing_guidance":["simulate state transitions","apply legal holds","test expiry boundaries","verify deletion audit trail"],"verification_method":"Verify that deletion occurs only when all applicable retention conditions are satisfied and that the decision is auditable.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST Privacy Framework"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-88 Guidelines for Media Sanitization"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B33-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B33-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Lifecycle Architecture","concept":"Immutable Audit Log vs Mutable Operational Record","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should historical information be stored as immutable events or audit records instead of updating a current operational record?","answer":"Mutable operational records are appropriate for current state, while immutable audit records are useful when historical accountability, reconstruction, or compliance requires preservation of what happened. Maintaining both introduces storage and consistency responsibilities. The immutable record should have explicit provenance and integrity semantics rather than simply copying operational data.","principle":"Separate current state from historical evidence when auditability or reconstruction requires facts to remain independently preserved.","requirements":["Define authoritative history.","Define event/audit schema.","Define integrity protection.","Define retention and access policy."],"constraints":["Immutable history increases storage volume.","Reconstructing current state from history requires deterministic semantics.","Dual representations can diverge if poorly designed."],"alternatives":["Mutable state only","Immutable audit plus mutable state","Event-sourced state","Append-only audit log"],"tradeoff":{"mutable_only":["simple current-state access","limited historical reconstruction"],"immutable_plus_state":["stronger history","additional storage and lifecycle complexity"]},"decision_factors":["auditability","reconstruction","compliance","storage cost","event semantics"],"failure_modes":["missing audit event","tampered history","state/history divergence","non-deterministic reconstruction"],"security_implication":"Audit records should have controlled write access, integrity protection, access auditing, and appropriate retention.","performance_implication":"Append-only logging can shift workload toward write volume and storage management; query performance may require indexes or derived views.","testing_guidance":["tamper attempts","reconstruct state from history","simulate missing events","verify audit completeness"],"verification_method":"Verify that protected state transitions produce complete, ordered or appropriately sequenced audit evidence according to the declared model.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-92 Guide to Computer Security Log Management"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B13-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B33-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Lifecycle Architecture","concept":"Delete-on-Request vs Tombstone-Based Deletion","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should deletion physically remove data versus recording a tombstone or deletion marker?","answer":"Physical deletion is appropriate when the system can safely remove all relevant copies and no downstream reconciliation requires knowledge of the deleted record. Tombstones are useful in replicated or eventually consistent systems where consumers must learn that a record was deleted. Tombstones require lifecycle management so they do not accumulate indefinitely.","principle":"Deletion representation should preserve the information necessary for correct synchronization while satisfying actual deletion obligations.","requirements":["Define deletion semantics.","Identify replicated consumers.","Define tombstone lifetime.","Define physical deletion scope."],"constraints":["Physical deletion can race with replication.","Tombstones consume storage and require compaction.","Deletion obligations may apply to derived copies as well."],"alternatives":["Physical deletion","Tombstone","Soft deletion","Hybrid tombstone followed by physical purge"],"tradeoff":{"physical":["stronger immediate removal","harder distributed synchronization"],"tombstone":["supports replication correctness","requires lifecycle management"]},"decision_factors":["replication","consistency model","legal deletion requirements","recovery model","storage volume"],"failure_modes":["resurrected deleted record","tombstone loss","indefinite tombstone growth","incomplete deletion"],"security_implication":"Soft deletion must not be treated as complete removal when sensitive data must actually be deleted; access controls must prevent deleted records from appearing operationally.","performance_implication":"Tombstones can increase storage and query/compaction overhead if not managed.","testing_guidance":["delete before replication","delay consumers","restore from backup","verify derived-data deletion"],"verification_method":"Verify that deletion propagates correctly to every authoritative and derived representation and that tombstone lifecycle matches retention requirements.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Apache Cassandra Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B23-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B33-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B33","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Lifecycle Architecture","concept":"Full Dataset Rebuild vs Incremental Maintenance","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should derived data be rebuilt from authoritative sources instead of maintained incrementally?","answer":"Incremental maintenance is appropriate when updates are frequent and deterministic propagation is reliable. Full rebuilds are valuable when derived state can be reproduced from authoritative data and incremental correctness becomes difficult to maintain. Mature architectures often support both so that incremental processing provides normal operation while rebuild capability provides recovery from corruption or logic changes.","principle":"A reproducible rebuild path reduces long-term dependence on perfect incremental synchronization.","requirements":["Identify authoritative source.","Define deterministic derivation.","Track derivation version.","Define rebuild procedure.","Validate rebuilt output."],"constraints":["Full rebuilds can be resource intensive.","Incremental updates can accumulate subtle errors.","Some external state cannot be deterministically reconstructed."],"alternatives":["Incremental only","Full rebuild only","Incremental plus rebuild"],"tradeoff":{"incremental":["efficient normal updates","synchronization complexity"],"rebuild":["strong recovery capability","high compute and time cost"]},"decision_factors":["dataset size","update rate","rebuild cost","derivation determinism","recovery requirements"],"failure_modes":["corrupted derived state","missed update","non-reproducible rebuild","version mismatch"],"security_implication":"Rebuild processes must preserve access-control classifications and must not expose data beyond the authority of the derived dataset.","performance_implication":"Rebuild cost depends on dataset size and computation; it should be measured for recovery planning rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":["perform rebuild from clean source","compare incremental and rebuilt output","interrupt rebuild","test historical derivation versions"],"verification_method":"Verify that derived state can be reconstructed from authoritative inputs with a declared derivation version and deterministic validation criteria.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B24-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B34-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Architecture","concept":"Backup vs Replication","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should replication not be treated as a replacement for backups?","answer":"Replication can improve availability by maintaining additional copies of current data, but logical corruption, accidental deletion, malicious modification, or application-level errors can propagate to replicas. Backups provide historical recovery points when designed and retained appropriately. A resilient architecture often uses replication for availability and independent backups for recovery from data corruption or historical-state loss.","principle":"Availability copies and recovery copies solve different failure classes and should not be conflated.","requirements":["Define recovery objectives.","Define independent backup mechanism.","Define retention periods.","Test restoration.","Protect backups from the same failure domain where appropriate."],"constraints":["Backups consume storage and operational resources.","Replication may not preserve historical state.","Backup systems themselves can fail or become compromised."],"alternatives":["Replication only","Backup only","Replication plus independent backups"],"tradeoff":{"replication":["rapid availability recovery","current-state corruption can propagate"],"backup":["historical recovery","restore time and storage requirements"]},"decision_factors":["failure modes","RPO","RTO","retention","corruption recovery"],"failure_modes":["corrupted replicas","backup failure","unrestorable backup","incorrect retention"],"security_implication":"Backups contain sensitive data and require encryption, access controls, isolation, integrity protection, and secure lifecycle management.","performance_implication":"Backup creation can consume I/O, CPU, and network resources; impact depends on mechanism and workload.","testing_guidance":["restore backups","simulate corrupted primary","test deletion recovery","verify backup integrity"],"verification_method":"Verify successful restoration from independently protected recovery points at intervals appropriate to the declared recovery objective.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B32-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B34-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Architecture","concept":"Snapshot-Based Backup vs Logical Backup","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture use storage snapshots versus logical application-level backups?","answer":"Snapshots can provide efficient point-in-time copies at the storage layer and can be useful for rapid infrastructure recovery. Logical backups represent application or database semantics and can provide selective restoration, portability, or recovery of specific objects. Neither is universally sufficient; recovery requirements determine which backup representation is needed.","principle":"Backup representation should match the required recovery granularity and the layer at which correctness must be restored.","requirements":["Define restore granularity.","Define consistency point.","Define portability requirement.","Define restoration procedure."],"constraints":["Snapshots can depend on storage/platform technology.","Logical backups may take longer for large datasets.","Crash-consistent copies may not equal application-consistent backups."],"alternatives":["Storage snapshot","Logical export","Application-consistent backup","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":{"snapshot":["fast infrastructure recovery potential","platform coupling and granularity limits"],"logical":["semantic portability and selective restore","potentially higher processing cost"]},"decision_factors":["restore granularity","dataset size","platform coupling","RTO","application consistency"],"failure_modes":["inconsistent snapshot","unreadable export","missing dependent objects","restore incompatibility"],"security_implication":"Backup access must be restricted independently from ordinary application access because backups can contain broad datasets.","performance_implication":"Snapshot and logical backup resource costs vary substantially by storage engine and workload; validate actual backup windows.","testing_guidance":["restore complete snapshot","restore individual records from logical backup","test cross-version restore","verify consistency"],"verification_method":"Verify that the selected backup format can restore each required recovery scenario, not merely create a backup artifact.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"PostgreSQL Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Backup Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B34-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B34-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Architecture","concept":"Point-in-Time Recovery vs Periodic Full Restore","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture provide point-in-time recovery rather than relying only on periodic full backups?","answer":"Point-in-time recovery is useful when the acceptable data-loss window is smaller than the interval between full backups or when recovery from an exact pre-failure state is required. Periodic full backups can be simpler but may produce a larger recovery point gap. Point-in-time recovery requires reliable transaction or change capture and tested replay semantics.","principle":"Recovery granularity should match the declared RPO and the failure scenarios the architecture must recover from.","requirements":["Define RPO.","Capture sufficient change history.","Define recovery target time.","Test replay and restoration."],"constraints":["Change logs consume storage.","Replay can increase recovery complexity and time.","Corrupted change history can compromise recovery."],"alternatives":["Periodic full backup","Full plus incremental backup","Point-in-time recovery"],"tradeoff":{"PITR":["finer recovery point","more operational and storage complexity"],"periodic_full":["simpler recovery model","larger potential data-loss window"]},"decision_factors":["RPO","transaction volume","storage cost","restore complexity","recovery time"],"failure_modes":["missing WAL/change segment","replay failure","incorrect recovery target","incomplete backup chain"],"security_implication":"Recovery logs can contain sensitive information and require the same protection as primary data.","performance_implication":"Continuous change capture adds workload-dependent overhead; exact impact must be measured on the actual datastore.","testing_guidance":["restore to arbitrary timestamps","simulate missing change segment","test backup-chain integrity","measure restore duration"],"verification_method":"Verify that the system can restore to representative target times within the declared RPO and recovery procedure.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"PostgreSQL Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B34-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B34-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Architecture","concept":"Hot Standby vs Restore-on-Demand Recovery","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should recovery use a continuously maintained standby instead of restoring infrastructure after failure?","answer":"A hot or warm standby can reduce recovery time when rapid failover is required, while restore-on-demand can reduce ongoing infrastructure cost when recovery objectives allow longer restoration. Standbys do not replace independent backups because replicated corruption can propagate. The architecture should define failover authority, synchronization state, and recovery verification.","principle":"Maintain standby capacity when rapid recovery is a requirement that cannot be satisfied economically by restoration alone.","requirements":["Define RTO.","Define standby synchronization state.","Define failover authority.","Define backup independence.","Define failback procedure."],"constraints":["Standbys increase ongoing cost.","Standby state may lag primary.","Automatic failover can produce split-brain or incorrect authority if poorly designed."],"alternatives":["No standby","Warm standby","Hot standby","Active-active"],"tradeoff":{"standby":["faster recovery potential","ongoing resource and synchronization cost"],"restore_on_demand":["lower steady-state cost","longer recovery"]},"decision_factors":["RTO","cost","state synchronization","failure frequency","operational maturity"],"failure_modes":["stale standby","split-brain","failed promotion","replicated corruption"],"security_implication":"Standby environments require equivalent security controls and credentials; failover must preserve authorization and audit continuity.","performance_implication":"Continuous replication consumes resources and network bandwidth; exact impact depends on workload.","testing_guidance":["promote standby","simulate stale replica","test failback","verify independent backups"],"verification_method":"Verify promotion, service restoration, data consistency, and audit continuity through controlled recovery exercises.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B32-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B34-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B34","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Architecture","concept":"Centralized Backup Platform vs Service-Owned Backup","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should backup management be centralized versus owned by individual service teams?","answer":"Centralized backup platforms can standardize policy, retention, encryption, monitoring, and recovery governance. Service-owned backups can better capture application-specific consistency and restoration requirements. A hybrid model often centralizes infrastructure and governance while keeping application teams accountable for semantic recovery correctness.","principle":"Backup ownership should align operational governance with the team that understands application recovery semantics.","requirements":["Define backup ownership.","Define recovery accountability.","Define policy enforcement.","Define restoration testing.","Define audit visibility."],"constraints":["Centralization can miss application semantics.","Service ownership can produce inconsistent controls.","Hybrid models require clear responsibility boundaries."],"alternatives":["Centralized backup","Service-owned backup","Centralized infrastructure plus service-defined recovery policy"],"tradeoff":{"centralized":["consistent governance","possible semantic mismatch"],"service_owned":["application-specific recovery","policy inconsistency risk"]},"decision_factors":["application complexity","regulation","team maturity","recovery semantics","fleet size"],"failure_modes":["backup without usable restore","missing service coverage","policy drift","unclear recovery ownership"],"security_implication":"Central backup platforms require privileged access controls and strong separation between backup administration and ordinary application operations.","performance_implication":"Centralization can consolidate backup workloads and create shared capacity bottlenecks; service-level backup windows should be measured.","testing_guidance":["audit backup coverage","run service-specific restore","test central platform outage","verify ownership escalation"],"verification_method":"Verify that every critical service has an accountable recovery owner and a demonstrated restoration path.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B34-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B35-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"Centralized Identity Provider vs Service-Local Identity Management","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should identity management be centralized rather than implemented independently by services?","answer":"Centralized identity management is generally useful when many services share users, lifecycle, authentication policy, and federation requirements. Service-local identity can be appropriate for isolated machine identities or domains with genuinely independent trust models. Duplicating user authentication independently across services increases policy inconsistency and credential-management risk.","principle":"Centralize identity authority when identities and authentication policy are shared; decentralize only when trust domains are genuinely independent.","requirements":["Define identity authority.","Define authentication protocol.","Define credential lifecycle.","Define service authorization responsibilities."],"constraints":["Centralized identity creates a critical dependency.","Service-local identity can create duplicated credentials and inconsistent controls.","Authentication and authorization remain distinct responsibilities."],"alternatives":["Centralized identity provider","Federated identity","Service-local identity","Hybrid machine/user identity architecture"],"tradeoff":{"centralized":["consistent identity lifecycle","dependency and blast-radius concerns"],"local":["independence","credential and policy duplication"]},"decision_factors":["trust domains","user population","federation","availability","credential lifecycle"],"failure_modes":["identity-provider outage","credential duplication","inconsistent MFA policy","token misuse"],"security_implication":"Identity architecture must enforce secure authentication, credential lifecycle, token validation, and least privilege; centralized identity does not automatically provide correct authorization.","performance_implication":"Central authentication can add network dependencies; validated tokens and appropriate local verification can reduce repeated dependency calls where security semantics permit.","testing_guidance":["simulate identity-provider outage","test token expiration","test revoked identity","verify service authorization independently"],"verification_method":"Verify that every protected operation has a trusted identity source and an explicit authorization decision.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-63 Digital Identity Guidelines"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B24-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B35-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"Central Authorization Service vs Distributed Authorization Enforcement","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should authorization decisions be centralized versus evaluated close to each protected resource?","answer":"Central authorization can provide consistent policy management and auditability, while local enforcement can reduce network dependency and ensure decisions are made close to resources. A hybrid model can centralize policy authority and distribute policy evaluation. The architecture must explicitly define policy freshness, failure behavior, and enforcement points.","principle":"Policy management and policy enforcement can be separated when central consistency and local availability are both required.","requirements":["Define policy authority.","Define enforcement points.","Define policy freshness.","Define failure behavior.","Define authorization audit trail."],"constraints":["Central services can become availability dependencies.","Distributed enforcement can produce inconsistent versions.","Policy caching can create stale authorization decisions."],"alternatives":["Central decision point","Local policy evaluation","Central policy with distributed evaluation"],"tradeoff":{"central":["policy consistency","network dependency"],"distributed":["local availability","policy distribution complexity"]},"decision_factors":["security criticality","latency","connectivity","policy update frequency","auditability"],"failure_modes":["stale policy","fail-open","policy divergence","unavailable authorization service"],"security_implication":"Security-critical authorization should fail according to an explicit policy when authorization state cannot be trusted.","performance_implication":"Local policy evaluation can reduce network latency while increasing policy distribution overhead.","testing_guidance":["disconnect authorization service","inject stale policy","test policy version transitions","verify fail-closed requirements"],"verification_method":"Verify authorization decisions against trusted policy versions and validate behavior during policy-service unavailability.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B24-R002","related_to:D41-B35-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B35-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"End-to-End Encryption vs Termination at Trusted Proxy","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should encrypted traffic remain protected end-to-end versus terminate at an intermediate trusted proxy?","answer":"End-to-end encryption can reduce the number of components that can inspect plaintext, while trusted termination enables centralized routing, inspection, authentication integration, and protocol handling. Termination is appropriate only when the intermediary is an explicitly trusted security boundary and its access to plaintext is acceptable. Sensitive workloads may require encryption beyond the proxy boundary.","principle":"TLS termination changes the trust boundary; it must be treated as an architectural security decision rather than a transport implementation detail.","requirements":["Define trust boundaries.","Define plaintext exposure points.","Define certificate ownership.","Define downstream encryption requirements."],"constraints":["Termination exposes plaintext to the proxy.","End-to-end encryption can complicate inspection and routing.","Certificate and key management remain critical in both models."],"alternatives":["End-to-end TLS","TLS termination at edge","TLS re-encryption after proxy","Mutual TLS between services"],"tradeoff":{"termination":["centralized traffic handling","expanded plaintext trust boundary"],"end_to_end":["stronger confidentiality boundary","more complex inspection/routing"]},"decision_factors":["data sensitivity","proxy trust","inspection needs","regulation","service identity"],"failure_modes":["plaintext exposure","certificate misconfiguration","weak internal transport","incorrect trust assumptions"],"security_implication":"Every TLS termination point becomes a location where plaintext and cryptographic keys require protection.","performance_implication":"TLS processing and connection management depend on implementation; architectural claims should be measured where performance is critical.","testing_guidance":["inspect certificate chains","test internal encryption","verify hostname/service identity","attempt unauthorized plaintext interception"],"verification_method":"Verify encryption coverage against declared trust boundaries and confirm that every plaintext exposure point is authorized and protected.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-52 Guidelines for TLS"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B11-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B35-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"Application-Level Encryption vs Storage-Level Encryption","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should sensitive fields be encrypted by the application rather than relying only on storage-layer encryption?","answer":"Storage-level encryption protects data at rest within storage infrastructure and is useful as a broad baseline. Application-level or field-level encryption can reduce exposure to database administrators, backups, or compromised storage access, but introduces key-management, queryability, rotation, and application complexity. The required protection boundary determines whether storage encryption is sufficient.","principle":"Encryption scope should correspond to the threat model and the trust boundary from which data must remain confidential.","requirements":["Define protected assets.","Define threat model.","Define key ownership.","Define rotation and recovery.","Define query requirements."],"constraints":["Field encryption can prevent ordinary indexing or searching.","Key management becomes application responsibility.","Encryption does not replace authorization."],"alternatives":["Storage-level encryption","Application/field-level encryption","Envelope encryption","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":{"storage":["broad protection","trusted storage layer can access plaintext"],"application":["narrower plaintext exposure","greater key and application complexity"]},"decision_factors":["threat model","data sensitivity","queryability","key ownership","compliance"],"failure_modes":["key loss","unencrypted logs","partial field coverage","incorrect key rotation"],"security_implication":"Application encryption is only effective if keys are separated from protected data and cryptographic lifecycle is correctly managed.","performance_implication":"Application encryption can increase CPU and affect indexing/query behavior; exact impact must be measured.","testing_guidance":["verify encrypted fields","test key rotation","test backup restoration","inspect logs and error paths for plaintext"],"verification_method":"Verify that protected data remains confidential across primary storage, replicas, backups, logs, and application error paths according to the threat model.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-57 Key Management"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B33-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B35-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B35","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Security Architecture","concept":"Network Segmentation vs Application-Level Authorization","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should network segmentation not be treated as a replacement for application-level authorization?","answer":"Network segmentation limits which systems can communicate, while application authorization determines which identities and actions are permitted after communication is possible. Segmentation can reduce blast radius and attack paths, but it does not express fine-grained business permissions. Robust architectures use both where their respective threat and trust models require them.","principle":"Network reachability and application authorization solve different security problems and should not be conflated.","requirements":["Define network trust boundaries.","Define identity-based authorization.","Define resource permissions.","Define administrative access."],"constraints":["Segmentation can be bypassed through compromised allowed paths.","Fine-grained authorization requires application context.","Over-segmentation can increase operational complexity."],"alternatives":["Network-only control","Application-only authorization","Defense-in-depth combination"],"tradeoff":{"segmentation":["blast-radius reduction","coarse-grained access"],"application_authorization":["fine-grained control","application implementation burden"]},"decision_factors":["threat model","identity context","network topology","resource sensitivity","administrative complexity"],"failure_modes":["trusted-network assumption","overbroad service permissions","lateral movement","authorization bypass"],"security_implication":"Protected operations must enforce authorization independently of network location or source subnet.","performance_implication":"Network controls can add routing or inspection overhead, while application authorization adds request processing; exact impact depends on implementation.","testing_guidance":["access service from permitted network with unauthorized identity","test lateral movement paths","bypass expected network boundary where possible"],"verification_method":"Verify that unauthorized identities cannot perform protected actions even when network connectivity is permitted.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B24-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B36-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Integration Architecture","concept":"Point-to-Point Integration vs Integration Hub","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should systems integrate directly with one another versus through a shared integration hub?","answer":"Point-to-point integration can be simple for a small number of stable relationships but grows in coupling as systems and interfaces multiply. An integration hub can centralize routing, transformation, policy, and observability but may become a shared dependency and bottleneck. The choice should consider integration count, transformation requirements, ownership, and failure isolation.","principle":"Use an integration hub when shared mediation provides concrete value that outweighs centralized dependency and complexity.","requirements":["Map integration relationships.","Define ownership.","Define transformation responsibility.","Define failure isolation.","Define observability."],"constraints":["Point-to-point coupling grows with integration count.","Hubs can become central failure or governance points.","Transformation logic can become difficult to own."],"alternatives":["Point-to-point","Integration hub","Event backbone","Domain-specific integration gateways"],"tradeoff":{"point_to_point":["simple local integration","connection proliferation"],"hub":["central mediation and governance","shared dependency and complexity"]},"decision_factors":["number of systems","transformation needs","protocol diversity","ownership","failure isolation"],"failure_modes":["integration spaghetti","hub outage","central transformation bottleneck","unclear ownership"],"security_implication":"An integration hub can become a high-value trust boundary and requires strict authentication, authorization, validation, and secret management.","performance_implication":"A hub adds a mediation hop but can reduce duplicated transformations; performance must be evaluated against real message volumes.","testing_guidance":["test hub failure","test malformed messages","test routing isolation","measure mediation overhead"],"verification_method":"Verify that each integration path has explicit ownership, contract, failure behavior, and security enforcement.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Enterprise Integration Patterns"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B03-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B36-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Integration Architecture","concept":"Canonical Data Model vs Native Domain Models with Translation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an integration architecture use a canonical shared data model instead of translating between domain-specific models?","answer":"A canonical model can simplify integrations when many systems share stable semantics, but it creates a shared semantic contract that can become difficult to evolve. Domain-specific models with explicit translation preserve local autonomy and allow independent evolution but require mapping logic. The architecture should prefer the model that minimizes semantic coupling while meeting integration requirements.","principle":"Shared integration schemas should be introduced only when their common semantics are genuinely stable and valuable.","requirements":["Define semantic ownership.","Define mapping responsibility.","Define schema evolution.","Define compatibility rules."],"constraints":["Canonical models can become overly generic.","Translation logic can be duplicated.","Semantic mismatches cannot always be solved by field mapping."],"alternatives":["Canonical model","Point-to-point translation","Domain-owned contracts","Hybrid canonical subset plus domain extensions"],"tradeoff":{"canonical":["shared vocabulary","high semantic coupling"],"translation":["domain autonomy","mapping complexity"]},"decision_factors":["number of integrations","semantic stability","domain autonomy","schema evolution","transformation complexity"],"failure_modes":["canonical model becomes lowest common denominator","semantic ambiguity","translation drift","shared schema blocking evolution"],"security_implication":"Canonical models can unintentionally expose fields across domains; data minimization and authorization must remain explicit.","performance_implication":"Translation adds processing and serialization work; canonicalization may reduce repeated mappings in some architectures.","testing_guidance":["test semantic mappings","evolve one domain independently","verify unauthorized fields are excluded","test backward compatibility"],"verification_method":"Verify that every translated field has explicit semantic ownership and that incompatible meanings are not silently merged.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Enterprise Integration Patterns"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Sam Newman, Building Microservices"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B25-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B36-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Integration Architecture","concept":"Anti-Corruption Layer vs Direct Domain Integration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a domain isolate itself from an external system through an anti-corruption layer?","answer":"An anti-corruption layer is useful when an external model, protocol, or semantic vocabulary would otherwise contaminate a domain's internal model. Direct integration is simpler when models are genuinely compatible and the external dependency is treated as part of the same conceptual boundary. Translation layers add code and maintenance cost but can preserve domain autonomy.","principle":"Protect a domain from incompatible external semantics when the long-term cost of coupling exceeds the cost of translation.","requirements":["Define internal domain model.","Define external model.","Define translation ownership.","Define synchronization semantics."],"constraints":["Translation layers require maintenance.","Direct integration can spread external assumptions through the domain.","Mappings can become incomplete as external contracts evolve."],"alternatives":["Direct integration","Anti-corruption layer","Adapter plus domain translation"],"tradeoff":{"ACL":["domain isolation","translation complexity"],"direct":["simplicity","external semantic coupling"]},"decision_factors":["semantic mismatch","dependency longevity","domain autonomy","external change rate","mapping complexity"],"failure_modes":["semantic leakage","incomplete mapping","external contract change","translation drift"],"security_implication":"The boundary should validate and constrain external data before it enters trusted domain logic.","performance_implication":"Translation introduces processing overhead but may reduce downstream complexity; critical paths should be measured.","testing_guidance":["inject malformed external data","change external schema","test mapping completeness","verify domain invariants"],"verification_method":"Verify that external representations cannot bypass internal domain invariants or introduce unsupported semantics.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Eric Evans, Domain-Driven Design"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Enterprise Integration Patterns"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B27-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B36-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Integration Architecture","concept":"Polling Integration vs Push Notifications","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an integration poll a source instead of receiving pushed notifications?","answer":"Polling is appropriate when the source cannot push reliably, when consumer control over request frequency is important, or when eventual freshness is acceptable. Push notifications can reduce unnecessary requests and improve freshness but require endpoint availability, delivery semantics, authentication, retry handling, and subscription lifecycle management.","principle":"Choose polling or push according to source capabilities, freshness requirements, delivery reliability, and operational control.","requirements":["Define freshness objective.","Define delivery semantics.","Define retry behavior.","Define authentication.","Define subscription lifecycle."],"constraints":["Polling can generate unnecessary load and delayed detection.","Push requires receiver availability and secure endpoint handling.","Both models require duplicate handling where delivery can repeat."],"alternatives":["Polling","Webhook/push","Event broker","Hybrid polling plus push"],"tradeoff":{"polling":["consumer control","request overhead and freshness delay"],"push":["lower unnecessary traffic","endpoint and delivery complexity"]},"decision_factors":["source capability","freshness","traffic volume","receiver availability","delivery guarantees"],"failure_modes":["missed notification","duplicate delivery","polling overload","stale subscription"],"security_implication":"Push endpoints require authentication, authorization, signature validation where applicable, replay protection, and input validation.","performance_implication":"Polling cost depends on interval and response size; push reduces unnecessary polling but can create burst traffic.","testing_guidance":["drop notifications","duplicate notifications","delay polling","rotate credentials","test subscription expiry"],"verification_method":"Verify that the integration remains correct when notifications are delayed, duplicated, or unavailable.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"RFC 9110 HTTP Semantics"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Enterprise Integration Patterns"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B21-R002","related_to:D41-B16-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B36-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B36","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Integration Architecture","concept":"Synchronous API Composition vs Precomputed Integration View","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an application compose data synchronously from multiple systems versus maintaining a precomputed integration view?","answer":"Synchronous composition is suitable when current data is required and dependency latency is acceptable. A precomputed integration view is suitable when repeated cross-system composition is expensive, availability of dependencies is variable, or slightly stale data is acceptable. The architecture must define freshness and rebuild semantics for the precomputed view.","principle":"Choose runtime composition when freshness dominates and materialization when dependency reduction or read efficiency dominates.","requirements":["Define freshness requirement.","Define dependency failure behavior.","Define view refresh mechanism.","Define rebuild capability."],"constraints":["Synchronous composition creates dependency chains.","Precomputed views can become stale.","Cross-system schemas can evolve independently."],"alternatives":["Synchronous composition","Materialized integration view","Cached composition","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":{"composition":["fresh data","latency and dependency coupling"],"materialized":["lower dependency cost","staleness and synchronization complexity"]},"decision_factors":["freshness","dependency count","read frequency","latency","availability","rebuild cost"],"failure_modes":["partial response","stale view","failed refresh","dependency cascade"],"security_implication":"Materialized views can aggregate sensitive data and may broaden access unless permissions are explicitly designed.","performance_implication":"Materialization can reduce repeated cross-system calls while increasing refresh workload and storage.","testing_guidance":["disable dependencies","delay refresh","compare view with live composition","test rebuild"],"verification_method":"Verify that the selected model meets freshness and availability requirements under dependency failure and delayed updates.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Enterprise Integration Patterns"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B24-R004","related_to:D41-B27-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B37-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Migration Architecture","concept":"Big-Bang Migration vs Incremental Migration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a system migrate all consumers and data in one coordinated change versus incrementally?","answer":"Big-bang migration can provide a clean target state when the system is small, tightly controlled, and downtime or coordinated change is acceptable. Incremental migration reduces change scope and enables progressive validation but requires coexistence, compatibility, and reconciliation mechanisms. Migration architecture should be chosen from dependency control, data volume, risk, and rollback feasibility.","principle":"Migration strategy should minimize the highest-risk failure mode while preserving a credible rollback or recovery path.","requirements":["Inventory dependencies.","Define migration stages.","Define compatibility period.","Define rollback/recovery.","Define data reconciliation."],"constraints":["Big-bang migration concentrates risk.","Incremental migration creates temporary architectural complexity.","Some schemas or protocols are difficult to operate in mixed versions."],"alternatives":["Big-bang migration","Incremental migration","Strangler migration","Dual-write migration"],"tradeoff":{"big_bang":["clean target transition","high coordinated failure risk"],"incremental":["smaller change steps","temporary dual-system complexity"]},"decision_factors":["system size","consumer control","data volume","downtime tolerance","rollback complexity"],"failure_modes":["partial migration","data divergence","unknown consumer","rollback failure"],"security_implication":"Migration paths must preserve authorization, encryption, secrets, and auditability across old and new components.","performance_implication":"Dual operation can temporarily increase resource consumption; migration load must be measured and bounded.","testing_guidance":["test mixed-version operation","reconcile source and target","exercise rollback","identify hidden consumers"],"verification_method":"Verify migration completion through objective source/target reconciliation rather than deployment status alone.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Strangler Fig Application"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Sam Newman, Building Microservices"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B30-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B37-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Migration Architecture","concept":"Dual Write vs Change Data Capture During Migration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should migration synchronize old and new data through dual writes versus change data capture?","answer":"Dual writes can make application-level migration explicit but introduce consistency risk if one write succeeds and the other fails. Change data capture can derive changes from an authoritative datastore and reduce application modification, but depends on database change semantics and capture reliability. Neither approach is inherently safe without reconciliation and failure handling.","principle":"Migration synchronization must have a recoverable source of truth and a mechanism for detecting and repairing divergence.","requirements":["Define source of truth.","Define synchronization ordering.","Define duplicate handling.","Define reconciliation.","Define cutover criteria."],"constraints":["Dual writes can partially succeed.","CDC may miss or reinterpret changes depending on datastore semantics.","Both can produce lag and temporary divergence."],"alternatives":["Dual write","CDC","Application event stream","Periodic reconciliation"],"tradeoff":{"dual_write":["explicit application control","partial-write consistency risk"],"CDC":["less application modification","capture dependency and semantic constraints"]},"decision_factors":["source datastore","change volume","application control","reconciliation capability","cutover strategy"],"failure_modes":["one-sided write","capture gap","duplicate change","ordering error","silent divergence"],"security_implication":"Migration replication must preserve data classification, access controls, encryption, and deletion semantics.","performance_implication":"Synchronization adds write or capture overhead; actual migration capacity should be measured against production-like load.","testing_guidance":["fail one destination write","pause CDC","inject duplicate changes","compare source and target continuously"],"verification_method":"Verify that every source change is accounted for exactly according to the migration's declared delivery semantics and that divergence is detectable.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Debezium Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B13-R003","related_to:D41-B37-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B37-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Migration Architecture","concept":"Expand-Contract Schema Migration vs Destructive Schema Change","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why is an expand-contract approach often used when database schemas must evolve across independently deployed application versions?","answer":"Expand-contract introduces compatible schema additions before consumers migrate, then removes obsolete structures after all consumers have transitioned. This supports mixed-version operation and reduces dependence on atomic deployment. Direct destructive changes can be simpler when all consumers are strictly coordinated and downtime is acceptable.","principle":"Schema changes should preserve compatibility for as long as independently deployed consumers may depend on the previous contract.","requirements":["Identify all schema consumers.","Add compatible structures first.","Migrate consumers.","Verify old-field inactivity.","Remove obsolete structures only after confirmation."],"constraints":["Temporary duplicate columns/tables increase complexity.","Backfills can be expensive.","Hidden consumers can block cleanup."],"alternatives":["Expand-contract","Atomic destructive migration","Versioned schema"],"tradeoff":{"expand_contract":["mixed-version compatibility","temporary schema complexity"],"destructive":["simpler final state","high coordination risk"]},"decision_factors":["deployment independence","data volume","consumer count","downtime tolerance","rollback needs"],"failure_modes":["old consumer breaks","partial backfill","premature removal","dual-field divergence"],"security_implication":"New schema fields must receive correct access controls and must not expose sensitive information merely because they were added for migration.","performance_implication":"Backfills and dual-field writes can increase database load; migration workload must be bounded and observed.","testing_guidance":["run old and new application versions together","validate backfill","interrupt migration","verify field parity"],"verification_method":"Verify that old and new consumers can coexist throughout the migration window and that obsolete structures are removed only after evidence of non-use.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Evolutionary Database Design"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"PostgreSQL Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B25-R002","related_to:D41-B37-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B37-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Migration Architecture","concept":"Shadow Traffic vs Production Cutover","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a new implementation receive shadow traffic before becoming authoritative?","answer":"Shadow traffic can validate behavior against realistic request patterns without exposing the new implementation's responses to users, making it useful when correctness or performance uncertainty is high. It requires careful handling of side effects, privacy, resource consumption, and request replay semantics. Direct cutover is simpler but provides less production evidence before authority changes.","principle":"Shadow execution is valuable when realistic traffic evidence can reduce uncertainty without allowing duplicate side effects.","requirements":["Define shadow isolation.","Prevent unintended side effects.","Define comparison criteria.","Protect sensitive request data.","Bound shadow resource usage."],"constraints":["Shadow execution doubles or increases workload.","Non-idempotent operations require special handling.","Production data may contain sensitive information."],"alternatives":["Direct cutover","Shadow traffic","Canary traffic","Offline replay"],"tradeoff":{"shadow":["realistic validation","resource and side-effect complexity"],"direct":["simple deployment","higher uncertainty at cutover"]},"decision_factors":["uncertainty","traffic realism","side-effect risk","privacy","capacity"],"failure_modes":["duplicate side effects","shadow overload","false equivalence","data leakage"],"security_implication":"Shadow systems must receive only the data and privileges necessary for validation; production secrets and side effects should not be duplicated unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"Shadow traffic adds resource consumption and can affect production if capacity is not isolated.","testing_guidance":["verify side-effect suppression","compare responses","test sensitive data handling","measure additional resource use"],"verification_method":"Verify that shadow execution cannot produce unauthorized external effects and that comparison results are attributable to equivalent inputs.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Canary Release"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B19-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B37-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B37","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Migration Architecture","concept":"Compatibility Layer vs Immediate Consumer Migration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a temporary compatibility layer be used instead of migrating every consumer immediately?","answer":"A compatibility layer is useful when consumer migration cannot be coordinated and preserving the old interface temporarily reduces operational risk. Immediate migration can produce a cleaner architecture but requires strong consumer control and coordination. Compatibility layers should have explicit ownership, telemetry, retirement criteria, and security review so they do not become permanent hidden architecture.","principle":"Compatibility layers are migration mechanisms, not permanent substitutes for coherent contracts unless their long-term role is explicitly accepted.","requirements":["Identify legacy consumers.","Define translation semantics.","Track usage.","Define retirement condition.","Test both paths."],"constraints":["Compatibility logic increases maintenance.","Unknown consumers can prevent removal.","Translation can conceal semantic differences."],"alternatives":["Immediate migration","Temporary adapter","Versioned API","Permanent compatibility gateway"],"tradeoff":{"compatibility_layer":["reduces migration coordination","temporary complexity"],"immediate_migration":["cleaner target state","higher coordinated change risk"]},"decision_factors":["consumer control","migration duration","legacy population","security impact","maintenance cost"],"failure_modes":["permanent legacy path","semantic translation bug","untracked consumer","security bypass"],"security_implication":"Compatibility layers must enforce the same authorization and validation requirements as the target interface and must not create weaker legacy paths.","performance_implication":"Translation adds processing overhead; usage should be measured and the layer removed when no longer necessary.","testing_guidance":["test legacy consumers","track compatibility usage","test unauthorized legacy access","verify retirement readiness"],"verification_method":"Verify that compatibility usage is observable and that removal can be demonstrated through consumer inventory and telemetry.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Sam Newman, Building Microservices"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Martin Fowler, Strangler Fig Application"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B25-R002","related_to:D41-B37-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B38-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Synchronous Telemetry vs Asynchronous Telemetry Export","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should telemetry be exported synchronously with application execution versus asynchronously through buffering or agents?","answer":"Asynchronous telemetry usually reduces direct coupling between application latency and telemetry transport, while synchronous export can provide stronger delivery immediacy at the cost of request-path dependency. The architecture should define whether telemetry loss is acceptable and ensure buffering does not become an unbounded resource consumer.","principle":"Telemetry architecture should prevent observability mechanisms from becoming an uncontrolled availability dependency while preserving the evidence required for operations and compliance.","requirements":["Define telemetry durability requirement.","Define buffer limits.","Define drop policy.","Define privacy controls.","Define backpressure behavior."],"constraints":["Asynchronous buffers can lose data during overflow or failure.","Synchronous export can add request latency and dependency risk.","Telemetry itself can contain sensitive information."],"alternatives":["Synchronous export","Local asynchronous buffer","Agent-based export","Durable telemetry queue"],"tradeoff":{"synchronous":["immediate export potential","request-path dependency"],"asynchronous":["reduced request coupling","buffering and loss management complexity"]},"decision_factors":["telemetry criticality","latency sensitivity","failure tolerance","buffer capacity","compliance"],"failure_modes":["telemetry loss","buffer exhaustion","request slowdown","telemetry pipeline outage"],"security_implication":"Telemetry must avoid exposing secrets or sensitive payloads and must use controlled transport and access.","performance_implication":"Synchronous telemetry can directly affect request latency; asynchronous telemetry shifts cost to buffering and export workers.","testing_guidance":["disable telemetry destination","fill buffers","measure request latency","verify sensitive-data redaction"],"verification_method":"Verify that telemetry failure cannot violate the application's primary availability contract unless explicitly required by policy.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OpenTelemetry Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B18-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B38-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"High-Cardinality Telemetry vs Bounded Dimensions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should telemetry include high-cardinality identifiers such as request IDs, user IDs, or object IDs?","answer":"High-cardinality dimensions can make individual traces and investigations easier but can increase storage, indexing, query, and cost complexity in metrics systems. Metrics should generally use bounded dimensions, while detailed identifiers can be placed in logs or traces where the telemetry system supports them appropriately. The architecture should distinguish diagnostic detail from aggregation dimensions.","principle":"Use telemetry dimensions according to their aggregation purpose rather than placing every identifier into every signal.","requirements":["Classify telemetry signals.","Define allowed metric dimensions.","Define correlation identifiers.","Define retention and privacy policy."],"constraints":["High-cardinality metrics can strain storage and query systems.","Identifiers may contain sensitive information.","Removing all identifiers can make incident correlation difficult."],"alternatives":["Bounded metric labels","High-cardinality metrics","Correlated logs/traces","Separate diagnostic metadata"],"tradeoff":{"high_cardinality":["fine-grained analysis","higher cost and operational risk"],"bounded":["predictable aggregation","less direct per-entity analysis"]},"decision_factors":["diagnostic need","cardinality","query workload","privacy","retention"],"failure_modes":["metric explosion","PII leakage","unqueryable telemetry","excessive storage cost"],"security_implication":"User and object identifiers in telemetry must be classified and protected; sensitive identifiers may need hashing, redaction, or omission depending on context.","performance_implication":"High-cardinality dimensions can increase ingestion, indexing, and query resource consumption.","testing_guidance":["estimate cardinality","load telemetry pipeline","test redaction","verify metric label limits"],"verification_method":"Verify that each telemetry dimension has an explicit aggregation purpose, cardinality expectation, and privacy classification.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OpenTelemetry Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Prometheus Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B18-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B38-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Full Tracing vs Sampling","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should distributed tracing use full capture versus sampling?","answer":"Full capture can maximize diagnostic detail but may increase telemetry volume and cost substantially. Sampling reduces overhead while retaining representative or strategically selected traces. Sampling policies should account for error traces, high-latency requests, security incidents, and rare events so that important evidence is not systematically excluded.","principle":"Sampling should optimize observability value rather than simply reduce telemetry volume.","requirements":["Define sampling policy.","Prioritize errors and important events where required.","Define retention.","Define privacy controls.","Measure dropped telemetry."],"constraints":["Sampling can miss rare events.","Tail-based sampling requires buffering or centralized decision logic.","Full capture may be operationally expensive."],"alternatives":["Head sampling","Tail sampling","Adaptive sampling","Full capture"],"tradeoff":{"full":["maximum coverage","higher resource and storage cost"],"sampling":["lower cost","risk of missing rare evidence"]},"decision_factors":["traffic volume","incident rarity","telemetry cost","diagnostic requirements","compliance"],"failure_modes":["sampling away failures","biased observability","buffer overflow","inconsistent trace selection"],"security_implication":"Sensitive trace data requires access control and redaction regardless of sampling strategy.","performance_implication":"Tracing overhead depends on instrumentation and export architecture; sampling can reduce volume but should be measured.","testing_guidance":["generate rare failures","verify error retention","test sampling rules","measure telemetry overhead"],"verification_method":"Verify that defined high-value events remain observable under the sampling policy and that sampling behavior is deterministic enough for operational use.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OpenTelemetry Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B18-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B38-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Centralized Observability Platform vs Team-Owned Telemetry","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should observability infrastructure be centralized versus owned independently by service teams?","answer":"Centralized observability can standardize collection, retention, access, and correlation across the organization, while team-owned telemetry can better match domain-specific needs and reduce central bottlenecks. A platform model can centralize infrastructure and standards while allowing teams to define domain-specific instrumentation.","principle":"Centralize shared observability capabilities while preserving service ownership of meaningful instrumentation and operational interpretation.","requirements":["Define platform responsibilities.","Define service ownership.","Define telemetry standards.","Define access control.","Define retention policy."],"constraints":["Centralization can create a bottleneck or single dependency.","Team-owned systems can fragment tooling and standards.","Sensitive telemetry may require domain-specific access controls."],"alternatives":["Central platform","Team-owned telemetry","Federated observability platform"],"tradeoff":{"centralized":["consistent infrastructure and governance","central dependency"],"team_owned":["domain flexibility","tooling fragmentation"]},"decision_factors":["organization size","compliance","telemetry diversity","operational ownership","platform maturity"],"failure_modes":["missing telemetry","tool fragmentation","central outage","unclear ownership"],"security_implication":"Central observability systems aggregate sensitive information and therefore require strong tenant/team access controls and retention governance.","performance_implication":"Central collection can create ingestion bottlenecks; distributed collection agents can buffer and batch data but add local resource use.","testing_guidance":["simulate central platform outage","verify local buffering","test access boundaries","audit telemetry coverage"],"verification_method":"Verify that every critical service has defined telemetry ownership and that observability remains useful during platform degradation.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OpenTelemetry Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B38-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B38-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B38","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Application Instrumentation vs Platform-Level Observability","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should observability rely on application instrumentation versus infrastructure-level signals?","answer":"Application instrumentation provides semantic information about business operations, errors, and domain behavior that infrastructure metrics cannot infer. Platform-level observability provides resource and network signals with less application modification. A robust architecture uses both when operational decisions require correlation between application behavior and infrastructure conditions.","principle":"Infrastructure signals reveal resource behavior while application signals reveal semantic behavior; neither fully substitutes for the other.","requirements":["Define operational questions.","Define application metrics/traces.","Define infrastructure metrics.","Define correlation identifiers.","Define ownership."],"constraints":["Application instrumentation requires code changes and maintenance.","Platform signals can lack domain context.","Excess instrumentation can create telemetry noise."],"alternatives":["Application-only","Platform-only","Combined observability"],"tradeoff":{"application":["semantic depth","instrumentation maintenance"],"platform":["broad infrastructure visibility","limited domain context"]},"decision_factors":["diagnostic goals","application complexity","platform maturity","incident patterns"],"failure_modes":["infrastructure-only blind spots","instrumentation drift","uncorrelated signals","telemetry overload"],"security_implication":"Application instrumentation must avoid exposing secrets and sensitive business data; platform telemetry must also respect access boundaries.","performance_implication":"Instrumentation adds overhead that depends on signal volume and implementation; critical paths should be measured.","testing_guidance":["correlate application and infrastructure failure","disable instrumentation","verify metric meaning","test sensitive-data filtering"],"verification_method":"Verify that important operational scenarios can be diagnosed using correlated application and infrastructure evidence.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"OpenTelemetry Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B38-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B39-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Governance","concept":"Overprovisioned Capacity vs Autoscaling","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should architecture maintain spare capacity instead of relying primarily on autoscaling?","answer":"Spare capacity can absorb sudden demand and reduce scaling delay, while autoscaling can adapt resources to changing workload levels. Autoscaling cannot always react before overload occurs, especially when initialization or resource acquisition is slow. The architecture should combine capacity planning and scaling according to workload burst characteristics and recovery objectives.","principle":"Autoscaling is a control mechanism, not a substitute for understanding capacity and scaling latency.","requirements":["Define capacity limits.","Define scaling signals.","Define scale-up latency.","Define minimum capacity.","Define overload behavior."],"constraints":["Overprovisioning increases idle resource cost.","Autoscaling can lag sudden demand.","Poor scaling signals can amplify instability."],"alternatives":["Fixed overprovisioned capacity","Reactive autoscaling","Predictive scaling","Hybrid baseline plus autoscaling"],"tradeoff":{"overprovisioned":["fast burst absorption","higher steady-state cost"],"autoscaling":["resource efficiency","scaling delay and control complexity"]},"decision_factors":["burst size","scale-up latency","cost","traffic predictability","resource startup time"],"failure_modes":["scaling too late","oscillation","capacity exhaustion","unnecessary scale-out"],"security_implication":"Autoscaling and resource provisioning identities require least privilege; scaling mechanisms must not become an uncontrolled privilege escalation path.","performance_implication":"Scaling behavior should be evaluated using measured workload patterns, startup times, and saturation points.","testing_guidance":["generate sudden bursts","simulate scaling failure","measure scale-up latency","test maximum capacity"],"verification_method":"Verify that declared traffic bursts can be handled within the system's overload and scaling objectives.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B21-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B39-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Governance","concept":"Resource Reservation vs Opportunistic Resource Sharing","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should workloads receive reserved capacity instead of consuming opportunistic shared resources?","answer":"Reserved capacity is appropriate for latency-sensitive, critical, or predictable workloads that require resource guarantees. Opportunistic sharing can improve utilization for flexible workloads that tolerate interruption or variable performance. Resource classes should be based on explicit service objectives rather than informal priority assumptions.","principle":"Resource guarantees should be allocated according to workload criticality and tolerance for interruption or contention.","requirements":["Classify workload priority.","Define guaranteed resources.","Define preemption behavior.","Define fairness policy."],"constraints":["Reservations can strand unused capacity.","Opportunistic workloads can be interrupted.","Priority mechanisms can create starvation if poorly bounded."],"alternatives":["Reserved capacity","Shared best-effort capacity","Priority-based scheduling","Hybrid reservations"],"tradeoff":{"reserved":["predictability","potential idle capacity"],"opportunistic":["higher utilization","less predictable availability"]},"decision_factors":["criticality","latency","interruptibility","resource utilization","fairness"],"failure_modes":["resource starvation","priority inversion","idle reservations","unexpected preemption"],"security_implication":"Resource priority must not permit one tenant or identity to bypass authorization boundaries or consume protected capacity without entitlement.","performance_implication":"Reservations improve predictability but reduce pooling flexibility; actual utilization should be measured.","testing_guidance":["saturate shared pool","test priority behavior","simulate preemption","measure reserved utilization"],"verification_method":"Verify that resource allocation honors declared priority and isolation rules under sustained contention.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Kubernetes Resource Management Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Google SRE, Site Reliability Engineering"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B31-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B39-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Governance","concept":"Shared Connection Pool vs Per-Component Connection Pools","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should services share a connection pool versus maintain independent pools for downstream resources?","answer":"A shared pool can improve connection reuse and simplify capacity management when consumers have compatible requirements. Independent pools can isolate workloads and prevent one component from exhausting connections needed by another. Pool limits should account for downstream capacity rather than only local application capacity.","principle":"Connection pooling should balance reuse against resource isolation and downstream capacity constraints.","requirements":["Define pool ownership.","Define maximum connections.","Define timeout behavior.","Define downstream capacity.","Define fairness between callers."],"constraints":["Shared pools can create contention.","Independent pools can multiply total connections.","Incorrect limits can overload downstream systems."],"alternatives":["Shared pool","Per-service pool","Per-workload pool","Hierarchical pooling"],"tradeoff":{"shared":["connection reuse","contention risk"],"independent":["isolation","aggregate connection amplification"]},"decision_factors":["downstream capacity","workload diversity","connection cost","fairness","failure isolation"],"failure_modes":["connection exhaustion","pool starvation","connection storm","idle connection waste"],"security_implication":"Connections must preserve appropriate identity and authorization; pooling must not accidentally reuse credentials or security context across tenants or users.","performance_implication":"Pooling can reduce connection establishment overhead but excessive pools can exhaust downstream resources.","testing_guidance":["exhaust pool","fail downstream","test tenant isolation","measure connection churn","test timeout behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify that aggregate connection limits remain within downstream capacity and that security context is not incorrectly shared.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"PostgreSQL Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B39-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B39-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Governance","concept":"Resource Isolation by Process vs Resource Limits Within a Process","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should workloads be isolated into separate processes instead of sharing a process with explicit resource limits?","answer":"Separate processes provide stronger fault and resource isolation when one workload can crash, leak, or consume disproportionate resources. In-process limits can be simpler and cheaper when workloads are trusted and failure isolation is less critical. The decision should consider memory safety, crash behavior, CPU contention, trust boundaries, and operational overhead.","principle":"Process isolation is justified when failure or resource contention in one workload must not compromise another workload.","requirements":["Define failure boundary.","Define resource limits.","Define communication mechanism.","Define lifecycle management."],"constraints":["Separate processes add IPC and deployment complexity.","In-process limits may not protect against all failure modes.","Language/runtime characteristics affect isolation strength."],"alternatives":["Shared process","Separate processes","Containers/sandboxed workloads"],"tradeoff":{"shared":["lower operational overhead","weaker failure isolation"],"separate":["stronger isolation","higher resource and lifecycle overhead"]},"decision_factors":["trust","failure impact","resource behavior","language/runtime","operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["process crash propagation","memory exhaustion","CPU starvation","IPC failure"],"security_implication":"Process isolation can reduce privilege and blast radius but is only as strong as the underlying operating-system or sandbox boundary.","performance_implication":"Separate processes add IPC and memory overhead while potentially improving predictability under contention.","testing_guidance":["crash one workload","exhaust memory","saturate CPU","test IPC failure","verify privilege separation"],"verification_method":"Verify that failure or resource exhaustion in one workload cannot violate the availability or isolation requirements of another.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Linux cgroups Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Kubernetes Documentation"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B31-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B39-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B39","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Governance","concept":"Central Resource Scheduler vs Decentralized Scheduling","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should resource allocation be controlled by a central scheduler versus decentralized local schedulers?","answer":"Central scheduling can optimize global resource placement and enforce consistent policies, while decentralized scheduling can reduce central dependency and improve local autonomy. Central schedulers can become bottlenecks or high-value control points; decentralized scheduling can produce globally inefficient allocations without coordination.","principle":"Scheduling architecture should reflect whether global optimization or local autonomy is more important to the resource model.","requirements":["Define resource ownership.","Define scheduling objectives.","Define failure behavior.","Define fairness and priority rules."],"constraints":["Central scheduling requires availability and scalability.","Decentralized scheduling can create contention and fragmented capacity.","Scheduling decisions can become difficult to explain without sufficient telemetry."],"alternatives":["Central scheduler","Decentralized scheduler","Hierarchical scheduler"],"tradeoff":{"central":["global visibility and policy","central dependency"],"decentralized":["local autonomy and fault isolation","global optimization challenges"]},"decision_factors":["resource scale","placement constraints","failure domains","fairness","global optimization need"],"failure_modes":["scheduler outage","double allocation","fragmented capacity","priority starvation"],"security_implication":"Scheduler authority can control privileged resource placement and therefore requires strict authorization and audit controls.","performance_implication":"Central scheduling can optimize placement but introduces scheduling overhead; decentralized decisions can reduce coordination but may waste capacity.","testing_guidance":["disconnect scheduler","simulate conflicting allocations","test fairness","measure scheduling latency"],"verification_method":"Verify that resource ownership cannot be double-assigned and that scheduler failure has a defined safe recovery behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Kubernetes Scheduler Documentation"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Operating Systems scheduling literature"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B39-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B40-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Technology Selection","concept":"Build vs Buy vs Adopt Open Source","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should architecture evaluate building a capability internally, purchasing a managed product, or adopting an open-source implementation?","answer":"Build decisions should compare required capability, strategic differentiation, total lifecycle cost, control, integration effort, security, operational ownership, and switching risk. Buying can reduce implementation burden but creates vendor and contract dependencies. Open source can provide control and transparency but still requires lifecycle ownership, security maintenance, and operational expertise.","principle":"Technology acquisition decisions should evaluate lifecycle ownership and strategic requirements rather than comparing initial implementation cost alone.","requirements":["Define required capabilities.","Estimate lifecycle ownership.","Evaluate security and compliance.","Assess integration effort.","Define exit or replacement strategy."],"constraints":["Vendor products can constrain architecture.","Open-source components still require maintenance.","Internal builds create long-term ownership obligations."],"alternatives":["Build internally","Commercial product/service","Open-source component","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":{"build":["maximum control","highest ownership burden"],"buy":["faster capability acquisition potential","vendor dependency"],"open_source":["source availability and flexibility","maintenance and security responsibility"]},"decision_factors":["strategic differentiation","TCO","security","integration","vendor risk","team capability","exit cost"],"failure_modes":["underestimated maintenance","vendor lock-in","unpatched dependency","feature mismatch"],"security_implication":"Security responsibility remains with the organization even when capability is purchased or adopted from open source; responsibility boundaries must be explicit.","performance_implication":"Product and open-source performance claims must be evaluated using workload-specific evidence rather than vendor or community claims alone.","testing_guidance":["run capability proof against requirements","evaluate failure behavior","audit dependencies","test replacement/exit assumptions"],"verification_method":"Verify that the selected option satisfies mandatory requirements and has an explicit long-term ownership and exit model.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST Secure Software Development Framework"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B27-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B40-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Technology Selection","concept":"Stable Mature Technology vs Newer Technology Adoption","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should architecture adopt a newer technology instead of a mature established technology?","answer":"A newer technology can be justified when it provides a capability that materially improves required outcomes and the organization can absorb its maturity, tooling, support, and migration risks. Mature technology is generally preferable when the requirement is already satisfied and reliability or ecosystem depth dominates. Technology age alone is not sufficient evidence of suitability.","principle":"Adopt newer technology only when its demonstrated capability or strategic value outweighs additional uncertainty and lifecycle risk.","requirements":["Define capability gap.","Evaluate maturity evidence.","Assess ecosystem and support.","Define fallback or migration strategy.","Evaluate security lifecycle."],"constraints":["New technology may have incomplete tooling or operational knowledge.","Mature technology can carry legacy constraints.","Technology evaluation is sensitive to version and ecosystem changes."],"alternatives":["Mature technology","New technology","Pilot before adoption","Hybrid migration"],"tradeoff":{"mature":["known operational characteristics","possible feature limitations"],"new":["potential capability improvements","higher uncertainty and adoption risk"]},"decision_factors":["capability gap","maturity","support","security","team expertise","migration cost"],"failure_modes":["premature adoption","ecosystem abandonment","insufficient expertise","migration lock-in"],"security_implication":"New technology requires evaluation of vulnerability response, dependency provenance, secure defaults, and maintenance lifecycle.","performance_implication":"Performance claims for new technology must be measured in the target workload and environment; benchmark results from other environments are not automatically transferable.","testing_guidance":["run representative proof of concept","test failure behavior","evaluate upgrade path","measure workload-specific performance"],"verification_method":"Verify the claimed capability gap and operational assumptions through bounded evidence before architectural commitment.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Thoughtworks Technology Radar methodology"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"NIST Secure Software Development Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B30-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B40-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Technology Selection","concept":"Single Technology Standard vs Purpose-Fit Technology Portfolio","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an organization standardize on one technology stack versus permit multiple technologies chosen for workload fit?","answer":"Standardization can reduce training, operational tooling, dependency, and support complexity. A purpose-fit portfolio can better match specialized workloads but increases organizational and operational diversity. Exceptions should require a concrete requirement and an ownership plan rather than being driven solely by developer preference.","principle":"Technology diversity should be proportional to demonstrated workload or architectural requirements and supported by sustainable ownership.","requirements":["Define standard stack.","Define exception criteria.","Define support ownership.","Define lifecycle policy.","Track technology inventory."],"constraints":["Over-standardization can force unsuitable tools.","Technology diversity increases operational complexity.","Exceptions can become uncontrolled fragmentation."],"alternatives":["Single standard stack","Approved technology portfolio","Open technology choice"],"tradeoff":{"standardized":["lower operational diversity","possible workload mismatch"],"portfolio":["better specialization potential","higher support and skill complexity"]},"decision_factors":["workload diversity","team expertise","operational tooling","security lifecycle","support cost"],"failure_modes":["technology sprawl","forced poor fit","unsupported exception","duplicated platform capabilities"],"security_implication":"Every technology introduces its own dependency, patching, configuration, and security expertise requirements.","performance_implication":"Purpose-fit technologies may improve workload suitability, but gains require workload-specific evidence.","testing_guidance":["evaluate exception rationale","audit technology inventory","test lifecycle support","compare operational overhead"],"verification_method":"Verify that each non-standard technology has a documented requirement, accountable owner, security lifecycle, and retirement path.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Thoughtworks Technology Radar methodology"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B40-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B40-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Technology Selection","concept":"Managed Service vs Self-Hosted Infrastructure","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an organization use a managed infrastructure service instead of operating the capability itself?","answer":"Managed services can reduce infrastructure lifecycle responsibilities and provide operational capabilities that would otherwise require significant expertise. Self-hosting can provide greater control over configuration, data placement, network topology, and lifecycle. The decision must explicitly account for responsibility boundaries, portability, service limits, cost, security, and operational capability.","principle":"Choose managed or self-hosted operation according to the required control boundary and the organization's ability to own the resulting operational responsibilities.","requirements":["Map responsibility boundaries.","Define data and network requirements.","Evaluate service limits.","Assess exit strategy.","Define operational ownership."],"constraints":["Managed services introduce provider dependency.","Self-hosting requires patching, monitoring, scaling, and recovery ownership.","Managed service behavior can change by provider version or policy."],"alternatives":["Fully managed service","Self-hosted service","Managed control plane with self-managed data plane","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":{"managed":["reduced operational ownership","provider dependency and control limits"],"self_hosted":["greater control","higher operational responsibility"]},"decision_factors":["control requirements","team capability","compliance","cost","portability","operational burden"],"failure_modes":["unclear shared responsibility","provider outage","service limit discovery","self-hosted maintenance failure"],"security_implication":"Managed services do not eliminate security responsibility; identity, configuration, data protection, and application security remain explicit responsibilities.","performance_implication":"Managed services may offer platform capabilities but performance remains workload and service-tier dependent; self-hosted performance requires capacity engineering.","testing_guidance":["test provider failure modes","validate service limits","test restore/exit procedure","audit shared responsibility"],"verification_method":"Verify responsibility boundaries and demonstrate recovery procedures for failures within both provider and customer control domains.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Microsoft Azure Well-Architected Framework"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B40-R001","related_to:D41-B27-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B40-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B40","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Technology Selection","concept":"Architecture Simplicity vs Capability Maximization","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should architecture balance a simpler design against adopting additional capabilities that could satisfy more future requirements?","answer":"A simpler architecture reduces cognitive load, operational surface, failure modes, and maintenance cost. Additional capabilities are justified when they address known requirements, materially reduce risk, or create a strategically required capability. Designing for hypothetical future needs can increase complexity without evidence that the capability will be used.","principle":"Prefer the simplest architecture that satisfies known requirements and preserves explicitly valuable future change paths.","requirements":["Identify current mandatory requirements.","Identify high-confidence future constraints.","Document complexity introduced by optional capabilities.","Define extension points only where justified."],"constraints":["Oversimplification can block required evolution.","Overengineering creates unused complexity.","Future requirements are uncertain."],"alternatives":["Minimal architecture","Capability-rich architecture","Incremental extensibility","Modular core with optional capabilities"],"tradeoff":{"simple":["lower operational and cognitive complexity","may require future extension"],"capability_rich":["broader immediate capability","higher complexity and maintenance burden"]},"decision_factors":["known requirements","uncertainty","change cost","operational burden","strategic capability","reversibility"],"failure_modes":["overengineering","premature abstraction","capability sprawl","architecture unable to satisfy required evolution"],"security_implication":"Every additional capability expands the attack and trust surface; unused security-sensitive components should not be introduced merely for hypothetical future needs.","performance_implication":"Additional layers and capabilities can consume resources, but exact impact must be measured rather than inferred from architecture complexity alone.","testing_guidance":["trace each capability to a requirement","measure operational burden","test extension path","remove unused capability where appropriate"],"verification_method":"Verify that each major architectural capability maps to a documented requirement, risk reduction, or justified future constraint and that unnecessary complexity is excluded.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"CONTENT_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":[{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"SEI Architecture Tradeoff Analysis Method"},{"type":"REFERENCE","source":"Robert C. Martin, Clean Architecture"}],"relationships":["related_to:D41-B30-R001","related_to:D41-B40-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B41-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Dedicated compute versus shared compute","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture use dedicated compute resources instead of shared compute resources?","answer":"Dedicated compute is appropriate when workload isolation, predictable capacity, regulatory separation, deterministic performance, or tenant-specific scaling materially outweighs the utilization and operational-efficiency benefits of shared infrastructure. Shared compute is generally preferable when workloads are compatible, isolation requirements are satisfied through stronger logical boundaries, and utilization efficiency is important.","principle":"Choose the smallest compute isolation boundary that satisfies security, performance, availability, compliance, and operational requirements.","requirements":["Define workload isolation requirements","Measure or estimate resource variability","Identify regulatory or contractual isolation requirements","Define scaling and capacity objectives","Define operational ownership boundaries"],"constraints":["Dedicated resources increase cost and may reduce utilization","Shared resources require effective isolation and noisy-neighbor controls","Capacity planning differs between pooled and dedicated models"],"preconditions":["Workload resource characteristics are understood","Isolation requirements are explicit","Capacity constraints are identified"],"objective":"Balance resource efficiency against workload isolation and predictability.","alternatives":["Shared compute pool","Dedicated host or node","Dedicated cluster","Dedicated environment"],"tradeoff":{"benefits_of_dedicated":["Predictable capacity","Stronger workload isolation","Independent scaling","Reduced noisy-neighbor exposure"],"costs_of_dedicated":["Higher infrastructure cost","Lower potential utilization","More operational boundaries"]},"decision_factors":["Security isolation","Performance predictability","Utilization","Scaling behavior","Compliance","Cost","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Over-isolation causing excessive infrastructure cost","Under-isolation causing resource contention","Incorrect capacity assumptions","Inconsistent resource policies"],"security_implication":"Dedicated infrastructure can reduce cross-workload exposure but does not replace identity, authorization, network, or application-level controls.","performance_implication":"Dedicated resources can improve predictability when contention is the dominant source of variability; this must be established through workload measurements rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":["Load-test shared and isolated configurations","Test resource exhaustion","Test noisy-neighbor scenarios","Verify scaling boundaries"],"verification_method":"Verify that selected compute boundaries satisfy documented isolation, capacity, scaling, and availability requirements.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Cloud architecture engineering patterns","AWS Well-Architected Framework","Microsoft Azure Architecture Center"],"related_records":["D41-B31-R004","D41-B35-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B41-R002","requires:D41-B41-R003","tested_by:D41-B41-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B41-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Static capacity versus elastic capacity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the architectural trade-offs between static capacity and elastic capacity?","answer":"Static capacity reserves a predictable amount of resources and can simplify operations for stable workloads. Elastic capacity dynamically changes resources in response to demand and can improve resource efficiency for variable workloads, but introduces scaling latency, control complexity, and dependence on reliable demand signals.","principle":"Use elasticity when workload variability and scaling economics justify the additional control-plane complexity and when the system can tolerate scaling delay.","requirements":["Define expected demand range","Define scaling triggers","Define minimum and maximum capacity","Define scale-up and scale-down behavior","Define overload behavior"],"constraints":["Elastic scaling cannot eliminate finite capacity limits","Scaling decisions may lag sudden demand changes","Poor scaling signals can cause oscillation or under-provisioning"],"preconditions":["Demand characteristics are measurable","Capacity metrics are available","Scaling mechanisms are reliable"],"objective":"Match infrastructure capacity to workload demand while maintaining required service levels.","alternatives":["Fixed capacity","Scheduled scaling","Reactive autoscaling","Predictive autoscaling"],"tradeoff":{"static_capacity":["Predictability","Simpler operations","Potential idle capacity"],"elastic_capacity":["Better utilization","Variable capacity cost","Additional control complexity","Scaling latency"]},"decision_factors":["Demand variability","Startup time","Cost model","Availability target","Scaling signal quality","Operational maturity"],"failure_modes":["Scaling too slowly","Scaling too aggressively","Capacity oscillation","Maximum capacity exhaustion","Scaling based on misleading metrics"],"security_implication":"Elastic environments require consistent security controls across dynamically created and removed resources.","performance_implication":"Elasticity can improve capacity availability under variable load but may introduce cold-start or provisioning latency.","testing_guidance":["Test rapid demand increases","Test scale-down behavior","Test maximum capacity","Test failed provisioning","Test scaling oscillation"],"verification_method":"Verify scaling behavior against documented demand profiles, capacity limits, and service objectives.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Google SRE engineering principles","AWS Well-Architected Framework","Microsoft Azure Architecture Center"],"related_records":["D41-B41-R001","D41-B41-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B41-R001","requires:D41-B41-R004","tested_by:D41-B41-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B41-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Resource isolation boundary selection","knowledge_type":"DECISION_RULE","question":"How should an architecture select the boundary at which compute resources are isolated?","answer":"Select the isolation boundary according to the strongest applicable requirement among security, regulatory separation, failure containment, performance predictability, lifecycle independence, and operational ownership. Isolation should not be introduced merely because it is technically possible; every boundary creates provisioning, monitoring, deployment, and capacity-management overhead.","principle":"Place isolation boundaries where they materially reduce a documented risk or operational dependency.","requirements":["Identify shared-resource risks","Define failure domains","Define ownership boundaries","Define lifecycle independence requirements","Evaluate operational overhead"],"constraints":["More boundaries increase management complexity","Logical isolation may be sufficient for some requirements","Physical isolation may be required for stronger separation requirements"],"preconditions":["Threat model exists","Failure modes are identified","Resource dependencies are known"],"objective":"Choose an isolation boundary that provides sufficient protection without unnecessary architectural fragmentation.","alternatives":["Process-level isolation","Container-level isolation","Node-level isolation","Cluster-level isolation","Environment-level isolation"],"tradeoff":{"stronger_isolation":["Reduced blast radius","Higher separation","Higher operational overhead"],"weaker_isolation":["Higher resource efficiency","Lower management overhead","Greater shared-resource exposure"]},"decision_factors":["Blast radius","Threat model","Compliance","Performance interference","Deployment independence","Cost"],"failure_modes":["Fragmentation without meaningful risk reduction","Insufficient isolation for the threat model","Hidden shared dependencies","Central resource becoming a common failure domain"],"security_implication":"Isolation boundaries should align with actual trust boundaries and threat assumptions; infrastructure separation alone does not establish complete trust isolation.","performance_implication":"Isolation can reduce contention but may introduce network, scheduling, or data-transfer overhead.","testing_guidance":["Exercise failure propagation","Test cross-boundary access","Test resource exhaustion","Verify isolation controls"],"verification_method":"Map every isolation boundary to an explicit requirement and verify that the boundary prevents the intended failure or access path.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.93,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST security architecture principles","Cloud architecture engineering patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B41-R001","D41-B35-R001"],"relationships":["refines:D41-B41-R001","requires:D41-B41-R004","contrasts_with:D41-B41-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B41-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Capacity validation for architecture selection","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should compute architecture decisions be validated before production adoption?","answer":"Validate the decision against representative workload characteristics, resource saturation behavior, scaling behavior, failure scenarios, and operational constraints. Architectural reasoning should identify assumptions explicitly and replace important assumptions with measurements where practical.","principle":"Architecture decisions involving capacity should be validated against representative workload behavior rather than theoretical capacity alone.","requirements":["Representative workload model","Defined capacity metrics","Defined saturation thresholds","Failure scenarios","Acceptance criteria"],"constraints":["Synthetic workloads may not reproduce production behavior","Capacity measurements are environment-dependent","Performance results require context"],"preconditions":["Candidate architectures are implementable","Test environment is sufficiently representative","Metrics are available"],"objective":"Determine whether the selected compute architecture satisfies its capacity and reliability requirements.","alternatives":["Analytical capacity model","Load testing","Stress testing","Production canary measurement"],"tradeoff":{"benefit":"Reduces architecture uncertainty","cost":"Requires test infrastructure and representative workload design"},"decision_factors":["Workload representativeness","Measurement quality","Failure coverage","Scaling behavior","Acceptance thresholds"],"failure_modes":["Testing unrealistic workloads","Ignoring resource saturation","Confusing average performance with tail behavior","Using measurements from incompatible environments"],"security_implication":"Validation environments should prevent test workloads and credentials from creating unintended access to production resources.","performance_implication":"Capacity validation should examine saturation and tail behavior, not only average utilization or average latency.","testing_guidance":["Run baseline load tests","Run stress tests","Test resource exhaustion","Test recovery after saturation","Compare results against acceptance criteria"],"verification_method":"Record test configuration, workload characteristics, measurements, thresholds, and observed outcomes; classify unexecuted assumptions separately.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Google SRE engineering practices","Performance engineering methodology"],"related_records":["D41-B41-R002","D41-B41-R003"],"relationships":["verifies:D41-B41-R002","verifies:D41-B41-R003","requires:D41-B41-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B41-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B41","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Graceful capacity exhaustion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should architecture decisions explicitly define behavior when compute capacity is exhausted?","answer":"Capacity exhaustion is a bounded condition that should have an intentional response. Depending on the workload, the architecture may reject excess work, shed optional work, queue requests, degrade non-critical functionality, or preserve capacity for higher-priority operations. Undefined exhaustion behavior can turn resource saturation into cascading failure.","principle":"Treat capacity exhaustion as a designed operating state rather than an impossible condition.","requirements":["Capacity limits","Admission control or overload behavior","Priority rules","Degradation strategy","Operational visibility"],"constraints":["Capacity cannot be expanded indefinitely","Queueing can increase latency","Load shedding can reject legitimate requests","Degradation can reduce functionality"],"preconditions":["Criticality classes are defined","Resource limits are observable","Overload behavior is implementable"],"objective":"Contain failure when demand exceeds available capacity.","alternatives":["Queue excess work","Reject excess requests","Load shed","Degrade optional functionality","Reserve capacity for critical workloads"],"tradeoff":{"queueing":["Preserves work","Increases latency"],"rejection":["Protects system","Drops work"],"degradation":["Preserves core functionality","Reduces feature availability"],"reservation":["Protects critical workloads","May reduce overall utilization"]},"decision_factors":["Request criticality","Latency sensitivity","Work durability","Recovery behavior","Capacity elasticity"],"failure_modes":["Cascading overload","Unbounded queues","Priority inversion","Retry storms","Resource starvation"],"security_implication":"Admission controls should not create authorization bypasses or allow lower-priority traffic to exhaust resources reserved for protected operations.","performance_implication":"Overload protection can preserve system stability but may intentionally reduce throughput or increase rejection rates during saturation.","testing_guidance":["Inject capacity exhaustion","Test queue limits","Test priority handling","Test retry behavior","Test recovery after overload"],"verification_method":"Demonstrate that overload behavior preserves defined critical functions and terminates or bounds resource consumption.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Google SRE engineering principles","Distributed systems resilience patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B41-R002","D41-B41-R004"],"relationships":["depends_on:D41-B41-R002","tested_by:D41-B41-R004","mitigates:D41-B41-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B42-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Network Architecture","concept":"Centralized versus distributed network control","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the trade-offs between centralized and distributed network control?","answer":"Centralized network control can provide consistent policy and simplified management but may create control-plane dependencies and scaling constraints. Distributed control can improve locality and autonomy but increases coordination, policy consistency, and operational complexity.","principle":"Centralize policy decisions when consistency is more important than local autonomy, and distribute control when latency, resilience, or independent operation requires it.","requirements":["Define control-plane availability requirements","Define policy consistency requirements","Define latency constraints","Define failure behavior"],"constraints":["Centralized control introduces dependency on the control plane","Distributed control requires synchronization or policy propagation","Network partitions can affect distributed coordination"],"preconditions":["Network topology is understood","Policy ownership is defined","Failure domains are identified"],"objective":"Select an appropriate network-control topology for consistency, availability, latency, and operational requirements.","alternatives":["Centralized controller","Hierarchical controller","Distributed controllers","Autonomous local enforcement"],"tradeoff":{"centralized":["Consistency","Simpler policy management","Potential central dependency"],"distributed":["Local autonomy","Potential resilience and latency benefits","Higher coordination complexity"]},"decision_factors":["Control-plane availability","Latency","Policy consistency","Scale","Failure isolation","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Central controller outage","Stale distributed policies","Configuration divergence","Control-plane overload"],"security_implication":"Distributed enforcement requires assurance that security policies remain consistent across independent enforcement points.","performance_implication":"Local enforcement can reduce control-path latency but may require policy propagation and synchronization mechanisms.","testing_guidance":["Test controller failure","Test policy propagation delay","Test partition behavior","Test configuration divergence"],"verification_method":"Verify policy consistency and defined failure behavior across all control points.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.94,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST network security architecture guidance","Distributed systems engineering patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B35-R001","D41-B42-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B42-R002","requires:D41-B42-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B42-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Network Architecture","concept":"Hub-and-spoke versus full mesh connectivity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should hub-and-spoke connectivity be preferred over full mesh connectivity?","answer":"Hub-and-spoke connectivity is useful when centralized inspection, policy enforcement, routing control, or simplified connectivity management is important. Full mesh connectivity can reduce dependence on a central transit point and may provide direct paths, but the number of relationships and policy surfaces grows with the number of participants.","principle":"Choose connectivity topology based on the required communication graph, inspection points, failure domains, and operational complexity.","requirements":["Define communication relationships","Define inspection requirements","Define routing requirements","Define failure-domain requirements"],"constraints":["Hub designs can create central bottlenecks or dependencies","Mesh designs increase connection and policy complexity","Large meshes require automation"],"preconditions":["Participant count is known","Traffic patterns are understood","Network policy ownership is defined"],"objective":"Select a topology that balances connectivity, control, resilience, and operational complexity.","alternatives":["Hub-and-spoke","Full mesh","Partial mesh","Hierarchical mesh"],"tradeoff":{"hub_spoke":["Centralized control","Simpler relationship management","Potential central bottleneck"],"full_mesh":["Direct connectivity","Reduced central dependency","Greater relationship complexity"]},"decision_factors":["Participant count","Traffic locality","Inspection","Latency","Failure isolation","Policy complexity"],"failure_modes":["Hub saturation","Single transit dependency","Mesh configuration explosion","Uncontrolled lateral connectivity"],"security_implication":"A centralized hub can simplify inspection but can also become a high-value control point; mesh connectivity must explicitly control lateral paths.","performance_implication":"Direct paths may reduce transit hops, while centralized routing can add network latency and throughput constraints.","testing_guidance":["Test hub failure","Test path redundancy","Test unauthorized lateral paths","Test high connection counts"],"verification_method":"Verify that every required communication path exists and every prohibited path is blocked according to the approved topology.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Network architecture engineering patterns","NIST zero trust architecture concepts"],"related_records":["D41-B42-R001","D41-B42-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B42-R001","tested_by:D41-B42-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B42-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Network Architecture","concept":"East-west versus north-south traffic architecture","knowledge_type":"DECISION_RULE","question":"Why should architecture decisions distinguish east-west and north-south traffic?","answer":"North-south traffic crosses a system boundary such as an external client boundary, while east-west traffic occurs between internal workloads or services. These traffic classes often have different security, routing, latency, observability, and scaling requirements, so treating them identically can create inappropriate control points or incomplete internal protection.","principle":"Design network controls according to actual trust boundaries and communication patterns rather than assuming internal traffic is inherently trusted.","requirements":["Map traffic flows","Identify trust boundaries","Define inspection requirements","Define service-to-service identity requirements"],"constraints":["Additional internal controls can add latency and operational overhead","Not every internal flow requires identical controls","Traffic classification can change as architecture evolves"],"preconditions":["Network flows are known","Trust boundaries are documented","Service identities are available where required"],"objective":"Ensure network architecture reflects actual communication and trust relationships.","alternatives":["Perimeter-focused control","Internal segmentation","Identity-aware service communication","Hybrid controls"],"tradeoff":{"perimeter_focus":"Lower internal complexity but weaker internal containment","internal_controls":"Stronger lateral protection with additional complexity"},"decision_factors":["Threat model","Lateral movement risk","Latency","Inspection cost","Identity maturity","Segmentation requirements"],"failure_modes":["Assuming internal traffic is trusted","Missing lateral movement controls","Over-inspection causing bottlenecks","Inconsistent service identity"],"security_implication":"East-west traffic can cross meaningful trust boundaries even when it remains inside a private network.","performance_implication":"Deep inspection or proxying of high-volume east-west traffic can materially affect latency and throughput.","testing_guidance":["Map representative flows","Test unauthorized lateral communication","Measure control-plane and data-plane overhead","Test service identity failures"],"verification_method":"Verify every significant traffic class against its documented trust boundary, identity, authorization, and inspection requirements.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture","NIST network security guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B35-R001","D41-B42-R002"],"relationships":["requires:D41-B42-R001","refines:D41-B42-R002","tested_by:D41-B42-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B42-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Network Architecture","concept":"Network policy enforcement placement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should an architecture choose where network policy is enforced?","answer":"Policy enforcement should be placed at boundaries where the required context is available and where failure, access, and traffic controls can be observed and enforced reliably. Enforcement may occur at the edge, network layer, workload boundary, application layer, or multiple layers when distinct controls address different risks.","principle":"Place enforcement where the relevant identity, resource, context, and traffic information is available.","requirements":["Identify policy decision inputs","Identify enforcement points","Define fail-open or fail-closed behavior","Define observability requirements"],"constraints":["Enforcement points can introduce latency","Central enforcement can become a bottleneck","Application-layer controls may require code changes"],"preconditions":["Policy requirements are explicit","Traffic flows are mapped","Identity context is available"],"objective":"Ensure security and traffic policies are enforceable at the correct architectural boundaries.","alternatives":["Edge enforcement","Network enforcement","Workload enforcement","Application enforcement","Layered enforcement"],"tradeoff":{"centralized":["Consistency","Simpler policy management","Potential bottleneck"],"distributed":["Contextual enforcement","Failure isolation","More operational complexity"]},"decision_factors":["Policy context","Latency","Failure behavior","Scalability","Security boundary","Operational ownership"],"failure_modes":["Policy enforced too early without sufficient context","Policy enforced too late","Inconsistent duplicate policies","Uncontrolled fail-open behavior"],"security_implication":"A policy that cannot observe the required identity or resource context may provide weaker protection than intended.","performance_implication":"Additional enforcement layers can increase latency and resource consumption and should be measured for high-volume paths.","testing_guidance":["Test each enforcement boundary","Test missing context","Test enforcement-point failure","Test policy consistency"],"verification_method":"Trace representative requests through every enforcement point and verify that required policy decisions occur before protected actions.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture","OWASP application security guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B42-R003","D41-B35-R001"],"relationships":["implements:D41-B42-R003","requires:D41-B42-R005","verifies:D41-B42-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B42-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B42","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Network Architecture","concept":"Network architecture failure testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What network architecture failures should be explicitly tested?","answer":"Network architecture testing should include control-plane loss, routing failure, segmentation errors, dependency failure, partition behavior, capacity exhaustion, unauthorized path creation, and recovery. The goal is to verify both availability and security properties under degraded conditions.","principle":"Network architecture is not validated until expected failure modes and prohibited communication paths are tested.","requirements":["Failure scenarios","Expected behavior","Security invariants","Recovery criteria","Observability"],"constraints":["Some network failures are difficult to reproduce safely","Test environments may differ from production topology","Security testing must avoid unintended impact"],"preconditions":["Topology is documented","Critical flows are identified","Expected failure behavior is defined"],"objective":"Validate network resilience and security boundaries.","alternatives":["Fault injection","Controlled integration tests","Configuration analysis","Recovery exercises"],"tradeoff":{"fault_injection":"Higher realism but higher operational risk","configuration_analysis":"Lower risk but cannot validate runtime behavior"},"decision_factors":["Failure severity","Reproducibility","Environment safety","Coverage","Recovery requirements"],"failure_modes":["Hidden routing dependency","Fail-open control","Stale policy","Unexpected transitive connectivity"],"security_implication":"Tests should verify that failures do not silently create unauthorized communication paths.","performance_implication":"Network controls should be evaluated under congestion and degraded-control conditions.","testing_guidance":["Simulate route loss","Disable control-plane dependency","Test segmentation boundaries","Test capacity exhaustion","Test recovery"],"verification_method":"Compare observed network behavior against documented security and availability invariants for each failure scenario.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Google SRE resilience practices","NIST network security guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B42-R002","D41-B42-R004"],"relationships":["tests:D41-B42-R002","tests:D41-B42-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B43-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API and Contract Architecture","concept":"Strict contracts versus tolerant consumers","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the architectural trade-offs between strict API consumers and tolerant API consumers?","answer":"Strict consumers detect contract changes early but can become brittle when providers evolve. Tolerant consumers can survive additive or irrelevant provider changes but may hide contract violations or ambiguous behavior. The appropriate choice depends on contract guarantees, change frequency, compatibility policy, and validation needs.","principle":"Tolerate changes that are explicitly compatible while failing clearly on changes that violate required semantics.","requirements":["Define compatibility policy","Define required fields and semantics","Define unknown-field behavior","Define versioning strategy"],"constraints":["Excessive tolerance can hide defects","Strict validation can increase deployment coupling","Semantic changes may be breaking even when schemas remain syntactically compatible"],"preconditions":["Contract semantics are documented","Compatibility rules are explicit","Consumer responsibilities are defined"],"objective":"Balance forward compatibility against early detection of incompatible changes.","alternatives":["Strict schema validation","Tolerant parsing","Versioned contracts","Consumer-driven contracts"],"tradeoff":{"strict":["Early failure","Clear contract enforcement","Higher coupling"],"tolerant":["Greater compatibility","Lower immediate coupling","Potential hidden defects"]},"decision_factors":["Contract stability","Change frequency","Criticality","Backward compatibility","Failure visibility"],"failure_modes":["Silent semantic incompatibility","Unexpected provider changes","False compatibility","Delayed detection"],"security_implication":"Tolerant parsing must not allow unexpected fields or ambiguous representations to bypass security validation.","performance_implication":"Additional validation and compatibility handling generally add processing overhead, but the magnitude is implementation-dependent.","testing_guidance":["Test additive fields","Test missing required fields","Test incompatible type changes","Test semantic contract violations"],"verification_method":"Validate consumer behavior against explicitly defined compatible and incompatible contract-change cases.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["API compatibility engineering practices","Microsoft API design guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B43-R002","D41-B43-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B43-R002","tested_by:D41-B43-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B43-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API and Contract Architecture","concept":"Synchronous API versus asynchronous contract","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should architecture decide between synchronous APIs and asynchronous contracts?","answer":"Synchronous APIs are suitable when the caller requires an immediate response and the operation can complete within an acceptable latency and availability envelope. Asynchronous contracts are useful for long-running, bursty, decoupled, or independently retriable work but require explicit state, delivery, ordering, and failure semantics.","principle":"Use synchronous communication when immediate completion is part of the business interaction; use asynchronous communication when temporal decoupling provides material architectural value.","requirements":["Define completion semantics","Define timeout behavior","Define retry behavior","Define delivery semantics","Define operation state"],"constraints":["Synchronous calls create temporal coupling","Asynchronous systems require state and message handling","Async does not automatically improve correctness or reliability"],"preconditions":["Business interaction semantics are understood","Failure behavior is defined","Idempotency requirements are known"],"objective":"Select communication semantics that match business and operational requirements.","alternatives":["Synchronous request-response","Asynchronous command","Event notification","Hybrid request plus status tracking"],"tradeoff":{"synchronous":["Simple interaction model","Immediate response","Higher temporal coupling"],"asynchronous":["Temporal decoupling","Independent processing","More state and failure complexity"]},"decision_factors":["Latency","Operation duration","Coupling","Retryability","User interaction","Failure isolation"],"failure_modes":["Timeout ambiguity","Duplicate asynchronous processing","Lost status","Unbounded queues","Incorrect assumption of completion"],"security_implication":"Asynchronous workflows require authorization context and identity propagation to remain valid across delayed execution.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous processing can absorb bursts but may increase end-to-end completion latency.","testing_guidance":["Test timeout boundaries","Test duplicate delivery","Test delayed processing","Test status recovery","Test authorization context"],"verification_method":"Verify that completion, retry, failure, and authorization semantics remain unambiguous under normal and degraded operation.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed systems engineering literature","Google Cloud architecture guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B43-R003","D41-B43-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B43-R003","requires:D41-B43-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B43-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API and Contract Architecture","concept":"Request-response versus job-resource architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"When should a long-running operation be represented as a job resource rather than a blocking API request?","answer":"A job-resource model is appropriate when work can exceed request timeout limits, requires asynchronous processing, needs progress or status reporting, or must survive client disconnects. The architecture should expose explicit operation state rather than implying that request acceptance equals task completion.","principle":"Separate request acceptance from operation completion when execution is not bounded by a reliable synchronous interaction window.","requirements":["Stable operation identifier","Explicit status model","Completion state","Failure representation","Authorization checks for status access"],"constraints":["Introduces lifecycle state","Requires cleanup or retention policy","Clients must handle asynchronous state"],"preconditions":["Operation can be represented independently","Durable state is available","Completion semantics are defined"],"objective":"Provide reliable control and visibility for long-running operations.","alternatives":["Blocking request","Job resource","Callback","Event notification"],"tradeoff":{"job_resource":["Durability","Visibility","Long-running support","Additional state"],"blocking_request":["Simpler client interaction","Timeout and connection dependency"]},"decision_factors":["Execution duration","Client timeout","Retry behavior","Progress visibility","Durability"],"failure_modes":["Orphaned jobs","Ambiguous completion","Duplicate submission","Stale status","Unauthorized status access"],"security_implication":"Job identifiers must not become authorization substitutes; status and result access require independent authorization checks.","performance_implication":"Job resources decouple request latency from execution time but require additional persistence and status-management operations.","testing_guidance":["Disconnect client during execution","Retry submission","Poll status repeatedly","Expire old jobs","Test unauthorized access"],"verification_method":"Verify that request acceptance, execution, completion, failure, and result retrieval have distinct and correctly authorized states.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["API architecture patterns","Cloud asynchronous API design guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B43-R002","D41-B43-R004"],"relationships":["implements:D41-B43-R002","tested_by:D41-B43-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B43-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"API and Contract Architecture","subtopic":"API and Contract Architecture","concept":"Contract compatibility testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should API contract compatibility be validated?","answer":"Compatibility testing should evaluate schema changes, required and optional fields, types, enumerations, semantic behavior, error contracts, authentication requirements, and representative consumer interactions. Schema compatibility alone is insufficient when behavior or business semantics can change.","principle":"Test both structural compatibility and semantic compatibility.","requirements":["Contract versions","Representative consumers","Compatibility rules","Expected behavior","Negative cases"],"constraints":["Consumer coverage may be incomplete","Semantic compatibility requires behavioral tests","Version-specific behavior must be retained where supported"],"preconditions":["Contract specification exists","Compatibility policy is defined","Consumers are identifiable"],"objective":"Detect breaking contract changes before dependent systems are affected.","alternatives":["Schema validation","Consumer-driven contract tests","Integration tests","Compatibility analysis"],"tradeoff":{"schema_only":"Lower test complexity but incomplete semantic coverage","behavioral":"Higher coverage with greater test maintenance"},"decision_factors":["Contract criticality","Consumer count","Change frequency","Semantic complexity"],"failure_modes":["False compatibility","Uncovered consumer behavior","Undetected authorization change","Error-contract drift"],"security_implication":"Compatibility testing must include authentication, authorization, validation, and error behavior where those are part of the contract.","performance_implication":"Contract tests should detect accidental changes in latency-sensitive or payload-heavy interactions when those are explicit requirements.","testing_guidance":["Test old consumers against new provider","Test new consumers against supported provider versions","Test negative cases","Test authorization behavior"],"verification_method":"A change is compatible only when it satisfies the documented structural and semantic compatibility criteria for supported consumers.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Consumer-driven contract testing practices","API engineering standards"],"related_records":["D41-B43-R001","D41-B43-R003"],"relationships":["tests:D41-B43-R001","tests:D41-B43-R003","requires:D41-B43-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B43-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B43","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API and Contract Architecture","concept":"Contract evolution policy","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an architecture define before allowing API contracts to evolve?","answer":"A contract evolution policy should define supported versions, compatibility guarantees, deprecation periods, breaking-change criteria, migration responsibilities, validation mechanisms, and retirement conditions. Without these rules, individually reasonable changes can create uncontrolled system coupling.","principle":"Contract evolution must be governed as a lifecycle rather than treated as unrestricted schema editing.","requirements":["Version policy","Compatibility policy","Deprecation policy","Migration ownership","Retirement criteria"],"constraints":["Long compatibility periods increase maintenance cost","Short migration periods increase coordination pressure","Some semantic changes require migration even without schema changes"],"preconditions":["Consumers are identifiable","Contract ownership is defined","Change-management process exists"],"objective":"Make contract evolution predictable and bounded.","alternatives":["Versioned contracts","Backward-compatible evolution","Parallel contracts","Migration gateway"],"tradeoff":{"long_compatibility":"Lower consumer disruption but higher provider maintenance","short_compatibility":"Lower maintenance but greater migration pressure"},"decision_factors":["Consumer diversity","Criticality","Release cadence","Migration cost","Contract stability"],"failure_modes":["Permanent deprecated versions","Unowned migrations","Breaking changes disguised as additive changes","Inconsistent compatibility rules"],"security_implication":"Deprecated versions can retain vulnerable behavior if they remain exposed longer than necessary.","performance_implication":"Supporting multiple contract versions can increase routing, transformation, testing, and operational overhead.","testing_guidance":["Test version coexistence","Test deprecation paths","Test migration compatibility","Test retirement behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify every supported contract version has an owner, compatibility definition, migration path, and explicit retirement condition.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["API lifecycle engineering practices","Microsoft API design guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B43-R001","D41-B43-R004"],"relationships":["governs:D41-B43-R001","requires:D41-B43-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B44-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Object storage versus filesystem storage","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What architectural factors distinguish object storage from filesystem storage?","answer":"Object storage organizes data as objects addressed through an object API and is generally suited to large unstructured datasets, durable blobs, and independently accessed objects. Filesystems provide directory and file semantics useful when applications require hierarchical namespace behavior, file locking, POSIX-like operations, or filesystem-oriented tooling.","principle":"Choose storage semantics according to the application's access model rather than treating storage capacity as the primary criterion.","requirements":["Define access semantics","Define consistency needs","Define metadata requirements","Define file-operation requirements","Define lifecycle requirements"],"constraints":["Object storage does not provide all filesystem semantics","Filesystem storage may require more tightly coupled infrastructure","Migration between models can require application changes"],"preconditions":["Data access patterns are known","Object/file semantics are explicit","Durability requirements are defined"],"objective":"Select storage semantics compatible with application behavior.","alternatives":["Object storage","Distributed filesystem","Local filesystem","Network filesystem"],"tradeoff":{"object_storage":["Scalable object model","Lifecycle features","API-based access","Different semantics"],"filesystem":["File semantics","Directory operations","Compatibility with filesystem-oriented applications","Potential infrastructure coupling"]},"decision_factors":["Access pattern","Namespace requirements","File locking","Object size","Lifecycle","Tool compatibility"],"failure_modes":["Assuming object storage behaves like a filesystem","Excessive metadata operations","Filesystem dependency becoming a deployment constraint"],"security_implication":"Storage authorization should be based on explicit identities and resource boundaries rather than relying only on path secrecy.","performance_implication":"Performance depends heavily on access pattern, object size, locality, metadata operations, and network path; architecture-specific measurement is required.","testing_guidance":["Test representative access patterns","Test concurrent access","Test large-object behavior","Test failure and retry behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify that all required application storage operations are supported with the selected storage semantics.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Cloud storage architecture guidance","Distributed storage engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B33-R001","D41-B44-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B44-R002","requires:D41-B44-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B44-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Local storage versus remote shared storage","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should architecture prefer local storage over remote shared storage?","answer":"Local storage is useful when low-latency access, temporary state, cache-like workloads, or node-local processing is important and data loss can be tolerated or independently recovered. Remote shared storage is appropriate when data must survive node replacement, be shared across workers, or remain available independently of a compute instance.","principle":"Align storage locality with data durability, sharing, recovery, and latency requirements.","requirements":["Define durability requirement","Define sharing requirement","Define recovery behavior","Define latency requirements"],"constraints":["Local storage can disappear with the compute resource","Remote storage adds network dependency","Shared storage can become a contention point"],"preconditions":["Data durability classification exists","Failure model is defined","Access locality is understood"],"objective":"Choose a storage location that satisfies durability and access requirements without unnecessary coupling.","alternatives":["Local ephemeral storage","Local durable storage","Remote shared filesystem","Object storage","Distributed block storage"],"tradeoff":{"local":["Low locality cost","Potentially lower latency","Limited durability and sharing"],"remote":["Independent durability","Shared access","Network dependency and potential latency"]},"decision_factors":["Durability","Latency","Sharing","Failure domain","Recovery","Cost"],"failure_modes":["Unexpected data loss","Network dependency outage","Storage contention","Inconsistent local replicas"],"security_implication":"Local and remote storage require equivalent data-classification and access-control analysis; locality does not inherently make data safer.","performance_implication":"Local access can reduce network overhead, while remote storage can provide scalable capacity and shared access.","testing_guidance":["Test node loss","Test network partition","Test recovery","Test concurrent access","Measure representative latency"],"verification_method":"Verify that data remains available or recoverable across the failure scenarios defined for its durability class.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Cloud architecture guidance","Distributed systems engineering principles"],"related_records":["D41-B44-R001","D41-B34-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B44-R001","requires:D41-B44-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B44-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Storage abstraction versus native storage API","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When is a storage abstraction layer justified instead of using a provider-native storage API directly?","answer":"An abstraction layer is justified when the application genuinely needs interchangeable storage implementations, stable domain-level semantics, isolation from provider-specific behavior, or controlled migration. It can become harmful when it hides important storage semantics, prevents use of required capabilities, or creates a lowest-common-denominator API.","principle":"Abstract stable domain semantics, not every underlying storage capability.","requirements":["Identify stable application semantics","Define provider-specific features","Define portability requirement","Define abstraction ownership"],"constraints":["Abstraction adds maintenance cost","Provider-specific features may be inaccessible","Incorrect abstractions can hide failure semantics"],"preconditions":["Portability or semantic isolation has a concrete requirement","Storage behaviors are understood","Abstraction boundary is testable"],"objective":"Avoid unnecessary coupling without concealing important storage behavior.","alternatives":["Direct provider API","Thin adapter","Domain storage interface","Multi-provider abstraction"],"tradeoff":{"abstraction":["Portability","Testability","Provider isolation","Potential semantic loss"],"direct":["Full provider capabilities","Less abstraction code","Greater provider coupling"]},"decision_factors":["Portability requirement","Provider feature usage","Migration probability","Semantic stability","Maintenance cost"],"failure_modes":["Leaky abstraction","Lowest-common-denominator design","Hidden consistency differences","Difficult debugging"],"security_implication":"An abstraction must preserve security-relevant semantics such as authorization scope, encryption behavior, and object ownership.","performance_implication":"Extra translation layers can add overhead, but the actual impact depends on implementation and workload; it should be measured where material.","testing_guidance":["Test provider-specific behavior","Test abstraction contract","Test failure semantics","Test migration implementation if portability is required"],"verification_method":"Verify that the abstraction preserves every storage behavior required by the application and intentionally excludes only non-required provider-specific behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.94,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Software architecture design principles","API abstraction engineering patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B44-R001","D41-B44-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B44-R004","requires:D41-B44-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B44-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Storage consistency versus availability behavior","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should storage architecture reason about consistency and availability under failure?","answer":"The architecture should explicitly define which operations require authoritative current state and which can tolerate stale or temporarily unavailable data. Stronger consistency can constrain availability or partition tolerance depending on the storage system, while weaker consistency can improve availability and scale for suitable workloads but requires application-level handling of stale state.","principle":"Assign consistency requirements per data operation rather than choosing one consistency model for every dataset.","requirements":["Define authoritative operations","Define acceptable staleness","Define conflict behavior","Define failure semantics"],"constraints":["Consistency guarantees are system-specific","Eventual consistency can create temporary divergence","Strong consistency can increase coordination cost"],"preconditions":["Data correctness requirements are explicit","Conflict semantics are understood","Failure modes are documented"],"objective":"Match storage consistency behavior to business correctness requirements.","alternatives":["Strong consistency","Session consistency","Bounded staleness","Eventual consistency","Application-managed reconciliation"],"tradeoff":{"strong":["Current authoritative state","Potential coordination overhead"],"weak":["Availability and scalability opportunities","Stale or conflicting state handling"]},"decision_factors":["Business correctness","Read/write pattern","Conflict frequency","Latency","Partition behavior"],"failure_modes":["Stale decision data","Lost updates","Conflict amplification","Incorrect assumption of immediate visibility"],"security_implication":"Authorization decisions should not depend on stale state when stale state could grant access incorrectly.","performance_implication":"Consistency coordination can increase latency or reduce concurrency depending on implementation.","testing_guidance":["Test stale reads","Test concurrent writes","Test partition behavior","Test reconciliation","Test authorization-sensitive state"],"verification_method":"Map every critical operation to a documented consistency requirement and verify observed behavior under normal and degraded conditions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed systems literature","Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"related_records":["D41-B09-R001","D41-B44-R005"],"relationships":["refines:D41-B09-R001","requires:D41-B44-R005","tested_by:D41-B44-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B44-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B44","topic":"Storage Architecture","subtopic":"Storage Architecture","concept":"Storage failure and recovery validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified before selecting a storage architecture?","answer":"Verify data durability assumptions, failure-domain behavior, backup or replication behavior, recovery procedures, consistency semantics, access controls, and operational observability. Storage architecture is incomplete if it describes normal reads and writes without defining what happens when the storage system or its dependency fails.","principle":"A storage architecture must define both data-path behavior and data-recovery behavior.","requirements":["Failure model","Recovery objectives","Data integrity checks","Backup or replication strategy","Access-control model","Observability"],"constraints":["Recovery results depend on actual configuration","Replication is not equivalent to backup","Testing recovery may require isolated environments"],"preconditions":["Data classification exists","Recovery objectives are documented","Storage dependencies are identified"],"objective":"Ensure storage behavior remains within documented durability and recovery requirements.","alternatives":["Replication","Backup and restore","Snapshots","Application-level reconstruction","Hybrid recovery"],"tradeoff":{"replication":"Fast availability recovery but can replicate corruption or deletion","backup":"Independent recovery point but restoration takes time","reconstruction":"Potentially lower storage dependency but higher application complexity"},"decision_factors":["RPO","RTO","Corruption risk","Deletion risk","Recovery complexity","Data criticality"],"failure_modes":["Unrecoverable corruption","Unverified backups","Replica propagation of deletion","Recovery procedure drift"],"security_implication":"Backup and recovery paths must preserve authorization, encryption, and data-classification controls.","performance_implication":"Replication, integrity checking, and backup operations consume storage and network resources and should be scheduled or isolated appropriately.","testing_guidance":["Perform restore tests","Test corrupted data","Test storage outage","Test credential loss","Verify integrity after restoration"],"verification_method":"Execute controlled recovery tests and compare restored data and service behavior against defined RPO, RTO, integrity, and authorization requirements.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST backup and recovery guidance","Google SRE reliability practices"],"related_records":["D41-B34-R001","D41-B44-R004"],"relationships":["verifies:D41-B44-R004","requires:D41-B34-R001","tests:D41-B44-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B45-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Identity Architecture","concept":"Central identity provider versus federated identity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the trade-offs between centralized identity and federated identity architecture?","answer":"Centralized identity can simplify identity lifecycle, policy enforcement, and application integration within a controlled environment. Federation allows identities and trust relationships to span organizational or administrative boundaries but introduces trust configuration, protocol, lifecycle, and interoperability complexity.","principle":"Choose identity topology according to administrative boundaries and trust relationships rather than organizational preference alone.","requirements":["Define identity ownership","Define trust relationships","Define authentication protocols","Define lifecycle responsibilities","Define failure behavior"],"constraints":["Central identity can become a dependency","Federation requires trust management","Multiple identity authorities complicate lifecycle governance"],"preconditions":["Identity ownership is known","Trust boundaries are documented","Authentication requirements are explicit"],"objective":"Provide reliable identity management across the system's administrative boundaries.","alternatives":["Central identity authority","Federated identity","Hybrid identity","Local service identities"],"tradeoff":{"centralized":["Simpler governance","Consistent lifecycle","Central dependency"],"federated":["Cross-domain interoperability","Administrative autonomy","Higher trust-management complexity"]},"decision_factors":["Administrative boundaries","User population","Trust model","Availability","Compliance","Interoperability"],"failure_modes":["Identity-provider outage","Stale federation trust","Incorrect claim mapping","Orphaned identities"],"security_implication":"Federated trust must validate issuer, audience, claims, lifetime, and authorization mapping according to the established trust model.","performance_implication":"Centralized authentication can create traffic concentration; federation can introduce additional protocol exchanges and external dependencies.","testing_guidance":["Test identity-provider failure","Test expired credentials","Test claim mapping","Test trust revocation","Test lifecycle events"],"verification_method":"Verify that every identity source has an explicit owner, trust relationship, lifecycle policy, and failure behavior.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST Digital Identity Guidelines","OpenID Connect specifications","OAuth specifications"],"related_records":["D41-B35-R002","D41-B45-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B45-R002","requires:D41-B45-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B45-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Identity Architecture","concept":"Human identity versus workload identity","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should architectures distinguish human identities from workload identities?","answer":"Human identities represent people and often require interactive authentication, lifecycle management, and user-oriented authorization. Workload identities represent software components and require automated issuance, rotation, non-interactive authentication, and service-to-service authorization. Treating workloads as human accounts can create inappropriate credential handling and lifecycle risks.","principle":"Use identity mechanisms whose lifecycle and assurance model match the identity type.","requirements":["Identify identity type","Define credential lifecycle","Define authorization scope","Define rotation behavior"],"constraints":["Human and workload identity systems may need integration","Workload identities must operate without interactive credentials","Identity propagation across services requires explicit design"],"preconditions":["Identity categories are defined","Service communication paths are known","Authorization model exists"],"objective":"Prevent identity semantics from becoming ambiguous across human and machine actors.","alternatives":["Human identity provider","Workload identity system","Unified identity plane with separate identity classes"],"tradeoff":{"human_identity":["User lifecycle","Interactive authentication","Human governance"],"workload_identity":["Automation","Short-lived credentials","Service lifecycle"]},"decision_factors":["Actor type","Credential lifetime","Automation","Authorization scope","Auditability"],"failure_modes":["Shared service accounts","Long-lived machine secrets","Human credentials embedded in automation","Incorrect identity propagation"],"security_implication":"Workload identity should minimize long-lived shared credentials and preserve explicit service identity and authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Short-lived credential issuance and validation can add control-plane operations; caching and token validation must respect security requirements.","testing_guidance":["Test identity rotation","Test service impersonation","Test expired credentials","Test authorization scope"],"verification_method":"Trace representative actions to a unique actor identity and verify that credential lifecycle and authorization match the actor class.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST Digital Identity Guidelines","NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture"],"related_records":["D41-B35-R002","D41-B45-R001"],"relationships":["refines:D41-B35-R002","contrasts_with:D41-B45-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B45-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Identity Architecture","concept":"Long-lived credentials versus short-lived credentials","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why are short-lived credentials often preferred for workload identity?","answer":"Short-lived credentials reduce the exposure window if a credential is compromised and can better align credential validity with workload lifecycle. They require reliable issuance, renewal, clock handling, and failure recovery. Long-lived credentials simplify some operational paths but increase the potential impact of credential exposure.","principle":"Minimize credential lifetime to the shortest period compatible with reliable system operation.","requirements":["Credential issuance","Renewal mechanism","Revocation or expiry behavior","Clock synchronization where applicable","Failure recovery"],"constraints":["Short lifetimes increase dependence on identity infrastructure","Renewal failures can interrupt workloads","Revocation semantics differ by credential type"],"preconditions":["Automated identity lifecycle exists","Workloads can renew credentials","Failure behavior is defined"],"objective":"Reduce credential exposure while preserving reliable operation.","alternatives":["Long-lived secret","Short-lived token","Certificate-based identity","Workload identity federation"],"tradeoff":{"short_lived":["Reduced exposure window","Higher lifecycle complexity"],"long_lived":["Operational simplicity","Greater exposure duration"]},"decision_factors":["Credential compromise impact","Renewal reliability","Identity infrastructure availability","Workload duration"],"failure_modes":["Credential expiration during outage","Renewal storm","Clock skew","Credential leakage"],"security_implication":"Short-lived credentials reduce exposure duration but do not prevent compromise during their valid lifetime.","performance_implication":"Frequent issuance or renewal can increase identity-service load and should be engineered with bounded renewal behavior.","testing_guidance":["Test renewal failure","Test expiry during dependency outage","Test clock skew","Test concurrent renewal"],"verification_method":"Verify credential lifetime, renewal behavior, failure handling, and authorization scope against documented identity requirements.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST Digital Identity Guidelines","NIST SP 800-57"],"related_records":["D41-B45-R002","D41-B45-R004"],"relationships":["refines:D41-B45-R002","tested_by:D41-B45-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B45-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Identity Architecture","subtopic":"Identity Architecture","concept":"Identity failure-domain architecture","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should identity architecture handle identity-provider failure?","answer":"Critical systems should define behavior for identity-provider unavailability, including cached validation where appropriate, existing-session behavior, emergency access, dependency isolation, and explicit fail-closed or bounded fail-open decisions. The correct behavior depends on the security impact of granting access without current identity evidence.","principle":"Identity availability and authorization safety must be designed together rather than treating identity as an infinitely available dependency.","requirements":["Identity dependency map","Failure behavior","Emergency access policy","Credential validation policy","Audit requirements"],"constraints":["Fail-open behavior can weaken security","Fail-closed behavior can interrupt legitimate operations","Caching introduces staleness"],"preconditions":["Identity dependencies are known","Protected operations are classified","Failure policies are explicit"],"objective":"Maintain controlled operation during identity-service degradation.","alternatives":["Fail closed","Bounded cached validation","Emergency break-glass access","Limited degraded mode"],"tradeoff":{"fail_closed":"Stronger authorization assurance but possible service interruption","cached":"Greater availability with bounded staleness","emergency_access":"Operational continuity with high governance requirements"},"decision_factors":["Operation criticality","Credential validity","Staleness tolerance","Security impact","Recovery time"],"failure_modes":["Unauthorized fail-open access","Total operational lockout","Uncontrolled emergency access","Stale authorization"],"security_implication":"Any degraded identity mode must have explicitly bounded authority and strong audit controls.","performance_implication":"Local validation or caching can reduce dependency latency but introduces lifecycle and freshness management.","testing_guidance":["Disable identity provider","Test cached validation","Test emergency access","Test recovery","Verify audit trail"],"verification_method":"Execute controlled identity-dependency failure tests and verify access behavior against the documented security policy.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST Digital Identity Guidelines","Zero trust engineering patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B45-R001","D41-B45-R003"],"relationships":["depends_on:D41-B45-R001","tested_by:D41-B45-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B45-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B45","topic":"Identity Architecture","subtopic":"Identity Architecture","concept":"Identity architecture verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified in an identity architecture review?","answer":"Verify actor identity types, authentication assurance, authorization boundaries, credential lifecycle, trust relationships, failure behavior, auditability, and administrative ownership. Identity architecture should be traceable from actor to credential to authorization decision to protected resource.","principle":"Every protected action should have an attributable identity and an explicit authorization decision.","requirements":["Identity inventory","Authentication model","Authorization model","Credential lifecycle","Audit trail","Failure policy"],"constraints":["Identity assurance differs by actor and operation","Distributed identity creates propagation complexity","Emergency access requires special governance"],"preconditions":["Protected resources are identified","Actors are classified","Authorization rules exist"],"objective":"Establish complete and auditable identity control.","alternatives":["Architecture review","Threat modeling","Identity flow testing","Access-control testing"],"tradeoff":{"central_review":"Broad coverage but may miss implementation-specific behavior","runtime_testing":"Concrete validation but requires representative environments"},"decision_factors":["Coverage","Assurance level","Protected-resource criticality","Failure behavior","Audit completeness"],"failure_modes":["Unattributed actions","Overprivileged identities","Credential reuse","Missing lifecycle controls","Unlogged administrative access"],"security_implication":"Identity verification is foundational to authentication, authorization, auditability, and least-privilege enforcement.","performance_implication":"Identity validation can affect request latency; caching and token validation strategies should be evaluated without weakening security guarantees.","testing_guidance":["Trace end-to-end identity flows","Test authorization boundaries","Test credential lifecycle","Test failure modes","Review audit events"],"verification_method":"Trace representative protected operations from actor authentication through authorization and audit recording, including failure cases.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST Digital Identity Guidelines","NIST SP 800-207"],"related_records":["D41-B45-R002","D41-B45-R004"],"relationships":["verifies:D41-B45-R002","verifies:D41-B45-R003","verifies:D41-B45-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B46-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Shared-state concurrency versus message-passing concurrency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the architectural trade-offs between shared-state concurrency and message-passing concurrency?","answer":"Shared-state concurrency allows workers to access common state directly but requires synchronization and careful ownership rules. Message-passing concurrency isolates state more strongly and can make ownership explicit, but introduces queues, serialization, delivery semantics, and potentially higher coordination latency.","principle":"Choose concurrency semantics according to state ownership, synchronization requirements, throughput characteristics, and failure behavior.","requirements":["Define state ownership","Define synchronization rules","Define delivery semantics","Define concurrency limits"],"constraints":["Shared state requires synchronization","Message passing introduces communication overhead","Neither model automatically prevents race conditions or logical conflicts"],"preconditions":["Concurrency model is explicit","Mutable state is identified","Failure behavior is defined"],"objective":"Control concurrent state changes without introducing unnecessary coordination complexity.","alternatives":["Shared memory","Actor/message passing","Task queues","Immutable data with message passing"],"tradeoff":{"shared_state":["Direct access","Potentially lower communication overhead","Synchronization complexity"],"message_passing":["Explicit ownership","Isolation","Serialization and delivery complexity"]},"decision_factors":["State sharing","Mutation frequency","Synchronization cost","Failure isolation","Serialization cost"],"failure_modes":["Race conditions","Deadlocks","Lost messages","Duplicate processing","State divergence"],"security_implication":"Shared mutable state can create unintended cross-request or cross-tenant data exposure if ownership boundaries are weak.","performance_implication":"Shared memory can avoid serialization overhead, while message passing can improve isolation but adds communication and serialization costs.","testing_guidance":["Stress concurrent access","Test race conditions","Test duplicate delivery","Test ordering assumptions","Test failure recovery"],"verification_method":"Verify that every mutable state transition has an explicit owner and concurrency-control mechanism.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Concurrent programming principles","Distributed systems engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B46-R002","D41-B46-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B46-R002","requires:D41-B46-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B46-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Optimistic versus pessimistic concurrency control","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should optimistic concurrency control be preferred over pessimistic locking?","answer":"Optimistic concurrency is appropriate when conflicts are relatively infrequent and operations can detect and retry or reconcile conflicts. Pessimistic locking is appropriate when conflicts are costly and preventing concurrent modification is more important than maximizing concurrency. The decision depends on contention, operation duration, correctness requirements, and recovery cost.","principle":"Use optimistic control when conflicts are acceptable and recoverable; use pessimistic control when conflict prevention materially simplifies correctness.","requirements":["Conflict model","Retry behavior","Ownership rules","Transaction boundaries"],"constraints":["Optimistic retries can amplify load under high contention","Pessimistic locks can reduce concurrency and create deadlocks","Long-held locks increase resource contention"],"preconditions":["Conflict semantics are understood","Correctness requirements are explicit","Retry behavior is bounded"],"objective":"Maintain correctness under concurrent updates with appropriate resource utilization.","alternatives":["Version checks","Compare-and-swap","Pessimistic locks","Serialized processing"],"tradeoff":{"optimistic":["High concurrency under low conflict","Retry complexity"],"pessimistic":["Conflict prevention","Lock contention and deadlock risk"]},"decision_factors":["Conflict frequency","Operation duration","Retry cost","Data criticality","Contention"],"failure_modes":["Retry storms","Lost updates","Deadlocks","Lock starvation","Stale versions"],"security_implication":"Concurrency controls must protect authorization-sensitive state from stale updates or time-of-check/time-of-use races.","performance_implication":"Optimistic control can perform well under low contention; high conflict rates can cause repeated work and increased latency.","testing_guidance":["Test high contention","Test concurrent updates","Test retry limits","Test lock timeout","Test deadlock recovery"],"verification_method":"Verify that concurrent updates either serialize correctly or produce an explicit conflict outcome without silent data loss.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Database concurrency-control literature","Distributed systems engineering principles"],"related_records":["D41-B46-R001","D41-B46-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B46-R001","tested_by:D41-B46-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B46-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Concurrency boundary selection","knowledge_type":"DECISION_RULE","question":"How should architecture choose the boundary at which concurrent work is serialized?","answer":"Serialize only the smallest critical section required to preserve correctness. Broader serialization can simplify reasoning but reduces concurrency and may create bottlenecks. The boundary should correspond to the actual invariant being protected rather than the entire workflow whenever possible.","principle":"Protect invariants, not entire workflows, with serialization.","requirements":["Identify invariants","Identify conflicting operations","Define transaction or lock scope","Define recovery behavior"],"constraints":["Narrow boundaries require careful invariant analysis","Broad boundaries reduce concurrency","Distributed serialization introduces coordination dependencies"],"preconditions":["Invariants are explicit","Mutation paths are known","Concurrency behavior is testable"],"objective":"Preserve correctness while retaining safe concurrency.","alternatives":["Global serialization","Partition-level serialization","Entity-level serialization","Optimistic conflict detection"],"tradeoff":{"broad":"Simpler reasoning, lower concurrency","narrow":"Higher concurrency, greater reasoning complexity"},"decision_factors":["Invariant scope","Conflict frequency","Throughput","Operation duration","Failure behavior"],"failure_modes":["Over-serialization","Under-protection of invariants","Deadlocks","Cross-partition inconsistency"],"security_implication":"Security-critical invariants must not be split across independently committed operations without a defined correctness strategy.","performance_implication":"Serialization scope directly affects concurrency and throughput; narrow scopes can improve throughput when correctly designed.","testing_guidance":["Test concurrent invariant updates","Test partition boundaries","Test failure during serialized operation","Measure contention"],"verification_method":"Identify each protected invariant and verify that all mutation paths use a compatible concurrency-control boundary.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Concurrency engineering principles","Database transaction design literature"],"related_records":["D41-B46-R002","D41-B46-R004"],"relationships":["refines:D41-B46-R002","requires:D41-B46-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B46-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Concurrency stress validation","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should concurrency architecture be validated?","answer":"Concurrency validation should exercise simultaneous reads and writes, contention, delayed operations, retries, partial failures, duplicate execution, and recovery. Tests should verify invariants and final state rather than only measuring throughput.","principle":"Concurrency correctness must be evaluated by invariant preservation under interleaving, not merely by successful individual operations.","requirements":["Concurrency scenarios","Known invariants","Expected final state","Failure injection","Repeatability"],"constraints":["Race conditions can be nondeterministic","High-concurrency tests may be environment-sensitive","Reproducing distributed interleavings can be difficult"],"preconditions":["Protected invariants are defined","Operations are independently testable","Failure cases are identified"],"objective":"Detect race conditions, lost updates, deadlocks, and incorrect conflict handling.","alternatives":["Stress tests","Deterministic scheduler tests","Property-based tests","Fault injection"],"tradeoff":{"stress":"Broad runtime coverage but nondeterministic","deterministic":"Reproducible but potentially narrower"},"decision_factors":["Concurrency level","Criticality","Reproducibility","Failure coverage"],"failure_modes":["Race condition","Deadlock","Lost update","Duplicate side effect","Starvation"],"security_implication":"Concurrency tests should include security-sensitive state transitions such as authorization changes or resource ownership changes.","performance_implication":"Measure contention, queueing, lock duration, retries, and throughput under representative concurrency rather than relying on single-thread measurements.","testing_guidance":["Run repeated concurrency stress","Inject delays","Test conflicting updates","Test retries","Verify final invariants"],"verification_method":"A concurrency design passes validation only when defined invariants remain true across representative interleavings and failure scenarios.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Concurrency testing practices","Distributed systems verification methods"],"related_records":["D41-B46-R002","D41-B46-R003"],"relationships":["tests:D41-B46-R002","tests:D41-B46-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B46-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B46","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Concurrency Architecture","concept":"Idempotency as concurrency control","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can idempotency reduce the architectural impact of retries and duplicate execution?","answer":"Idempotency allows repeated processing of the same logical operation to converge on the same intended result or an explicitly equivalent outcome. It is especially useful when retries, duplicate messages, client retransmission, or uncertain completion can occur. Idempotency requires a well-defined operation identity and appropriate state retention.","principle":"Design retryable side effects so repeated execution cannot silently produce unintended additional effects.","requirements":["Stable operation identity","Duplicate detection","Result semantics","Retention policy","Atomicity between deduplication and side effect"],"constraints":["Idempotency state consumes storage","Keys require correct scope","Not every side effect can be made inherently idempotent"],"preconditions":["Duplicate execution is possible","Operation semantics are definable","State can be retained sufficiently"],"objective":"Prevent duplicate execution from creating unintended repeated effects.","alternatives":["Idempotency key","Natural idempotent operation","Transactional deduplication","Single-writer serialization"],"tradeoff":{"benefit":"Safer retries and recovery","cost":"Additional state and lifecycle management"},"decision_factors":["Retry probability","Side-effect criticality","Deduplication scope","Retention duration","Atomicity"],"failure_modes":["Key collision","Expired deduplication state","Side effect before deduplication commit","Incorrect scope"],"security_implication":"Idempotency keys must not substitute for authorization and must not allow one principal to replay another principal's operation.","performance_implication":"Deduplication lookups add work but can reduce expensive duplicate downstream operations.","testing_guidance":["Replay identical requests","Replay after partial failure","Test concurrent duplicate submissions","Test expired keys","Test cross-principal key reuse"],"verification_method":"Verify that repeated authorized submissions with the same operation identity produce the documented single logical outcome.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed systems reliability patterns","API reliability engineering practices"],"related_records":["D41-B43-R002","D41-B46-R002"],"relationships":["mitigates:D41-B43-R002","mitigates:D41-B46-R002","tested_by:D41-B46-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B47-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Static configuration versus dynamic configuration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the trade-offs between static and dynamic configuration?","answer":"Static configuration is simpler to reason about and can be versioned with deployments, while dynamic configuration can change behavior without redeployment and can support operational responsiveness. Dynamic configuration introduces runtime dependency, propagation, validation, rollback, and consistency concerns.","principle":"Use dynamic configuration only when runtime change provides material operational or business value that justifies the additional control-plane complexity.","requirements":["Configuration ownership","Validation rules","Change authorization","Rollback behavior","Propagation semantics"],"constraints":["Dynamic configuration can introduce runtime failure modes","Static configuration requires deployment for changes","Configuration values can become an undocumented control plane"],"preconditions":["Configuration classes are identified","Change frequency is known","Safe defaults exist"],"objective":"Balance operational flexibility against runtime complexity and change risk.","alternatives":["Build-time configuration","Deployment-time configuration","Runtime configuration service","Hybrid configuration"],"tradeoff":{"static":["Predictability","Version control","Deployment dependency"],"dynamic":["Operational flexibility","Runtime dependency","Propagation complexity"]},"decision_factors":["Change frequency","Criticality","Rollback needs","Runtime dependency","Auditability"],"failure_modes":["Invalid runtime configuration","Partial propagation","Configuration drift","Untracked changes"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive configuration requires authorization, auditability, validation, and protection against unauthorized runtime modification.","performance_implication":"Dynamic configuration retrieval can introduce startup or runtime dependency; local caching must have explicit freshness semantics.","testing_guidance":["Test invalid configuration","Test propagation delay","Test rollback","Test configuration-service outage","Test authorization"],"verification_method":"Verify configuration changes are validated, authorized, observable, reversible, and bounded in scope.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Cloud architecture configuration practices","NIST configuration management guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B47-R002","D41-B47-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B47-R002","requires:D41-B47-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B47-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Configuration service versus environment configuration","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should configuration be provided through a centralized configuration service rather than deployment environment configuration?","answer":"A configuration service is useful when many workloads require coordinated runtime configuration, centralized governance, dynamic changes, or common configuration lifecycle management. Environment configuration is simpler when configuration is deployment-scoped, changes infrequently, and does not justify a runtime dependency.","principle":"Centralize configuration only when its shared lifecycle or governance provides concrete value.","requirements":["Configuration ownership","Scope model","Availability requirements","Change auditability","Secret separation"],"constraints":["A configuration service becomes another dependency","Environment configuration may drift across deployments","Secrets require stronger handling than ordinary configuration"],"preconditions":["Configuration classes are separated","Runtime dependency tolerance is known","Ownership is defined"],"objective":"Select an appropriate configuration distribution mechanism.","alternatives":["Environment variables","Configuration files","Configuration service","Configuration embedded in deployment manifests"],"tradeoff":{"service":["Central governance","Dynamic updates","Runtime dependency"],"environment":["Simplicity","Deployment coupling","Lower runtime dependency"]},"decision_factors":["Change frequency","Number of consumers","Consistency requirement","Availability","Governance"],"failure_modes":["Configuration service outage","Stale configuration","Cross-environment leakage","Uncontrolled shared changes"],"security_implication":"Secrets should not be treated as ordinary configuration merely because both are externally supplied values.","performance_implication":"Central configuration retrieval can add startup or runtime latency and should generally avoid becoming a hot-path dependency without explicit design.","testing_guidance":["Test configuration outage","Test stale values","Test environment isolation","Test unauthorized configuration changes"],"verification_method":"Verify that configuration scope, lifecycle, availability, and authorization match the selected distribution mechanism.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Cloud configuration architecture practices","NIST configuration management guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B47-R001","D41-B47-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B47-R001","requires:D41-B47-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B47-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Configuration rollout strategy","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should high-impact configuration changes be rolled out architecturally?","answer":"High-impact configuration changes should be validated, versioned, authorized, introduced progressively where feasible, monitored for expected effects, and reversible. Progressive rollout can reduce blast radius, but it requires explicit targeting and rollback semantics.","principle":"Treat important configuration changes as controlled releases rather than instantaneous edits.","requirements":["Versioned configuration","Validation","Authorization","Progressive targeting","Rollback","Monitoring"],"constraints":["Progressive rollout adds operational complexity","Some configuration cannot be safely staged","Rollback may not undo external side effects"],"preconditions":["Configuration can be versioned","Targeting mechanism exists","Success criteria are defined"],"objective":"Reduce the blast radius of configuration changes.","alternatives":["Immediate rollout","Canary configuration","Percentage rollout","Environment-by-environment rollout"],"tradeoff":{"progressive":["Reduced blast radius","More operational complexity"],"immediate":["Simple","Higher change impact if incorrect"]},"decision_factors":["Change criticality","Rollback capability","Targeting support","Blast radius","Observability"],"failure_modes":["Partial rollout inconsistency","Rollback failure","Incorrect targeting","Configuration drift"],"security_implication":"Configuration targeting must not accidentally expose privileged settings to unauthorized workloads or tenants.","performance_implication":"Configuration changes affecting resource limits or routing can alter system load and should be monitored during rollout.","testing_guidance":["Validate before rollout","Test canary behavior","Test rollback","Compare affected and unaffected populations"],"verification_method":"Verify that rollout state, configuration version, target scope, observed behavior, and rollback state are all recorded.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Progressive delivery engineering practices","Google SRE change management principles"],"related_records":["D41-B47-R001","D41-B47-R004"],"relationships":["implements:D41-B47-R001","tested_by:D41-B47-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B47-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Configuration versus secret separation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should secrets be architecturally separated from ordinary configuration?","answer":"Secrets require stronger confidentiality, access control, rotation, exposure prevention, and audit requirements than ordinary configuration. Treating them as ordinary configuration can cause them to appear in source control, logs, deployment metadata, diagnostics, or broad configuration interfaces.","principle":"Model secret material as a distinct security-sensitive resource with its own lifecycle and access policy.","requirements":["Secret classification","Access control","Rotation","Auditability","Exposure prevention"],"constraints":["Secret-management infrastructure adds operational dependencies","Applications must handle secret retrieval and renewal correctly","Secret rotation can affect running workloads"],"preconditions":["Sensitive values are identified","Secret ownership is defined","Access paths are known"],"objective":"Prevent configuration mechanisms from becoming uncontrolled secret-distribution channels.","alternatives":["Dedicated secret manager","Workload identity","Encrypted configuration","Environment-injected secrets with controlled lifecycle"],"tradeoff":{"dedicated":["Strong lifecycle and access controls","Additional infrastructure"],"generic_config":["Simpler integration","Higher exposure risk"]},"decision_factors":["Secret sensitivity","Rotation frequency","Workload count","Audit requirements","Failure behavior"],"failure_modes":["Secret leakage in logs","Long-lived credentials","Overbroad access","Uncontrolled copies"],"security_implication":"Secret storage and retrieval should enforce least privilege and minimize plaintext exposure.","performance_implication":"Secret retrieval can add startup or renewal latency; caching should preserve required rotation and revocation semantics.","testing_guidance":["Scan logs and artifacts","Test rotation","Test unauthorized retrieval","Test secret-manager outage","Verify no plaintext persistence"],"verification_method":"Trace secret lifecycle from creation through retrieval, use, rotation, and destruction and verify each transition against policy.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","NIST secure configuration guidance","OWASP Secrets Management guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B47-R001","D41-B45-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B47-R002","requires:D41-B47-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B47-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B47","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Configuration architecture verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified before configuration architecture is considered complete?","answer":"Verify configuration ownership, scope, validation, authorization, propagation, versioning, rollback, auditability, failure behavior, and secret separation. Every runtime-configurable value should have an explicit lifecycle and safe behavior for invalid or unavailable state.","principle":"Configuration is part of the control plane and must be governed with the same rigor as other operational state.","requirements":["Configuration inventory","Lifecycle policy","Validation","Authorization","Audit trail","Failure behavior"],"constraints":["Dynamic configuration expands runtime state","Configuration dependencies can create hidden coupling","Some changes require application restart"],"preconditions":["Configuration sources are enumerated","Sensitive values are classified","Change ownership exists"],"objective":"Ensure configuration cannot silently undermine system correctness or security.","alternatives":["Static review","Configuration schema validation","Runtime tests","Change simulations"],"tradeoff":{"static":"Broad structural coverage","runtime":"Better validation of actual propagation and failure behavior"},"decision_factors":["Configuration criticality","Dynamic behavior","Security sensitivity","Blast radius"],"failure_modes":["Unvalidated values","Configuration drift","Silent propagation failure","Unauthorized changes"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive configuration must have explicit access, audit, and rollback controls.","performance_implication":"Configuration changes affecting performance parameters require measurement rather than assumption.","testing_guidance":["Validate schemas","Test propagation","Test invalid values","Test rollback","Test dependency failure"],"verification_method":"Trace each critical configuration value from source to consumer and verify validation, authorization, propagation, and failure behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST configuration management guidance","Google SRE change-management practices"],"related_records":["D41-B47-R001","D41-B47-R003","D41-B47-R004"],"relationships":["verifies:D41-B47-R001","verifies:D41-B47-R003","verifies:D41-B47-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B48-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Control Plane and Data Plane","concept":"Control-plane and data-plane separation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should architecture distinguish control-plane and data-plane responsibilities?","answer":"The control plane manages configuration, policy, topology, lifecycle, or orchestration, while the data plane performs the operational workload. Separating them can improve failure containment, scaling, and reasoning because data-plane operation need not always depend on synchronous control-plane availability.","principle":"Keep operational traffic and management control paths distinct when control-plane failure must not unnecessarily interrupt existing data-plane operation.","requirements":["Identify control functions","Identify operational paths","Define dependency behavior","Define state propagation"],"constraints":["Separation introduces synchronization and lifecycle complexity","Some systems legitimately require synchronous control decisions","Stale control state must be bounded"],"preconditions":["Control and operational responsibilities can be identified","Failure behavior is defined","State propagation requirements are known"],"objective":"Reduce unnecessary coupling between management operations and workload execution.","alternatives":["Fully coupled architecture","Separated control/data planes","Hierarchical control plane"],"tradeoff":{"separated":["Failure isolation","Independent scaling","More state propagation complexity"],"coupled":["Simpler implementation","Higher dependency coupling"]},"decision_factors":["Control-plane availability","Data-plane continuity","Latency","State freshness","Failure isolation"],"failure_modes":["Control-plane outage stopping data plane","Stale policy","Uncontrolled divergence","Control-plane overload"],"security_implication":"Security policies may require current control-plane decisions, so any cached or propagated policy must have explicit validity bounds.","performance_implication":"Separating planes can reduce synchronous control-path overhead but may introduce propagation latency.","testing_guidance":["Disable control plane","Test existing workload behavior","Test stale policy","Test recovery and reconciliation"],"verification_method":"Verify defined data-plane behavior for every documented control-plane failure state.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed systems architecture patterns","Network control/data plane architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B42-R001","D41-B48-R002"],"relationships":["requires:D41-B48-R004","contrasts_with:D41-B48-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B48-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Control Plane and Data Plane","concept":"Strong versus eventual control-state propagation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When can control-plane state be propagated asynchronously instead of synchronously?","answer":"Asynchronous propagation is suitable when bounded staleness is acceptable and the data plane can safely operate with the previous state during propagation. Synchronous control is more appropriate when stale policy could violate critical correctness or security invariants.","principle":"Allow control-state staleness only when its maximum impact is explicitly understood and acceptable.","requirements":["Maximum staleness bound","Policy validity period","Propagation mechanism","Failure behavior"],"constraints":["Asynchronous propagation can create temporary inconsistency","Synchronous coordination can increase latency and dependency coupling","Security-sensitive policy may have stricter freshness requirements"],"preconditions":["State freshness requirements are classified","Failure behavior is explicit","Propagation can be observed"],"objective":"Balance control consistency against operational independence and latency.","alternatives":["Synchronous decision","Asynchronous propagation","Versioned policy snapshots","Hybrid model"],"tradeoff":{"synchronous":["Fresh state","Higher dependency and latency"],"asynchronous":["Lower coupling","Potential stale state"]},"decision_factors":["Staleness impact","Security criticality","Latency","Control-plane availability","Propagation reliability"],"failure_modes":["Stale authorization","Policy divergence","Delayed revocation","Partial rollout"],"security_implication":"Authorization and security policy changes may require immediate enforcement or bounded revocation delay; this must be explicit rather than assumed.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous propagation can reduce control-path latency but may increase temporary state divergence.","testing_guidance":["Measure propagation delay","Test delayed updates","Test revocation","Test control-plane outage","Test reconciliation"],"verification_method":"Verify that maximum observed or bounded policy staleness remains within the requirement for each control category.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed systems consistency principles","Zero trust architecture patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B48-R001","D41-B48-R003"],"relationships":["refines:D41-B48-R001","contrasts_with:D41-B48-R003","tested_by:D41-B48-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B48-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Control Plane and Data Plane","concept":"Control-plane dependency minimization","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can architecture reduce unnecessary runtime dependency on the control plane?","answer":"The data plane can use validated local state, immutable configuration snapshots, cached policy with explicit expiration, or pre-provisioned resources when the security and correctness requirements permit. The objective is not to eliminate control-plane dependencies universally, but to prevent avoidable synchronous dependencies from turning management outages into workload outages.","principle":"Cache or pre-provision only state whose freshness and authority requirements are explicitly bounded.","requirements":["State validity period","Local state integrity","Update mechanism","Failure behavior"],"constraints":["Cached state can become stale","Local snapshots require secure distribution","Some decisions cannot safely use cached state"],"preconditions":["State freshness requirements are classified","Integrity protection exists","Reconciliation mechanism exists"],"objective":"Preserve operational continuity during control-plane degradation without violating policy requirements.","alternatives":["Synchronous lookup","Local validated cache","Immutable snapshot","Pre-provisioned configuration"],"tradeoff":{"local_state":["Resilience","Lower runtime dependency","Staleness management"],"synchronous":["Freshness","Higher dependency and latency"]},"decision_factors":["Freshness","Failure impact","Security criticality","Latency","Reconciliation"],"failure_modes":["Expired state used indefinitely","Tampered snapshot","Failed reconciliation","Revoked policy remaining active"],"security_implication":"Local control state must have integrity protection, explicit expiration, and bounded authority.","performance_implication":"Local state can reduce control-plane latency and load but consumes local memory/storage and introduces synchronization work.","testing_guidance":["Corrupt local state","Expire cached policy","Disable control plane","Test reconciliation","Test emergency revocation"],"verification_method":"Verify local state integrity, validity period, update mechanism, and behavior after expiration or control-plane failure.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed systems resilience patterns","NIST SP 800-207"],"related_records":["D41-B48-R001","D41-B48-R002"],"relationships":["implements:D41-B48-R001","contrasts_with:D41-B48-R002","tested_by:D41-B48-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B48-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Control Plane and Data Plane","concept":"Control-plane recovery and reconciliation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should happen when a control plane returns after an outage?","answer":"Recovery should reconcile authoritative control state with data-plane state, detect divergence, apply required updates safely, and record unresolved discrepancies. Recovery should not assume that all data-plane components remained synchronized during the outage.","principle":"Control-plane recovery must explicitly reconcile state rather than merely restoring service availability.","requirements":["Authoritative state","Versioning","Reconciliation algorithm","Conflict handling","Audit trail"],"constraints":["Data-plane state may have advanced independently","Some changes may no longer be applicable","Reconciliation can itself generate load"],"preconditions":["State versions are identifiable","Authority is defined","Divergence is detectable"],"objective":"Restore a consistent and governed state after control-plane failure.","alternatives":["Full replay","Snapshot reconciliation","Incremental synchronization","Manual reconciliation"],"tradeoff":{"automatic":["Fast recovery","Potential incorrect automated resolution"],"manual":["Higher control","Slower recovery and operational cost"]},"decision_factors":["State criticality","Divergence size","Recovery time","Conflict frequency","Automation confidence"],"failure_modes":["Lost updates","Stale policy","Duplicate application","Unresolved conflicts"],"security_implication":"Reconciliation must preserve authorization boundaries and should not automatically re-enable revoked access without current authority.","performance_implication":"Large reconciliation operations can create temporary load spikes and should be bounded or staged.","testing_guidance":["Create divergent state","Restore control plane","Run reconciliation","Inject conflicts","Verify final authoritative state"],"verification_method":"Compare pre-failure authoritative state, observed data-plane state, reconciliation actions, and final state against documented invariants.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed systems recovery patterns","Google SRE reliability practices"],"related_records":["D41-B48-R001","D41-B48-R003"],"relationships":["implements:D41-B48-R001","requires:D41-B48-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B48-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B48","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Control Plane and Data Plane","concept":"Control-plane independence verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should control-plane and data-plane separation be verified?","answer":"Verify behavior when the control plane is unavailable, delayed, inconsistent, partially restored, or issuing changed policy. The architecture should demonstrate bounded behavior rather than merely documenting separation.","principle":"Separation is meaningful only if control-plane failure produces the documented data-plane behavior.","requirements":["Failure scenarios","Expected degraded behavior","State validity rules","Recovery criteria"],"constraints":["Some control-plane functions may legitimately block operation","Security-sensitive changes may require immediate control interaction","Testing must reflect real dependency paths"],"preconditions":["Control/data dependencies are mapped","Failure behavior is documented","State validity is explicit"],"objective":"Validate operational independence and safe degradation.","alternatives":["Failure injection","Integration testing","Configuration analysis","Recovery exercises"],"tradeoff":{"runtime_testing":"Validates real behavior but requires controlled environment","static_analysis":"Broad dependency visibility but cannot establish runtime behavior alone"},"decision_factors":["Dependency criticality","Security impact","Failure reproducibility","Recovery requirements"],"failure_modes":["Hidden synchronous dependency","Unbounded stale state","Unsafe fail-open behavior","Failed reconciliation"],"security_implication":"Verification must explicitly test revocation and policy-change behavior during control-plane failure.","performance_implication":"Tests should measure behavior under control-plane outage without treating synthetic results as universal performance guarantees.","testing_guidance":["Disable control plane","Delay updates","Test policy revocation","Restore control plane","Verify reconciliation"],"verification_method":"Execute controlled failure scenarios and verify all observed transitions against documented control/data-plane invariants.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Google SRE reliability practices","NIST SP 800-207"],"related_records":["D41-B48-R001","D41-B48-R004"],"relationships":["verifies:D41-B48-R001","verifies:D41-B48-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B49-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Platform Architecture","concept":"Central platform team versus embedded platform capabilities","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What are the trade-offs between a centralized platform team and capabilities embedded within application teams?","answer":"A centralized platform team can standardize infrastructure, security controls, and operational tooling while reducing duplicated implementation. Embedded capabilities can improve local autonomy and domain alignment but may create inconsistent implementations and duplicated operational work.","principle":"Centralize capabilities that benefit strongly from standardization while preserving application-team ownership of domain-specific behavior.","requirements":["Define platform scope","Define service ownership","Define supported interfaces","Define governance boundaries"],"constraints":["Central platforms can become bottlenecks","Embedded capabilities can diverge","Platform abstractions require ongoing maintenance"],"preconditions":["Organizational ownership is clear","Common capabilities are identifiable","Platform consumers have defined needs"],"objective":"Balance standardization with team autonomy.","alternatives":["Central platform","Embedded capability","Platform-as-product","Federated platform model"],"tradeoff":{"central":["Consistency","Reuse","Governance","Potential bottleneck"],"embedded":["Autonomy","Local optimization","Potential duplication"]},"decision_factors":["Capability commonality","Security requirements","Team expertise","Change frequency","Operational scale"],"failure_modes":["Platform becoming mandatory bottleneck","Local divergence","Unowned platform components","Lowest-common-denominator APIs"],"security_implication":"Central platforms can enforce common security controls but become high-value trust boundaries requiring strong governance.","performance_implication":"Shared platforms may improve infrastructure utilization but can become capacity or latency bottlenecks.","testing_guidance":["Test platform failure impact","Test tenant isolation","Test platform upgrades","Test consumer compatibility"],"verification_method":"Verify that platform boundaries have explicit ownership, service-level expectations, compatibility policy, and failure behavior.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.94,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Platform engineering practices","Team Topologies concepts"],"related_records":["D41-B49-R002","D41-B50-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B49-R002","requires:D41-B49-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B49-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Platform Architecture","concept":"Platform golden path versus flexible platform","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a platform provide a standardized golden path instead of unrestricted implementation flexibility?","answer":"A golden path is useful when repeated implementation patterns have known security, reliability, compliance, or operational requirements. Excessive standardization can constrain legitimate workloads and encourage teams to bypass the platform. A platform should provide strong defaults while defining explicit escape mechanisms where justified.","principle":"Standardize proven common paths while preserving governed escape routes for materially different requirements.","requirements":["Reference implementation","Default controls","Supported extension points","Exception process","Lifecycle ownership"],"constraints":["Golden paths can become obsolete","Exceptions increase platform complexity","Over-standardization can reduce innovation"],"preconditions":["Common workload patterns are understood","Platform capabilities are maintained","Exception governance exists"],"objective":"Reduce repeated design effort and implementation risk without forcing inappropriate architecture.","alternatives":["Golden path","Flexible toolkit","Mandatory platform","Reference architecture"],"tradeoff":{"golden_path":["Consistency","Lower decision overhead","Potential constraint"],"flexible":["Autonomy","Adaptability","Higher variation"]},"decision_factors":["Commonality","Risk","Compliance","Workload diversity","Platform maturity"],"failure_modes":["Bypass of platform","Outdated defaults","Uncontrolled exceptions","Hidden coupling"],"security_implication":"Golden paths can encode secure defaults, but exceptions require equivalent security review rather than automatic approval.","performance_implication":"Standardized infrastructure can optimize common paths but may be suboptimal for specialized workloads.","testing_guidance":["Test default path","Test extension points","Test exception process","Test platform upgrade compatibility"],"verification_method":"Verify that the golden path satisfies defined requirements and that exceptions are explicit, justified, and governed.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.94,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Platform engineering practices","Internal developer platform patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B49-R001","D41-B49-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with:D41-B49-R001","requires:D41-B49-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B49-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Platform Architecture","concept":"Platform abstraction depth","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How deep should a platform abstraction be before it becomes counterproductive?","answer":"A platform abstraction should expose stable capabilities required by consumers while preserving access to important underlying semantics. Excessive abstraction can conceal operational behavior, make debugging difficult, and force unrelated workloads into a common model. The correct boundary follows stable consumer needs rather than infrastructure taxonomy.","principle":"Abstract repeated stable capabilities, not every infrastructure detail.","requirements":["Consumer needs","Stable interface","Failure semantics","Escape mechanism","Ownership"],"constraints":["Deep abstractions increase maintenance","Provider-specific features may be hidden","Debugging can cross multiple abstraction layers"],"preconditions":["Consumer patterns are understood","Underlying semantics are known","Interface ownership exists"],"objective":"Provide useful platform capabilities without creating a leaky or restrictive abstraction.","alternatives":["Thin wrapper","Opinionated platform","Capability API","Direct infrastructure access"],"tradeoff":{"deep":["Consistency","Less consumer complexity","Potential loss of control"],"thin":["Flexibility","More consumer responsibility"]},"decision_factors":["Commonality","Complexity","Operational transparency","Provider coupling","Debuggability"],"failure_modes":["Leaky abstraction","Hidden failure behavior","Lowest-common-denominator API","Excessive platform dependency"],"security_implication":"Abstraction layers must preserve identity, authorization, tenancy, and audit semantics rather than merely forwarding infrastructure operations.","performance_implication":"Translation layers can add latency and resource usage; performance-sensitive paths require measurement.","testing_guidance":["Test abstraction contract","Test provider-specific behavior","Test failure propagation","Test escape paths"],"verification_method":"Verify that abstraction semantics remain explicit and that consumers can diagnose failures without bypassing required governance.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.95,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Software architecture abstraction principles","Platform engineering practices"],"related_records":["D41-B49-R002","D41-B49-R004"],"relationships":["refines:D41-B49-R002","contrasts_with:D41-B49-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B49-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Platform Architecture","concept":"Platform ownership and lifecycle","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should platform architecture define explicit ownership and lifecycle boundaries?","answer":"A platform is a dependency used by other systems, so unclear ownership can cause compatibility drift, unmaintained components, unresolved incidents, and uncontrolled breaking changes. Platform architecture should define ownership, support scope, lifecycle stages, compatibility policy, and deprecation mechanisms.","principle":"Every platform capability should have an explicit owner and lifecycle state.","requirements":["Owner","Support policy","Version policy","Deprecation policy","Incident responsibility"],"constraints":["Platform ownership can become organizationally complex","Long compatibility commitments increase maintenance","Deprecation requires consumer coordination"],"preconditions":["Platform consumers are identifiable","Capabilities are catalogued","Ownership model exists"],"objective":"Keep platform dependencies governable throughout their lifecycle.","alternatives":["Central ownership","Federated ownership","Capability-specific ownership"],"tradeoff":{"central":"Clear governance but potential bottleneck","federated":"Local autonomy but coordination complexity"},"decision_factors":["Consumer count","Criticality","Change frequency","Capability commonality"],"failure_modes":["Abandoned capability","Unannounced breaking change","Unclear incident ownership","Permanent deprecated interfaces"],"security_implication":"Unowned platform components can retain outdated security controls or vulnerable dependencies without a responsible remediation owner.","performance_implication":"Platform-wide changes can alter resource consumption across many consumers and require coordinated capacity analysis.","testing_guidance":["Test upgrade compatibility","Test deprecation path","Test failure ownership","Test consumer migration"],"verification_method":"Verify that each platform capability has a named ownership model, lifecycle state, support contract, and retirement mechanism.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Platform engineering lifecycle practices","Software dependency governance principles"],"related_records":["D41-B49-R001","D41-B49-R005"],"relationships":["requires:D41-B49-R001","verifies:D41-B49-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B49-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B49","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Platform Architecture","concept":"Platform adoption and bypass analysis","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an architecture evaluate whether a platform capability is actually useful to its consumers?","answer":"Evaluate adoption through documented consumer requirements, supported use cases, operational outcomes, failure behavior, migration cost, and reasons for bypass. Low adoption may indicate poor fit, excessive constraints, missing capabilities, or unnecessary platform scope; the architecture should use evidence rather than adoption alone as the sole quality measure.","principle":"Platform quality should be evaluated by consumer outcomes and operational fit, not merely platform centralization or adoption percentage.","requirements":["Consumer feedback","Use-case coverage","Failure data","Migration effort","Capability coverage"],"constraints":["Adoption metrics can be misleading","Bypass can be justified for specialized workloads","Short-term adoption can precede long-term operational problems"],"preconditions":["Consumers are identifiable","Platform objectives are defined","Bypass reasons can be observed"],"objective":"Validate whether platform architecture provides meaningful reusable value.","alternatives":["Consumer interviews","Usage telemetry","Architecture reviews","Incident analysis","Cost analysis"],"tradeoff":{"central_measurement":"Efficient but may hide qualitative problems","consumer_analysis":"Richer context but higher analysis cost"},"decision_factors":["Outcome quality","Operational burden","Security posture","Developer effort","Platform reliability"],"failure_modes":["Forced adoption","Shadow platforms","Repeated exceptions","Unmeasured consumer harm"],"security_implication":"Bypass analysis should identify whether consumers are avoiding security controls or merely avoiding inappropriate platform constraints.","performance_implication":"Platform usage should be evaluated for actual latency, throughput, and resource effects rather than assumed efficiency.","testing_guidance":["Measure representative workflows","Review bypass cases","Test platform failure","Compare platform and non-platform implementations where appropriate"],"verification_method":"Verify platform claims using consumer evidence, operational measurements, and documented requirements rather than adoption count alone.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.93,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Platform engineering practices","Software architecture governance principles"],"related_records":["D41-B49-R001","D41-B49-R004"],"relationships":["verifies:D41-B49-R001","verifies:D41-B49-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B50-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Closure","concept":"Architecture decision completeness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What information should be present before an architecture trade-off decision is considered complete?","answer":"A complete architecture decision should identify the problem, requirements, constraints, alternatives, decision factors, selected approach, rejected alternatives, assumptions, risks, consequences, validation plan, and lifecycle triggers for reconsideration. The record should distinguish evidence from inference and unresolved uncertainty.","principle":"An architecture decision is complete only when its reasoning, evidence, consequences, and validation path are explicit.","requirements":["Problem statement","Requirements","Constraints","Alternatives","Decision rationale","Consequences","Validation plan","Reconsideration triggers"],"constraints":["Some architectural uncertainty cannot be eliminated before implementation","Evidence quality varies","Decisions can become invalid as requirements change"],"preconditions":["Decision scope is defined","Relevant evidence is available","Stakeholders and ownership are identified"],"objective":"Create architecture decisions that remain understandable and reviewable over time.","alternatives":["ADR","Architecture decision record plus test","Reference architecture decision","Architecture review record"],"tradeoff":{"detail":["Improves traceability","Requires maintenance"],"minimal":["Lower documentation cost","Higher future ambiguity"]},"decision_factors":["Decision criticality","Reversibility","Uncertainty","Blast radius","Evidence quality"],"failure_modes":["Undocumented assumptions","False certainty","Missing rejected alternatives","No validation plan","Stale decision record"],"security_implication":"Security assumptions and security-relevant consequences must be explicit rather than hidden inside general architecture rationale.","performance_implication":"Performance assumptions should be classified as measured, estimated, inferred, or unknown and validated when material to the decision.","testing_guidance":["Review decision completeness","Validate assumptions","Execute planned architecture tests","Reassess after material changes"],"verification_method":"Apply a structured decision-completeness checklist and verify that every material claim has an appropriate evidence classification.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Architecture Decision Record practices","Software architecture governance literature"],"related_records":["D41-B10-R001","D41-B50-R002"],"relationships":["extends:D41-B10-R001","requires:D41-B50-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B50-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Closure","concept":"Reversible versus irreversible architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should architecture treat decisions that are difficult or expensive to reverse?","answer":"Irreversible or expensive-to-reverse decisions warrant stronger evidence, explicit risk analysis, validation, and stakeholder review than easily reversible decisions. Reversible decisions can often be made later when uncertainty is expected to decrease, provided delaying the decision does not create unacceptable dependencies or cost.","principle":"Match decision rigor to the cost of being wrong and the cost of reversing the decision.","requirements":["Reversibility assessment","Decision cost","Evidence requirements","Deferral criteria","Review triggers"],"constraints":["Some decisions become harder to reverse over time","Premature standardization can create long-term coupling","Delaying decisions can also create cost"],"preconditions":["Decision consequences are identifiable","Migration or reversal costs can be estimated","Uncertainty is documented"],"objective":"Allocate architecture effort according to decision risk and reversibility.","alternatives":["Decide immediately","Prototype first","Defer decision","Choose reversible architecture"],"tradeoff":{"early_decision":"May reduce delay but increases risk under uncertainty","deferred":"Allows more evidence but can increase later migration cost"},"decision_factors":["Reversibility","Blast radius","Evidence quality","Time sensitivity","Migration cost"],"failure_modes":["Premature commitment","Analysis paralysis","Hidden irreversible dependency","Expensive migration discovered late"],"security_implication":"Security architecture decisions involving trust boundaries or identity models can become expensive to reverse and should receive stronger upfront validation.","performance_implication":"Early performance architecture commitments should be supported by workload evidence when they constrain future scaling options.","testing_guidance":["Prototype high-risk choices","Test migration path","Document reversal conditions","Measure critical assumptions"],"verification_method":"Classify the decision's reversibility and verify that evidence and validation effort are proportional to the consequence of being wrong.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Architecture decision-making principles","Software architecture governance practices"],"related_records":["D41-B50-R001","D41-B50-R004"],"relationships":["refines:D41-B50-R001","requires:D41-B50-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B50-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Closure","concept":"Architecture assumption ledger","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should important architecture assumptions be recorded separately from established facts?","answer":"Assumptions represent conditions believed to be true but not yet sufficiently verified. Recording them separately prevents inferred or provisional statements from becoming indistinguishable from established facts. Each important assumption should have an owner, evidence target, validation method, and status.","principle":"Unverified assumptions should remain explicitly marked until evidence changes their truth classification.","requirements":["Assumption identifier","Owner","Evidence target","Validation method","Status"],"constraints":["Not every assumption can be immediately validated","Assumption tracking creates maintenance work","Some assumptions are external dependencies"],"preconditions":["Decision context is known","Uncertainty is recognized","Validation path exists"],"objective":"Prevent hidden uncertainty from becoming architectural fact through repetition.","alternatives":["Assumption ledger","Risk register","Decision record annotations","Architecture hypothesis tests"],"tradeoff":{"explicit_tracking":"Improves uncertainty visibility","cost":"Requires lifecycle maintenance"},"decision_factors":["Impact if false","Validation cost","Decision reversibility","Dependency on external conditions"],"failure_modes":["Assumption silently becoming fact","Unowned uncertainty","Expired assumption","Conflicting assumptions"],"security_implication":"Security assumptions such as trusted network location, identity freshness, or dependency behavior should never silently become implicit trust rules.","performance_implication":"Performance assumptions should remain classified until supported by representative measurements.","testing_guidance":["Review assumptions periodically","Test high-impact assumptions","Expire obsolete assumptions","Link assumptions to evidence"],"verification_method":"Verify that every high-impact assumption has an explicit status and validation path and is not represented as a verified fact until evidence supports that classification.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Architecture decision-record practices","Evidence-driven engineering principles"],"related_records":["D41-B50-R001","D41-B50-R002"],"relationships":["supports:D41-B50-R001","supports:D41-B50-R002","verified_by:D41-B50-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B50-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Closure","concept":"Architecture decision reconsideration triggers","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When should an architecture decision be reconsidered?","answer":"A decision should be reconsidered when material requirements, workload characteristics, threat assumptions, regulatory constraints, technology capabilities, cost structure, failure evidence, or operational outcomes change enough to invalidate its original rationale. Reconsideration should be evidence-driven rather than triggered by technology novelty alone.","principle":"Revisit architecture when the assumptions or constraints that justified the decision materially change.","requirements":["Decision assumptions","Trigger definitions","Review ownership","Evidence collection","Versioned decision history"],"constraints":["Not every change warrants redesign","Frequent reconsideration can create instability","Historical rationale should remain preserved"],"preconditions":["Original rationale is recorded","Material assumptions are identifiable","Review ownership exists"],"objective":"Prevent architecture decisions from becoming permanently authoritative after their evidence base has changed.","alternatives":["Scheduled review","Event-triggered review","Incident-triggered review","Metric-triggered review"],"tradeoff":{"scheduled":"Predictable governance but may miss rapid change","triggered":"More responsive but requires reliable detection"},"decision_factors":["Magnitude of change","Decision criticality","Operational evidence","Reversibility","Risk"],"failure_modes":["Stale architecture decision","Technology-driven redesign without need","Uncontrolled redesign churn","Loss of historical rationale"],"security_implication":"Threat-model changes and newly discovered vulnerabilities can invalidate architecture assumptions even when functional requirements remain unchanged.","performance_implication":"Material workload growth or changed latency requirements can invalidate previous capacity assumptions and should trigger reassessment.","testing_guidance":["Review after major incidents","Review after material workload changes","Review after security model changes","Review after major dependency changes"],"verification_method":"For each trigger, verify whether the original decision assumptions remain valid and record the resulting disposition as unchanged, revised, superseded, or unresolved.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Architecture governance practices","Google SRE post-incident learning principles"],"related_records":["D41-B50-R001","D41-B50-R003"],"relationships":["governs:D41-B50-R001","refines:D41-B50-R003","tested_by:D41-B50-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B50-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B50","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Closure","concept":"Architecture trade-off evidence validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should the evidence supporting an architecture trade-off be validated before the decision is locked?","answer":"Validate that claims are correctly classified as facts, measurements, inferences, recommendations, heuristics, assumptions, or unknowns; verify that important claims have appropriate provenance; confirm that incompatible versions or contexts are not silently combined; and ensure that performance or reliability claims are not presented as measured unless they were actually measured.","principle":"Architecture reasoning is only as reliable as the evidence classification and provenance supporting its claims.","requirements":["Claim classification","Source provenance","Version context","Measurement status","Contradiction handling","Validation records"],"constraints":["Evidence may be incomplete","Sources can disagree","Measurements may not generalize across environments","Historical evidence can become stale"],"preconditions":["Decision record exists","Evidence sources are identifiable","Validation criteria are defined"],"objective":"Prevent unsupported architectural claims from becoming authoritative knowledge.","alternatives":["Document review","Source verification","Prototype validation","Benchmarking","Architecture testing"],"tradeoff":{"documentation_review":"Broad and low-cost but limited runtime evidence","execution_validation":"Higher confidence for executable claims but environment-specific","prototype":"Useful for uncertainty reduction but not equivalent to production validation"},"decision_factors":["Claim importance","Decision impact","Evidence quality","Reproducibility","Environmental similarity"],"failure_modes":["Fabricated benchmark","Unverified implementation claim","Stale source","Version mismatch","Unresolved contradiction"],"security_implication":"Security claims require authoritative provenance and should not be inferred solely from architectural intent.","performance_implication":"Performance claims must distinguish measured, estimated, inferred, and unknown values; no benchmark should be represented as measured without actual execution evidence.","testing_guidance":["Audit provenance","Validate versions","Execute material prototypes where needed","Review contradictions","Check execution status"],"verification_method":"Audit each material claim for provenance, truth classification, version compatibility, execution status, and contradiction status before locking the architecture record.","evidence_level":"EVIDENCE_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Evidence-driven software engineering practices","Architecture governance principles"],"related_records":["D41-B50-R001","D41-B50-R003","D41-B50-R004"],"relationships":["verifies:D41-B50-R001","verifies:D41-B50-R003","verifies:D41-B50-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B51-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Virtual machines vs containers","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture use virtual machines instead of containers?","answer":"Virtual machines provide stronger workload isolation and independent operating-system environments, while containers generally provide lower startup and resource overhead through kernel sharing. The choice depends on isolation requirements, workload density, operating-system requirements, operational tooling, and deployment constraints.","principle":"Choose the isolation boundary that satisfies the security and operational requirements without introducing unnecessary infrastructure complexity.","requirements":["Define required isolation strength","Identify operating-system requirements","Assess workload density and startup requirements","Evaluate operational tooling and lifecycle management"],"constraints":["Containers share a host kernel","Virtual machines require a hypervisor or equivalent virtualization layer","Actual isolation properties depend on platform configuration"],"objective":"Select an appropriate compute isolation model.","alternatives":["Virtual machines","Containers","A mixed VM and container architecture"],"tradeoff":"VMs generally provide stronger environment separation at the cost of additional resource and lifecycle overhead; containers generally improve density and deployment speed but require careful host-kernel isolation.","decision_factors":["Isolation","OS independence","Startup time","Resource density","Security boundary","Operational complexity","Cost"],"failure_modes":["Selecting containers where strong isolation is a hard requirement","Selecting VMs for every workload without evaluating operational overhead","Assuming containers provide the same isolation boundary as separate kernels"],"security_implication":"The architecture must treat the shared host kernel as an important security boundary when containers are used.","performance_implication":"Containerized workloads can generally achieve higher workload density than full VMs, but actual performance depends on workload and platform characteristics.","testing_guidance":"Validate isolation assumptions, resource limits, startup behavior, failure handling, and workload-specific performance.","verification_method":"Review documented platform isolation guarantees and validate deployment behavior in the intended environment.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established virtualization and container architecture practices","NIST SP 800-190"],"related_records":["D41-B51-R002","D41-B51-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B51-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Containers vs serverless functions","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should containers and serverless functions be compared architecturally?","answer":"Containers provide explicit control over runtime packaging and service lifecycle, while serverless functions delegate more infrastructure management to the platform and commonly favor event-driven or request-driven workloads. The decision depends on execution duration, runtime control, scaling behavior, operational requirements, and platform constraints.","principle":"Delegate infrastructure management only when the platform abstraction remains compatible with workload requirements.","requirements":["Identify runtime-control requirements","Identify workload invocation model","Define execution-duration requirements","Evaluate scaling and operational constraints"],"constraints":["Serverless platforms impose provider-specific execution and lifecycle constraints","Container platforms require more infrastructure management"],"objective":"Select an appropriate compute abstraction.","alternatives":["Containers","Serverless functions","Hybrid architecture"],"tradeoff":"Serverless can reduce infrastructure management but may impose execution, runtime, networking, and platform constraints; containers provide greater control but increase operational responsibility.","decision_factors":["Runtime control","Execution duration","Scaling model","Cold-start sensitivity","Networking","Portability","Operations"],"failure_modes":["Using functions for workloads requiring long-lived processes","Using containers when the workload gains little from custom runtime control","Ignoring provider-specific execution limits"],"security_implication":"Identity, network access, secrets, and execution permissions must be explicitly bounded regardless of compute model.","performance_implication":"Startup behavior, concurrency limits, scheduling, and platform scaling can materially affect latency and throughput.","testing_guidance":"Test invocation, scaling, timeout, retry, concurrency, and failure behavior under realistic workload patterns.","verification_method":"Compare workload requirements against documented platform limits and validate representative execution scenarios.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established serverless and container architecture practices","Cloud provider architecture documentation families"],"related_records":["D41-B51-R001","D41-B51-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B51-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Stateless vs stateful service architecture","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a service remain stateless rather than retaining application state locally?","answer":"Stateless services are generally easier to replicate, replace, and recover because request-specific state is externalized or reconstructed. Stateful services can reduce external-state operations or preserve locality but introduce stronger placement, recovery, replication, and lifecycle requirements.","principle":"Keep service-local state only when its operational and performance value justifies the resulting lifecycle and recovery complexity.","requirements":["Identify state ownership","Define recovery behavior","Define replication requirements","Define scaling model"],"constraints":["State cannot be safely discarded if it represents authoritative business information","Externalizing state adds network and consistency considerations"],"objective":"Choose an appropriate state ownership model.","alternatives":["Stateless service","Stateful service","Hybrid state model"],"tradeoff":"Statelessness simplifies horizontal scaling and replacement; statefulness may improve locality or specialized workloads but increases lifecycle complexity.","decision_factors":["State durability","Scalability","Recovery","Locality","Consistency","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Treating ephemeral local state as durable state","Scaling stateful instances without a state-coordination strategy","Externalizing all state without evaluating latency and consistency"],"security_implication":"State placement determines exposure, access-control boundaries, and data-residency requirements.","performance_implication":"Local state can reduce network access but may constrain scaling and recovery; external state can introduce network latency.","testing_guidance":"Test restart, replacement, scaling, failover, concurrent access, and state recovery.","verification_method":"Demonstrate that state survives or is intentionally discarded according to the documented lifecycle contract.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established distributed-systems architecture principles"],"related_records":["D41-B51-R004","D41-B51-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B51-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Long-lived services vs short-lived jobs","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should long-lived services and short-lived jobs be selected?","answer":"Long-lived services suit continuously available request or event processing, while short-lived jobs suit bounded workloads that can be scheduled, queued, or retried independently. The choice should follow workload lifetime, interaction model, scheduling requirements, and recovery semantics.","principle":"Align process lifetime with the natural lifecycle of the workload rather than forcing every workload into a continuously running service.","requirements":["Define workload lifetime","Define invocation mechanism","Define retry behavior","Define scheduling requirements"],"constraints":["Long-lived processes require lifecycle supervision","Short-lived jobs require reliable scheduling and result handling"],"objective":"Select an appropriate execution lifecycle.","alternatives":["Long-lived service","Batch job","Queued worker","Scheduled job"],"tradeoff":"Long-lived services simplify continuously available interaction but consume persistent runtime resources; jobs improve isolation and scheduling but require explicit orchestration.","decision_factors":["Lifetime","Invocation","Scheduling","Retry","State","Resource utilization"],"failure_modes":["Implementing periodic work as an always-running service without need","Running interactive workloads as jobs with unsuitable startup latency","Losing job state on worker termination"],"security_implication":"Job identities and permissions should be scoped to the operations required by the job.","performance_implication":"Job startup overhead can matter for high-frequency small tasks; persistent services can reduce repeated initialization.","testing_guidance":"Test termination, retry, duplicate execution, scheduling delays, and partial completion.","verification_method":"Verify lifecycle behavior against the workload contract using representative job and service scenarios.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established job-processing and service architecture patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B51-R002","D41-B51-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B51-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B51","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Compute Architecture","concept":"Control plane vs data plane separation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why separate control-plane and data-plane responsibilities?","answer":"Separating control-plane management from data-plane execution isolates configuration, policy, lifecycle, and orchestration concerns from latency-sensitive or high-volume operational traffic. The separation can improve scalability and fault isolation but introduces coordination and consistency requirements.","principle":"Separate management operations from high-volume execution paths when their reliability, scaling, or security characteristics materially differ.","requirements":["Define control responsibilities","Define data-path responsibilities","Define communication boundaries","Define failure behavior"],"constraints":["Control-plane unavailability may affect configuration changes without necessarily requiring data-plane interruption","The separation creates additional interfaces"],"objective":"Improve architectural isolation between management and execution.","alternatives":["Unified architecture","Control/data-plane separation","Partially separated architecture"],"tradeoff":"Separation improves independent scaling and fault isolation but adds coordination, API, and consistency complexity.","decision_factors":["Traffic volume","Latency","Fault isolation","Security boundaries","Independent scaling","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Making runtime execution depend synchronously on every control-plane request","Allowing stale control-plane state without explicit semantics","Creating duplicated policy implementations"],"security_implication":"Control-plane privileges should be more restricted because they can influence broad execution behavior.","performance_implication":"Data-plane traffic can avoid repeated management-plane interactions, reducing dependency on control-plane latency.","testing_guidance":"Test control-plane outages, stale configuration, propagation delays, and data-plane operation during management failures.","verification_method":"Demonstrate documented behavior during control-plane failure and configuration propagation scenarios.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established distributed-system control/data-plane architecture patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B51-R001","D41-B52-R001"],"relationships":["related_to","contrasts_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B52-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Processing Architecture","concept":"Batch processing vs stream processing","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should a workload use batch processing instead of stream processing?","answer":"Batch processing groups data for bounded periodic processing, while stream processing reacts to continuously arriving events. Batch processing can simplify operational and consistency models; streaming can reduce processing latency but introduces event-time, ordering, state, and failure-handling complexity.","principle":"Choose the processing model according to required freshness rather than defaulting to continuous processing.","requirements":["Define freshness requirement","Define event volume and arrival pattern","Define ordering semantics","Define recovery requirements"],"constraints":["Streaming systems require explicit handling of late, duplicate, and out-of-order events","Batch systems introduce processing delay"],"objective":"Select an appropriate data-processing model.","alternatives":["Batch","Streaming","Micro-batch"],"tradeoff":"Batch favors simplicity and bounded processing; streaming favors lower freshness latency at the cost of greater operational and semantic complexity.","decision_factors":["Latency","Volume","Ordering","State","Recovery","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Using streaming without a genuine freshness requirement","Using batch when near-real-time behavior is mandatory","Ignoring duplicate or late-event semantics"],"security_implication":"Streaming pipelines require continuous authorization and data-flow controls; batch pipelines require protection of accumulated datasets.","performance_implication":"Streaming reduces freshness latency but may require persistent processing infrastructure and state management.","testing_guidance":"Test late events, duplicate events, restarts, backlog growth, and processing correctness.","verification_method":"Compare measured or documented freshness and processing guarantees against the workload requirement.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established data-processing architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B52-R002","D41-B52-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B52-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Processing Architecture","concept":"Synchronous request processing vs asynchronous job processing","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a request be converted into an asynchronous job?","answer":"Asynchronous processing is appropriate when work can complete independently of the client interaction, may take significant time, or benefits from queue-based load smoothing. Synchronous processing is preferable when the caller requires an immediate result and the work has bounded latency.","principle":"Use asynchronous boundaries when decoupling execution time from request lifetime provides a meaningful reliability or scalability benefit.","requirements":["Define caller expectation","Define completion semantics","Define retry behavior","Define result retrieval mechanism"],"constraints":["Asynchronous execution requires durable job state or equivalent tracking","Clients must handle delayed completion"],"objective":"Select an appropriate interaction model for long-running or variable-duration work.","alternatives":["Synchronous request","Asynchronous job","Hybrid synchronous/asynchronous response"],"tradeoff":"Asynchrony improves decoupling and load management but increases state, observability, and completion-handling complexity.","decision_factors":["Latency","Duration","Load variability","Retry semantics","User experience","Durability"],"failure_modes":["Returning synchronous requests for unbounded work","Creating asynchronous jobs without durable status","Allowing duplicate job execution without idempotency"],"security_implication":"Job identifiers must not become authorization bypasses; status and result retrieval require access control.","performance_implication":"Queues can absorb bursts but add scheduling and queueing latency.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate submission, retries, worker failure, delayed completion, and status polling or callback behavior.","verification_method":"Verify completion semantics, idempotency behavior, and failure recovery against the job contract.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established asynchronous processing patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B52-R001","D41-B52-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B52-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Processing Architecture","concept":"Pull-based workers vs push-based delivery","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What architectural factors distinguish pull workers from push delivery?","answer":"Pull workers control when they retrieve work and can naturally provide backpressure through polling behavior, while push delivery lets a producer or broker initiate delivery and can reduce polling overhead. The choice affects load control, retry behavior, consumer availability, and operational complexity.","principle":"Choose the delivery model that gives the system sufficient control over load and failure behavior.","requirements":["Define producer and consumer responsibilities","Define backpressure","Define retry semantics","Define delivery guarantees"],"constraints":["Push systems need explicit consumer-overload handling","Pull systems may introduce polling overhead or delayed pickup"],"objective":"Select a controllable work-delivery mechanism.","alternatives":["Pull","Push","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Pull provides consumer-side pacing; push can reduce polling but requires explicit delivery and overload controls.","decision_factors":["Backpressure","Latency","Consumer capacity","Broker capabilities","Retry","Operational simplicity"],"failure_modes":["Push delivery overwhelming slow consumers","Aggressive polling creating unnecessary load","Assuming either model automatically guarantees exactly-once processing"],"security_implication":"Delivery endpoints and credentials must be authenticated and authorized; pull consumers need scoped access to work queues.","performance_implication":"Polling interval affects latency and overhead; push delivery can reduce idle polling but may create burst pressure.","testing_guidance":"Test consumer overload, producer bursts, unavailable consumers, retries, and duplicate delivery.","verification_method":"Validate delivery and backpressure behavior under representative load and failure conditions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established messaging and distributed-processing practices"],"related_records":["D41-B52-R002","D41-B52-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B52-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Processing Architecture","subtopic":"Workload Scheduling","concept":"Central scheduler vs distributed scheduling","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should scheduling responsibility be centralized?","answer":"Centralized scheduling simplifies global coordination and policy enforcement, while distributed scheduling can reduce central bottlenecks and allow domain-specific autonomy. Central scheduling introduces a potentially critical dependency; distributed scheduling introduces coordination and duplicate-execution risks.","principle":"Centralize scheduling when global coordination is more important than independent scheduling autonomy; distribute it when scale or domain isolation justifies the complexity.","requirements":["Define scheduling scope","Define uniqueness guarantees","Define failover","Define ownership"],"constraints":["Central schedulers require high availability","Distributed schedulers require coordination or idempotency"],"objective":"Select a scheduling responsibility model.","alternatives":["Central scheduler","Distributed schedulers","Hierarchical scheduling"],"tradeoff":"Centralization simplifies coordination but creates a shared dependency; distribution improves autonomy but complicates consistency and duplicate prevention.","decision_factors":["Scale","Coordination","Availability","Ownership","Duplicate prevention","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Single scheduler becoming a single point of failure","Distributed schedulers producing duplicate jobs","Unclear ownership of schedules"],"security_implication":"Scheduling authority can trigger privileged operations and should therefore have narrowly scoped permissions.","performance_implication":"Central scheduling can become a throughput bottleneck at scale; distributed scheduling adds coordination overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test scheduler failure, leader changes, duplicate scheduling, clock differences, and missed schedules.","verification_method":"Verify scheduling uniqueness, recovery, and timing semantics under failure scenarios.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established distributed scheduling architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B52-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B52-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B52","topic":"Processing Architecture","subtopic":"Workload Scheduling","concept":"Timer-based scheduling vs event-triggered execution","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should work be triggered by a schedule rather than by events?","answer":"Timer-based scheduling is appropriate for periodic or deadline-driven work, while event-triggered execution is appropriate when work should respond directly to state changes or incoming events. Combining both can be useful when periodic reconciliation complements event processing.","principle":"Trigger work according to the actual causal condition: time, event, or both.","requirements":["Identify trigger semantics","Define missed-trigger behavior","Define duplicate handling","Define reconciliation needs"],"constraints":["Timer schedules depend on clock and scheduler behavior","Event triggers depend on reliable event delivery"],"objective":"Choose an appropriate workload trigger.","alternatives":["Timer","Event","Timer plus event reconciliation"],"tradeoff":"Timers provide predictable periodic execution; events can reduce unnecessary work and improve responsiveness but require reliable event semantics.","decision_factors":["Freshness","Periodicity","Event reliability","Reconciliation","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Polling frequently for state that already produces reliable events","Assuming events cannot be lost or duplicated","Failing to reconcile missed events"],"security_implication":"Both scheduling and event identities require authorization appropriate to the operation being triggered.","performance_implication":"Event-driven execution can reduce unnecessary polling; periodic reconciliation introduces bounded recurring load.","testing_guidance":"Test missed events, duplicate events, scheduler delays, and reconciliation correctness.","verification_method":"Verify trigger coverage and recovery behavior using both normal and missed-trigger scenarios.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established event-driven and scheduled-processing patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B52-R001","D41-B52-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B53-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Search Architecture","concept":"Database queries vs dedicated search engine","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should search move from a database query layer to a dedicated search engine?","answer":"A database query layer may be sufficient for transactional filtering and modest search requirements. A dedicated search engine becomes useful when relevance ranking, full-text search, specialized indexing, faceting, or search-oriented scaling requirements justify an additional system.","principle":"Introduce a dedicated search system only when search requirements materially exceed the capabilities or operational economics of the primary datastore.","requirements":["Define search semantics","Define relevance requirements","Define freshness requirements","Define indexing lifecycle"],"constraints":["Dedicated search introduces another data representation and synchronization path","Search indexes are generally not the authoritative transactional source"],"objective":"Select an appropriate search architecture.","alternatives":["Database search","Dedicated search engine","Hybrid search architecture"],"tradeoff":"Dedicated search can improve search capabilities but adds operational, consistency, and indexing complexity.","decision_factors":["Relevance","Full-text capability","Scale","Freshness","Operational cost","Consistency"],"failure_modes":["Adding a search engine for simple filtering","Treating an index as authoritative without explicit design","Allowing index synchronization failures to remain invisible"],"security_implication":"Search indexes must preserve authorization boundaries and avoid exposing fields that users cannot access in the source system.","performance_implication":"Specialized indexes can accelerate search workloads but introduce indexing and storage overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test relevance, authorization filtering, index lag, deletion propagation, and rebuild behavior.","verification_method":"Verify search results against authoritative data and explicitly test stale-index and authorization scenarios.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established search architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B53-R002","D41-B53-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B53-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Search Architecture","concept":"Precomputed indexes vs on-demand computation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture precompute searchable or derived data?","answer":"Precomputation is useful when repeated reads justify the storage and update cost of derived representations. On-demand computation avoids synchronization complexity but can increase read latency and compute cost.","principle":"Precompute when repeated access patterns make the maintenance cost of derived state worthwhile.","requirements":["Identify read frequency","Measure or estimate computation cost","Define freshness requirements","Define invalidation or update strategy"],"constraints":["Derived data can become stale","Precomputation consumes storage and update resources"],"objective":"Choose between computation at read time and precomputed state.","alternatives":["On-demand computation","Precomputed representation","Hybrid caching/precomputation"],"tradeoff":"Precomputation reduces repeated computation but introduces consistency and maintenance costs.","decision_factors":["Read/write ratio","Freshness","Computation cost","Storage","Update complexity"],"failure_modes":["Precomputing rarely accessed data","Allowing derived state to become silently stale","Building complex invalidation without clear ownership"],"security_implication":"Derived representations must inherit the authorization constraints of their source data.","performance_implication":"Precomputation can reduce read latency at the cost of additional write/update work.","testing_guidance":"Test update propagation, stale-state behavior, rebuilds, and consistency after partial failures.","verification_method":"Verify freshness guarantees and compare derived values against authoritative computation.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established caching and derived-data architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B53-R001","D41-B53-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B53-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Search Architecture","concept":"Central search service vs domain-owned search","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Should search be centralized or owned by individual domains?","answer":"A centralized search service can provide consistent cross-domain discovery and common operational controls, while domain-owned search keeps ownership closer to source data and domain semantics. The choice depends on search scope, authorization complexity, ownership, and indexing requirements.","principle":"Place search ownership where source-data authority and search semantics can be maintained without creating excessive coupling.","requirements":["Define search ownership","Define cross-domain requirements","Define authorization model","Define indexing responsibilities"],"constraints":["Central search can become a cross-domain dependency","Domain-owned search can produce inconsistent search behavior"],"objective":"Choose an appropriate search ownership model.","alternatives":["Centralized search","Domain-owned search","Federated search"],"tradeoff":"Centralization improves uniformity but increases coupling; domain ownership improves autonomy but can complicate global discovery.","decision_factors":["Domain boundaries","Cross-domain queries","Authorization","Ownership","Consistency"],"failure_modes":["Central service becoming a bottleneck for unrelated domains","Federated search producing inconsistent ranking or access behavior","Unclear index ownership"],"security_implication":"Cross-domain search must enforce authorization independently of search-engine visibility.","performance_implication":"Federated queries can introduce fan-out latency; centralized indexing can optimize global retrieval but increase indexing concentration.","testing_guidance":"Test cross-domain authorization, partial domain availability, result aggregation, and ranking consistency.","verification_method":"Validate ownership, access-control, and failure behavior across domain boundaries.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established domain-oriented architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B53-R001","D41-B53-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B53-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Data Access Architecture","concept":"Read replica vs cache","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should a read replica be preferred over a cache?","answer":"Read replicas preserve database query semantics against replicated database state, while caches store selected representations and can provide faster access with explicit freshness semantics. A replica is useful when database read capacity is the constraint; a cache is useful when repeated access to predictable data justifies reuse.","principle":"Use replicas to scale database reads and caches to avoid repeated computation or retrieval when stale data is acceptable.","requirements":["Identify read bottleneck","Define freshness","Identify access patterns","Define failure behavior"],"constraints":["Replicas can lag the primary","Caches can contain stale or missing data"],"objective":"Select an appropriate read-scaling mechanism.","alternatives":["Read replica","Cache","Combined replica and cache"],"tradeoff":"Replicas preserve more database semantics but add replication infrastructure; caches can reduce latency and load but introduce invalidation and freshness concerns.","decision_factors":["Consistency","Freshness","Query complexity","Access locality","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Using a cache for strongly consistent reads","Assuming replicas are immediately consistent","Caching authorization-sensitive results without correct keying"],"security_implication":"Cache keys and replica access must preserve authorization boundaries and prevent cross-user data leakage.","performance_implication":"Caches can provide lower latency for repeated access; replicas increase database read capacity but retain query processing costs.","testing_guidance":"Test replica lag, cache misses, invalidation, stale data, and authorization isolation.","verification_method":"Verify documented consistency and freshness behavior under normal and degraded conditions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established database scaling and caching patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B53-R002","D41-B53-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B53-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B53","topic":"Search Architecture","subtopic":"Index Lifecycle","concept":"Online indexing vs offline index rebuild","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should indexes be rebuilt online instead of offline?","answer":"Online rebuilding is preferred when service availability must be maintained during index maintenance and the underlying platform supports safe concurrent rebuilding. Offline rebuilding can simplify maintenance but requires planned downtime or traffic isolation.","principle":"Select index maintenance strategy according to availability requirements and the guarantees of the storage/search platform.","requirements":["Define availability requirement","Define index rebuild duration","Understand platform locking behavior","Define rollback/recovery"],"constraints":["Online rebuild support varies by technology and version","Offline rebuild can interrupt dependent workloads"],"objective":"Choose an index maintenance architecture.","alternatives":["Online rebuild","Offline rebuild","Blue/green index replacement"],"tradeoff":"Online maintenance improves availability but may consume additional resources; offline maintenance can be simpler but affects availability.","decision_factors":["Availability","Platform support","Resource usage","Index size","Recovery"],"failure_modes":["Assuming all platforms support non-blocking rebuilds","Starting a large rebuild without resource budgeting","Leaving an incomplete replacement index active"],"security_implication":"Index rebuild processes must preserve access-control metadata and avoid exposing intermediate data.","performance_implication":"Rebuild operations can consume CPU, memory, storage I/O, and database resources.","testing_guidance":"Test rebuild interruption, rollback, resource contention, and correctness during concurrent reads/writes.","verification_method":"Verify platform-specific rebuild guarantees and compare the resulting index against expected query behavior.","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTATION_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Official database and search-platform documentation families"],"related_records":["D41-B53-R001","D41-B53-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B54-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Extensibility Architecture","concept":"Plugin architecture vs built-in extension points","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should extensibility be implemented as plugins rather than built-in extension points?","answer":"Plugins are appropriate when independent components need to be added or replaced without modifying the core system. Built-in extension points are simpler when the extension surface is small, trusted, and controlled by the core development team.","principle":"Introduce runtime or deployment-level plugin isolation only when independent extensibility provides sufficient value to justify its complexity and trust boundary.","requirements":["Define extension ownership","Define lifecycle","Define compatibility contract","Define trust model"],"constraints":["Plugins introduce compatibility and security boundaries","Built-in extension points can couple extensions to core releases"],"objective":"Choose an extensibility mechanism.","alternatives":["Built-in extension points","Plugin system","External service extensions"],"tradeoff":"Plugins increase extensibility and independence but add loading, compatibility, isolation, and security complexity.","decision_factors":["Third-party extensibility","Release independence","Trust","Compatibility","Isolation"],"failure_modes":["Allowing untrusted plugins to execute with unrestricted privileges","Creating an unstable plugin API","Using plugins when simple internal hooks are sufficient"],"security_implication":"Plugin execution is a code-execution boundary and requires strict trust, permission, and isolation controls.","performance_implication":"Plugin discovery and indirection can add overhead; external plugins may introduce network latency.","testing_guidance":"Test plugin compatibility, failure isolation, lifecycle management, permissions, and version upgrades.","verification_method":"Verify plugin contracts and enforce documented compatibility and security boundaries.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established extensibility and plugin architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B54-R002","D41-B54-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B54-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Extensibility Architecture","concept":"Library extension vs service extension","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should an extension be implemented as a library instead of a separate service?","answer":"A library is appropriate when the extension needs local execution, low latency, and shared process context. A service is appropriate when the extension requires independent scaling, isolation, deployment, or ownership.","principle":"Use a service boundary when independent lifecycle or isolation materially matters; otherwise avoid distributed complexity when in-process composition is sufficient.","requirements":["Define lifecycle ownership","Define latency requirements","Define deployment independence","Define isolation requirements"],"constraints":["Libraries share process lifecycle and runtime environment","Services introduce network and operational dependencies"],"objective":"Select an extension boundary.","alternatives":["Library","Service","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Libraries reduce network and operational overhead but couple releases; services improve isolation and independence but add distributed-system complexity.","decision_factors":["Latency","Ownership","Scaling","Isolation","Release independence"],"failure_modes":["Creating a service for a trivial in-process function","Embedding independently evolving components as tightly coupled libraries","Ignoring library dependency conflicts"],"security_implication":"A library inherits application privileges; a service can provide a stronger authorization and isolation boundary.","performance_implication":"Libraries avoid network serialization and transport overhead; services can scale independently.","testing_guidance":"Test API compatibility, deployment changes, network failure where applicable, and dependency conflicts for libraries.","verification_method":"Verify that the selected boundary matches lifecycle, isolation, and latency requirements.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established modular and service-oriented architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B54-R001","D41-B54-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B54-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Extensibility Architecture","concept":"Open extension model vs constrained extension model","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the architectural trade-off between open and constrained extension models?","answer":"Open extension models maximize customization but increase compatibility, security, testing, and support complexity. Constrained extension models limit the extension surface and therefore simplify governance and compatibility at the cost of flexibility.","principle":"Constrain extension points to the capabilities that can be safely governed and supported.","requirements":["Define supported extension capabilities","Define compatibility policy","Define security boundary","Define support scope"],"constraints":["More extension freedom increases possible interaction combinations","Narrow interfaces can exclude legitimate use cases"],"objective":"Define an extensibility boundary.","alternatives":["Open extension model","Constrained extension model","Tiered extension model"],"tradeoff":"Flexibility increases ecosystem possibilities while increasing governance and compatibility complexity.","decision_factors":["Customization","Security","Compatibility","Supportability","Testing surface"],"failure_modes":["Exposing internal implementation details as extension APIs","Allowing extensions to bypass governance","Over-constraining extensions and forcing forks"],"security_implication":"Open extension models expand the trusted computing base and must constrain privileges explicitly.","performance_implication":"Flexible extension mechanisms may add indirection and runtime discovery overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test supported extension combinations and explicitly reject unsupported capabilities.","verification_method":"Review extension contracts against documented supported and prohibited behaviors.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established extensibility governance principles"],"related_records":["D41-B54-R001","D41-B54-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B54-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Extensibility Architecture","concept":"Process boundary vs module boundary","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When does an extension require a process boundary rather than a module boundary?","answer":"A process boundary is justified when failure isolation, privilege separation, independent resource control, or runtime incompatibility cannot be achieved safely inside a module boundary. A module boundary is preferable when components share a trusted runtime and require efficient local interaction.","principle":"Use a stronger isolation boundary only when the required isolation cannot be achieved reliably within the weaker boundary.","requirements":["Identify failure-isolation requirements","Identify privilege requirements","Identify runtime compatibility","Define communication contract"],"constraints":["Process boundaries introduce IPC or network overhead","Module boundaries share process privileges"],"objective":"Choose an appropriate isolation boundary.","alternatives":["Module boundary","Process boundary","Service boundary"],"tradeoff":"Process isolation improves fault and privilege separation but increases operational and communication complexity.","decision_factors":["Trust","Failure isolation","Privileges","Performance","Deployment independence"],"failure_modes":["Using modules for mutually untrusted components","Using separate processes without a meaningful isolation requirement","Assuming process separation automatically provides complete security"],"security_implication":"A process boundary can reduce privilege sharing but must still enforce operating-system and application-level controls.","performance_implication":"IPC and serialization can increase latency compared with in-process calls.","testing_guidance":"Test process crashes, malformed messages, permission boundaries, and resource exhaustion.","verification_method":"Verify the actual isolation guarantees of the selected runtime and operating system.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established process isolation and modular architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B54-R002","D41-B54-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B54-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B54","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Extensibility Architecture","concept":"Configuration-driven behavior vs code-driven behavior","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should behavior be represented as configuration rather than code?","answer":"Configuration is appropriate for bounded, frequently changing parameters or policies whose semantics are already implemented. Code is required when behavior requires new computation, control flow, or capabilities that configuration cannot safely express.","principle":"Represent data as configuration and behavior as code unless a deliberately constrained policy language provides the required semantics.","requirements":["Define configuration schema","Define allowed values","Define validation","Define rollout and rollback"],"constraints":["Configuration cannot safely substitute for arbitrary application logic","Highly dynamic configuration can become an undeclared programming language"],"objective":"Choose the correct representation for variable behavior.","alternatives":["Configuration","Code","Constrained policy language"],"tradeoff":"Configuration can reduce deployment frequency for bounded changes but increases validation and runtime-state complexity if overused.","decision_factors":["Change frequency","Complexity","Validation","Governance","Rollback","Security"],"failure_modes":["Embedding arbitrary logic in configuration","Allowing invalid configuration to reach production","Creating configuration dependencies that are undocumented"],"security_implication":"Configuration can alter authorization or execution behavior and therefore requires equivalent security review when it controls sensitive operations.","performance_implication":"Runtime configuration interpretation can add overhead, usually depending on design and caching.","testing_guidance":"Test valid, invalid, missing, incompatible, and rollback configuration states.","verification_method":"Validate configuration schema and verify behavior for each supported configuration mode.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established configuration-management and policy-engineering practices"],"related_records":["D41-B54-R003","D41-B55-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B55-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Static configuration vs dynamic configuration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should configuration be changeable without application redeployment?","answer":"Dynamic configuration is useful for operational parameters that legitimately need controlled runtime changes, while static configuration is simpler and easier to reason about for values that define deployment identity or immutable behavior.","principle":"Make configuration dynamic only when operational value justifies runtime-state and consistency complexity.","requirements":["Classify configuration values","Define ownership","Define validation","Define propagation semantics","Define rollback"],"constraints":["Dynamic configuration requires reliable distribution and consistency semantics","Static configuration may require redeployment for changes"],"objective":"Select an appropriate configuration lifecycle.","alternatives":["Static configuration","Dynamic configuration","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Dynamic configuration improves operational agility but creates runtime state and propagation complexity.","decision_factors":["Change frequency","Risk","Propagation","Rollback","Availability","Auditability"],"failure_modes":["Changing critical configuration without validation","Allowing partially propagated configuration","Treating runtime configuration as permanently authoritative without versioning"],"security_implication":"Sensitive configuration changes require authorization, auditing, and secure storage.","performance_implication":"Dynamic configuration lookup or propagation can add overhead unless safely cached.","testing_guidance":"Test configuration propagation, rollback, stale consumers, invalid updates, and service restart behavior.","verification_method":"Verify versioned configuration state and propagation behavior under failure conditions.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established configuration-management architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B55-R002","D41-B55-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B55-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Central configuration service vs local configuration","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should configuration be centralized?","answer":"Central configuration can provide common governance, versioning, and coordinated updates, while local configuration reduces runtime dependencies and can simplify isolated deployments. Centralization is most valuable when configuration must be consistently governed across many components.","principle":"Centralize configuration when coordinated governance outweighs the availability and dependency costs of a shared configuration system.","requirements":["Define configuration ownership","Define availability requirements","Define update semantics","Define fallback behavior"],"constraints":["Central services can become shared dependencies","Local configuration can drift across deployments"],"objective":"Choose configuration ownership and distribution architecture.","alternatives":["Central service","Local configuration","Central source with local snapshots"],"tradeoff":"Centralization improves consistency and governance but adds dependency risk; local configuration improves independence but increases drift risk.","decision_factors":["Governance","Availability","Scale","Drift","Deployment independence"],"failure_modes":["Making startup dependent on unavailable configuration service","Allowing unversioned local configuration drift","Using central configuration for secrets without appropriate protection"],"security_implication":"Central configuration systems require strict access control because compromise can affect many workloads.","performance_implication":"Runtime configuration fetches add network dependency; local snapshots reduce repeated network access.","testing_guidance":"Test configuration-service outage, stale snapshots, startup, rollback, and access control.","verification_method":"Verify configuration ownership, versioning, fallback, and authorization behavior.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established configuration-distribution patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B55-R001","D41-B55-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B55-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Feature flags vs deployment-based release control","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should feature flags be used instead of separate deployments?","answer":"Feature flags allow controlled activation of already-deployed behavior and can support gradual rollout or rapid disablement. Separate deployments provide stronger isolation between versions but require deployment operations for switching behavior.","principle":"Use feature flags for controlled behavioral rollout when the code paths can safely coexist; use deployment boundaries when isolation between versions is required.","requirements":["Define flag ownership","Define flag lifecycle","Define default behavior","Define rollback strategy"],"constraints":["Flags increase code-path combinations","Old flags must eventually be removed"],"objective":"Select a release-control mechanism.","alternatives":["Feature flags","Separate deployments","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Flags improve rollout flexibility but increase testing and lifecycle complexity; deployments provide stronger version separation but slower switching.","decision_factors":["Rollout control","Testing complexity","Rollback speed","Isolation","Flag lifetime"],"failure_modes":["Permanent stale flags","Untested combinations","Using flags to hide incompatible data-model changes"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive flags require strict authorization and auditability because a flag can change access behavior.","performance_implication":"Flag evaluation generally adds some runtime logic; poorly designed remote flag evaluation can introduce latency or availability dependencies.","testing_guidance":"Test both flag states, transitions, defaults, stale configurations, and rollback.","verification_method":"Verify each supported flag state and confirm obsolete flags are removed through lifecycle controls.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established progressive-delivery and feature-flag practices"],"related_records":["D41-B55-R001","D41-B55-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B55-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Configuration Architecture","concept":"Environment-specific configuration vs environment-neutral configuration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should environment-specific configuration be separated from application behavior?","answer":"Environment-specific values should generally be externalized from application logic while preserving a common behavioral contract. Excessive environment-specific branching can create effectively different applications and reduce deployment confidence.","principle":"Keep application behavior as environment-neutral as practical and isolate genuine environment-specific parameters.","requirements":["Identify true environmental differences","Define common configuration schema","Validate per-environment values","Avoid environment-specific code paths where unnecessary"],"constraints":["Some infrastructure capabilities genuinely differ by environment","Over-normalization can hide meaningful environmental constraints"],"objective":"Reduce environment drift while preserving legitimate deployment differences.","alternatives":["Environment-specific configuration","Environment-specific code","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"External configuration reduces code divergence but cannot eliminate real platform differences.","decision_factors":["Portability","Drift","Infrastructure differences","Testing","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Hardcoding environment branches","Testing only one environment","Assuming configuration can hide incompatible infrastructure behavior"],"security_implication":"Environment-specific secrets must be isolated and independently authorized.","performance_implication":"Configuration differences can affect resource limits and performance characteristics between environments.","testing_guidance":"Validate configuration schemas in every supported environment and test environment-specific capabilities explicitly.","verification_method":"Compare effective configuration and supported behavior across environments.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established twelve-factor and deployment architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B55-R001","D41-B55-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B55-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B55","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Release Architecture","concept":"Runtime rollback vs redeployment rollback","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When is runtime rollback preferable to redeployment rollback?","answer":"Runtime rollback can rapidly deactivate a compatible behavior when feature controls support it. Redeployment rollback is appropriate when the deployed artifact itself is faulty or incompatible and runtime configuration cannot safely restore the prior state.","principle":"Use runtime rollback only when the previously supported behavior remains safely present in the deployed artifact.","requirements":["Define rollback mechanism","Define compatibility requirements","Define state/data migration constraints","Define authorization"],"constraints":["Runtime rollback cannot repair arbitrary binary or schema incompatibilities","Rollback can be unsafe after irreversible data changes"],"objective":"Choose a safe rollback mechanism.","alternatives":["Runtime rollback","Artifact redeployment","Forward fix"],"tradeoff":"Runtime rollback can be faster but is limited by compatibility; redeployment provides artifact-level control but takes deployment time.","decision_factors":["Failure type","Data compatibility","Rollback speed","Artifact integrity","Operational access"],"failure_modes":["Rolling back application behavior after incompatible schema changes","Assuming flags can undo irreversible state changes","Using rollback without verifying database compatibility"],"security_implication":"Rollback controls can alter security behavior and require privileged authorization and auditing.","performance_implication":"Runtime rollback can reduce recovery transition time when supported; redeployment introduces deployment and startup overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test rollback before incidents using representative state and migration scenarios.","verification_method":"Verify artifact, configuration, and data compatibility before executing rollback.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established release engineering and progressive-delivery practices"],"related_records":["D41-B55-R003","D41-B55-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B56-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Evolution","concept":"Versioned API vs backward-compatible API evolution","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should an API use explicit versions instead of backward-compatible evolution?","answer":"Backward-compatible evolution is preferable when changes can preserve existing contracts. Explicit versions are appropriate when compatibility cannot be maintained or when independently supported contract generations are required.","principle":"Prefer additive and backward-compatible evolution before introducing a new contract version.","requirements":["Define compatibility policy","Identify breaking changes","Define client migration path","Define version lifecycle"],"constraints":["Multiple versions increase support and testing cost","Backward compatibility can constrain future design"],"objective":"Choose an API evolution strategy.","alternatives":["Backward-compatible evolution","Explicit versioning","Hybrid versioning"],"tradeoff":"Compatibility reduces client migration burden but can accumulate legacy constraints; explicit versions isolate breaking contracts but increase operational complexity.","decision_factors":["Client diversity","Breaking changes","Migration control","Support lifetime","Testing burden"],"failure_modes":["Creating versions for every minor change","Breaking clients while claiming compatibility","Keeping unsupported versions indefinitely"],"security_implication":"All supported versions must receive appropriate security fixes and authorization semantics.","performance_implication":"Supporting multiple versions can increase code and test surface but does not inherently determine runtime performance.","testing_guidance":"Run contract tests against every supported version and compatibility path.","verification_method":"Compare proposed changes against documented backward-compatibility rules and execute contract tests where available.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established API evolution practices","RFC 9110"],"related_records":["D41-B56-R002","D41-B56-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B56-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Evolution","concept":"Tolerant readers vs strict schema validation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should consumers reject unknown fields or tolerate them?","answer":"Tolerant readers can improve forward compatibility when additional fields are expected and safely ignorable. Strict validation can detect malformed or unexpected data early and is preferable when unknown fields could indicate contract violations or security-sensitive ambiguity.","principle":"Tolerance should be explicit and bounded by the semantics and security requirements of the contract.","requirements":["Define unknown-field policy","Define required fields","Define compatibility expectations","Define validation behavior"],"constraints":["Tolerance can hide producer mistakes","Strictness can reduce forward compatibility"],"objective":"Choose consumer validation behavior.","alternatives":["Strict validation","Tolerant reader","Field-specific policy"],"tradeoff":"Tolerance improves compatibility but can conceal unexpected producer behavior; strictness improves contract enforcement but increases coupling.","decision_factors":["Forward compatibility","Security","Contract strictness","Producer diversity","Error detection"],"failure_modes":["Ignoring security-sensitive unknown fields","Rejecting harmless additive fields unnecessarily","Using tolerance to avoid fixing malformed producers"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive protocols should avoid ambiguity caused by multiple interpretations of unknown or duplicate fields.","performance_implication":"Validation cost depends on serialization format and schema complexity; neither strategy universally dominates.","testing_guidance":"Test unknown fields, missing required fields, malformed values, duplicate fields, and version transitions.","verification_method":"Verify parser behavior against the explicit contract and compatibility policy.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established API compatibility and schema-validation practices"],"related_records":["D41-B56-R001","D41-B56-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B56-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Evolution","concept":"Synchronous API composition vs asynchronous integration","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should cross-service composition remain synchronous?","answer":"Synchronous composition is suitable when callers require immediate combined results and dependencies have bounded latency and availability requirements. Asynchronous integration is preferable when work can be decoupled, delayed, retried, or processed independently.","principle":"Do not introduce synchronous dependency chains when the business requirement does not require immediate completion.","requirements":["Define response deadline","Define dependency availability","Define partial-result semantics","Define retry behavior"],"constraints":["Synchronous chains amplify latency and failure dependencies","Asynchronous integration requires durable state and completion semantics"],"objective":"Choose a cross-service interaction model.","alternatives":["Synchronous composition","Asynchronous integration","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Synchronous composition is simpler for immediate reads but couples latency and availability; asynchronous integration improves decoupling but complicates state and user feedback.","decision_factors":["Latency","Availability","Coupling","Consistency","User interaction","Failure recovery"],"failure_modes":["Deep synchronous dependency chains","Using asynchronous processing where immediate response is mandatory","Ignoring partial failure in composed requests"],"security_implication":"Each downstream service must independently enforce authorization rather than relying only on the upstream caller.","performance_implication":"Synchronous fan-out increases aggregate latency and tail-latency risk.","testing_guidance":"Test dependency timeouts, partial failures, retries, and degraded responses.","verification_method":"Validate latency budgets and failure semantics against the end-to-end requirement.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established distributed-service interaction patterns"],"related_records":["D41-B52-R002","D41-B56-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B56-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Evolution","concept":"API gateway composition vs client-side composition","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should aggregation happen at the gateway rather than in clients?","answer":"Gateway composition can centralize cross-service aggregation and reduce client knowledge of backend topology. Client-side composition can preserve backend flexibility and reduce gateway responsibility but exposes clients to more service contracts and network interactions.","principle":"Place composition where ownership, latency, authorization, and client diversity make the responsibility sustainable.","requirements":["Define composition ownership","Define client diversity","Define authorization model","Define latency budget"],"constraints":["Gateway composition can become a business-logic bottleneck","Client composition can multiply network calls"],"objective":"Choose the location of API composition.","alternatives":["Gateway composition","Client composition","Backend-for-frontend"],"tradeoff":"Centralized composition simplifies clients but increases gateway responsibility; client composition distributes logic but increases client coupling.","decision_factors":["Client diversity","Aggregation complexity","Latency","Authorization","Ownership"],"failure_modes":["Turning gateway into an uncontrolled monolith","Duplicating aggregation logic across many clients","Creating excessive client fan-out"],"security_implication":"Gateway aggregation must not bypass service-level authorization checks.","performance_implication":"Gateway aggregation can reduce client round trips but may create server-side fan-out and tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Test partial downstream failure, authorization, aggregation correctness, and latency under fan-out.","verification_method":"Verify ownership and authorization boundaries and validate composition against end-to-end latency requirements.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established API gateway and backend-for-frontend architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B56-R003","D41-B56-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B56-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B56","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"API Evolution","concept":"Resource-oriented API vs operation-oriented API","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should an API expose resources rather than explicit operations?","answer":"Resource-oriented APIs can provide consistent CRUD-like semantics around identifiable entities, while operation-oriented APIs can better represent domain actions that do not map naturally to resource manipulation.","principle":"Model the contract around domain semantics rather than forcing every operation into a uniform resource shape.","requirements":["Identify domain entities","Identify domain commands","Define idempotency","Define state transitions"],"constraints":["Resource models can obscure complex business operations","Operation models can become inconsistent if every endpoint is ad hoc"],"objective":"Choose an API abstraction aligned with domain behavior.","alternatives":["Resource-oriented","Operation-oriented","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Resource orientation improves uniformity; operation orientation can represent complex domain actions more directly.","decision_factors":["Domain semantics","State transitions","Idempotency","Client usability","Consistency"],"failure_modes":["Representing complex commands as misleading CRUD updates","Creating arbitrary action endpoints without consistent semantics","Hiding important state transitions"],"security_implication":"Authorization should correspond to actual domain actions and resource ownership.","performance_implication":"API style does not inherently determine performance; inefficient resource expansion or excessive operation calls can affect it.","testing_guidance":"Test state transitions, idempotency, invalid operations, authorization, and concurrent updates.","verification_method":"Verify that API operations accurately represent domain invariants and documented state transitions.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established API design principles"],"related_records":["D41-B56-R001","D41-B56-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B57-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Architecture","concept":"Dedicated resources vs shared resource pools","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should workloads receive dedicated resources instead of sharing a resource pool?","answer":"Dedicated resources can provide stronger performance isolation and predictable capacity, while shared pools improve utilization and elasticity. The choice depends on workload interference tolerance, utilization variability, compliance requirements, and cost constraints.","principle":"Use dedicated resources when isolation or predictability materially outweighs utilization benefits from pooling.","requirements":["Define isolation requirements","Define capacity expectations","Identify workload variability","Define resource governance"],"constraints":["Dedicated capacity can remain underutilized","Shared pools require noisy-neighbor controls"],"objective":"Choose a resource-allocation architecture.","alternatives":["Dedicated resources","Shared pool","Tiered resource allocation"],"tradeoff":"Dedicated resources improve isolation but can reduce utilization; shared pools improve utilization but require stronger scheduling and resource controls.","decision_factors":["Isolation","Utilization","Predictability","Cost","Compliance","Elasticity"],"failure_modes":["Sharing workloads with incompatible resource profiles","Overprovisioning dedicated infrastructure","Failing to enforce resource limits in shared pools"],"security_implication":"Dedicated resources can simplify some isolation requirements, but security controls remain necessary in both models.","performance_implication":"Shared resources can experience contention; dedicated resources improve predictability when capacity is correctly provisioned.","testing_guidance":"Test resource contention, throttling, exhaustion, and workload interference.","verification_method":"Measure or observe resource isolation under representative concurrent workloads; do not infer performance without evidence.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established capacity-management and multi-tenant architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B57-R002","D41-B57-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B57-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Architecture","concept":"Vertical scaling vs horizontal scaling","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should an architecture scale vertically instead of horizontally?","answer":"Vertical scaling increases the capacity of individual resources and can preserve a simpler architecture, while horizontal scaling adds instances and generally requires distribution-aware application design. Vertical scaling is useful when workload characteristics or software constraints make distribution difficult; horizontal scaling is useful when independent replication is feasible.","principle":"Scale in the dimension that the workload and software architecture can support reliably.","requirements":["Identify resource bottleneck","Determine scalability mechanism","Assess statefulness","Assess distribution complexity"],"constraints":["Vertical capacity has platform limits","Horizontal scaling can introduce coordination and consistency complexity"],"objective":"Select an appropriate scaling dimension.","alternatives":["Vertical scaling","Horizontal scaling","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Vertical scaling simplifies application topology but has capacity limits; horizontal scaling improves aggregate capacity and redundancy but increases distributed-system complexity.","decision_factors":["State","Bottleneck","Capacity limits","Consistency","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Horizontally scaling stateful workloads without coordination","Assuming vertical scaling is unlimited","Scaling the wrong resource dimension"],"security_implication":"Additional horizontal instances expand the number of execution identities and network endpoints.","performance_implication":"Scaling direction must match the actual bottleneck; no universal performance advantage exists.","testing_guidance":"Test scaling transitions, state consistency, instance failure, and resource saturation.","verification_method":"Use profiling and capacity evidence to identify the actual bottleneck before selecting a scaling strategy.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established scalability engineering principles"],"related_records":["D41-B57-R001","D41-B57-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B57-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Architecture","concept":"Shared infrastructure vs isolated infrastructure","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should infrastructure be isolated between workloads?","answer":"Isolation is appropriate when workloads have incompatible security, compliance, availability, resource, or failure-domain requirements. Shared infrastructure is efficient when workloads can safely share governance and resource boundaries.","principle":"Align infrastructure isolation with actual trust, failure, and compliance boundaries rather than organizational preference alone.","requirements":["Define trust boundaries","Define failure domains","Define compliance requirements","Define resource constraints"],"constraints":["Isolation increases infrastructure and operational overhead","Shared infrastructure requires stronger multi-tenant controls"],"objective":"Select an infrastructure isolation boundary.","alternatives":["Shared infrastructure","Partially isolated infrastructure","Fully isolated infrastructure"],"tradeoff":"Isolation reduces interference and can simplify some compliance controls but increases cost and management overhead.","decision_factors":["Security","Compliance","Availability","Cost","Resource contention","Blast radius"],"failure_modes":["Sharing workloads with incompatible trust requirements","Creating excessive isolation without operational justification","Assuming infrastructure isolation eliminates application vulnerabilities"],"security_implication":"Infrastructure isolation can reduce blast radius but should not replace application authorization or secure configuration.","performance_implication":"Dedicated infrastructure may reduce contention but can lower aggregate utilization.","testing_guidance":"Test cross-boundary access, failure propagation, resource contention, and recovery.","verification_method":"Verify actual infrastructure boundaries and access paths rather than relying only on logical labels.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","Established isolation architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B57-R001","D41-B57-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B57-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Architecture","concept":"Elastic autoscaling vs reserved capacity","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should an architecture use autoscaling instead of fixed reserved capacity?","answer":"Autoscaling is useful when workload demand varies materially and resources can be added or removed safely. Reserved capacity is useful when workloads are stable or when startup and scaling delays make reactive capacity unsuitable.","principle":"Use elasticity where demand variability and workload startup characteristics make dynamic capacity practical.","requirements":["Define demand variability","Define scaling latency","Define minimum capacity","Define maximum capacity"],"constraints":["Autoscaling cannot eliminate finite capacity limits","Scaling decisions require reliable signals and safe workload behavior"],"objective":"Select a capacity-management strategy.","alternatives":["Fixed capacity","Autoscaling","Baseline plus autoscaling"],"tradeoff":"Autoscaling can improve utilization during variable demand but introduces scaling delay and control complexity; fixed capacity provides predictability but may waste resources or fail under bursts.","decision_factors":["Demand variability","Startup time","Capacity limits","Cost","Predictability"],"failure_modes":["Autoscaling on noisy signals","Scaling too slowly for workload bursts","Autoscaling stateful components without safe coordination"],"security_implication":"Automated resource creation must use controlled identities and approved configuration templates.","performance_implication":"Scaling latency can cause temporary saturation; baseline capacity should reflect known minimum demand and scaling characteristics.","testing_guidance":"Test scale-up, scale-down, burst demand, metric failures, and maximum-capacity behavior.","verification_method":"Validate scaling policy against measured workload characteristics; do not claim capacity performance without measurements.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established autoscaling and capacity-planning practices"],"related_records":["D41-B57-R002","D41-B57-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B57-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B57","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Resource Architecture","concept":"Shared failure domain vs independent failure domains","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"When should components be placed into separate failure domains?","answer":"Independent failure domains reduce correlated failure risk when components have sufficiently independent infrastructure or operational dependencies. Shared domains simplify communication and operations but allow a common infrastructure failure to affect multiple components.","principle":"Separate critical components across failure domains when correlated failure risk materially affects availability objectives.","requirements":["Identify common failure causes","Define availability objectives","Identify independence of domains","Define failover behavior"],"constraints":["Independent domains can increase network and operational complexity","Not all apparent domains are actually failure-independent"],"objective":"Control correlated failure risk.","alternatives":["Shared domain","Independent domains","Tiered failure-domain strategy"],"tradeoff":"Separation can improve resilience against correlated failures but increases cost and coordination complexity.","decision_factors":["Blast radius","Availability","Cost","Operational complexity","Network dependency"],"failure_modes":["Assuming geographic or infrastructure separation guarantees independence","Creating nominally separate domains sharing the same critical dependency","Failing to test domain-level failure"],"security_implication":"Separate domains can also provide stronger isolation but must retain independent identity and authorization controls.","performance_implication":"Cross-domain communication can increase latency and network dependency.","testing_guidance":"Perform controlled failure-domain tests where safe and validate dependency independence.","verification_method":"Map shared dependencies and demonstrate that documented failure domains actually fail independently enough for the intended objective.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established reliability and failure-domain architecture practices","Google SRE engineering practices"],"related_records":["D41-B57-R003","D41-B57-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B58-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Centralized observability vs domain-owned observability","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Should observability be centralized or owned by individual teams?","answer":"Centralized observability infrastructure can provide consistent collection, retention, access control, and correlation, while domain ownership keeps instrumentation close to application semantics. A hybrid architecture often separates platform collection from domain-specific interpretation.","principle":"Centralize shared observability infrastructure while preserving domain ownership of meaningful application signals.","requirements":["Define telemetry ownership","Define common schema","Define retention","Define access controls"],"constraints":["Centralization can create bottlenecks and broad access concerns","Local ownership can create inconsistent telemetry"],"objective":"Choose an observability ownership model.","alternatives":["Centralized","Domain-owned","Hybrid platform plus domain ownership"],"tradeoff":"Centralization improves consistency and governance; domain ownership improves semantic quality and autonomy.","decision_factors":["Consistency","Ownership","Security","Cost","Semantic context"],"failure_modes":["Central platform becoming responsible for application semantics","Teams emitting incompatible telemetry formats","Broad telemetry access exposing sensitive data"],"security_implication":"Telemetry frequently contains sensitive operational or user data and requires explicit access controls and minimization.","performance_implication":"Telemetry collection adds resource and network overhead; asynchronous pipelines can reduce application-path coupling.","testing_guidance":"Test telemetry loss, overload, redaction, access control, and correlation across services.","verification_method":"Verify required signals are available while sensitive data is appropriately controlled.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["OpenTelemetry documentation family","Google SRE engineering practices"],"related_records":["D41-B58-R002","D41-B58-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B58-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Synchronous telemetry vs asynchronous telemetry","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should telemetry transmission be decoupled from the application request path?","answer":"Asynchronous telemetry reduces direct dependency between application latency and telemetry transport, while synchronous transmission can provide stronger immediate delivery semantics but increases request-path coupling.","principle":"Keep observability transport off critical application paths when telemetry loss can be tolerated or buffered safely.","requirements":["Define telemetry loss tolerance","Define buffering","Define delivery guarantees","Define shutdown behavior"],"constraints":["Asynchronous buffering can lose data during process termination or resource exhaustion","Synchronous telemetry can amplify telemetry-system failures into application failures"],"objective":"Choose a telemetry delivery architecture.","alternatives":["Synchronous","Asynchronous","Buffered hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Asynchronous delivery reduces critical-path coupling but introduces buffering and possible loss; synchronous delivery improves immediate handoff but increases latency and dependency risk.","decision_factors":["Latency","Loss tolerance","Buffering","Availability","Shutdown semantics"],"failure_modes":["Blocking application requests on telemetry infrastructure","Dropping buffered telemetry without monitoring","Allowing telemetry queues to exhaust application resources"],"security_implication":"Buffered telemetry must remain protected and must not bypass data-classification controls.","performance_implication":"Synchronous export adds request-path work; asynchronous export consumes background resources.","testing_guidance":"Test exporter outage, buffer exhaustion, process shutdown, and telemetry loss behavior.","verification_method":"Verify documented loss and latency behavior under telemetry infrastructure failure.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["OpenTelemetry documentation family","Established observability architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B58-R001","D41-B58-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B58-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Full-fidelity telemetry vs sampling","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should observability data be sampled rather than retained at full fidelity?","answer":"Sampling can control storage and processing cost when telemetry volume is high and complete retention is unnecessary. Full fidelity is appropriate when every event is required for correctness, audit, incident investigation, or compliance.","principle":"Sample only when the information lost through sampling is acceptable for the intended analytical and operational purpose.","requirements":["Define required evidence","Define retention objectives","Define sampling policy","Preserve critical events"],"constraints":["Sampling can remove evidence needed for rare failures","Adaptive or tail-based sampling requires additional processing logic"],"objective":"Control telemetry volume without losing required evidence.","alternatives":["Full fidelity","Head sampling","Tail/adaptive sampling","Selective critical-event retention"],"tradeoff":"Sampling reduces cost and volume but can reduce visibility into rare or low-frequency events.","decision_factors":["Cost","Failure rarity","Compliance","Diagnostic value","Traffic volume"],"failure_modes":["Sampling security-critical events","Sampling before critical context becomes available","Assuming sampled telemetry represents all traffic"],"security_implication":"Security and audit events may require stronger retention guarantees than ordinary diagnostic telemetry.","performance_implication":"Sampling reduces telemetry processing and storage overhead but adds sampling computation.","testing_guidance":"Test sampling policy, critical-event preservation, and representative rare-event visibility.","verification_method":"Review sampling policy against required observability and compliance evidence.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["OpenTelemetry documentation family","Established observability practices"],"related_records":["D41-B58-R001","D41-B58-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B58-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"In-process instrumentation vs sidecar or agent instrumentation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should observability instrumentation run inside the application process versus externally?","answer":"In-process instrumentation can provide rich application semantics and direct context, while external instrumentation can reduce application-code changes and standardize collection. External approaches may have less access to application-specific semantics.","principle":"Use in-process instrumentation for semantic signals and external collection where platform-level signals or low application intrusion are more valuable.","requirements":["Identify required telemetry context","Define instrumentation ownership","Define deployment constraints","Define performance overhead"],"constraints":["In-process instrumentation shares application resources","External agents cannot automatically observe all application semantics"],"objective":"Select an instrumentation boundary.","alternatives":["In-process","External agent","Sidecar","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"In-process provides semantic richness but increases application coupling; external instrumentation improves standardization but can have less context.","decision_factors":["Semantic depth","Deployment model","Language support","Overhead","Ownership"],"failure_modes":["Expecting external instrumentation to infer business semantics","Embedding excessive instrumentation in hot paths","Running unbounded external agents"],"security_implication":"Instrumentation must not expose secrets or sensitive payloads regardless of placement.","performance_implication":"Instrumentation overhead must be measured or characterized for latency-sensitive paths; no universal overhead figure should be assumed.","testing_guidance":"Test telemetry correctness, redaction, instrumentation failure, and application behavior with instrumentation disabled.","verification_method":"Verify that the selected mechanism captures required signals without violating performance or security constraints.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["OpenTelemetry documentation family","Established observability practices"],"related_records":["D41-B58-R001","D41-B58-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B58-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B58","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Observability Architecture","concept":"Application-level health checks vs infrastructure-level health checks","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the architectural difference between application health and infrastructure health checks?","answer":"Infrastructure checks establish whether a process or endpoint is reachable, while application-level checks determine whether meaningful application dependencies and invariants are functioning. Deep checks can provide better semantic coverage but may create dependency or load problems if executed too aggressively.","principle":"Health checks should test the minimum condition required by their purpose and should not turn every dependency into a critical synchronous probe.","requirements":["Define health-check purpose","Define shallow and deep checks","Define dependency policy","Define failure semantics"],"constraints":["Deep checks can overload dependencies","A process can be reachable while the application is functionally degraded"],"objective":"Design health checks that accurately represent the intended health signal.","alternatives":["Infrastructure-level check","Application-level check","Layered checks"],"tradeoff":"Deep checks provide stronger semantic information but increase dependency coupling and probe cost.","decision_factors":["Probe purpose","Dependency count","Failure isolation","Probe frequency","Recovery behavior"],"failure_modes":["Making every dependency mandatory for liveness","Using deep probes at excessive frequency","Confusing readiness with liveness"],"security_implication":"Health endpoints should expose minimal information and should not leak sensitive dependency details.","performance_implication":"Expensive health checks can generate unnecessary dependency load and affect service performance.","testing_guidance":"Test dependency failures, startup readiness, process liveness, and recovery transitions.","verification_method":"Verify that each health signal corresponds to its documented operational meaning.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Kubernetes probe documentation family","Google SRE engineering practices"],"related_records":["D41-B58-R001","D41-B58-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B59-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"Local state vs external state store","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should application state be externalized from the process?","answer":"External state is useful when multiple instances must share state, state must survive process replacement, or independent scaling is required. Local state can be simpler and faster when it is intentionally ephemeral and tied to a single process lifecycle.","principle":"Externalize state only when lifecycle, sharing, durability, or scaling requirements require it.","requirements":["Classify state durability","Define ownership","Define sharing requirements","Define recovery behavior"],"constraints":["External state introduces network and consistency dependencies","Local state is lost when the process lifecycle ends unless separately persisted"],"objective":"Select state placement.","alternatives":["Local state","External state store","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"External state improves sharing and durability options but adds operational and network complexity.","decision_factors":["Durability","Sharing","Latency","Scaling","Recovery","Consistency"],"failure_modes":["Treating local process state as durable","Externalizing state without defining consistency","Creating a shared state store with no ownership boundaries"],"security_implication":"External stores become additional sensitive-data and authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Local state generally avoids network access; external state adds I/O and serialization overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test process restart, concurrent access, store failure, stale state, and recovery.","verification_method":"Verify lifecycle and consistency semantics against the state contract.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established distributed-state architecture principles"],"related_records":["D41-B51-R003","D41-B59-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B59-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"Session affinity vs shared session state","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Should session state use sticky routing or shared storage?","answer":"Session affinity keeps a client associated with a particular instance and can reduce shared-state requirements, while shared session state allows instances to serve requests interchangeably. Sticky routing can simplify local state but complicates failover and load distribution.","principle":"Use session affinity only when its operational constraints are acceptable; shared state is preferable when interchangeable instances are required.","requirements":["Define session lifetime","Define failover behavior","Define scaling model","Define state-sharing requirements"],"constraints":["Sticky sessions reduce routing flexibility","Shared state introduces storage dependency and synchronization requirements"],"objective":"Select a session architecture.","alternatives":["Session affinity","Shared session store","Stateless tokens"],"tradeoff":"Affinity can simplify local state but weakens instance interchangeability; shared state improves routing flexibility but adds infrastructure.","decision_factors":["Failover","Scaling","Latency","State size","Storage availability"],"failure_modes":["Losing sessions when an instance fails","Uneven load caused by sticky routing","Storing excessive session state remotely"],"security_implication":"Session identifiers require secure handling regardless of storage strategy; authorization must not depend solely on routing affinity.","performance_implication":"Remote session storage adds network access; sticky routing can reduce that access but may create load imbalance.","testing_guidance":"Test instance failure, scaling, routing changes, session expiration, and concurrent session access.","verification_method":"Verify session continuity and security across instance replacement and routing changes.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established session-management architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B59-R001","D41-B59-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B59-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"Server-side session state vs signed client state","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should session state remain server-side instead of being represented in client-held signed state?","answer":"Server-side sessions provide direct control over mutable state and revocation but require server-side storage. Signed client state can reduce server-side storage requirements but must be carefully bounded because integrity protection does not automatically provide confidentiality, revocation, or unlimited storage capacity.","principle":"Choose client-held state only when its mutability, sensitivity, size, revocation, and trust requirements are compatible with the security model.","requirements":["Define confidentiality requirements","Define revocation requirements","Define state size","Define signing and key management"],"constraints":["Signed data is not automatically encrypted","Revocation can be harder with self-contained client state"],"objective":"Select a secure session-state representation.","alternatives":["Server-side session","Signed client state","Encrypted and authenticated client state"],"tradeoff":"Client-held state reduces server storage but shifts state validation, key management, and revocation complexity to the protocol.","decision_factors":["Revocation","Confidentiality","State size","Key management","Scalability"],"failure_modes":["Putting sensitive data into merely signed state","Failing to validate signatures and claims","Assuming expiration alone provides immediate revocation"],"security_implication":"Cryptographic integrity, confidentiality, key rotation, replay handling, and claim validation must be explicitly designed.","performance_implication":"Client-held state can reduce session-store lookups but increases request payload size and cryptographic processing.","testing_guidance":"Test tampering, expiration, replay, key rotation, malformed state, and revocation requirements.","verification_method":"Verify cryptographic validation and security properties using the selected protocol and implementation documentation.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["RFC 7519","Established session and token security practices"],"related_records":["D41-B59-R002","D41-B59-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B59-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"Strongly centralized state vs partitioned state","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should state be partitioned instead of kept in a single shared state system?","answer":"Partitioning state can improve scalability, ownership boundaries, and fault isolation when access patterns and domain ownership permit it. A centralized state system can simplify cross-domain operations but may become a bottleneck and create broad coupling.","principle":"Partition state along meaningful ownership and access boundaries when scaling or isolation requirements justify it.","requirements":["Define ownership boundaries","Analyze access patterns","Define cross-partition operations","Define consistency requirements"],"constraints":["Partitioning complicates cross-partition queries and transactions","Centralized state can become a shared bottleneck"],"objective":"Choose an appropriate state partitioning model.","alternatives":["Centralized state","Partitioned state","Hierarchical partitioning"],"tradeoff":"Partitioning improves independent scaling and ownership but increases coordination complexity for cross-partition operations.","decision_factors":["Scale","Ownership","Access locality","Cross-partition operations","Consistency"],"failure_modes":["Partitioning along arbitrary technical boundaries","Creating excessive cross-partition transactions","Centralizing all state despite independent domain ownership"],"security_implication":"Partitions can reinforce data-isolation boundaries but must not replace application authorization.","performance_implication":"Partition-local operations can reduce contention; cross-partition operations may increase network and coordination cost.","testing_guidance":"Test partition routing, rebalancing, cross-partition operations, and partial partition failure.","verification_method":"Validate partition ownership, routing correctness, and cross-partition consistency guarantees.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established distributed-data architecture principles"],"related_records":["D41-B59-R001","D41-B59-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B59-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B59","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"State Management","concept":"Ephemeral state vs durable state","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should ephemeral and durable state be distinguished architecturally?","answer":"Ephemeral state can be safely reconstructed or discarded without violating system correctness. Durable state is required to preserve information across process or infrastructure failures. Confusing these categories creates recovery and correctness failures.","principle":"Classify state according to the consequences of loss, not according to where it happens to be stored.","requirements":["Define loss consequences","Define reconstruction path","Define persistence guarantees","Define recovery objectives"],"constraints":["Some state can be reconstructed only from expensive or unavailable sources","Persistence does not automatically imply correct recovery"],"objective":"Establish correct durability semantics.","alternatives":["Ephemeral state","Durable state","Mixed state"],"tradeoff":"Ephemeral state simplifies storage and lifecycle but requires reliable reconstruction; durable state adds persistence cost and operational complexity.","decision_factors":["Loss impact","Recovery","Reconstruction","Storage cost","Consistency"],"failure_modes":["Treating cached state as authoritative","Persisting unnecessary transient state","Failing to test recovery from durable state"],"security_implication":"Durable state increases the lifetime and exposure of sensitive information and therefore requires appropriate protection.","performance_implication":"Durability mechanisms introduce I/O and storage overhead that should be characterized rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":"Test process termination, storage failure, recovery, corruption handling, and reconstruction.","verification_method":"Demonstrate that documented durability semantics match actual recovery behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established reliability and state-management principles"],"related_records":["D41-B59-R001","D41-B59-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B60-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Simplification","concept":"Abstraction layer vs direct dependency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture introduce an abstraction layer over a dependency?","answer":"An abstraction is justified when it protects meaningful variability, enforces a stable contract, or isolates a dependency whose replacement is plausible. An abstraction with no meaningful boundary can add indirection without reducing coupling.","principle":"Create abstractions around real variability or stable domain contracts, not merely around every external dependency.","requirements":["Identify expected variability","Define abstraction contract","Assess replacement likelihood","Assess maintenance cost"],"constraints":["Abstractions can hide important dependency semantics","Premature abstractions can make simple code harder to understand"],"objective":"Determine whether an abstraction boundary provides sufficient architectural value.","alternatives":["Direct dependency","Thin abstraction","Full adapter or port"],"tradeoff":"Abstraction can reduce coupling and preserve options but introduces conceptual and maintenance overhead.","decision_factors":["Variability","Replacement likelihood","Domain semantics","Complexity","Testing"],"failure_modes":["Generic abstraction that leaks all underlying APIs","Creating abstractions without actual alternate implementations","Hiding important dependency-specific behavior"],"security_implication":"Abstractions should not weaken security semantics or hide required authorization and validation behavior.","performance_implication":"Additional abstraction layers may introduce indirection; actual impact depends on implementation and runtime.","testing_guidance":"Test both abstraction contract behavior and important dependency-specific semantics.","verification_method":"Review whether the abstraction isolates a documented variability or contract boundary.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established software architecture and dependency inversion practices"],"related_records":["D41-B60-R002","D41-B60-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B60-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Simplification","concept":"Platform standardization vs local optimization","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should teams follow a common platform standard instead of optimizing locally?","answer":"Standardization reduces operational variation and enables shared tooling, while local optimization can better satisfy specialized workload requirements. The architecture should distinguish mandatory platform constraints from areas where local divergence provides measurable value.","principle":"Standardize where consistency creates material organizational or operational value and permit justified divergence where requirements genuinely differ.","requirements":["Define platform standards","Define exception criteria","Define ownership","Define lifecycle"],"constraints":["Standards can become outdated or overgeneralized","Local divergence increases support and knowledge costs"],"objective":"Balance organizational consistency with workload-specific optimization.","alternatives":["Strict standardization","Team autonomy","Governed exceptions"],"tradeoff":"Standardization improves repeatability and operational leverage; autonomy can improve local fit but increases ecosystem diversity.","decision_factors":["Operational leverage","Workload uniqueness","Security","Supportability","Cost","Performance evidence"],"failure_modes":["Forcing inappropriate technology through standards","Allowing uncontrolled technology proliferation","Approving performance exceptions without measurements"],"security_implication":"Standard platforms can provide consistent security controls, but exceptions require equivalent security review.","performance_implication":"Local optimization should be supported by workload-specific measurements rather than assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Test standard and exception paths against their documented operational requirements.","verification_method":"Require evidence for exceptions and periodically review whether divergence remains justified.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established platform-engineering and architecture-governance practices"],"related_records":["D41-B60-R001","D41-B60-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B60-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Simplification","concept":"Generic framework vs purpose-built component","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should a generic framework be preferred over a purpose-built component?","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","answer":"Generic frameworks can reduce duplicated infrastructure work and provide established capabilities, while purpose-built components can better fit specialized requirements and reduce irrelevant complexity. Selection should consider actual requirements, framework constraints, operational maturity, and long-term ownership.","principle":"Prefer reusable frameworks when their abstraction matches the workload; avoid adopting generality that creates more complexity than it removes.","requirements":["Define required capabilities","Evaluate framework constraints","Assess operational maturity","Assess ownership burden"],"constraints":["Framework adoption creates dependency and upgrade obligations","Purpose-built components create direct maintenance responsibility"],"objective":"Choose the appropriate level of generality.","alternatives":["Generic framework","Purpose-built component","Thin framework usage"],"tradeoff":"Frameworks can accelerate common capabilities but impose conventions and lifecycle dependencies; purpose-built systems offer control at higher ownership cost.","decision_factors":["Fit","Complexity","Maturity","Customization","Lifecycle","Maintenance"],"failure_modes":["Adopting a framework for capabilities that are not needed","Reimplementing mature common functionality without justification","Becoming locked into framework-specific semantics"],"security_implication":"Framework security defaults and update processes become part of the system security boundary.","performance_implication":"Framework abstraction can introduce overhead depending on implementation; performance should be measured for critical paths.","testing_guidance":"Test framework integration, upgrade compatibility, security configuration, and critical workload behavior.","verification_method":"Compare required capabilities and constraints against actual framework behavior and documented lifecycle support.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established framework-selection and software architecture practices"],"related_records":["D41-B60-R001","D41-B60-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B60-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Simplification","concept":"Architecture consolidation vs independent systems","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should multiple similar systems be consolidated?","answer":"Consolidation can reduce duplicated operational infrastructure, skills, monitoring, and lifecycle management when systems have sufficiently similar requirements. Independent systems remain appropriate when trust, scaling, availability, lifecycle, or domain requirements materially differ.","principle":"Consolidate only when the resulting shared architecture preserves the important boundaries and does not create disproportionate coupling or blast radius.","requirements":["Identify duplicated capabilities","Compare requirements","Assess failure domains","Assess ownership","Define migration strategy"],"constraints":["Consolidation can create a larger blast radius","Similar-looking systems may have materially different requirements"],"objective":"Determine whether consolidation reduces architectural complexity safely.","alternatives":["Consolidation","Independent systems","Shared platform with isolated workloads"],"tradeoff":"Consolidation reduces duplication but increases shared dependency and potential blast radius.","decision_factors":["Similarity","Failure isolation","Security","Scaling","Ownership","Operational cost"],"failure_modes":["Combining systems with incompatible trust requirements","Creating a shared bottleneck","Migrating without preserving workload-specific guarantees"],"security_implication":"Consolidation can expand the blast radius of privilege or configuration errors and therefore requires strong tenant and workload isolation.","performance_implication":"Shared infrastructure can improve utilization but can also introduce contention.","testing_guidance":"Test workload isolation, failure propagation, scaling, migration correctness, and resource contention.","verification_method":"Demonstrate that consolidated workloads retain their documented security, availability, and performance requirements.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established platform-consolidation and architecture-governance practices"],"related_records":["D41-B57-R003","D41-B60-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","depends_on"]}
{"record_id":"D41-B60-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch_id":"B60","topic":"Architecture Trade-offs","subtopic":"Architecture Evolution","concept":"Architecture simplification vs preserving optionality","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture remove flexibility instead of preserving future options?","answer":"Removing unused flexibility can reduce complexity and maintenance burden when there is insufficient evidence that the option will be needed. Preserving optionality is useful when a future decision is genuinely uncertain and the cost of keeping the option is low.","principle":"Preserve options deliberately when their future value justifies their ongoing complexity; otherwise simplify and make the current architecture explicit.","requirements":["Identify actual uncertainty","Estimate option-maintenance cost","Identify irreversible decisions","Define revisit conditions"],"constraints":["Over-preserving options creates permanent complexity","Removing an option can increase future migration cost"],"objective":"Balance present simplicity against future architectural flexibility.","alternatives":["Simplify now","Preserve option","Design a reversible intermediate architecture"],"tradeoff":"Simplification lowers current complexity; optionality can reduce future migration cost but carries ongoing cognitive and operational overhead.","decision_factors":["Uncertainty","Reversibility","Maintenance cost","Migration cost","Current complexity"],"failure_modes":["Preserving speculative extension points indefinitely","Deleting a low-cost high-value option prematurely","Confusing hypothetical future requirements with actual constraints"],"security_implication":"Unused flexibility can create additional attack surface and configuration paths.","performance_implication":"Additional abstraction or indirection can affect performance, but claims should be measured for critical paths.","testing_guidance":"Test both the simplified current architecture and any deliberately preserved extension boundary.","verification_method":"Document the uncertainty, option cost, and revisit trigger; review the decision when evidence changes.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","truth_class":"RECOMMENDATION","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Established architecture decision and option-value principles","Architecture decision record practices"],"related_records":["D41-B60-R001","D41-B60-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B61-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Edge and locality architecture","subtopic":"CDN versus origin delivery","concept":"Content delivery network versus origin-served delivery","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a system use a CDN instead of serving content directly from the origin?","answer":"A CDN is useful when geographically distributed users, cacheable content, latency reduction, or origin offloading materially matter. Direct origin delivery is simpler and may be preferable for low-volume, highly dynamic, private, or non-cacheable workloads.","principle":"Introduce an edge caching layer when its latency, bandwidth, availability, or origin-offload benefits justify cache invalidation, consistency, cost, and operational complexity.","requirements":["Content must have a defined cacheability policy","Cache behavior must not expose data across authorization boundaries","Origin remains authoritative unless the architecture explicitly assigns another authority"],"constraints":["Private or highly personalized responses may have limited cacheability","Incorrect cache keys can cause data exposure","Cache invalidation introduces consistency considerations"],"preconditions":["Content delivery requirements are understood","Cache-control semantics can be defined"],"objective":"Select an appropriate content-delivery topology","alternatives":["Direct origin delivery","CDN-backed delivery"],"tradeoff":"CDN architecture can reduce user-perceived latency and origin load but adds cache management, invalidation, routing, and observability complexity.","decision_factors":["Geographic distribution","Content cacheability","Latency requirements","Origin bandwidth","Invalidation frequency","Security boundaries","Operational maturity"],"failure_modes":["Serving stale content","Incorrect cache-key partitioning","Unexpected cache bypass","Origin overload after cache expiry"],"security_implication":"Authenticated or tenant-specific content must not become publicly or cross-tenant cacheable through incorrect cache configuration.","performance_implication":"A CDN can reduce origin requests and network distance for cacheable content; exact gains require measurement in the target workload.","testing_guidance":"Test cache hits, misses, invalidation, authorization boundaries, stale responses, and origin fallback.","verification_method":"Review cache policy, cache keys, authorization behavior, and measured request paths; execute integration tests where possible.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["HTTP caching specifications","Established distributed-systems and web-delivery engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B07-R001","D41-B18-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B61-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Edge and locality architecture","subtopic":"Edge compute versus origin compute","concept":"Edge execution versus centralized origin execution","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should computation execute near users or devices instead of at a centralized origin?","answer":"Edge execution is appropriate when latency, locality, bandwidth reduction, or regional processing requirements justify distributing computation. Centralized execution is preferable when computation requires centralized state, complex coordination, sensitive dependencies, or simpler operational management.","principle":"Place computation where the dominant constraint—latency, bandwidth, locality, coordination, or operational simplicity—requires it.","requirements":["Execution location must be explicitly defined","Data and authority boundaries must remain clear","Deployment and observability must cover every execution location"],"constraints":["Edge environments may have fewer resources","Distributed deployment increases operational complexity","State synchronization can become difficult"],"objective":"Choose the appropriate computation location","alternatives":["Centralized origin execution","Edge execution","Hybrid edge-origin execution"],"tradeoff":"Edge execution can reduce latency and upstream traffic but increases deployment distribution, consistency, security, and observability complexity.","decision_factors":["Latency sensitivity","Data volume","Bandwidth cost","State requirements","Privacy/locality constraints","Edge resource limits","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Stale edge configuration","Partial regional deployment","Inconsistent policy versions","Loss of origin connectivity"],"security_implication":"Every edge execution environment becomes an additional trust boundary requiring independent authentication, authorization, update, and secret-management controls.","performance_implication":"Edge placement can reduce network round trips but may increase cold-start, synchronization, or resource constraints depending on the platform.","testing_guidance":"Test execution with origin connectivity, without origin connectivity, across version skew, and under partial edge availability.","verification_method":"Validate deployment topology, policy propagation, data locality, failure behavior, and edge-origin contract behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed-systems engineering literature","Cloud and edge-computing architecture guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B51-R001","D41-B57-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B61-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Edge and locality architecture","subtopic":"Compute-to-data versus data-to-compute","concept":"Moving computation to data versus moving data to computation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should a system move data to the compute environment or execute computation close to where the data resides?","answer":"Compute-to-data is attractive when datasets are large, sensitive, expensive to transfer, or locality constrained. Data-to-compute can be simpler when datasets are small, centralized, or repeatedly consumed by a stable compute environment.","principle":"Minimize unnecessary data movement when transfer cost, latency, privacy, or locality dominates computation cost.","requirements":["Data ownership and locality are known","Transfer requirements are measurable","The execution environment can access required data safely"],"constraints":["Compute placement may limit available libraries or resources","Data movement may be necessary for some algorithms","Distributed computation can increase operational complexity"],"objective":"Select the lower-risk computation/data placement","alternatives":["Move data to compute","Move compute to data","Hybrid partitioning"],"tradeoff":"Compute-to-data reduces data movement but can constrain execution placement; data-to-compute simplifies compute management but may increase transfer, latency, and exposure.","decision_factors":["Dataset size","Transfer cost","Privacy","Data residency","Compute requirements","Latency","Network reliability"],"failure_modes":["Unexpected large data transfers","Unauthorized data replication","Local compute resource exhaustion","Stale replicated data"],"security_implication":"Moving data creates additional copies and trust boundaries; moving compute requires secure execution and access controls near the data.","performance_implication":"Data movement can dominate total execution time for large datasets; actual behavior should be measured for the target topology.","testing_guidance":"Measure transfer volume, execution latency, failure recovery, and authorization behavior for both placement models where practical.","verification_method":"Validate data-flow diagrams, access controls, transfer boundaries, and measured workload behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed data-processing engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B61-R002","D41-B59-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B61-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Edge and locality architecture","subtopic":"Geographic request routing","concept":"Latency-based routing versus deterministic regional routing","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should request routing optimize measured latency versus explicitly selecting a geographic region?","answer":"Latency-oriented routing can adapt to network conditions, while deterministic regional routing provides stronger predictability for residency, compliance, tenancy, or operational boundaries.","principle":"Choose routing based on the dominant requirement rather than assuming the lowest network latency is always the correct destination.","requirements":["Routing policy must be explicit","Regional eligibility must be enforceable","Health and capacity signals must be trustworthy"],"constraints":["Latency measurements can change","Regional routing can increase latency","Failover may conflict with residency or tenancy constraints"],"objective":"Select a routing policy consistent with system requirements","alternatives":["Latency-based routing","Geographic routing","Tenant-pinned routing","Policy-constrained hybrid routing"],"tradeoff":"Latency optimization improves responsiveness under suitable conditions but can conflict with residency, tenancy, and deterministic operational boundaries.","decision_factors":["Data residency","Tenant placement","Latency","Capacity","Health","Failover policy","Regulatory constraints"],"failure_modes":["Routing to an ineligible region","Oscillating routes","Failover violating residency requirements","Routing based on stale health information"],"security_implication":"Routing must enforce authorization and data-location constraints rather than treating geography as a security control by itself.","performance_implication":"Latency-based routing may reduce network delay but can introduce route instability if measurements fluctuate.","testing_guidance":"Test normal routing, unhealthy-region routing, policy-restricted regions, and route changes during network degradation.","verification_method":"Verify routing decisions against explicit policy constraints and simulated regional failures.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed-routing engineering practice","Cloud architecture guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B07-R004","D41-B19-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B61-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B61","topic":"Edge and locality architecture","subtopic":"Edge state placement","concept":"Local edge state versus centralized state","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should state be maintained near the edge instead of centrally?","answer":"Local edge state can improve latency and tolerate temporary disconnection, while centralized state simplifies consistency and authority management. Edge state is appropriate only when synchronization and conflict semantics are explicitly designed.","principle":"Do not distribute mutable state merely to reduce latency; distribute it when locality or availability requirements justify the synchronization complexity.","requirements":["State authority must be defined","Synchronization semantics must be explicit","Conflict handling must be specified"],"constraints":["Distributed mutable state increases consistency complexity","Disconnected operation may create conflicting updates","State replication increases storage and security surfaces"],"objective":"Choose an appropriate state-placement model","alternatives":["Centralized authoritative state","Replicated edge state","Hybrid authoritative-plus-local state"],"tradeoff":"Edge state improves locality and resilience to connectivity loss but introduces synchronization, conflict, and authority complexity.","decision_factors":["Offline requirements","Latency","Consistency requirements","Conflict frequency","Data sensitivity","State size"],"failure_modes":["Conflicting updates","Stale state","Lost synchronization","Incorrect authority selection"],"security_implication":"Every replicated state copy increases the number of locations requiring access control, encryption, retention, and deletion guarantees.","performance_implication":"Local state can reduce network round trips but synchronization overhead may offset the benefit.","testing_guidance":"Test disconnected operation, reconnection, conflicting updates, stale state, and synchronization failure.","verification_method":"Verify state-authority rules and execute conflict/recovery scenarios where possible.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed-systems engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B59-R001","D41-B61-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B62-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Multi-tenant architecture","subtopic":"Tenant isolation model","concept":"Pooled versus silo tenant architecture","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should tenants share infrastructure versus receive dedicated infrastructure?","answer":"Pooled architecture improves resource utilization and operational efficiency, while silo architecture provides stronger isolation and tenant-specific control. Hybrid models can assign isolation according to risk, contractual, or workload requirements.","principle":"Tenant isolation should match the required security, performance, compliance, and operational boundary rather than defaulting to maximum or minimum isolation.","requirements":["Tenant identity must propagate through every relevant boundary","Cross-tenant access must be prevented","Resource ownership must be auditable"],"constraints":["Silos increase infrastructure cost","Pools increase noisy-neighbor and isolation risks","Hybrid models require explicit placement rules"],"objective":"Select an appropriate tenant-isolation topology","alternatives":["Pooled","Siloed","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Pooling improves utilization and simplifies fleet management; silos strengthen isolation but increase cost and operational overhead.","decision_factors":["Security requirements","Compliance","Tenant size","Workload variability","Data residency","Cost","Operational complexity"],"failure_modes":["Cross-tenant data access","Noisy-neighbor degradation","Incorrect tenant routing","Inconsistent tenant configuration"],"security_implication":"Tenant isolation must be enforced through data, authorization, compute, and operational boundaries rather than relying on tenant identifiers alone.","performance_implication":"Pooling can improve utilization but can expose tenants to shared-resource contention.","testing_guidance":"Test cross-tenant authorization, data isolation, resource contention, tenant lifecycle operations, and placement changes.","verification_method":"Use isolation tests, authorization tests, resource-boundary tests, and architecture review.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Multi-tenant architecture engineering practice","Cloud security architecture guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B57-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","specializes"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B62-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Multi-tenant architecture","subtopic":"Tenant configuration","concept":"Shared configuration versus tenant-specific configuration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should configuration be globally shared or explicitly scoped per tenant?","answer":"Shared configuration reduces duplication when behavior is genuinely uniform. Tenant-specific configuration is necessary when contractual, security, workflow, or feature differences exist.","principle":"Configuration scope should follow the ownership and behavioral boundary of the setting.","requirements":["Configuration scope must be explicit","Defaults must be deterministic","Tenant overrides must be auditable"],"constraints":["Uncontrolled overrides create configuration drift","Global changes can affect every tenant","Tenant-specific configuration increases testing combinations"],"objective":"Choose configuration scope","alternatives":["Global configuration","Tenant-scoped configuration","Layered defaults plus tenant overrides"],"tradeoff":"Global configuration simplifies governance; tenant-scoped configuration increases flexibility at the cost of configuration complexity and test surface.","decision_factors":["Behavioral variance","Security requirements","Tenant contracts","Operational control","Configuration cardinality"],"failure_modes":["Tenant override leakage","Unexpected global changes","Configuration precedence ambiguity","Untested combinations"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive settings should have explicit scope and authorization around modification.","performance_implication":"High-cardinality tenant configuration can increase lookup and caching complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test default resolution, override precedence, tenant isolation, configuration changes, and rollback.","verification_method":"Verify configuration precedence and execute representative tenant-isolation tests.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Configuration-management engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B55-R002","D41-B62-R001"],"relationships":["refines","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B62-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Multi-tenant architecture","subtopic":"Noisy-neighbor control","concept":"Shared resource pools versus tenant resource reservations","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should architecture balance shared utilization with tenant resource guarantees?","answer":"Shared pools maximize utilization but allow workloads to compete. Reservations or quotas provide stronger tenant guarantees but can leave capacity unused and require capacity management.","principle":"Use resource isolation when tenant-level service objectives are stronger than the efficiency benefits of unrestricted pooling.","requirements":["Resource dimensions must be measurable","Tenant limits must be enforceable","Overload behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Hard reservations can reduce utilization","Soft limits may not prevent contention","Different workloads consume resources differently"],"objective":"Control tenant resource contention","alternatives":["Unrestricted pooling","Quotas","Reservations","Priority-based allocation"],"tradeoff":"Stronger resource guarantees improve predictability but increase capacity-planning complexity and potentially reduce utilization.","decision_factors":["SLOs","Workload variability","Resource scarcity","Tenant criticality","Cost"],"failure_modes":["Noisy-neighbor impact","Quota starvation","Unused reserved capacity","Priority inversion"],"security_implication":"Resource isolation can be part of tenant isolation but must not substitute for authorization controls.","performance_implication":"Resource limits affect throughput, queueing, and tail latency; capacity assumptions should be measured.","testing_guidance":"Test contention, quota enforcement, priority behavior, exhaustion, and recovery after resource release.","verification_method":"Load-test resource policies where practical and verify enforcement independently of application intent.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Capacity-planning and multi-tenant systems engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B57-R003","D41-B17-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B62-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Multi-tenant architecture","subtopic":"Tenant routing","concept":"Shared routing layer versus tenant-dedicated routing","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should tenant routing be centralized through shared infrastructure or dedicated per tenant?","answer":"Shared routing reduces infrastructure duplication, while dedicated routing can provide stronger isolation, tenant-specific policies, or independent scaling.","principle":"Routing architecture should reflect tenant isolation and policy requirements rather than merely infrastructure convenience.","requirements":["Tenant identity must be established before tenant-specific routing decisions","Routing policies must be auditable","Fallback behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Shared routing creates a high-value control boundary","Dedicated routing increases operational components","Incorrect routing can cause cross-tenant exposure"],"objective":"Select tenant routing topology","alternatives":["Shared routing","Dedicated routing","Hybrid routing"],"tradeoff":"Shared routing is operationally efficient but concentrates tenant-routing responsibility; dedicated routing improves isolation at increased complexity.","decision_factors":["Isolation","Tenant scale","Policy variance","Failure domains","Operational cost"],"failure_modes":["Wrong-tenant routing","Routing-policy drift","Shared router overload","Incomplete tenant migration"],"security_implication":"Tenant routing is security-sensitive because a routing error can expose another tenant's resources.","performance_implication":"Shared routing can become a bottleneck; dedicated routing may increase network hops or operational overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test tenant identification, routing correctness, migration, failover, and unauthorized tenant access.","verification_method":"Verify routing invariants and execute cross-tenant negative tests.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Multi-tenant architecture engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B62-R001","D41-B61-R004"],"relationships":["requires","contrasts_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B62-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B62","topic":"Multi-tenant architecture","subtopic":"Tenant data locality","concept":"Centralized tenant data versus tenant-local data placement","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should tenant data be centralized versus placed according to tenant-specific locality requirements?","answer":"Centralization simplifies operations and cross-tenant platform capabilities, while tenant-local placement can satisfy residency, latency, contractual, or isolation requirements.","principle":"Data placement should follow explicit ownership, residency, latency, and isolation requirements.","requirements":["Tenant data location must be discoverable","Placement policy must be enforceable","Migration behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Local placement increases operational fragmentation","Centralization may violate locality requirements","Moving tenant data can require careful migration controls"],"objective":"Select tenant data-placement architecture","alternatives":["Centralized data platform","Region- or tenant-local placement","Hybrid placement"],"tradeoff":"Centralization reduces operational complexity; locality improves compliance and isolation but increases placement and migration complexity.","decision_factors":["Residency","Latency","Compliance","Tenant isolation","Operational complexity","Migration cost"],"failure_modes":["Data placed in an unauthorized location","Incomplete migration","Incorrect tenant-region mapping","Cross-region access violating policy"],"security_implication":"Data location is an important security and compliance boundary and must be enforced technically where required.","performance_implication":"Local placement can reduce network latency while increasing infrastructure fragmentation.","testing_guidance":"Test placement policy, tenant migration, unauthorized cross-location access, and failover behavior.","verification_method":"Verify placement metadata, access policy, and migration evidence.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Data-residency and distributed-data architecture guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B61-R004","D41-B62-R001"],"relationships":["requires","contrasts_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B63-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Security architecture trade-offs","subtopic":"Policy enforcement placement","concept":"Centralized policy enforcement versus distributed enforcement","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should security policy be enforced through a central policy component or independently at each service boundary?","answer":"Centralized policy can improve consistency and governance, while distributed enforcement can reduce dependency on a central component and place decisions close to protected resources. Robust systems may combine centralized policy definition with local enforcement.","principle":"Policy authority and policy enforcement need not be physically located in the same component.","requirements":["Every protected boundary must have an enforceable policy","Policy changes must be traceable","Failure behavior must be explicit"],"constraints":["Central policy services can become dependencies","Distributed policy creates consistency challenges","Security-critical decisions cannot silently fail open"],"objective":"Choose a policy architecture that balances consistency and resilience","alternatives":["Centralized enforcement","Distributed enforcement","Central policy with local enforcement"],"tradeoff":"Centralization simplifies policy governance but adds dependency and availability concerns; distributed enforcement improves locality but increases policy consistency challenges.","decision_factors":["Number of services","Failure tolerance","Policy complexity","Latency","Auditability","Security criticality"],"failure_modes":["Policy drift","Central policy outage","Fail-open behavior","Inconsistent enforcement"],"security_implication":"High-impact authorization boundaries should have explicit fail-safe behavior and independently testable enforcement.","performance_implication":"Remote policy decisions add network latency; local enforcement reduces dependency latency but may require policy distribution.","testing_guidance":"Test allow/deny decisions, policy updates, central-policy unavailability, stale policies, and fail-safe behavior.","verification_method":"Verify policy invariants and execute negative authorization tests.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST Zero Trust Architecture SP 800-207","Established authorization architecture practice"],"related_records":["D41-B11-R004","D41-B62-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B63-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Security architecture trade-offs","subtopic":"Authentication boundary","concept":"Central identity service versus service-local authentication logic","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should authentication be centralized or implemented independently by services?","answer":"Central identity systems reduce duplicated authentication logic and can provide consistent identity lifecycle management. Service-local authentication may be appropriate for specialized protocols or isolated systems but increases implementation responsibility.","principle":"Centralize identity authority where possible while keeping service authorization decisions aligned with local resource ownership.","requirements":["Identity lifecycle must be controlled","Tokens or credentials must be validated according to their protocol","Services must not trust identity claims without appropriate validation"],"constraints":["Central identity becomes an infrastructure dependency","Local implementations can diverge","Different protocols may require specialized validation"],"objective":"Select an authentication architecture","alternatives":["Central identity provider","Service-local authentication","Hybrid identity architecture"],"tradeoff":"Central identity improves consistency and lifecycle control; local authentication provides independence but increases duplication and security review surface.","decision_factors":["Protocol diversity","Identity lifecycle","Availability requirements","Service autonomy","Security governance"],"failure_modes":["Inconsistent identity validation","Expired credential acceptance","Identity-provider dependency failure","Duplicated insecure authentication logic"],"security_implication":"Authentication architecture must define credential validation, trust relationships, expiration, revocation, and failure behavior.","performance_implication":"Central authentication can add network dependencies; cached or locally verifiable credentials can reduce repeated dependency calls but introduce freshness considerations.","testing_guidance":"Test expiration, invalid credentials, identity-provider failure, replay resistance where applicable, and authorization separation.","verification_method":"Verify authentication protocol handling and execute negative authentication tests.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST Digital Identity Guidelines","Established identity architecture practice"],"related_records":["D41-B63-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B63-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Security architecture trade-offs","subtopic":"Secret management","concept":"Central secret management versus workload-local secret storage","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should secrets be managed centrally or stored within individual workload environments?","answer":"Central secret management provides lifecycle control, auditing, rotation, and access policy capabilities. Workload-local storage can reduce runtime dependencies but increases distribution and rotation risks.","principle":"Sensitive credentials should have controlled lifecycle management and the smallest practical exposure surface.","requirements":["Secret access must be authenticated and authorized","Secrets must not be embedded in source code","Rotation and revocation behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Central secret systems introduce dependencies","Local copies increase exposure and rotation complexity","Secret managers themselves become high-value infrastructure"],"objective":"Select an appropriate secret-management architecture","alternatives":["Central secret manager","Workload-local secret injection","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Centralization improves governance and rotation while adding an operational dependency; local storage can improve independence but expands secret exposure.","decision_factors":["Rotation frequency","Number of workloads","Availability requirements","Auditability","Secret sensitivity"],"failure_modes":["Hard-coded secrets","Stale credentials","Unauthorized secret retrieval","Incomplete rotation"],"security_implication":"Secrets should be protected in transit and at rest and access should follow least privilege.","performance_implication":"Central secret retrieval can add startup or request latency if performed synchronously; caching must preserve security requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test missing secrets, expired secrets, rotation, authorization failures, and secret leakage through logs.","verification_method":"Review secret lifecycle architecture and execute negative-access and rotation tests.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST SSDF SP 800-218","Established secret-management practice"],"related_records":["D41-B63-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B63-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Security architecture trade-offs","subtopic":"Network trust boundaries","concept":"Flat trusted network versus explicit service trust boundaries","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What architectural difference exists between a broadly trusted network and explicit service-level trust boundaries?","answer":"A broadly trusted network assumes more trust from network placement, while explicit service boundaries require identity and authorization at individual interactions. Explicit boundaries can reduce the impact of compromised components but require more comprehensive policy enforcement.","principle":"Network reachability should not automatically imply application authorization.","requirements":["Protected resources require explicit authorization","Service identities must be distinguishable","Boundary failures must be observable"],"constraints":["Fine-grained boundaries increase configuration and operational complexity","Legacy systems may assume network-level trust","Identity infrastructure must be reliable"],"objective":"Define appropriate trust boundaries","alternatives":["Network-centric trust","Service-centric trust","Layered trust boundaries"],"tradeoff":"Explicit boundaries increase control and containment potential but require more identity, policy, and observability infrastructure.","decision_factors":["Threat model","Service count","Legacy dependencies","Identity maturity","Compliance"],"failure_modes":["Implicit trust through network reachability","Overly broad service permissions","Unmonitored boundary bypasses"],"security_implication":"Explicit authentication and authorization boundaries reduce reliance on network location as a security assumption.","performance_implication":"Additional identity and policy checks may add overhead; the impact depends on implementation and caching.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized service-to-service access and boundary enforcement independently of network reachability.","verification_method":"Review trust-boundary diagrams and execute negative access tests.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST Zero Trust Architecture SP 800-207"],"related_records":["D41-B63-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B63-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B63","topic":"Security architecture trade-offs","subtopic":"Security gateway placement","concept":"Security controls at gateway versus defense in depth","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should security controls be concentrated at an ingress gateway or repeated at downstream resource boundaries?","answer":"Gateway controls simplify common ingress enforcement, but downstream controls remain important when requests can arrive through alternate paths or when services have independent authorization boundaries.","principle":"A gateway can be a control point but should not automatically become the sole security boundary for independently exposed resources.","requirements":["Every reachable protected resource must enforce its required authorization","Gateway policy and downstream policy must have defined responsibilities","Alternate access paths must be accounted for"],"constraints":["Duplicated controls can increase operational complexity","Gateway-only security can fail when bypass paths exist","Downstream controls can increase latency or implementation burden"],"objective":"Choose a layered security-control topology","alternatives":["Gateway-centric controls","Distributed defense in depth","Hybrid layered controls"],"tradeoff":"Gateway-centric security simplifies centralized enforcement; layered controls increase resilience to bypasses but require clear responsibility boundaries.","decision_factors":["Exposure paths","Service autonomy","Threat model","Latency","Policy complexity"],"failure_modes":["Gateway bypass","Policy duplication with inconsistent behavior","Unprotected internal endpoint"],"security_implication":"Security-critical authorization should be enforced at the resource boundary when the resource has independent exposure or trust assumptions.","performance_implication":"Repeated controls can add processing overhead but may reduce security risk from bypass paths.","testing_guidance":"Test direct service access, gateway access, alternate routes, and inconsistent policy scenarios.","verification_method":"Map all access paths and verify authorization at each required security boundary.","evidence_level":"STANDARDS_BASED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["NIST Zero Trust Architecture SP 800-207","OWASP application security guidance"],"related_records":["D41-B63-R001","D41-B63-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B64-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Platform architecture","subtopic":"Shared platform versus embedded capability","concept":"Central shared platform capability versus independently embedded team capability","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a capability be implemented as a shared platform instead of independently by product teams?","answer":"A shared platform is justified when the capability has strong common semantics, repeated demand, governance requirements, or meaningful economies of scale. Embedded implementations may be preferable when requirements vary significantly or the capability is small and unstable.","principle":"Centralize capabilities whose commonality is proven and whose shared governance outweighs coupling costs.","requirements":["The capability must have stable enough semantics to share","Ownership and service boundaries must be explicit","Platform consumers need reliable contracts"],"constraints":["Platforms can become bottlenecks","Shared abstractions can become overly generic","Consumer requirements may diverge"],"objective":"Determine whether a capability belongs in a shared platform","alternatives":["Shared platform","Embedded implementation","Hybrid reusable library plus local policy"],"tradeoff":"Shared platforms reduce duplication and standardize behavior but can increase coupling and dependency on platform roadmaps.","decision_factors":["Commonality","Stability","Governance","Consumer count","Coupling","Operational burden"],"failure_modes":["Platform becoming a bottleneck","Lowest-common-denominator API","Uncontrolled platform scope","Consumer dependency lock-in"],"security_implication":"Shared platforms concentrate privilege and sensitive capabilities and therefore require strong isolation and authorization.","performance_implication":"Centralized platform services can introduce network or resource bottlenecks; embedded capabilities can duplicate work.","testing_guidance":"Test platform contracts, consumer isolation, failure behavior, backward compatibility, and capacity limits.","verification_method":"Review consumer requirements and verify that shared semantics are genuinely common.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Platform-engineering architecture literature"],"related_records":["D41-B54-R001","D41-B60-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B64-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Platform architecture","subtopic":"Platform autonomy","concept":"Centralized platform governance versus team-owned infrastructure","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should an organization balance centralized platform governance with team infrastructure autonomy?","answer":"Central governance can standardize security, reliability, and operational controls, while team ownership can improve local decision speed and workload-specific optimization. The architecture should define which controls are mandatory and where autonomy remains.","principle":"Centralize non-negotiable guardrails while preserving local choice where requirements legitimately differ.","requirements":["Mandatory controls must be explicit","Teams must have observable ownership","Exceptions require a controlled process"],"constraints":["Too much centralization can slow delivery","Too much autonomy can create inconsistent controls","Exceptions increase governance complexity"],"objective":"Define an appropriate platform operating boundary","alternatives":["Centralized platform ownership","Team-owned infrastructure","Guardrails plus team autonomy"],"tradeoff":"Centralization improves consistency; autonomy improves local optimization and decision speed but can increase fragmentation.","decision_factors":["Security","Compliance","Team maturity","Workload diversity","Operational ownership"],"failure_modes":["Unapproved infrastructure divergence","Central platform bottleneck","Unclear responsibility","Exception proliferation"],"security_implication":"Security guardrails should remain enforceable regardless of team implementation choices.","performance_implication":"Local optimization can improve workload-specific performance; standardized infrastructure may improve fleet-level efficiency.","testing_guidance":"Test mandatory guardrails independently from optional platform features and validate exception handling.","verification_method":"Audit policy enforcement and ownership boundaries.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Platform engineering and governance practice"],"related_records":["D41-B64-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","refines"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B64-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Platform architecture","subtopic":"Developer platform interface","concept":"Opinionated platform interfaces versus flexible infrastructure interfaces","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should an internal platform expose opinionated workflows or highly flexible infrastructure primitives?","answer":"Opinionated interfaces reduce decision complexity and standardize common paths, while flexible primitives support unusual workloads. A platform should provide strong defaults without preventing justified exceptions.","principle":"Optimize the common path for simplicity while preserving an explicit escape hatch for legitimate requirements.","requirements":["Common workflows must be well supported","Exceptions must be observable","Platform contracts must define supported behavior"],"constraints":["Overly opinionated platforms can block valid use cases","Highly flexible platforms shift complexity to every team","Escape hatches can bypass governance"],"objective":"Select the appropriate platform abstraction level","alternatives":["Opinionated platform","Primitive platform","Layered opinionated defaults with controlled escape hatches"],"tradeoff":"Opinionated interfaces reduce cognitive and operational load but can constrain specialized workloads.","decision_factors":["Workload diversity","Developer expertise","Governance","Commonality","Exception frequency"],"failure_modes":["Platform bypass","Unsupported workload forced into unsuitable abstraction","Configuration explosion"],"security_implication":"Escape hatches must retain required security controls and auditability.","performance_implication":"Opinionated defaults can encode optimized operational paths but may be suboptimal for specialized workloads.","testing_guidance":"Test standard paths and documented escape-hatch behavior separately.","verification_method":"Compare platform capabilities against representative workload classes and verify guardrails.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Platform engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B64-R001","D41-B64-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B64-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Platform architecture","subtopic":"Self-service operations","concept":"Self-service platform operations versus centrally approved provisioning","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should infrastructure provisioning be self-service instead of requiring central approval?","answer":"Self-service is effective for standardized low-risk operations with strong automated guardrails. Central approval remains appropriate for high-impact, exceptional, or poorly automated operations.","principle":"Automate low-risk policy-compliant paths and reserve human approval for decisions that automation cannot safely constrain.","requirements":["Policy must be machine-enforceable","Provisioning actions must be auditable","High-impact operations must have explicit governance"],"constraints":["Self-service can amplify configuration errors","Approval workflows create latency","Automation quality determines safety"],"objective":"Choose an appropriate provisioning-control model","alternatives":["Central approval","Self-service","Risk-tiered hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Self-service increases delivery speed and reduces operational bottlenecks; approval increases control for high-impact operations but adds delay.","decision_factors":["Risk","Reversibility","Standardization","Automation maturity","Audit requirements"],"failure_modes":["Unsafe self-service configuration","Approval bottleneck","Insufficient audit trail","Policy bypass"],"security_implication":"Provisioning permissions should follow least privilege and risk-based approval requirements.","performance_implication":"Self-service can reduce provisioning lead time but does not inherently improve runtime performance.","testing_guidance":"Test policy enforcement, unauthorized provisioning, rollback, approval requirements, and audit logging.","verification_method":"Verify that every self-service path is bounded by enforceable policy.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Infrastructure governance and platform engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B64-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B64-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B64","topic":"Platform architecture","subtopic":"Platform contract evolution","concept":"Strict platform contracts versus permissive compatibility layers","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should a shared platform enforce strict consumer contracts or provide broad backward-compatible behavior?","answer":"Strict contracts expose incompatibilities early and reduce ambiguous behavior, while compatibility layers can ease migration and support older consumers. Compatibility should have explicit lifecycle and removal criteria.","principle":"Compatibility is useful when it reduces migration risk, but indefinite compatibility becomes architectural debt.","requirements":["Contract behavior must be documented","Compatibility scope must be explicit","Deprecation and removal rules must exist"],"constraints":["Strict changes can block consumers","Compatibility layers increase implementation complexity","Multiple behaviors increase testing requirements"],"objective":"Select a sustainable platform contract strategy","alternatives":["Strict versioned contracts","Backward-compatible evolution","Compatibility adapter with defined sunset"],"tradeoff":"Strict contracts simplify long-term semantics; compatibility layers reduce migration disruption but increase complexity.","decision_factors":["Consumer count","Migration cost","Contract stability","Operational risk","Lifecycle discipline"],"failure_modes":["Undocumented compatibility behavior","Permanent deprecated paths","Consumer lock-in","Semantic ambiguity"],"security_implication":"Legacy compatibility paths must receive the same security review as current interfaces.","performance_implication":"Compatibility layers can add processing or translation overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test old and new consumers, contract compatibility, deprecation paths, and eventual removal.","verification_method":"Validate contract behavior against supported versions and migration rules.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["API and platform contract engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B56-R001","D41-B64-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B65-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Computational locality","subtopic":"Data replication versus remote access","concept":"Replicated data versus remote authoritative reads","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a workload replicate data locally instead of reading from an authoritative remote source?","answer":"Replication can reduce read latency and dependency on remote connectivity but introduces freshness, synchronization, storage, and invalidation concerns. Remote access preserves a single authority but may increase latency and dependency coupling.","principle":"Replicate only when the latency, availability, or load benefits justify the consistency and lifecycle cost.","requirements":["Authority must be explicit","Replication freshness expectations must be defined","Replication failures must be handled"],"constraints":["Replicas may be stale","Replication increases data copies","Sensitive data replication expands exposure"],"objective":"Choose a data-access locality strategy","alternatives":["Remote authoritative reads","Read replicas","Local materialization","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Replication improves locality and can reduce remote load but introduces freshness and synchronization complexity.","decision_factors":["Read latency","Read volume","Freshness requirement","Data sensitivity","Connectivity","Storage"],"failure_modes":["Stale reads","Replication lag","Replica divergence","Unauthorized replicated data"],"security_implication":"Every replica becomes another protected data location.","performance_implication":"Local reads can reduce network latency; replication itself consumes network, storage, and processing resources.","testing_guidance":"Test stale-data behavior, replication lag, failure recovery, authorization, and replica rebuild.","verification_method":"Measure freshness and validate replica lifecycle behavior under failure.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed data systems engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B12-R004","D41-B65-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B65-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Computational locality","subtopic":"Read locality","concept":"Local materialization versus remote query execution","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a system materialize frequently used data locally rather than repeatedly querying a remote source?","answer":"Local materialization is useful for stable, read-heavy data where freshness requirements permit replication. Remote queries are preferable when authoritative freshness or low duplication is more important.","principle":"Materialize data when repeated remote access is materially costly and acceptable freshness semantics can be guaranteed.","requirements":["Refresh policy must be defined","Source authority must remain identifiable","Staleness behavior must be explicit"],"constraints":["Materialized data consumes storage","Refresh failures create stale state","Materialized views can become inconsistent with source changes"],"objective":"Optimize repeated read access","alternatives":["Remote queries","Local materialized view","Hybrid freshness-aware reads"],"tradeoff":"Materialization improves read locality but adds refresh and consistency responsibilities.","decision_factors":["Read frequency","Data volatility","Freshness tolerance","Storage cost","Network latency"],"failure_modes":["Stale materialization","Refresh backlog","Partial refresh","Unexpected source schema changes"],"security_implication":"Materialized copies require equivalent access controls and retention policies.","performance_implication":"Local materialization can reduce repeated network and query costs; refresh work adds background load.","testing_guidance":"Test refresh success, refresh failure, staleness thresholds, schema changes, and fallback to authoritative reads.","verification_method":"Verify freshness guarantees and test refresh/recovery behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Data architecture engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B65-R001","D41-B59-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B65-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Computational locality","subtopic":"Storage locality","concept":"Embedded storage versus remote storage service","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an application use embedded local storage instead of a remote storage service?","answer":"Embedded storage can simplify deployment and reduce network dependency for single-node or local-first workloads. Remote storage is preferable when shared access, centralized durability, independent scaling, or centralized administration is required.","principle":"Choose storage locality based on ownership, sharing, durability, scaling, and connectivity requirements.","requirements":["Durability expectations must be defined","Concurrency model must be known","Backup and recovery must be designed"],"constraints":["Embedded storage is tied to its execution environment","Remote storage introduces network dependency","Moving from embedded to remote can require migration work"],"objective":"Select a storage locality model","alternatives":["Embedded storage","Remote storage service","Hybrid local cache plus remote authority"],"tradeoff":"Embedded storage simplifies local operation but limits shared access and independent scaling; remote storage provides centralized capabilities with network dependency.","decision_factors":["Sharing","Durability","Offline operation","Scale","Operational complexity","Recovery"],"failure_modes":["Local data loss","File locking contention","Remote connectivity failure","Divergent local copies"],"security_implication":"Local storage requires secure filesystem permissions and encryption where appropriate; remote storage requires network and service authorization controls.","performance_implication":"Embedded access can avoid network latency; remote storage may provide stronger shared concurrency and scaling mechanisms.","testing_guidance":"Test restart, corruption, backup/restore, concurrent access, network loss, and migration behavior.","verification_method":"Validate durability and recovery requirements against actual storage behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Database and storage architecture engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B12-R001","D41-B61-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B65-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Computational locality","subtopic":"Network-bound computation","concept":"Remote service call versus local library execution","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should functionality remain a remote service instead of being embedded as a local library?","answer":"A remote service is appropriate when the capability requires centralized state, independent scaling, organizational ownership, or strong isolation. A local library is appropriate for stable, lightweight, deterministic functionality where deployment coupling is acceptable.","principle":"Do not create a network boundary where a stable local abstraction provides sufficient isolation and reuse, but do not embed capabilities whose independent lifecycle is important.","requirements":["Ownership and lifecycle must be defined","Network dependency must be justified if remote","Library compatibility must be controlled if embedded"],"constraints":["Remote calls add latency and failure modes","Libraries create release coupling","Remote services can centralize scarce resources"],"objective":"Choose local versus remote capability placement","alternatives":["Local library","Remote service","Hybrid client with remote fallback"],"tradeoff":"Libraries reduce runtime network dependency but increase release coupling; services provide independent lifecycle and scaling but introduce distributed-system failure modes.","decision_factors":["Statefulness","Latency","Release independence","Scaling","Ownership","Failure isolation"],"failure_modes":["Network dependency failure","Library version conflicts","Service bottleneck","Incompatible client upgrades"],"security_implication":"Remote boundaries provide explicit access-control points; embedded code inherits the application's trust boundary.","performance_implication":"Local execution generally avoids network round trips, but actual performance depends on computation and serialization costs.","testing_guidance":"Test version compatibility for libraries and timeout/failure behavior for remote services.","verification_method":"Validate lifecycle, dependency, and failure assumptions against deployment topology.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Software architecture engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B02-R001","D41-B11-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B65-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B65","topic":"Computational locality","subtopic":"Data access aggregation","concept":"Many remote calls versus aggregated remote access","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a client make multiple remote calls versus use an aggregation endpoint or service?","answer":"Aggregation can reduce round trips and centralize composition for common access patterns. Direct calls preserve service autonomy and can avoid creating an aggregation bottleneck when clients need different data.","principle":"Aggregate calls when network round trips materially dominate and the aggregation semantics are stable enough to justify another architectural responsibility.","requirements":["Aggregation semantics must be defined","Partial-failure behavior must be specified","Timeout budget must account for dependencies"],"constraints":["Aggregation couples multiple dependencies","One aggregator can become a bottleneck","Partial responses require explicit semantics"],"objective":"Optimize distributed data retrieval without creating excessive coupling","alternatives":["Multiple direct calls","Aggregation service","Client-side parallel calls"],"tradeoff":"Aggregation can reduce network chatter but creates dependency coupling and another component to operate.","decision_factors":["Round-trip cost","Number of dependencies","Response composition stability","Failure isolation","Client diversity"],"failure_modes":["Aggregator timeout","Partial dependency failure","Aggregator bottleneck","Stale aggregation logic"],"security_implication":"Aggregators must preserve downstream authorization semantics and must not broaden data access merely because they can access multiple services.","performance_implication":"Aggregation may reduce network round trips but adds server-side composition and serialization work.","testing_guidance":"Test dependency timeout, partial failure, authorization propagation, and response composition.","verification_method":"Measure request topology and verify authorization and failure semantics.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed API architecture practice"],"related_records":["D41-B11-R001","D41-B06-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B66-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B66","topic":"AI system architecture","subtopic":"Model access topology","concept":"Direct model integration versus centralized model gateway","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should applications call models directly versus through a centralized model gateway?","answer":"Direct integration can minimize intermediary complexity and may be appropriate for simple or tightly controlled applications. A model gateway is useful when multiple applications need shared routing, policy, observability, quotas, fallback, or provider abstraction.","principle":"Centralize model access only when shared governance or routing capabilities provide enough value to justify the additional dependency.","requirements":["Model identity and version must be explicit","Request and response policies must be observable","Sensitive data handling must be defined"],"constraints":["A gateway becomes an additional dependency","Provider-specific features may be hidden by abstraction","Centralized routing requires careful capacity management"],"objective":"Select an AI model integration topology","alternatives":["Direct model calls","Central model gateway","Hybrid direct-plus-governed access"],"tradeoff":"A gateway improves centralized governance and routing but adds infrastructure and abstraction complexity.","decision_factors":["Number of applications","Model diversity","Policy requirements","Observability","Fallback needs","Latency"],"failure_modes":["Gateway outage","Incorrect model routing","Provider-specific feature loss","Policy bypass through direct access"],"security_implication":"Centralized model access can enforce data-handling and authorization policies, but direct bypass paths must be controlled when governance is mandatory.","performance_implication":"A gateway may add latency; batching, connection reuse, and routing optimization can offset some overhead depending on implementation.","testing_guidance":"Test model routing, timeout, fallback, policy enforcement, version selection, and sensitive-data handling.","verification_method":"Verify routing policy, model-version selection, access control, and failure behavior.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["AI platform architecture engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B64-R001","D41-B57-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B66-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B66","topic":"AI system architecture","subtopic":"Inference execution","concept":"Centralized inference versus application-local inference","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should inference execute in a centralized service versus inside the application environment?","answer":"Centralized inference is useful for shared accelerators, model governance, consistent versioning, and resource pooling. Local inference is useful for latency, offline operation, privacy, or workload-specific deployment.","principle":"Place inference according to latency, privacy, resource, lifecycle, and governance constraints rather than assuming centralization is universally superior.","requirements":["Model version must be identifiable","Inference resource requirements must be understood","Data-processing boundaries must be explicit"],"constraints":["Central inference adds network dependency","Local inference duplicates model deployment","Model updates can create version divergence"],"objective":"Select an inference topology","alternatives":["Central inference service","Application-local inference","Edge/local inference with central model management"],"tradeoff":"Central inference simplifies model fleet management; local inference reduces network dependency and can improve locality but increases deployment complexity.","decision_factors":["Latency","Privacy","Offline requirements","Hardware availability","Model size","Version governance"],"failure_modes":["Model-version drift","Inference service outage","Insufficient local resources","Unexpected model compatibility differences"],"security_implication":"Inference placement determines where sensitive input data is processed and stored.","performance_implication":"Local inference avoids network round trips but may have lower resource efficiency; centralized inference can pool resources.","testing_guidance":"Test model-version consistency, resource exhaustion, network failure, and sensitive-data boundaries.","verification_method":"Verify model identity, execution location, data flow, and fallback behavior.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["AI/ML systems architecture engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B61-R002","D41-B66-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B66-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B66","topic":"AI system architecture","subtopic":"Deterministic and probabilistic components","concept":"Deterministic rules versus probabilistic model components","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture use deterministic logic instead of a probabilistic model?","answer":"Deterministic logic is preferable when outputs must be reproducible, constrained, auditable, or governed by explicit rules. Probabilistic models are useful when inputs are ambiguous, high-dimensional, or difficult to encode deterministically.","principle":"Use deterministic mechanisms for hard constraints and probabilistic mechanisms where uncertainty is intrinsic, with explicit boundaries between them.","requirements":["Hard constraints must not depend solely on probabilistic output","Uncertainty must be represented where relevant","Fallback behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Deterministic rules can be brittle for ambiguous inputs","Probabilistic outputs can vary","Combining both requires clear responsibility boundaries"],"objective":"Choose the appropriate reasoning mechanism","alternatives":["Deterministic logic","Probabilistic model","Hybrid constrained-probabilistic architecture"],"tradeoff":"Deterministic logic improves predictability and verification; probabilistic models improve flexibility under uncertainty but require uncertainty handling and validation.","decision_factors":["Reproducibility","Ambiguity","Safety requirements","Auditability","Input variability","Verification cost"],"failure_modes":["Probabilistic output violating hard constraints","Overly rigid deterministic rules","Unbounded model behavior","Ambiguous responsibility between layers"],"security_implication":"Security-critical authorization and hard safety constraints should have explicit enforceable boundaries rather than relying solely on probabilistic outputs.","performance_implication":"Deterministic rules may be computationally inexpensive; model inference can require substantially more compute depending on model architecture.","testing_guidance":"Test hard constraints independently, probabilistic behavior across representative inputs, and hybrid boundary conditions.","verification_method":"Verify deterministic invariants separately from probabilistic quality claims.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["AI systems engineering practice","Safety-critical software engineering principles"],"related_records":["D41-B10-R005","D41-B66-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B66-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B66","topic":"AI system architecture","subtopic":"Knowledge augmentation","concept":"External retrieval versus model-internal knowledge","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an AI architecture retrieve external knowledge instead of relying only on model-internal knowledge?","answer":"External retrieval is useful when information is dynamic, domain-specific, provenance-sensitive, or too large to encode reliably in model parameters. Internal knowledge can simplify architecture when the required information is stable and retrieval overhead is not justified.","principle":"Use retrieval when freshness, provenance, domain specificity, or inspectability is a primary requirement.","requirements":["Retrieved sources require provenance handling","Retrieval failures must have defined behavior","Context selection must respect security boundaries"],"constraints":["Retrieval introduces indexing and ranking complexity","Incorrect retrieval can degrade output quality","External data may contain conflicting information"],"objective":"Select an appropriate knowledge-access architecture","alternatives":["Model-internal knowledge","External retrieval","Hybrid retrieval plus internal reasoning"],"tradeoff":"Retrieval improves access to dynamic or traceable information but introduces retrieval failure, ranking, context, and data-governance complexity.","decision_factors":["Freshness","Provenance","Domain specificity","Data volume","Latency","Security"],"failure_modes":["Wrong document retrieval","Stale index","Conflicting sources","Sensitive-data leakage through retrieval"],"security_implication":"Retrieval must enforce document-level and tenant-level access controls before context reaches downstream reasoning components.","performance_implication":"Retrieval adds indexing, query, ranking, and context-processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test relevant retrieval, irrelevant retrieval, missing documents, conflicting sources, stale indexes, and unauthorized documents.","verification_method":"Evaluate retrieval correctness separately from generated output correctness.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Information-retrieval engineering literature","AI system architecture practice"],"related_records":["D41-B66-R003","D41-B10-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B66-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B66","topic":"AI system architecture","subtopic":"Model fallback","concept":"Single-model architecture versus multi-model fallback architecture","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an AI system use multiple models instead of a single model?","answer":"Multiple models can provide specialization, fallback, cost/performance routing, or resilience against individual model failures. A single model reduces routing, evaluation, and compatibility complexity when one model adequately satisfies requirements.","principle":"Add model diversity only when the resulting specialization or resilience provides measurable architectural value.","requirements":["Model-selection policy must be explicit","Model outputs must be comparable where fallback is used","Version and capability metadata must be tracked"],"constraints":["Multiple models increase evaluation surface","Fallback outputs may differ semantically","Routing errors can select unsuitable models"],"objective":"Select an appropriate model topology","alternatives":["Single model","Specialized model routing","Primary-plus-fallback models"],"tradeoff":"Multi-model architecture improves specialization and resilience but increases routing, testing, observability, and compatibility complexity.","decision_factors":["Task diversity","Availability requirements","Latency","Cost","Capability variance","Governance"],"failure_modes":["Wrong model selection","Incompatible output schemas","Fallback semantic mismatch","Version drift"],"security_implication":"Every model path must enforce equivalent data-access and privacy policies.","performance_implication":"Routing can add latency; specialized models may reduce compute cost for suitable workloads but this requires measurement.","testing_guidance":"Test routing decisions, fallback activation, schema compatibility, model-version changes, and degraded operation.","verification_method":"Verify model-selection constraints and test every supported fallback path.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["AI platform architecture engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B66-R001","D41-B66-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B67-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Legacy integration architecture","subtopic":"Anti-corruption boundary","concept":"Direct legacy integration versus anti-corruption layer","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a new system translate legacy concepts through an anti-corruption layer instead of adopting legacy domain models directly?","answer":"An anti-corruption layer is useful when legacy models conflict with the new system's domain language or quality requirements. Direct integration may be simpler for temporary, stable, low-risk interactions where model translation adds little value.","principle":"Protect a new domain model from incompatible external semantics when the boundary is expected to persist or the semantic mismatch is material.","requirements":["Legacy contract must be understood","Translation rules must be explicit","Ownership of each model must be clear"],"constraints":["Translation code adds maintenance cost","Direct integration creates stronger coupling","Legacy behavior may be poorly documented"],"objective":"Control semantic coupling to legacy systems","alternatives":["Direct integration","Anti-corruption layer","Temporary adapter"],"tradeoff":"An anti-corruption layer preserves domain independence but introduces translation logic; direct integration reduces immediate complexity while increasing semantic coupling.","decision_factors":["Semantic mismatch","Integration lifetime","Legacy volatility","Domain independence","Migration plans"],"failure_modes":["Legacy semantics leaking into core domain","Incorrect translation","Hidden coupling","Adapter becoming permanent without governance"],"security_implication":"Translation layers must preserve authorization and data-classification semantics rather than accidentally broadening access.","performance_implication":"Translation adds processing overhead but can prevent repeated legacy-specific handling throughout the system.","testing_guidance":"Test mapping rules, unknown legacy values, contract changes, and round-trip behavior where applicable.","verification_method":"Verify mapping invariants and integration behavior against authoritative legacy contracts.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Domain-driven design and integration architecture literature"],"related_records":["D41-B20-R005","D41-B56-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B67-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Legacy integration architecture","subtopic":"Contract translation","concept":"Canonical integration model versus endpoint-specific translation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should integrations use one canonical model or translate data separately at each boundary?","answer":"A canonical model can reduce repeated translations across many integrations when semantics are genuinely shared. Endpoint-specific translation can preserve local semantics and avoid forcing unrelated systems into a single abstraction.","principle":"Use a canonical model only when shared semantics are stable and materially reduce integration complexity.","requirements":["Canonical semantics must be well defined","Translation ownership must be clear","Version evolution must be managed"],"constraints":["Canonical models can become overly generic","Endpoint-specific models increase mapping work","A canonical model can create central coupling"],"objective":"Choose an integration data-model strategy","alternatives":["Canonical model","Endpoint-specific translation","Layered domain-specific models"],"tradeoff":"Canonical models reduce repeated mapping when truly shared; endpoint-specific models preserve local semantics but may duplicate translation work.","decision_factors":["Number of integrations","Semantic overlap","Model stability","Ownership","Evolution rate"],"failure_modes":["Lowest-common-denominator canonical model","Canonical model becoming bottleneck","Inconsistent endpoint mappings"],"security_implication":"Canonical models must preserve classification and authorization semantics across all consumers.","performance_implication":"Canonical translation may add processing; repeated endpoint translation can increase aggregate CPU and development cost.","testing_guidance":"Test mapping completeness, unknown fields, version changes, and semantic preservation.","verification_method":"Compare source semantics against canonical or endpoint-specific representations and verify invariants.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Integration architecture engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B67-R001","D41-B56-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B67-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Legacy integration architecture","subtopic":"Protocol adaptation","concept":"Protocol gateway versus protocol-native implementation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a gateway translate between protocols instead of requiring every service to implement each protocol directly?","answer":"A protocol gateway is useful when many consumers need the same translation or when protocol-specific complexity should be isolated. Native implementation may be simpler when only one integration exists or protocol semantics are central to the application.","principle":"Centralize repeated protocol adaptation when it reduces duplicated complexity without hiding important protocol semantics.","requirements":["Protocol semantics must be preserved","Failure behavior must be explicit","Gateway ownership must be clear"],"constraints":["Gateway becomes a dependency","Translation may lose protocol-specific capabilities","Gateway scaling must accommodate aggregate traffic"],"objective":"Control protocol complexity","alternatives":["Direct protocol implementation","Shared protocol gateway","Dedicated adapter"],"tradeoff":"A gateway reduces repeated protocol logic but adds a centralized dependency and translation boundary.","decision_factors":["Integration count","Protocol complexity","Traffic volume","Feature requirements","Operational ownership"],"failure_modes":["Protocol semantic loss","Gateway bottleneck","Translation errors","Gateway-wide outage"],"security_implication":"Protocol translation must preserve authentication, authorization, integrity, and confidentiality requirements.","performance_implication":"Translation adds latency and processing; a gateway may also concentrate traffic.","testing_guidance":"Test protocol semantics, malformed messages, timeouts, backpressure, and gateway failure.","verification_method":"Validate protocol equivalence for supported operations and execute negative protocol tests.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Integration and protocol gateway engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B67-R001","D41-B65-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B67-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Legacy integration architecture","subtopic":"Legacy dependency isolation","concept":"Synchronous legacy dependency versus asynchronous integration boundary","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a new system isolate a legacy dependency behind an asynchronous boundary instead of calling it synchronously?","answer":"Asynchronous integration can isolate latency, availability, and throughput differences when immediate response is not required. Synchronous integration is appropriate when the caller cannot proceed without the legacy result and the dependency's latency and availability are acceptable.","principle":"Use asynchronous boundaries when business semantics permit delayed completion and dependency isolation provides meaningful resilience.","requirements":["Business semantics must tolerate delayed processing","Message delivery and retry semantics must be defined","Correlation and status tracking must exist"],"constraints":["Asynchronous processing complicates user-visible state","Retries can create duplicates","Immediate consistency may not be available"],"objective":"Reduce coupling to unreliable or slow legacy dependencies","alternatives":["Synchronous call","Asynchronous message","Hybrid command plus status query"],"tradeoff":"Asynchronous integration improves isolation and resilience but introduces eventual completion and operational tracking complexity.","decision_factors":["Latency","Dependency reliability","Business immediacy","Retry semantics","User experience"],"failure_modes":["Duplicate processing","Lost messages","Unknown completion state","Legacy backlog"],"security_implication":"Messages and asynchronous identities must retain authorization context without trusting unverified metadata.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous buffering can absorb bursts but adds queueing latency.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate delivery, legacy outage, backlog recovery, timeout, and status reconciliation.","verification_method":"Verify delivery semantics, idempotency requirements, and business completion rules.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed integration engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B16-R001","D41-B67-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B67-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B67","topic":"Legacy integration architecture","subtopic":"Integration ownership","concept":"Central integration platform versus domain-owned integration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should integrations be owned centrally or by the domain team that owns the business capability?","answer":"Central ownership can standardize cross-cutting integration infrastructure, while domain ownership preserves business context and local accountability. Hybrid models can centralize infrastructure while keeping semantic ownership with domain teams.","principle":"Separate technical integration infrastructure ownership from business-semantic ownership when the responsibilities differ.","requirements":["Ownership boundaries must be explicit","Operational responsibility must be identifiable","Integration contracts must have named owners"],"constraints":["Central teams can become bottlenecks","Domain teams may duplicate infrastructure","Shared ownership without clear boundaries causes gaps"],"objective":"Choose sustainable integration ownership","alternatives":["Central integration team","Domain-owned integration","Shared platform with domain semantic ownership"],"tradeoff":"Central ownership improves standardization; domain ownership improves contextual correctness; hybrid ownership separates infrastructure and semantics.","decision_factors":["Integration complexity","Business semantics","Team maturity","Governance","Operational load"],"failure_modes":["Ownership gaps","Central bottleneck","Duplicated infrastructure","Unmaintained mappings"],"security_implication":"Security responsibility must remain explicit even when infrastructure and semantic ownership are split.","performance_implication":"Central platforms can optimize shared infrastructure; domain-local implementations can optimize workload-specific behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test ownership handoffs, operational escalation, contract changes, and security responsibility boundaries.","verification_method":"Review ownership matrix and verify every integration has accountable technical and semantic owners.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Software architecture and platform governance practice"],"related_records":["D41-B64-R002","D41-B67-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B68-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Local-first architecture","subtopic":"Offline capability","concept":"Offline-capable architecture versus online-only architecture","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an application support meaningful operation while disconnected from the network?","answer":"Offline capability is justified when users or devices must continue operating during intermittent connectivity or when local responsiveness is a primary requirement. Online-only architecture is simpler when connectivity is reliable and server authority is required for each operation.","principle":"Support offline operation only when business requirements justify the additional state, synchronization, conflict, and security complexity.","requirements":["Offline-supported operations must be explicitly defined","Local state durability must be understood","Synchronization behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Offline state can become stale","Conflicting updates require resolution","Sensitive local data requires additional protection"],"objective":"Determine whether offline capability is architecturally justified","alternatives":["Online-only","Read-only offline","Fully offline-capable subset","Offline-first"],"tradeoff":"Offline capability improves continuity and responsiveness but increases local state and synchronization complexity.","decision_factors":["Connectivity reliability","Operational criticality","User workflow","Conflict frequency","Data sensitivity"],"failure_modes":["Stale state","Conflict loss","Unsynchronized actions","Local data exposure"],"security_implication":"Offline storage expands the security boundary and requires local access controls and data-protection measures.","performance_implication":"Local operation can reduce network latency but synchronization consumes background resources.","testing_guidance":"Test disconnected operation, reconnection, synchronization, conflicts, and device restart.","verification_method":"Verify explicitly supported offline operations and synchronization invariants.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Local-first software architecture literature"],"related_records":["D41-B61-R005","D41-B65-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B68-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Local-first architecture","subtopic":"Synchronization conflict strategy","concept":"Last-write-wins versus explicit conflict resolution","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should synchronization use automatic last-write-wins behavior versus explicit conflict resolution?","answer":"Automatic conflict resolution is appropriate only when losing one update is acceptable under the domain semantics. Explicit resolution is required when concurrent changes have materially different business meaning.","principle":"Conflict resolution must reflect business semantics rather than relying on a generic timestamp rule.","requirements":["Conflict conditions must be detectable","Resolution semantics must be documented","Unresolved conflicts must remain visible when required"],"constraints":["Timestamp ordering may not represent business correctness","Manual resolution adds workflow complexity","Automatic merges can silently discard information"],"objective":"Select a conflict-resolution model","alternatives":["Last-write-wins","Field-level merge","Domain-specific merge","Human-assisted resolution"],"tradeoff":"Automatic resolution reduces operational complexity but may discard valid updates; explicit resolution preserves semantics at higher workflow cost.","decision_factors":["Conflict cost","Data semantics","Frequency","User involvement","Audit requirements"],"failure_modes":["Silent data loss","Incorrect timestamp ordering","Unresolved conflict accumulation"],"security_implication":"Conflict resolution must not bypass authorization or allow one actor's update to overwrite protected data improperly.","performance_implication":"Automatic resolution is generally simpler to execute; complex merges increase computation and workflow overhead.","testing_guidance":"Create concurrent update scenarios and verify every supported conflict policy.","verification_method":"Verify conflict outcomes against explicit domain invariants rather than merely checking successful synchronization.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed-systems consistency literature"],"related_records":["D41-B68-R001","D41-B09-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B68-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Local-first architecture","subtopic":"Offline command handling","concept":"Local command queue versus immediate remote submission","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should user actions be queued locally instead of requiring immediate server submission?","answer":"Local queues are appropriate when actions can safely be delayed and connectivity is intermittent. Immediate submission is preferable when server-side authorization, state, or ordering must be checked before the action can be accepted.","principle":"Queue an action offline only when delayed execution is semantically valid and authorization remains enforceable at execution time.","requirements":["Queued actions need durable identity and status","Replay and idempotency must be defined","Authorization must be revalidated where required"],"constraints":["Queued actions may become stale","Execution order may change","Sensitive commands may be unsafe to replay"],"objective":"Choose a safe offline command model","alternatives":["Immediate remote submission","Durable local queue","Local draft with explicit later submission"],"tradeoff":"Local queuing improves continuity but creates replay, ordering, freshness, and authorization challenges.","decision_factors":["Action reversibility","Freshness","Authorization timing","Connectivity","Ordering requirements"],"failure_modes":["Duplicate command execution","Stale command execution","Unauthorized replay","Queue corruption"],"security_implication":"Offline queue contents and execution identities require protection; high-impact actions may require renewed authorization before execution.","performance_implication":"Local queuing removes immediate network latency from the user path but shifts work to synchronization.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate delivery, stale commands, authorization changes, queue corruption, and replay ordering.","verification_method":"Verify idempotency, command freshness, and authorization semantics before allowing offline execution.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Offline-first and distributed command-processing practice"],"related_records":["D41-B68-R001","D41-B67-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B68-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Local-first architecture","subtopic":"Synchronization topology","concept":"Central synchronization coordinator versus peer synchronization","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should offline-capable nodes synchronize through a central coordinator or directly with peers?","answer":"Central coordination simplifies authority, conflict visibility, and topology management. Peer synchronization can reduce central dependency but substantially increases conflict, discovery, trust, and topology complexity.","principle":"Prefer centralized synchronization when a single coordination authority is acceptable and operational simplicity is important.","requirements":["Synchronization authority must be defined","Peer trust must be explicit if peer synchronization is used","Conflict handling must be deterministic"],"constraints":["Central coordination creates dependency","Peer synchronization increases topology complexity","Disconnected partitions can produce divergent state"],"objective":"Select a synchronization topology","alternatives":["Central coordinator","Peer-to-peer synchronization","Hybrid"],"tradeoff":"Central coordination simplifies consistency and governance; peer synchronization improves independence but increases distributed-state complexity.","decision_factors":["Connectivity","Topology size","Trust model","Conflict frequency","Central availability"],"failure_modes":["Synchronization loops","Partition divergence","Unauthorized peer synchronization","Central coordinator outage"],"security_implication":"Peer synchronization requires explicit peer authentication and authorization; network reachability alone must not imply trust.","performance_implication":"Peer synchronization can reduce central bottlenecks but may increase synchronization traffic and conflict processing.","testing_guidance":"Test partitions, reconnect, duplicate updates, unauthorized peers, and topology changes.","verification_method":"Verify synchronization invariants and execute partition/recovery scenarios.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed synchronization architecture literature"],"related_records":["D41-B68-R002","D41-B63-R004"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B68-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B68","topic":"Local-first architecture","subtopic":"Local authority","concept":"Local authoritative state versus server authoritative state","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a disconnected client treat local state as authoritative for an operation?","answer":"Local authority is appropriate only for operations whose correctness can be established without server state or whose business semantics explicitly permit provisional decisions. Server authority is required when global uniqueness, authorization, inventory, or other centralized constraints determine correctness.","principle":"Do not treat local state as authoritative for globally constrained decisions unless the architecture explicitly defines provisional semantics.","requirements":["Authority scope must be explicit","Provisional versus committed state must be distinguishable","Reconciliation behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Local authority can conflict with global state","Provisional actions may later be rejected","Users need clear state semantics"],"objective":"Define authority during disconnected operation","alternatives":["Server authority","Local provisional authority","Domain-specific hybrid authority"],"tradeoff":"Local authority improves availability but can produce decisions inconsistent with global state; server authority preserves global correctness at the cost of availability during disconnection.","decision_factors":["Global constraints","Offline requirement","Conflict cost","User workflow","Authorization"],"failure_modes":["Duplicate global identifiers","Invalid local authorization","Conflicting resource allocation","Incorrect provisional state"],"security_implication":"Local authority must not bypass server-side security controls when global authorization is required.","performance_implication":"Local authority removes network latency but reconciliation can introduce later processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test provisional acceptance, server rejection, reconciliation, duplicate constraints, and authorization changes.","verification_method":"Classify every offline operation by authority scope and test reconciliation outcomes.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed-systems consistency engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B68-R001","D41-B68-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B69-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Architecture migration mechanics","subtopic":"Schema evolution","concept":"Expand-contract migration versus breaking schema replacement","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a database schema evolve through additive expansion and contraction instead of a breaking replacement?","answer":"Expand-contract migration is appropriate when old and new application versions must coexist during deployment. A breaking replacement can be simpler in tightly controlled environments where coordinated downtime or atomic deployment is acceptable.","principle":"Use compatibility-preserving schema evolution when deployment overlap prevents atomic application and database replacement.","requirements":["Old and new schema behavior must be understood","Migration order must be explicit","Rollback strategy must exist"],"constraints":["Expand-contract requires temporary duplicate structures","Dual compatibility increases testing surface","Breaking migration may require downtime"],"objective":"Select a safe schema-evolution strategy","alternatives":["Expand-contract","Breaking replacement","Versioned parallel schema"],"tradeoff":"Expand-contract reduces deployment coupling but adds temporary complexity; breaking replacement simplifies final state but increases coordination and downtime risk.","decision_factors":["Deployment overlap","Downtime tolerance","Data volume","Rollback requirements","Application version compatibility"],"failure_modes":["Old application incompatible with new schema","Incomplete backfill","Incorrect cleanup","Rollback blocked by irreversible changes"],"security_implication":"Migration scripts must preserve access controls and avoid exposing temporary structures or sensitive data.","performance_implication":"Backfills and dual-write/dual-read periods can increase database load; exact impact requires measurement.","testing_guidance":"Test old-version compatibility, new-version compatibility, migration rollback, backfill correctness, and cleanup.","verification_method":"Verify schema compatibility matrix and execute migration tests against representative data.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Database migration engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B13-R003","D41-B20-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B69-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Architecture migration mechanics","subtopic":"Compatibility migration","concept":"Dual-read migration versus single-read cutover","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a migration temporarily read from both old and new representations?","answer":"Dual reads can support gradual migration and comparison when old and new representations coexist. They add latency, complexity, and ambiguity and should be temporary with explicit removal criteria.","principle":"Use dual-read strategies only when they provide meaningful migration safety or comparison value.","requirements":["Source precedence must be explicit","Differences must be detectable","Cutover criteria must be defined"],"constraints":["Reads may disagree","Duplicate work increases load","Permanent dual reads create architectural debt"],"objective":"Reduce risk during representation migration","alternatives":["Single-source cutover","Dual-read comparison","Shadow-read validation"],"tradeoff":"Dual reads improve migration observability but increase complexity and resource consumption.","decision_factors":["Migration risk","Data consistency","Read volume","Cutover confidence","Rollback requirements"],"failure_modes":["Conflicting source values","Hidden read divergence","Excessive load","Migration never completing"],"security_implication":"Both data sources require equivalent access controls; comparison logs must not expose sensitive data.","performance_implication":"Dual reads increase query volume and latency; shadow reads should be measured and bounded.","testing_guidance":"Test source disagreement, missing records, cutover, rollback, and read-load behavior.","verification_method":"Compare representations using deterministic reconciliation rules and verify cutover criteria.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Database migration engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B69-R001","D41-B20-R005"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B69-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Architecture migration mechanics","subtopic":"Compatibility release","concept":"Backward-compatible release versus coordinated cutover","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should independently deployed components preserve backward compatibility or be upgraded through a coordinated cutover?","answer":"Backward compatibility reduces deployment coordination and supports rolling migration. Coordinated cutovers can simplify behavior when all components can be changed atomically or when compatibility would create excessive complexity.","principle":"Prefer compatibility when independent deployment is a core architectural property; use coordinated cutover only when coordination is reliably enforceable.","requirements":["Supported version combinations must be known","Cutover procedure must be reversible where practical","Compatibility behavior must be tested"],"constraints":["Compatibility increases code paths","Coordinated deployment creates scheduling dependency","Mixed-version states may persist longer than expected"],"objective":"Select a release compatibility model","alternatives":["Backward-compatible rolling release","Coordinated cutover","Temporary compatibility adapter"],"tradeoff":"Backward compatibility improves deployment independence but increases temporary complexity; coordinated cutover reduces mixed-state behavior but increases operational coupling.","decision_factors":["Deployment independence","Number of consumers","Rollback needs","Compatibility cost","Release coordination"],"failure_modes":["Unsupported mixed-version combination","Failed coordinated deployment","Old clients remaining beyond support window"],"security_implication":"Legacy compatibility paths must not preserve insecure behavior merely for backward compatibility.","performance_implication":"Compatibility adapters may add processing overhead; coordinated cutovers can reduce runtime complexity but increase deployment risk.","testing_guidance":"Test all supported version combinations and rollback paths.","verification_method":"Validate the compatibility matrix and enforce supported-version boundaries.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Distributed software release engineering practice"],"related_records":["D41-B56-R001","D41-B69-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B69-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Architecture migration mechanics","subtopic":"Data movement","concept":"Online migration versus offline migration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should data migration run while the system remains available versus during a maintenance window?","answer":"Online migration is appropriate when availability requirements prohibit downtime and the migration can safely coexist with normal operations. Offline migration is simpler when coordinated downtime is acceptable and data correctness is easier to guarantee with exclusive access.","principle":"Choose online migration when availability requirements justify its concurrency and synchronization complexity.","requirements":["Data consistency strategy must be defined","Migration progress must be observable","Failure and restart behavior must be safe"],"constraints":["Online migration competes with production workload","Offline migration requires downtime","Large migrations may take longer than maintenance windows"],"objective":"Select migration execution mode","alternatives":["Online migration","Offline migration","Phased migration"],"tradeoff":"Online migration preserves availability but adds concurrency and operational complexity; offline migration simplifies consistency at the cost of downtime.","decision_factors":["Availability requirement","Data volume","Write rate","Migration duration","Rollback feasibility"],"failure_modes":["Migration affecting production workload","Partial migration","Lock contention","Maintenance window overrun"],"security_implication":"Migration processes need controlled access and must avoid exposing temporary or copied sensitive data.","performance_implication":"Online migrations consume production resources; offline migrations concentrate resource usage into the maintenance period.","testing_guidance":"Test migration on representative data volumes and simulate interruption and restart.","verification_method":"Verify migration invariants, row/data counts where applicable, checksums or domain-level reconciliation, and recovery behavior.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Database migration engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B69-R001","D41-B69-R003"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B69-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B69","topic":"Architecture migration mechanics","subtopic":"Migration sequencing","concept":"Big-bang migration versus phased migration","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should a system migrate all components at once versus progressively?","answer":"Phased migration reduces blast radius and allows learning from early stages, while big-bang migration can reduce the period of coexistence and temporary compatibility complexity when coordination is reliable.","principle":"Use phased migration when uncertainty or blast radius is high; use coordinated migration when the system boundary is small enough to make atomic change practical.","requirements":["Migration boundaries must be explicit","Rollback or recovery must be defined","Data and contract compatibility must be controlled"],"constraints":["Phased migration creates temporary hybrid states","Big-bang migration concentrates risk","Migration strategy depends on dependency topology"],"objective":"Select an appropriate migration sequence","alternatives":["Big-bang","Phased","Pilot followed by phased rollout"],"tradeoff":"Phased migration limits immediate blast radius but increases coexistence complexity; big-bang reduces coexistence but concentrates deployment risk.","decision_factors":["System size","Dependency count","Uncertainty","Rollback capability","Downtime tolerance"],"failure_modes":["Migration drift","Hybrid-state bugs","Incomplete cutover","Large-scale coordinated failure"],"security_implication":"Security controls must remain consistent during every migration phase; temporary paths must not create weaker boundaries.","performance_implication":"Hybrid operation can consume additional resources; big-bang may avoid temporary duplication but has concentrated load during migration.","testing_guidance":"Test each migration stage, coexistence state, rollback, and final cutover independently.","verification_method":"Define measurable exit criteria for every phase and verify the final architecture separately.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Software migration engineering literature"],"related_records":["D41-B20-R005","D41-B69-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B70-R001","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Architecture evaluation","subtopic":"Decision validation","concept":"Architecture prototype versus full implementation validation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture decision be validated with a focused prototype instead of full implementation?","answer":"A focused prototype is useful when an uncertain technical assumption can be isolated and tested cheaply. Full implementation validation is necessary when integration effects, operational behavior, or production constraints cannot be represented by the prototype.","principle":"Use the smallest validation artifact that can falsify the architecture's highest-risk assumptions.","requirements":["Risk assumptions must be explicit","Prototype success criteria must be measurable","Prototype limitations must be documented"],"constraints":["Prototype results may not represent production behavior","Prototype code can accidentally become production without review","Integration risks may remain untested"],"objective":"Reduce architecture uncertainty efficiently","alternatives":["Prototype","Benchmark","Reference implementation","Full implementation validation"],"tradeoff":"Prototypes reduce validation cost and time but provide narrower evidence than production-like validation.","decision_factors":["Technical uncertainty","Integration complexity","Prototype fidelity","Cost of failure","Production constraints"],"failure_modes":["Overgeneralizing prototype results","Ignoring integration effects","Prototype becoming unsupported production code"],"security_implication":"Security assumptions should be validated at realistic trust boundaries rather than inferred solely from isolated prototypes.","performance_implication":"Prototype performance results are only meaningful when workload, environment, and measurement methodology are representative.","testing_guidance":"Define falsifiable hypotheses and explicit limits on what prototype evidence proves.","verification_method":"Record hypothesis, test method, observed evidence, and unresolved assumptions separately.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Architecture evaluation and engineering experimentation practice"],"related_records":["D41-B10-R001","D41-B70-R002"],"relationships":["refines","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B70-R002","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Architecture evaluation","subtopic":"Architecture fitness","concept":"Static architecture rules versus runtime architecture validation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should architecture be validated through static rules versus runtime tests?","answer":"Static rules are useful for structural invariants such as dependency direction and forbidden imports. Runtime validation is required for behavior that depends on actual execution, configuration, network conditions, or operational state.","principle":"Use static validation for structural constraints and runtime validation for behavioral constraints; neither should be treated as universally sufficient.","requirements":["Each architecture rule must identify its validation mechanism","Static and runtime checks must have defined scope","Violations must be actionable"],"constraints":["Static analysis cannot prove all runtime behavior","Runtime tests may miss unexecuted paths","Architecture rules can become stale"],"objective":"Choose appropriate architecture-validation mechanisms","alternatives":["Static rules","Runtime architecture tests","Combined validation"],"tradeoff":"Static checks are fast and deterministic; runtime checks capture actual behavior but require execution environments and broader test coverage.","decision_factors":["Rule type","Execution dependency","Coverage","Validation cost","Failure impact"],"failure_modes":["False confidence from static-only validation","Uncovered runtime violations","Outdated rules"],"security_implication":"Security architecture invariants should use static and runtime checks where the threat model requires both.","performance_implication":"Static checks usually have lower runtime cost; runtime validation consumes execution resources but can expose real operational behavior.","testing_guidance":"Map every important architecture invariant to at least one appropriate validation mechanism.","verification_method":"Maintain a rule-to-test mapping and verify that claimed coverage matches executed evidence.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Software architecture testing practice","Architecture fitness-function literature"],"related_records":["D41-B10-R003","D41-B70-R001"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","composes_with"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B70-R003","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Architecture evaluation","subtopic":"Architecture evidence","concept":"Single architecture metric versus multi-dimensional evidence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Should architecture decisions be evaluated using one dominant metric or multiple independent evidence dimensions?","answer":"A single metric can simplify decision-making but may hide important failures in security, reliability, cost, or maintainability. Multi-dimensional evidence better represents architecture trade-offs but requires explicit weighting and interpretation.","principle":"Architecture decisions should expose important dimensions separately before any aggregate judgment is made.","requirements":["Relevant dimensions must be identified","Measurements must retain provenance","Unknown dimensions must not be treated as zero risk"],"constraints":["More dimensions increase analysis complexity","Metrics can conflict","Some qualities are difficult to quantify"],"objective":"Build an evidence-based architecture evaluation","alternatives":["Single dominant metric","Multi-dimensional scorecard","Qualitative evidence matrix"],"tradeoff":"Single metrics are simple but lossy; multi-dimensional evaluation preserves trade-offs but requires more reasoning.","decision_factors":["Decision criticality","Quality attributes","Measurement availability","Stakeholder constraints"],"failure_modes":["Optimizing one metric while degrading another","False precision","Unmeasured dimension treated as irrelevant"],"security_implication":"Security cannot safely be reduced to a generic performance or cost metric.","performance_implication":"Performance evidence should retain workload, environment, measurement method, and uncertainty.","testing_guidance":"Review each decision against explicitly identified quality attributes and evidence sources.","verification_method":"Validate each evidence dimension independently before combining qualitative conclusions.","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Architecture decision analysis practice"],"related_records":["D41-B10-R001","D41-B70-R002"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B70-R004","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Architecture evaluation","subtopic":"Reversibility","concept":"Reversible architecture decision versus irreversible architecture commitment","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should reversibility influence an architecture decision?","answer":"Reversible decisions can tolerate more experimentation because later correction is comparatively inexpensive. Irreversible or expensive-to-reverse decisions require stronger evidence before commitment.","principle":"Increase evidence requirements as the cost and difficulty of reversing a decision increase.","requirements":["Reversal cost must be identified","Dependencies created by the decision must be documented","Exit or migration paths should be considered for high-impact commitments"],"constraints":["Some decisions become difficult to reverse after adoption","Overengineering for reversibility can itself add complexity","Not all future requirements can be known"],"objective":"Use reversibility as an architecture decision factor","alternatives":["Highly reversible design","Moderately reversible design","Irreversible commitment with stronger validation"],"tradeoff":"Reversible designs preserve future options but may add abstraction or migration overhead; irreversible choices can simplify the present but require stronger confidence.","decision_factors":["Migration cost","Vendor dependence","Data lock-in","Contract stability","Operational switching cost"],"failure_modes":["Unrecognized lock-in","Missing migration path","Premature abstraction","Underestimated switching cost"],"security_implication":"Irreversible security architecture choices can constrain future remediation; migration paths should be considered for critical controls.","performance_implication":"Reversibility mechanisms can add indirection or duplication; their cost should be justified by decision risk.","testing_guidance":"Test migration or replacement paths for decisions identified as high-cost to reverse.","verification_method":"Record reversal assumptions, estimated switching dependencies, and evidence supporting the commitment.","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","truth_class":"HEURISTIC","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW_ONLY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":["Architecture decision-recording and software architecture practice"],"related_records":["D41-B60-R005","D41-B70-R001"],"relationships":["refines","requires"]}

{"record_id":"D41-B70-R005","dataset_id":"D41","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D41.1","batch_id":"B70","topic":"Architecture evaluation","subtopic":"Decision review lifecycle","concept":"One-time architecture decision versus continuous architecture review","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture decision be revisited after initial adoption?","answer":"Architecture should be revisited when assumptions, workload, constraints, dependencies, security conditions, or operational evidence materially change. Continuous review does not mean arbitrary redesign; it means reassessing decisions against changed evidence.","principle":"Architecture decisions should remain stable until evidence invalidates their assumptions, then be explicitly re-evaluated rather than silently drifting.","requirements":["Decision assumptions must be recorded","Operational evidence must be observable","Review triggers must be defined"],"constraints":["Constant redesign creates instability","Insufficient monitoring hides assumption changes","Historical decisions may lose relevance as context changes"],"objective":"Maintain architecture decisions as evidence evolves","alternatives":["One-time decision","Scheduled review","Evidence-triggered review"],"tradeoff":"One-time decisions reduce governance overhead; evidence-triggered review adapts to change while avoiding unnecessary redesign.","decision_factors":["Decision criticality","Rate of environmental change","Operational evidence","Migration cost","Security exposure"],"failure_modes":["Stale architecture assumptions","Architecture drift","Repeated redesign without evidence","Unrecorded exceptions"],"security_implication":"Security-sensitive architecture should be reconsidered when threat models, dependencies, exposure, or regulatory requirements materially change.","performance_implication":"Workload changes can invalidate capacity and topology assumptions; architecture review should use actual measurements where