{"record_id":"D43-B12-R005","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B12","knowledge_type":"CONCURRENCY_OUTCOME","title":"Deadlock Avoidance Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior for operations subject to resource-lock dependencies.","precondition":"Multiple operations may acquire overlapping locks or resources and the system defines a deadlock prevention, detection, timeout, or recovery policy.","input":"Concurrent operations creating a lock dependency condition.","action":"Execute the operations under the documented locking policy.","expected_result":"The system either completes the operations or detects and safely resolves the deadlock condition without leaving protected state permanently locked.","observable_outcomes":["operation completion or deadlock classification","lock release","recovery evidence"],"pass_criteria":["Required resources are eventually released after failure or cancellation.","No false success is reported.","Recovery does not corrupt protected state."],"failure_interpretation":"Permanent lock retention or silent state corruption after a deadlock violates the expected outcome.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Lock recovery must not bypass authorization or expose resources while resolving contention.","performance_considerations":"Lock contention and timeout behavior should be measured separately from functional correctness.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Concurrent systems and locking engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B12"}]}

{"record_id":"D43-B13-R001","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B13","knowledge_type":"API_OUTCOME","title":"Successful API Contract Outcome","objective":"Define the expected result when an API request satisfies its protocol and application contract.","precondition":"Request syntax, authentication, authorization, parameters, and domain conditions are valid.","input":"Valid API request.","action":"Process the request through the API contract.","expected_result":"The response status, headers, representation, and side effects conform to the documented API contract.","observable_outcomes":["protocol status","response representation","required headers","documented state change"],"pass_criteria":["Response semantics match the contract.","Representation is structurally valid for the negotiated or declared format.","Actual side effects match the documented operation."],"failure_interpretation":"A response that looks successful but violates representation or side-effect semantics is not a successful API outcome.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Authentication and authorization remain independent outcome dimensions from protocol success.","performance_considerations":"Response latency and payload size require separate measurable requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","evidence_type":"PROTOCOL_STANDARD","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B52"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B13-R002","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B13","knowledge_type":"API_OUTCOME","title":"API Error Contract Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when an API operation fails.","precondition":"The request reaches the API boundary but cannot be completed successfully.","input":"Request causing a documented application, validation, authorization, or dependency failure.","action":"Process the request through the API error boundary.","expected_result":"The API returns a contract-defined error representation and does not misrepresent the failed operation as successful.","observable_outcomes":["error status","machine-readable error classification where defined","safe diagnostic representation","actual side-effect state"],"pass_criteria":["Status and body are semantically consistent.","Sensitive implementation details are not unnecessarily exposed.","The error does not claim completion that did not occur."],"failure_interpretation":"A technically valid HTTP response with misleading application semantics remains an API outcome failure.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Error responses must avoid credential, secret, stack-trace, and unauthorized resource disclosure.","performance_considerations":"Error handling should not trigger unbounded retries or expensive diagnostics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","evidence_type":"PROTOCOL_STANDARD","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B02-R001"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B13-R003","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B13","knowledge_type":"API_OUTCOME","title":"API Idempotent Retry Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior for a retryable API operation whose semantics require idempotent effect.","precondition":"The operation contract defines repeated equivalent requests as having the same intended effect.","input":"Repeated equivalent API requests.","action":"Process the original request and one or more permitted retries.","expected_result":"Repeated requests do not produce unintended additional logical effects.","observable_outcomes":["individual response outcomes","final authoritative state","operation identity where applicable"],"pass_criteria":["Final state matches the idempotency contract.","Retries do not create unintended duplicates.","A failed initial attempt is not incorrectly treated as committed solely because a response was lost."],"failure_interpretation":"Duplicate side effects from permitted retries indicate a mismatch between API and implementation semantics.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Idempotency is particularly important for financial, administrative, and privileged operations.","performance_considerations":"Idempotency records require retention and lookup strategies that should be bounded and measurable.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","section":"9.2.2","evidence_type":"PROTOCOL_STANDARD","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"related_to","target":"D43-B12-R004"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B13-R004","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B13","knowledge_type":"API_OUTCOME","title":"Representation Negotiation Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when the client and server negotiate a representation format.","precondition":"The protocol or API supports multiple representation formats and the request provides valid representation preferences or declarations.","input":"Request with supported or unsupported representation requirements.","action":"Perform representation negotiation.","expected_result":"The server returns a representation compatible with the applicable contract or explicitly reports that the requested representation cannot be provided.","observable_outcomes":["selected representation","content metadata","negotiation failure when applicable"],"pass_criteria":["Returned representation matches declared content semantics.","Unsupported requested representations are not silently mislabeled.","Serialization is valid for the selected format."],"failure_interpretation":"Returning content under an incorrect representation declaration creates an interoperability failure.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Content-type confusion must not permit security-sensitive interpretation of data in an unintended format.","performance_considerations":"Negotiation should not cause unnecessary serialization of multiple representations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source":"RFC 9110","evidence_type":"PROTOCOL_STANDARD","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B52"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B13-R005","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B13","knowledge_type":"API_OUTCOME","title":"Pagination Boundary Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior at page boundaries and result-set exhaustion.","precondition":"An API exposes paginated results with documented page size, cursor, offset, or continuation semantics.","input":"Valid pagination request including first, middle, final, empty, and boundary positions.","action":"Retrieve the requested page.","expected_result":"The response contains exactly the records permitted by the pagination contract and provides correct continuation information when applicable.","observable_outcomes":["returned item set","page metadata","continuation token or equivalent","exhaustion state"],"pass_criteria":["No item is silently skipped or duplicated under the declared pagination consistency model.","Page-size constraints are respected.","End-of-results is represented correctly."],"failure_interpretation":"Incorrect continuation metadata can produce silent data loss or duplicate processing even when each individual response is syntactically valid.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Pagination must enforce authorization consistently across every page and continuation token.","performance_considerations":"Large pages should respect configured resource limits; pagination correctness must not be sacrificed for payload efficiency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"API pagination engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B52"}]}

{"record_id":"D43-B14-R001","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B14","knowledge_type":"CONFIGURATION_OUTCOME","title":"Valid Configuration Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when all required configuration values satisfy the application contract.","precondition":"Configuration values are present, correctly typed, within permitted ranges, and mutually compatible.","input":"Complete valid configuration.","action":"Load and validate configuration before application startup or operation execution.","expected_result":"The application establishes the intended configuration and reports readiness only after required configuration validation succeeds.","observable_outcomes":["validated configuration state","startup/readiness result","effective configuration provenance where permitted"],"pass_criteria":["Required settings are present.","Values satisfy constraints.","Configuration-dependent components observe the intended values.","Secrets are not exposed in ordinary diagnostics."],"failure_interpretation":"Starting successfully with an invalid or silently substituted critical configuration violates the configuration outcome contract.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Secrets must be loaded through controlled mechanisms and excluded from logs or diagnostic output.","performance_considerations":"Configuration loading should not repeatedly perform expensive remote work when static configuration is sufficient.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Configuration-management engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B54"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B14-R002","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B14","knowledge_type":"CONFIGURATION_OUTCOME","title":"Invalid Configuration Startup Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a required configuration value is invalid or incompatible.","precondition":"At least one required configuration value violates its type, range, format, dependency, or security constraint.","input":"Invalid application configuration.","action":"Start or initialize the affected component.","expected_result":"The component refuses to enter a state that falsely indicates valid operation and reports a controlled configuration failure.","observable_outcomes":["startup failure or explicit degraded state","configuration validation evidence","safe diagnostic classification"],"pass_criteria":["Invalid critical configuration cannot silently become operational behavior.","The component does not report healthy readiness when required configuration is invalid.","Sensitive configuration values are not leaked in diagnostics."],"failure_interpretation":"Starting with invalid critical configuration can produce undefined or unsafe behavior and is a failed outcome.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Security-sensitive configuration failures should default to safe behavior rather than permissive behavior.","performance_considerations":"Configuration validation should occur before expensive initialization where possible.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Configuration-management engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","target":"D43-B14-R001"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B14-R003","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B14","knowledge_type":"CONFIGURATION_OUTCOME","title":"Configuration Precedence Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when multiple configuration sources provide values for the same setting.","precondition":"The system explicitly defines precedence among configuration sources such as defaults, files, environment variables, or runtime overrides.","input":"Multiple valid values for the same configuration key.","action":"Resolve the effective configuration using the documented precedence rules.","expected_result":"The effective value is selected deterministically according to the declared precedence model.","observable_outcomes":["effective value or redacted representation","source precedence decision","configuration version or context"],"pass_criteria":["Precedence is deterministic.","Undocumented implicit precedence is not used.","Security-sensitive overrides require the appropriate authorization and trust boundary."],"failure_interpretation":"Different executions selecting different configuration values under identical source conditions indicate a configuration determinism defect.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Higher-precedence untrusted sources must not override protected security configuration without explicit policy.","performance_considerations":"Configuration resolution should be deterministic and bounded; repeated source discovery should be avoided where unnecessary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Configuration-management engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B14-R001"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B14-R004","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B14","knowledge_type":"CONFIGURATION_OUTCOME","title":"Configuration Hot-Reload Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when configuration is intentionally changed while a component remains running.","precondition":"The system explicitly supports runtime configuration changes and defines which settings may be reloaded.","input":"Valid runtime configuration update.","action":"Apply the configuration update through the supported mechanism.","expected_result":"Only settings eligible for runtime change are updated, dependent components observe the new effective configuration according to the contract, and non-reloadable settings remain unchanged until their documented lifecycle event.","observable_outcomes":["configuration version transition","updated effective settings","reload success or rejection","component state"],"pass_criteria":["Reloadable settings change correctly.","Non-reloadable settings are not silently changed.","A failed reload does not leave partially applied invalid configuration."],"failure_interpretation":"Partial or inconsistent hot reload without explicit semantics is a configuration integrity failure.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Runtime security-policy changes require authorization and atomicity appropriate to their impact.","performance_considerations":"Reload mechanisms should avoid unnecessary component restarts or resource churn while preserving correctness.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Configuration lifecycle engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"related_to","target":"D43-B14-R003"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B14-R005","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B14","knowledge_type":"CONFIGURATION_OUTCOME","title":"Configuration Provenance Outcome","objective":"Define expected evidence for determining where the effective configuration originated.","precondition":"The system requires configuration traceability for debugging, governance, or reproducibility.","input":"Effective configuration assembled from one or more sources.","action":"Record or inspect configuration provenance under permitted access controls.","expected_result":"Authorized operators can determine the source and applicable version of each important effective setting without exposing protected secret values.","observable_outcomes":["configuration provenance","source identifier","version or timestamp where applicable","redacted effective value"],"pass_criteria":["Important settings have traceable provenance.","Secrets remain protected.","Configuration provenance corresponds to the actual effective value."],"failure_interpretation":"Configuration that cannot be traced to an authoritative source undermines reproducibility and diagnosis.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Provenance must redact credentials, tokens, keys, and other sensitive values.","performance_considerations":"Provenance collection should avoid excessive repeated configuration introspection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Configuration provenance and reproducibility engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B54"}]}

{"record_id":"D43-B15-R001","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B15","knowledge_type":"DEPENDENCY_OUTCOME","title":"Healthy Dependency Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a required dependency is reachable and satisfies its contract.","precondition":"The dependency is available, authenticated where required, and capable of returning contract-valid responses.","input":"Operation requiring the dependency.","action":"Invoke the dependency and consume its result.","expected_result":"The dependency interaction completes according to the integration contract and the caller uses the result without violating local invariants.","observable_outcomes":["dependency response","integration result","local state transition where applicable"],"pass_criteria":["Dependency response satisfies the expected schema and semantics.","The caller handles the response according to the contract.","No fabricated fallback is used when valid authoritative data is available."],"failure_interpretation":"Treating malformed or semantically invalid dependency output as successful authoritative data is an integration failure.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Dependency identity and authorization must be verified according to the trust boundary.","performance_considerations":"Dependency latency contributes to the caller's operation budget and should be observable separately.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Service integration engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B03-R004"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B15-R002","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B15","knowledge_type":"DEPENDENCY_OUTCOME","title":"Malformed Dependency Response Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when an external dependency returns data that violates its integration contract.","precondition":"The dependency responds but the response cannot be safely interpreted under the agreed contract.","input":"Malformed, incomplete, incompatible, or semantically invalid dependency response.","action":"Process the dependency response.","expected_result":"The caller rejects, quarantines, retries, or otherwise handles the response according to the integration failure policy without treating invalid data as authoritative success.","observable_outcomes":["contract-validation failure","dependency failure classification","safe local state"],"pass_criteria":["Invalid dependency data does not silently become valid application state.","The failure is distinguishable from valid business data.","Any fallback is explicitly permitted and correctly classified."],"failure_interpretation":"Accepting invalid dependency data as authoritative state is an integration integrity failure.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Malformed external data must not bypass local authorization or validation boundaries.","performance_considerations":"Contract validation should be bounded and should not permit unbounded parsing or resource consumption.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Service contract and defensive integration engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","target":"D43-B15-R001"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B15-R003","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B15","knowledge_type":"DEPENDENCY_OUTCOME","title":"Dependency Timeout Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a dependency fails to respond within the permitted deadline.","precondition":"A dependency call has a defined timeout or deadline.","input":"Dependency invocation that exceeds the permitted response time.","action":"Wait until the deadline and apply the dependency timeout policy.","expected_result":"The caller reports or handles the timeout according to the contract and does not claim that the dependency completed successfully.","observable_outcomes":["timeout classification","caller operation result","retry/fallback state if permitted"],"pass_criteria":["Timeout is distinguishable from a valid dependency response.","Retries are bounded and policy-controlled.","No false downstream state is committed based on an unavailable result."],"failure_interpretation":"Treating a timeout as a successful dependency result is an outcome-integrity failure.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Timeout must not cause security checks to fail open.","performance_considerations":"Timeouts should compose within an overall latency budget; retry multiplication must remain bounded.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems timeout engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B03-R004"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B15-R004","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B15","knowledge_type":"DEPENDENCY_OUTCOME","title":"Dependency Retry Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a dependency operation is retried after a transient failure.","precondition":"The dependency failure is classified as retryable and a bounded retry policy exists.","input":"Dependency request with a transient failure followed by one or more permitted retries.","action":"Apply the retry policy.","expected_result":"Retries occur only within the configured policy and the final result accurately reflects whether the dependency operation eventually succeeded or failed.","observable_outcomes":["attempt sequence","final dependency outcome","retry classification"],"pass_criteria":["Non-retryable failures are not repeatedly retried.","Retry count and timing remain bounded by policy.","Successful retry is distinguishable from initial success."],"failure_interpretation":"Unbounded retries, retrying non-retryable failures, or reporting success without a successful dependency result violates the expected outcome.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Retries must not multiply privileged side effects when the dependency operation is non-idempotent.","performance_considerations":"Retry backoff and overall timeout budgets must be explicit; no specific timing values are assumed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Distributed systems retry engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"related_to","target":"D43-B15-R003"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B15-R005","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B15","knowledge_type":"DEPENDENCY_OUTCOME","title":"Dependency Version Compatibility Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a dependency changes version while preserving or changing an integration contract.","precondition":"The consuming component declares a compatible dependency version range or contract.","input":"Dependency implementation at a candidate version.","action":"Execute the integration contract against the candidate dependency version.","expected_result":"The integration either satisfies all declared compatibility requirements or is rejected before unsafe operation.","observable_outcomes":["compatibility result","contract test result","dependency version identity"],"pass_criteria":["Required APIs and semantics remain compatible.","Breaking changes are detected rather than silently misinterpreted.","The effective dependency version is recorded for reproducibility."],"failure_interpretation":"Successful startup does not prove semantic compatibility if runtime behavior violates the integration contract.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Dependency upgrades must not silently weaken security defaults or authorization behavior.","performance_considerations":"Version upgrades require measurement when performance compatibility is part of the contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source":"Dependency compatibility engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B64"}]}

{"record_id":"D43-B16-R001","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B16","knowledge_type":"OBSERVABILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Successful Operation Observability Outcome","objective":"Define the minimum observable evidence required for a completed operation when observability is part of the system contract.","precondition":"The operation has required logs, metrics, traces, audit events, or other observability obligations.","input":"Successfully completed operation.","action":"Execute the operation and inspect required observability channels.","expected_result":"Required observability signals accurately represent the operation without changing its functional meaning.","observable_outcomes":["operation log or event","metric update where required","trace correlation where required","audit evidence where required"],"pass_criteria":["Required signals are present.","Signal content corresponds to actual execution.","Correlation identifiers permit the required diagnostic linkage."],"failure_interpretation":"Functional success without mandatory observability evidence is an incomplete operational outcome.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Observability must not expose secrets or sensitive payloads beyond authorized policy.","performance_considerations":"Telemetry overhead must be bounded and separately measurable where material.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Observability engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B19"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B16-R002","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B16","knowledge_type":"OBSERVABILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Failure Observability Outcome","objective":"Define expected evidence when an operation fails.","precondition":"The operation encounters a classified failure requiring diagnosis or operational handling.","input":"Failed operation with available execution context.","action":"Process the failure and inspect observability outputs.","expected_result":"The failure produces sufficient authorized evidence to identify the failure class, affected operation, and relevant causal context without falsely indicating success.","observable_outcomes":["failure event","correlation context","failure classification","relevant diagnostics"],"pass_criteria":["Failure is observable through the required channel.","Evidence corresponds to actual failure.","Sensitive information is appropriately protected."],"failure_interpretation":"A failure that cannot be diagnosed because required evidence was omitted is an observability outcome defect.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Diagnostic evidence must be access-controlled and redacted where necessary.","performance_considerations":"Failure diagnostics should avoid unbounded payload capture or repeated expensive collection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Observability engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"related_to","target":"D43-B16-R001"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B16-R003","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B16","knowledge_type":"OBSERVABILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Trace Correlation Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a logical operation crosses multiple components that support distributed tracing or equivalent correlation.","precondition":"The architecture defines a correlation mechanism across component boundaries.","input":"A logical operation traversing multiple components.","action":"Execute the operation across the supported boundaries.","expected_result":"Observability records can be correlated to the same logical operation according to the tracing contract.","observable_outcomes":["correlation identifier","component spans or equivalent records","parent-child or causal relationship"],"pass_criteria":["Required propagation occurs across supported boundaries.","Records correspond to the correct operation.","Missing propagation is distinguishable from an actual operation failure."],"failure_interpretation":"Incorrect correlation can make evidence appear to belong to another operation and invalidate downstream diagnosis.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Correlation identifiers must not themselves disclose sensitive information.","performance_considerations":"Tracing overhead and sampling behavior should be explicit; sampled absence must not be interpreted as execution failure.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Distributed observability engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B16-R001"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B16-R004","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B16","knowledge_type":"OBSERVABILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Metric Semantic Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when an operational metric represents a specific business or technical quantity.","precondition":"A metric has a defined name, unit, aggregation, population, and semantic meaning.","input":"Operational events contributing to the metric.","action":"Record and aggregate the events according to the metric definition.","expected_result":"The resulting metric represents the defined quantity and does not silently mix incompatible populations or units.","observable_outcomes":["metric value","unit","aggregation semantics","measurement population"],"pass_criteria":["Metric semantics match its definition.","Units and aggregation are correct.","Missing or sampled data does not become an undocumented fabricated value."],"failure_interpretation":"A numerically plausible metric with incorrect semantics is an invalid outcome and can mislead automated reasoning.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Metrics must not leak sensitive dimensions through uncontrolled labels or attributes.","performance_considerations":"High-cardinality metric dimensions can create resource pressure; cardinality requirements should be explicit.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Observability and measurement engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B06-R001"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B16-R005","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B16","knowledge_type":"OBSERVABILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Telemetry Loss Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when observability data is lost or unavailable.","precondition":"The operation executes but one or more telemetry channels fail, drop, or become unavailable.","input":"Normal or failed operation under telemetry degradation.","action":"Execute the operation while observability infrastructure is impaired.","expected_result":"The application follows the documented telemetry-loss policy and does not fabricate missing evidence; functional behavior remains governed by the telemetry dependency classification.","observable_outcomes":["telemetry loss classification","available remaining evidence","functional outcome"],"pass_criteria":["Missing telemetry is distinguishable from absent execution.","No fabricated telemetry is emitted as if observed.","Critical functions do not silently depend on optional telemetry unless specified."],"failure_interpretation":"Treating telemetry absence as proof that an operation did not occur is an evidence error.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Security audit requirements may make some telemetry dependencies mandatory; failure semantics must be explicit.","performance_considerations":"Telemetry backpressure must not cause uncontrolled application resource exhaustion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Observability reliability engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"related_to","target":"D43-B16-R002"}]}

{"record_id":"D43-B17-R001","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B17","knowledge_type":"DETERMINISM_OUTCOME","title":"Deterministic Repeated Execution Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when the same deterministic input and execution context are expected to produce the same result.","precondition":"The operation is documented as deterministic under a defined environment and input state.","input":"Identical input, configuration, relevant state, and deterministic execution context.","action":"Execute the operation repeatedly under equivalent conditions.","expected_result":"The outputs and specified state effects are equivalent across executions.","observable_outcomes":["output sequence","state effects","execution context"],"pass_criteria":["Equivalent inputs produce equivalent outputs according to the contract.","Uncontrolled environmental differences are excluded or represented.","No hidden mutable state changes the logical result unexpectedly."],"failure_interpretation":"Unexpected variation under genuinely equivalent conditions indicates hidden state, nondeterministic dependency, or implementation defect.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Determinism must not require weakening security controls or removing per-request authorization.","performance_considerations":"Repeatability tests should distinguish functional determinism from performance variability.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Deterministic software engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B62"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B17-R002","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B17","knowledge_type":"DETERMINISM_OUTCOME","title":"Seeded Randomness Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when pseudo-random behavior is intentionally controlled by a seed.","precondition":"The implementation uses a seedable pseudo-random source and the contract defines reproducibility under the same seed and relevant state.","input":"Identical input and identical documented random seed/context.","action":"Execute the operation repeatedly.","expected_result":"The generated pseudo-random sequence or resulting deterministic behavior is reproducible to the degree defined by the implementation contract.","observable_outcomes":["seed/context","generated sequence or result","execution environment"],"pass_criteria":["Same supported seed/context produces equivalent results.","Unseeded or external entropy sources are not incorrectly assumed deterministic.","Version-specific differences are preserved where relevant."],"failure_interpretation":"Non-reproducibility under a contract that promises seeded reproducibility indicates a deterministic-control defect.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Predictability introduced by deterministic seeds must not be used for security-sensitive randomness unless a cryptographically appropriate mechanism explicitly permits it.","performance_considerations":"Reproducibility controls should not be confused with performance optimization.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source":"Programming-language and pseudo-randomness engineering principles","evidence_type":"LANGUAGE_ENGINEERING","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B62"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B17-R003","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B17","knowledge_type":"DETERMINISM_OUTCOME","title":"Replay Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a recorded operation is replayed against an equivalent replay context.","precondition":"The system provides sufficient recorded inputs and state context for a defined replay model.","input":"Recorded operation evidence and replay environment.","action":"Replay the operation according to the replay contract.","expected_result":"The replay produces an equivalent logical outcome where deterministic replay is promised, or explicitly identifies permitted divergence when external state has changed.","observable_outcomes":["replay result","original result","difference classification","replay context"],"pass_criteria":["Equivalent replay produces equivalent logical results when promised.","External dependencies and state differences are identified rather than hidden.","Replay does not mutate production state unless explicitly authorized."],"failure_interpretation":"Unexplained divergence under a deterministic replay contract indicates missing state, nondeterminism, or replay-environment mismatch.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Replay must isolate secrets and privileged side effects; replaying an action must not automatically re-execute harmful production effects.","performance_considerations":"Replay may require snapshots or recorded inputs whose storage and restoration cost should be bounded.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Reproducibility and replay engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B62"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B17-R004","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B17","knowledge_type":"DETERMINISM_OUTCOME","title":"Environment-Sensitive Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when an operation's result legitimately depends on environment state.","precondition":"The operation contract identifies environment variables such as time, locale, platform, filesystem state, configuration, or dependency version as outcome inputs.","input":"Equivalent logical request executed under different declared environments.","action":"Execute the operation in each environment.","expected_result":"Observed differences correspond only to documented environment-dependent behavior.","observable_outcomes":["environment identity","operation result","difference classification"],"pass_criteria":["Documented environmental dependencies explain observed differences.","Undocumented hidden dependencies are not required to explain expected behavior.","Version or platform-specific behavior is represented explicitly."],"failure_interpretation":"Unexpected environment sensitivity is a reproducibility defect when the contract does not permit it.","implementation_relevance":"High","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Environment-derived security configuration must be treated as trusted only within its defined trust boundary.","performance_considerations":"Different environments may have different performance characteristics; functional equivalence must not be inferred from timing equality.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Reproducible-build and environment-dependency engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","target":"D43-B17-R001"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B17-R005","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B17","knowledge_type":"DETERMINISM_OUTCOME","title":"Nondeterminism Classification Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when output variation is observed and the source of variation is not yet established.","precondition":"Repeated executions produce differing outcomes under apparently similar conditions.","input":"Repeated execution observations and available environmental evidence.","action":"Compare executions and classify potential sources of variation.","expected_result":"The system records the variation as observed and avoids asserting an unverified cause until sufficient evidence establishes the cause.","observable_outcomes":["observed output differences","environment differences","candidate cause classification","verification status"],"pass_criteria":["Observed variation is preserved accurately.","Cause is separated from observation.","Unknown causes remain UNKNOWN or INFERENCE rather than becoming asserted facts."],"failure_interpretation":"Assigning an unsupported cause to nondeterministic behavior can misdirect debugging and training.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Sensitive environment differences must be protected while retaining enough evidence for authorized diagnosis.","performance_considerations":"Diagnostic replay and comparison should be bounded by available resources.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Reproducibility and debugging engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"related_to","target":"D43-B17-R001"},{"type":"contradicts","target":"unsupported_causal_assertion"}]}

{"record_id":"D43-B18-R001","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B18","knowledge_type":"COMPATIBILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Backward-Compatible Behavior Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a newer implementation must preserve a documented compatibility contract.","precondition":"A compatibility contract identifies behavior that existing consumers are entitled to rely upon.","input":"Existing consumer request executed against the newer implementation.","action":"Execute the compatibility-sensitive operation.","expected_result":"Required legacy behavior remains compatible while intentionally changed behavior is limited to documented compatibility boundaries.","observable_outcomes":["consumer-visible result","compatibility classification","version context"],"pass_criteria":["Required existing behavior remains valid.","Intentional breaking changes are explicitly identified rather than silently introduced.","Compatibility is evaluated against actual behavior, not version labels alone."],"failure_interpretation":"A newer version that starts successfully but breaks a required consumer contract has failed compatibility.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Compatibility must not preserve insecure behavior when a security boundary explicitly requires a controlled breaking change.","performance_considerations":"Backward compatibility may carry performance costs; such costs require measurement rather than assumption.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source":"Software compatibility engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B64"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B18-R002","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B18","knowledge_type":"COMPATIBILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Version-Specific Behavior Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when an implementation intentionally differs across supported versions.","precondition":"The system or dependency documents behavior differences between supported versions.","input":"Equivalent logical operation executed against different supported versions.","action":"Execute the operation under each version context.","expected_result":"Each result matches the behavior documented for that version and is not incorrectly merged into a single version-independent claim.","observable_outcomes":["version identity","version-specific result","compatibility classification"],"pass_criteria":["Version-specific behavior is preserved.","Unsupported version assumptions are not applied to supported versions.","The knowledge record identifies the version scope of each claim."],"failure_interpretation":"Applying one version's behavior universally can cause incorrect code generation or debugging decisions.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Security behavior must be evaluated separately for each supported version where defaults or controls changed.","performance_considerations":"Performance behavior is version-sensitive and must not be generalized without measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source":"Version-aware software engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B18-R001"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B18-R003","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B18","knowledge_type":"COMPATIBILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Schema Evolution Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a serialized data schema evolves while existing consumers remain supported.","precondition":"The schema evolution contract defines supported additions, removals, defaults, or transformations.","input":"Data encoded using old or new schema versions.","action":"Serialize, transmit, parse, or transform the data across the supported version boundary.","expected_result":"Supported consumers interpret the data according to the compatibility contract without silent semantic corruption.","observable_outcomes":["schema version","parsed representation","compatibility result"],"pass_criteria":["Required fields retain their intended semantics.","Unsupported changes are detected.","Defaulting or transformation behavior matches the documented schema contract."],"failure_interpretation":"Parsing successfully while assigning incorrect semantics to a field is a compatibility failure.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Schema changes must preserve security classification, authorization fields, and validation semantics where applicable.","performance_considerations":"Compatibility transformations may add processing and memory cost; no performance claim is assumed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Schema evolution engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B59"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B18-R004","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B18","knowledge_type":"COMPATIBILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Cross-Language Behavioral Compatibility Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when equivalent functionality is implemented in different programming languages.","precondition":"Two implementations are intended to satisfy the same documented behavioral contract.","input":"Equivalent inputs provided to each implementation under compatible execution assumptions.","action":"Execute both implementations and compare contract-relevant results.","expected_result":"Both implementations satisfy the shared contract even if internal representations or language-specific mechanisms differ.","observable_outcomes":["implementation identity","contract result","difference classification"],"pass_criteria":["Observable contract behavior is equivalent where equivalence is required.","Language-specific implementation details are not incorrectly treated as contract differences.","Known platform or numeric representation differences are explicitly classified."],"failure_interpretation":"Matching syntax or similar architecture is insufficient if observable behavior violates the shared contract.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Security-relevant behavior such as validation and authorization must remain equivalent across implementations.","performance_considerations":"Behavioral equivalence does not imply performance equivalence; performance requires separate measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Cross-language software engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B98"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B18-R005","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B18","knowledge_type":"COMPATIBILITY_OUTCOME","title":"Breaking Change Detection Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a proposed implementation change violates a declared compatibility contract.","precondition":"A candidate implementation changes observable behavior relied upon by supported consumers.","input":"Existing consumer contract plus candidate implementation.","action":"Evaluate compatibility-sensitive operations against the candidate implementation.","expected_result":"The breaking behavior is detected and classified before the candidate is treated as compatible.","observable_outcomes":["compatibility failure","affected contract","version context","required migration information where applicable"],"pass_criteria":["Breaking behavior is distinguishable from acceptable change.","Affected consumers or contract surfaces are identified where evidence permits.","No unsupported compatibility claim is produced."],"failure_interpretation":"Silent breaking behavior creates downstream failures and invalidates compatibility knowledge.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Security-related breaking changes require explicit treatment rather than accidental compatibility preservation.","performance_considerations":"Compatibility regression suites should focus on behaviorally relevant surfaces rather than measuring unrelated implementation details.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Software compatibility engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","target":"D43-B18-R001"}]}

{"record_id":"D43-B19-R001","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B19","knowledge_type":"RESOURCE_LIFECYCLE_OUTCOME","title":"Resource Acquisition Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when an operation acquires a managed resource.","precondition":"A resource such as a file, connection, lock, process handle, memory-backed object, or external session must be acquired before work can proceed.","input":"Valid operation requiring the resource.","action":"Acquire the resource and continue execution.","expected_result":"The resource is acquired according to ownership, lifetime, and capacity rules and becomes available to the intended operation.","observable_outcomes":["acquisition result","resource ownership","resource lifecycle state"],"pass_criteria":["Acquisition succeeds only within configured limits.","Ownership is clear.","Failure to acquire is represented as failure rather than fabricated availability."],"failure_interpretation":"Using an unavailable resource as though acquisition succeeded produces false execution state.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Resource acquisition must enforce access permissions and tenant/resource isolation.","performance_considerations":"Resource pools and acquisition queues must respect capacity and timeout policies.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Resource lifecycle engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B57"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B19-R002","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B19","knowledge_type":"RESOURCE_LIFECYCLE_OUTCOME","title":"Resource Cleanup Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior after an operation finishes or fails while owning resources.","precondition":"The operation has acquired resources with explicit cleanup or release obligations.","input":"Completed or failed operation holding owned resources.","action":"Exit the operation through success, failure, cancellation, or exceptional paths.","expected_result":"Owned resources are released or transferred according to the lifecycle contract.","observable_outcomes":["resource release","remaining ownership state","cleanup result"],"pass_criteria":["Every resource with mandatory release semantics is handled.","Failure paths receive the same required cleanup as normal paths.","Released resources are not subsequently used by the completed operation."],"failure_interpretation":"Resource leakage or use-after-release indicates a lifecycle outcome failure.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Unreleased locks, handles, credentials, or privileged sessions can create security exposure.","performance_considerations":"Resource leaks can accumulate over time and cause exhaustion; lifecycle verification is required for long-lived processes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source":"Resource management engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B57"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B19-R003","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B19","knowledge_type":"RESOURCE_LIFECYCLE_OUTCOME","title":"Resource Ownership Transfer Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when responsibility for a resource moves between components or scopes.","precondition":"The architecture explicitly permits ownership transfer of a resource.","input":"Resource currently owned by one component and a valid transfer request.","action":"Transfer ownership according to the lifecycle contract.","expected_result":"The receiving owner becomes responsible for the resource and the previous owner no longer performs actions reserved for the new owner.","observable_outcomes":["previous owner state","new owner state","resource lifecycle status"],"pass_criteria":["Ownership is transferred exactly once.","The receiving owner can safely manage the resource.","The previous owner does not release or mutate the resource after valid transfer unless explicitly permitted."],"failure_interpretation":"Ambiguous or duplicated ownership can produce leaks, double release, or concurrent unsafe use.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Ownership transfer must preserve access-control and tenant boundaries.","performance_considerations":"Ownership transfer should not create unnecessary resource duplication unless required by the lifecycle model.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Resource ownership engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B19-R002"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B19-R004","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B19","knowledge_type":"RESOURCE_LIFECYCLE_OUTCOME","title":"Resource Exhaustion Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when resource capacity is exhausted.","precondition":"A finite resource pool reaches its configured capacity.","input":"Operation requiring an additional resource while no permitted capacity remains.","action":"Attempt resource acquisition.","expected_result":"The operation waits, rejects, sheds load, or otherwise handles exhaustion according to the documented resource policy without uncontrolled exhaustion.","observable_outcomes":["capacity-exhaustion classification","wait/rejection/degradation outcome","resource pool state"],"pass_criteria":["No resource limit is silently bypassed.","The caller receives a truthful outcome.","Existing resource holders remain protected from unsafe forced reclamation unless explicitly authorized."],"failure_interpretation":"Continuing allocation beyond the defined safety boundary can cause cascading failure and is a failed resource outcome.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Resource exhaustion can be externally triggered and must be considered as a denial-of-service boundary.","performance_considerations":"Queueing, rejection, and backpressure behavior should be measured under representative load.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Resource exhaustion and resilience engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B44"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B19-R005","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B19","knowledge_type":"RESOURCE_LIFECYCLE_OUTCOME","title":"Cancellation Cleanup Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when an operation is cancelled while holding managed resources.","precondition":"The operation is cancellable and currently owns resources with cleanup obligations.","input":"Cancellation request during active resource use.","action":"Cancel the operation and process its cleanup path.","expected_result":"Cancellation is reflected in the operation state and owned resources are released or transferred according to the lifecycle contract.","observable_outcomes":["cancellation state","cleanup result","resource ownership state"],"pass_criteria":["Cancellation is distinguishable from successful completion.","Required cleanup occurs.","No unauthorized or orphaned resource remains because of cancellation."],"failure_interpretation":"Reporting cancellation as success or leaking resources after cancellation violates the expected outcome contract.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Cancellation must not leave privileged sessions, locks, or temporary credentials active beyond their permitted lifetime.","performance_considerations":"Cleanup should be bounded and should not create unbounded cancellation backlog.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Resource lifecycle and cancellation engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"related_to","target":"D43-B19-R002"}]}

{"record_id":"D43-B20-R001","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B20","knowledge_type":"COMPOSITION_OUTCOME","title":"Multi-Step Composition Success Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when multiple independently specified operations compose into one higher-level workflow.","precondition":"Each required component operation has a compatible contract and the workflow defines ordering, dependency, and completion semantics.","input":"Valid workflow request satisfying all component preconditions.","action":"Execute the composed operations according to the workflow contract.","expected_result":"The workflow reaches its declared terminal outcome and each required component operation contributes a valid result.","observable_outcomes":["component outcomes","workflow state","final workflow result"],"pass_criteria":["Required steps execute according to dependency order.","Intermediate outcomes are consistent with downstream preconditions.","Final workflow status accurately reflects all required component outcomes."],"failure_interpretation":"A workflow cannot be classified as successful merely because its final coordinator returned successfully if required component outcomes were not established.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Each component security boundary remains enforceable; composition must not weaken individual authorization controls.","performance_considerations":"Workflow latency should account for critical-path dependencies and parallelizable work without changing correctness semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Workflow composition engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B07-R001"},{"type":"supports","target":"D42-B14"}]}
{"record_id":"D43-B20-R002","dataset":"D43","dataset_title":"Expected Outcomes","batch":"B20","knowledge_type":"COMPOSITION_OUTCOME","title":"Composition Dependency Failure Outcome","objective":"Define expected behavior when a required component of a composed workflow fails.","precondition":"A workflow contains a required component operation and that component terminates unsuccessfully.","input":"Workflow execution with one required component failure.","action":"Apply the workflow's failure, compensation, retry, or termination policy.","expected_result":"The workflow enters the documented failure or compensation state and does not report full success unless all required success conditions are satisfied.","observable_outcomes":["failed component result","workflow state","compensation or termination result"],"pass_criteria":["Required component failure propagates according to policy.","Compensation, if required, is represented separately from original success.","The final workflow outcome is truthful."],"failure_interpretation":"Ignoring a required component failure and reporting workflow success is an outcome-integrity defect.","implementation_relevance":"Critical","code_applicability":"DIRECT","security_considerations":"Compensation must preserve authorization and ownership rules and must not perform broader actions than the original workflow permits.","performance_considerations":"Retry and compensation must remain bounded by workflow deadlines and resource budgets.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"source":"Workflow and distributed transaction engineering principles","evidence_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","status":"SOURCE_REFERENCED"}],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","target":"D43-B03-R003"}]}
