{"record_id":"D44-B04-R002","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B04","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Repeated Operation on Terminal State","problem_statement":"Shutdown, cancellation, completion, deletion, or close operations may be invoked after the object has already reached a terminal state.","context":"Resource lifecycle, workflows, futures, transactions, sessions, files, sockets, and service shutdown paths permit repeated or late calls.","preconditions":["A terminal state exists.","Calls can be duplicated or race with state transition."],"inputs":["first terminal operation","repeated terminal operation","operation after terminal state","late callback after termination"],"expected_behavior":["Repeated terminal operations follow an explicit idempotency or error contract.","No resource is released twice.","Late callbacks cannot resurrect invalid state."],"implementation_guidance":["Represent terminal state explicitly.","Make cleanup idempotent when safe.","Detach or invalidate callbacks that can arrive after termination."],"failure_modes":["double close","use-after-close","state resurrection","duplicate side effect"],"edge_cases":["close called concurrently","cancel after complete","delete after already deleted","callback after timeout"],"security_considerations":["Terminal-state confusion can expose stale resources or bypass lifecycle authorization.","Ensure terminated sessions cannot regain authority through delayed operations."],"performance_considerations":["Idempotency checks are generally preferable to expensive repeated cleanup; exact trade-offs depend on implementation."],"test_considerations":["Invoke terminal operations repeatedly and in different orders.","Include delayed callbacks and concurrent termination."],"verification_requirements":["Verify terminal-state transition invariants and cleanup ownership."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_cla