{"record_id":"D44-B04-R004","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B04","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Rollback From Partially Applied State","problem_statement":"A multi-step operation can fail after some steps have succeeded, leaving partial state that must either be compensated or explicitly represented.","context":"Transactions, workflows, provisioning, deployment, migration, and distributed operations execute multiple dependent changes.","preconditions":["The operation has multiple externally visible steps.","Failure can occur between steps."],"inputs":["failure before first step","failure between steps","failure after final step","compensation failure"],"expected_behavior":["Partial progress is either atomically rolled back, compensated, or represented as a recoverable state.","Compensation does not assume resources still exist unchanged.","The final state is observable and diagnosable."],"implementation_guidance":["Define recovery semantics per step.","Persist enough state to resume or compensate safely when operations are long-running.","Do not label a compensation attempt as successful without evidence."],"failure_modes":["partial commit","orphaned resource","duplicate compensation","irrecoverable intermediate state"],"edge_cases":["compensation itself fails","process crashes during rollback","external dependency unavailable","rollback order differs from forward order"],"security_considerations":["Compensation must preserve authorization boundaries and must not execute with broader authority than the original operation without explicit policy."],"performance_considerations":["Durable recovery metadata adds storage and I/O overhead; optimize only with evidence."],"test_considerations":["Inject failure after each externally visible step.","Test recovery after process interruption and dependency failure."],"verification_requirements":["Verify final state, not merely that rollback code executed.","Record evidence for each recovery step where auditability is required."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable transaction/workflow specification and distributed-systems reliability literature","evidence_role":"partial failure semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B04-R003","D44-B06-R003"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B04-R005","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B04","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Stale State After Reinitialization","problem_statement":"A component may retain references, caches, handles, or assumptions from an earlier lifecycle instance after being reset or recreated.","context":"Long-lived processes, reconnecting clients, caches, object pools, reloadable configuration, and service restarts recreate state.","preconditions":["State can be reset or recreated.","References to prior state can survive longer than the state itself."],"inputs":["fresh instance","reset instance","old reference after reset","cached value from prior lifecycle"],"expected_behavior":["Old state cannot contaminate the new lifecycle.","Stale references are invalidated or safely isolated.","Caches and derived state correspond to the current lifecycle identity."],"implementation_guidance":["Associate lifecycle-scoped state with an explicit generation or instance identity where needed.","Invalidate derived state during reset.","Avoid globally shared mutable state unless its lifecycle is explicitly managed."],"failure_modes":["stale cache read","use-after-reset","cross-generation state leakage","incorrect authorization context"],"edge_cases":["rapid reset/reinitialize cycle","callback from old generation","concurrent reset and read","persisted state from incompatible version"],"security_considerations":["Old credentials, permissions, or tenant context must not survive a lifecycle boundary unintentionally."],"performance_considerations":["Generation checks add small runtime work but can prevent expensive stale-state failures; exact cost should be measured when material."],"test_considerations":["Test operations from both old and new generations.","Test reset concurrent with callbacks and cached reads."],"verification_requirements":["Verify lifecycle generation isolation where stale state is possible."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"reference_source":"General software lifecycle and state-isolation engineering principles","evidence_role":"stale-state reasoning"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B04-R001","D44-B05-R004"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B05-R001","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B05","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Race Between Check and Use","problem_statement":"A condition can change between validation and the operation that relies on the validation result.","context":"Concurrent programs check resource existence, permissions, versions, locks, files, or state before acting.","preconditions":["Another actor can modify the relevant state between check and use.","The implementation performs separate check and use operations."],"inputs":["state valid at check","state changed before use","state unchanged"],"expected_behavior":["The implementation maintains correctness despite intervening changes.","Atomic operations or synchronization are used when the contract requires them.","Failure after a stale check is handled safely."],"implementation_guidance":["Prefer atomic primitives where available.","Use appropriate synchronization or transactional semantics.","Do not treat a prior check as permanent authority over mutable state."],"failure_modes":["TOCTOU race","stale authorization decision","file replacement","lost update"],"edge_cases":["state changes immediately after check","multiple concurrent writers","permission revoked between check and use","resource deleted and recreated"],"security_considerations":["TOCTOU conditions can become security vulnerabilities when checks protect sensitive resources.","Perform security checks as close as possible to the protected operation and use atomic mechanisms where available."],"performance_considerations":["Synchronization can reduce concurrency; select the narrowest mechanism that preserves required invariants and validate with measurement."],"test_considerations":["Use controlled concurrency to force the state change between check and use.","Test repeated interleavings rather than one lucky schedule."],"verification_requirements":["Verify the invariant under permitted interleavings, not only sequential execution."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"CWE-367 and applicable concurrency documentation","evidence_role":"TOCTOU semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B05-R002","D44-B09-R001"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B05-R002","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B05","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Simultaneous Update Conflict","problem_statement":"Two actors can read the same prior state and independently write changes, causing one update to overwrite or invalidate the other.","context":"Shared-memory state, databases, distributed services, configuration stores, and collaborative data systems support concurrent writers.","preconditions":["Multiple writers can operate concurrently.","Updates depend on previously observed state."],"inputs":["concurrent updates to different fields","concurrent updates to same field","stale version update","commutative updates"],"expected_behavior":["Conflict behavior follows the consistency and domain contract.","Non-conflicting updates remain independent when supported.","Stale writes are rejected, merged, or ordered according to explicit policy."],"implementation_guidance":["Use version checks, transactions, compare-and-swap, locks, or domain-specific merge rules as appropriate.","Avoid accidental last-write-wins when it violates domain semantics."],"failure_modes":["lost update","write skew","stale overwrite","inconsistent aggregate"],"edge_cases":["equal version writes","clock skew","retry after conflict","partial field update"],"security_considerations":["Concurrent updates to permissions, ownership, or policy require strong conflict semantics appropriate to the security model."],"performance_considerations":["Stronger coordination may reduce write concurrency; quantify the trade-off rather than assuming one strategy is universally faster."],"test_considerations":["Force simultaneous reads followed by writes.","Test conflict resolution with retries and stale versions."],"verification_requirements":["Verify the intended concurrency invariant under conflicting schedules."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable transaction/concurrency specification and database documentation","evidence_role":"concurrent update semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B02-R005","D44-B05-R001"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B05-R003","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B05","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Completion Versus Cancellation Race","problem_statement":"An asynchronous operation may complete at nearly the same time that cancellation is requested, creating ambiguous ownership of the final result.","context":"Futures, tasks, jobs, network requests, workflows, and background operations support cancellation.","preconditions":["Cancellation and completion can occur concurrently.","The operation has externally observable completion semantics."],"inputs":["cancel before execution","cancel during execution","cancel after completion","completion concurrent with cancellation"],"expected_behavior":["The final state is deterministic according to the cancellation contract.","A completed result is not incorrectly reported as cancelled, or vice versa.","Cleanup occurs exactly as required."],"implementation_guidance":["Define state precedence explicitly.","Make cancellation idempotent where possible.","Ensure callbacks observe a consistent terminal state."],"failure_modes":["lost result","double callback","resource leak","incorrect cancellation status"],"edge_cases":["zero-time operation","callback after cancellation","cancellation during finalization","repeated cancellation"],"security_considerations":["Cancellation must not bypass required audit or cleanup steps.","Sensitive operations may require explicit cancellation authorization."],"performance_considerations":["Cancellation checks can be placed at safe interruption points; overly frequent checks may have measurable cost in tight loops."],"test_considerations":["Force completion and cancellation into controlled races.","Test all terminal-state combinations."],"verification_requirements":["Verify terminal-state invariants and exactly-once cleanup requirements where applicable."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable asynchronous API and concurrency documentation","evidence_role":"cancellation semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B04-R002","D44-B05-R004"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B05-R004","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B05","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Delayed Event After State Change","problem_statement":"An event generated under an earlier state may arrive after the receiver has transitioned to a newer state.","context":"Asynchronous messaging, callbacks, queues, event-driven systems, and distributed workflows deliver events with nonzero delay.","preconditions":["Events can be delayed or reordered.","Receiver state can change independently of event arrival."],"inputs":["current-state event","stale event","duplicate delayed event","event after terminal state"],"expected_behavior":["Events are accepted, ignored, rejected, or compensated according to version and lifecycle rules.","Stale events cannot corrupt newer state.","Terminal-state behavior is explicit."],"implementation_guidance":["Include version, sequence, generation, or causality information where required.","Treat delayed events as normal distributed behavior rather than impossible cases.","Make event handlers idempotent when delivery can repeat."],"failure_modes":["state rollback","stale overwrite","duplicate side effect","resurrection of terminated workflow"],"edge_cases":["event delayed across restart","same sequence number from different producers","missing ordering metadata","event arrives after timeout"],"security_considerations":["Stale authorization events must not restore expired privileges or policies.","Validate event origin and authorization independently of event freshness."],"performance_considerations":["Retaining version metadata and deduplication state consumes resources; define bounded retention when possible."],"test_considerations":["Deliver events out of order and after state transitions.","Test duplicates and stale versions."],"verification_requirements":["Verify state transition behavior under delayed and reordered delivery."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable messaging/distributed-systems semantics","evidence_role":"ordering and stale-event behavior"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B05-R002","D44-B06-R002"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B05-R005","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B05","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Starvation and Progress Failure","problem_statement":"A system can remain technically active while one task, actor, or state transition receives insufficient progress because of scheduling, locking, priority, or resource allocation.","context":"Concurrent systems use queues, locks, executors, priority scheduling, worker pools, or cooperative progress mechanisms.","preconditions":["Multiple tasks compete for shared execution or resources.","Fairness or progress is part of the required behavior."],"inputs":["equal-priority tasks","one high-priority task","long-running holder","repeatedly delayed task"],"expected_behavior":["Required progress and fairness properties are preserved where specified.","Long-running or high-priority work cannot indefinitely block required work unless explicitly permitted."],"implementation_guidance":["Define fairness requirements before selecting synchronization or scheduling mechanisms.","Use bounded work units or preemption where the runtime supports it and the domain requires it.","Avoid unbounded priority inversion."],"failure_modes":["starvation","priority inversion","queue monopolization","livelock"],"edge_cases":["single worker","all tasks blocked on one resource","continuous arrival of higher-priority work","reentrant scheduling"],"security_considerations":["Resource starvation can become denial of service.","Per-tenant or per-principal limits may be required in shared systems."],"performance_considerations":["Throughput and fairness can conflict; measurements must distinguish average throughput from tail progress and starvation behavior."],"test_considerations":["Run adversarial scheduling patterns.","Measure progress guarantees separately from raw throughput."],"verification_requirements":["Verify explicit fairness/progress properties where required; do not infer them from successful completion of a single r