{"record_id":"D44-B06-R001","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B06","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Network Boundary Failure","problem_statement":"Network operations can encounter immediate refusal, connection reset, timeout, partial transfer, unreachable destination, or successful connection followed by protocol failure.","context":"Clients and services communicate over networks where connectivity and protocol execution are separate failure domains.","preconditions":["The operation depends on an external network endpoint.","The client can observe multiple network failure modes."],"inputs":["connection refused","connection timeout","connection reset","partial response","protocol error after connection"],"expected_behavior":["Each failure category maps to an appropriate retry, fallback, or terminal behavior.","Partial data is not treated as a complete successful response.","Resources are released after failed communication."],"implementation_guidance":["Separate transport failure from application-level failure.","Use explicit timeouts appropriate to the operation.","Validate complete protocol framing before accepting a response."],"failure_modes":["infinite retry","partial-data acceptance","resource leak","misclassified application error"],"edge_cases":["zero-byte response","response truncated after valid prefix","connection succeeds but server never completes response","DNS succeeds but route fails"],"security_considerations":["Untrusted network data requires protocol and size validation.","Retry behavior must not amplify requests against external systems."],"performance_considerations":["Timeouts and retries directly affect tail latency and resource consumption; choose them from explicit service objectives rather than arbitrary defaults."],"test_considerations":["Test each network failure mode independently.","Test partial reads/writes and timeout during different protocol phases."],"verification_requirements":["Verify timeout, retry, and partial-transfer semantics against the protocol/client contract."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"RFC 9110 and applicable network-client documentation","evidence_role":"HTTP/network failure semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B06-R002","D44-B06-R003"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B06-R002","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B06","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Duplicate Delivery","problem_statement":"Messages or requests may be delivered more than once because of retries, acknowledgements lost after processing, or broker delivery semantics.","context":"Messaging systems, webhooks, distributed jobs, and retrying clients process operations under at-least-once or uncertain delivery.","preconditions":["Duplicate delivery is possible.","The operation may have externally visible side effects."],"inputs":["single delivery","exact duplicate","duplicate after partial processing","duplicate after successful processing"],"expected_behavior":["Repeated delivery follows the idempotency contract.","Already-completed operations are recognized when required.","Conflicting duplicate payloads are treated as a separate error condition."],"implementation_guidance":["Use stable idempotency keys or operation identifiers where appropriate.","Persist idempotency state for at least the required replay window.","Do not rely solely on in-memory duplicate tracking for durable operations."],"failure_modes":["duplicate side effect","double charge","duplicate resource creation","inconsistent acknowledgement"],"edge_cases":["duplicate after process restart","same identifier with different payload","duplicate outside retention window","concurrent duplicates"],"security_considerations":["Replay of authenticated requests may still be authorized but unsafe; replay resistance may require idempotency and freshness controls."],"performance_considerations":["Idempotency storage adds persistence and lookup cost; retention policy should reflect actual replay risk."],"test_considerations":["Deliver duplicates sequentially and concurrently.","Test duplicates across restarts and retention boundaries."],"verification_requirements":["Verify the actual side-effect invariant rather than merely observing duplicate detection."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable messaging semantics and distributed-systems reliability documentation","evidence_role":"duplicate delivery behavior"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B02-R004","D44-B05-R004"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B06-R003","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B06","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Retry Amplification","problem_statement":"Independent retries at multiple layers can multiply traffic and work far beyond the original request rate.","context":"Clients, gateways, SDKs, service layers, queues, and workers may each implement retries.","preconditions":["More than one layer can retry the same logical operation.","The failure is persistent or correlated across layers."],"inputs":["single request with no retry","single-layer retry","multi-layer retry","retry during downstream overload"],"expected_behavior":["Retry ownership is explicit.","Retry counts are bounded.","Transient failures do not cause unbounded amplification."],"implementation_guidance":["Define which layer owns retries.","Use bounded attempts and appropriate backoff.","Avoid retrying non-transient failures.","Propagate deadlines where the architecture supports them."],"failure_modes":["retry storm","cascading overload","duplicate side effects","deadline violation"],"edge_cases":["all layers retry simultaneously","service recovers briefly between attempts","downstream returns retryable status without capacity","request deadline expires during retry"],"security_considerations":["Retry amplification can create denial-of-service conditions and can magnify attacker-controlled requests."],"performance_considerations":["Retry increases load and tail latency; quantify policy using measured workload behavior rather than assumed gains."],"test_considerations":["Simulate persistent downstream failure with multiple retrying layers.","Measure total attempts generated per original request."],"verification_requirements":["Verify retry budgets and deadline propagation from architecture to implementation."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"RFC 9110 and established distributed-systems reliability guidance","evidence_role":"retry and overload semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B06-R001","D44-B06-R004"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B06-R004","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B06","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Clock Skew and Time-Ordered Distributed Data","problem_statement":"Distributed nodes can disagree about wall-clock time, making timestamps unreliable as the sole source of causal or ordering information.","context":"Distributed systems use timestamps for expiry, conflict resolution, event ordering, logging, and security decisions.","preconditions":["Multiple clocks participate in a shared protocol.","Clock synchronization is imperfect or bounded rather than mathematically exact."],"inputs":["equal timestamps","clock ahead","clock behind","timestamp missing","events with misleading wall-clock order"],"expected_behavior":["The system does not assume wall-clock order represents causality unless the protocol guarantees it.","Expiration and ordering tolerate the documented clock uncertainty.","Logical/version metadata is used where stronger ordering is required."],"implementation_guidance":["Distinguish wall-clock time from monotonic duration measurement and logical ordering.","Define acceptable clock skew for time-sensitive protocols.","Avoid using local wall time as the sole distributed conflict-resolution signal without justification."],"failure_modes":["premature expiration","late acceptance","incorrect event order","signature/token validity errors","conflict oscillation"],"edge_cases":["daylight-saving transition","clock correction","leap-second handling where applicable","node restart with incorrect clock"],"security_considerations":["Time-based authentication and authorization must account for defined clock tolerance and replay windows.","Clock manipulation can affect expiry and audit interpretation."],"performance_considerations":["Clock synchronization and timestamp metadata have operational costs but should not be optimized away when protocol correctness depends on them."],"test_considerations":["Test positive and negative clock offsets.","Test boundary behavior at expiry and validity windows."],"verification_requirements":["Verify time assumptions against the protocol specification and clock model."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"RFC 5905 and applicable protocol specifications","evidence_role":"distributed time semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B06-R005","D44-B08-R004"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B06-R005","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B06","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Partition With Locally Valid State","problem_statement":"A node can continue operating with locally consistent information while disconnected from the authority or peers needed to establish global consistency.","context":"Distributed databases, caches, services, replicated systems, and offline-capable applications experience communication partitions.","preconditions":["The system can continue local operation during connectivity loss.","Global state may diverge during the partition."],"inputs":["partition starts","local write during partition","remote write during partition","partition heals"],"expected_behavior":["The system follows an explicit availability and consistency policy.","Conflicting state is detected and resolved according to domain rules after reconnection.","Operations requiring authoritative global state fail or degrade safely."],"implementation_guidance":["Classify operations by whether local authority is sufficient.","Track version or conflict metadata where reconciliation is required.","Do not assume successful local persistence implies global commitment."],"failure_modes":["split-brain","lost update","stale authorization","divergent replicas"],"edge_cases":["long partition","simultaneous writes","partition during failover","reconnection with incompatible schema"],"security_considerations":["Security policy changes made elsewhere may be stale during partition; high-risk actions may require current authority.","Tenant isolation and authorization must remain valid during degraded operation."],"performance_considerations":["Reconciliation can create bursts of work after recovery; resource limits should account for backlog."],"test_considerations":["Simulate partition, local operations, conflicting remote operations, and healing.","Verify reconciliation evidence rather than only final convergence."],"verification_requirements":["Verify stated consistency guarantees under partition conditions."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Distributed-systems consistency literature and applicable storage/protocol specification","evidence_role":"partition semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B06-R002","D44-B06-R004"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B07-R001","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B07","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Transaction Boundary Mismatch","problem_statement":"An operation may update multiple resources whose actual atomicity boundaries differ, allowing partial persistence when the caller assumes one transaction.","context":"Application transactions span database tables, external services, files, queues, or multiple databases.","preconditions":["The logical operation contains multiple state changes.","Not all changes necessarily share one atomic transaction mechanism."],"inputs":["all changes succeed","first change fails","later change fails","commit succeeds but external side effect fails"],"expected_behavior":["The actual atomicity boundary is explicit.","Partial outcomes are recovered or represented according to the contract.","Callers do not assume atomicity across systems that cannot provide it."],"implementation_guidance":["Keep transaction boundaries aligned with actual guarantees.","Use transactional messaging or compensation patterns when required.","Document externally visible intermediate states."],"failure_modes":["partial commit","inconsistent external state","duplicate retry side effect","false transaction success"],"edge_cases":["database commit followed by network failure","external call before database commit","multiple databases","transaction timeout during commit"],"security_considerations":["Security-sensitive state must not become inconsistent across authorization, ownership, and resource records.","Audit records should not claim atomicity that the implementation cannot guarantee."],"performance_considerations":["Large transaction scopes can increase contention and recovery cost; choose boundaries from correctness and measured workload requirements."],"test_considerations":["Inject failure at each cross-boundary step.","Verify durable state after process crash at each boundary."],"verification_requirements":["Verify actual atomicity using implementation and storage semantics, not method naming."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable database transaction specification and distributed transaction documentation","evidence_role":"atomicity boundaries"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B04-R004","D44-B07-R002"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B07-R002","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B07","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Commit, Crash, and Recovery Boundary","problem_statement":"A process can terminate immediately before, during, or after a persistence commit, creating ambiguity unless the storage system defines durable commit semantics.","context":"Databases, filesystems, journals, queues, and durable state stores persist application state across process failure.","preconditions":["The operation depends on durable persistence.","Process or machine failure can occur during the commit boundary."],"inputs":["crash before commit","crash during commit","crash after commit but before acknowledgement","recovery after restart"],"expected_behavior":["Recovery reflects the storage system's actual durability guarantees.","The caller can distinguish committed, uncommitted, and unknown outcomes where necessary.","Retries do not create unintended duplicate effects."],"implementation_guidance":["Understand the storage engine's durability contract.","Use durable identifiers and idempotency when outcome acknowledgement can be lost.","Do not equate a successful local function return with durable persistence without evidence."],"failure_modes":["lost write","duplicate write","ambiguous outcome","corrupt recovery state"],"edge_cases":["power loss","process kill after flush but before response","journal replay","filesystem cache semantics"],"security_considerations":["Durability failures can undermine audit trails and security-state persistence.","Sensitive operations should not report durable completion without the required persistence guarantee."],"performance_considerations":["Stronger durability may require additional synchronization or I/O; performance claims must be measured on the target storage environment."],"test_considerations":["Use controlled process termination where the environment supports it.","Verify recovered state after each crash point."],"verification_requirements":["Verify durability using storage-specific guarantees and recovery evidence."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable database/filesystem durability documentation","evidence_role":"commit and recovery semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B07-R001","D44-B07-R003"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B07-R003","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B07","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Unique Constraint Conflict During Retry","problem_statement":"A retry may encounter a uniqueness conflict because the original attempt actually succeeded even though its response was lost.","context":"Create operations use unique identifiers, natural keys, idempotency keys, or database constraints while clients retry uncertain outcomes.","preconditions":["The operation creates or claims a unique resource.","The first attempt's outcome can become unknown to the caller."],"inputs":["first attempt succeeds","first attempt fails before persistence","response lost after commit","retry with same identity"],"expected_behavior":["The system distinguishes duplicate-as-success from conflicting duplicate where the contract permits.","A retry does not create a second logical resource.","Conflicting payloads are rejected rather than silently replacing the existing resource."],"implementation_guidance":["Design idempotency semantics around stable operation identity.","Use uniqueness constraints as an integrity mechanism, not as the complete application-level retry protocol.","Return enough information to distinguish prior success from unrelated conflict where appropriate."],"failure_modes":["duplicate resource","false failure after successful creation","incorrect overwrite","ambiguous retry result"],"edge_cases":["same key with identical payload","same key with different payload","key reused after retention expiry","concurrent creates"],"security_considerations":["Do not reveal sensitive resource existence through overly detailed conflict responses.","Authorization must still be evaluated for retries and duplicate lookups."],"performance_considerations":["Idempotency lookups and unique constraints add storage operations; retention and indexing strategy should match workload."],"test_considerations":["Simulate lost response after commit.","Test identical and conflicting retry payloads."],"verification_requirements":["Verify logical exactly-once side-effect behavior where promised; do not infer it from uniqueness alone."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable database uniqueness and API idempotency documentation","evidence_role":"retry/constraint semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B02-R004","D44-B06-R002"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B07-R004","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B07","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Schema Evolution With Existing Data","problem_statement":"A new schema may be valid for new records but incompatible with older persisted records or intermediate migration states.","context":"Databases, serialized files, event stores, caches, and long-lived artifacts survive application upgrades.","preconditions":["Stored data can outlive one application version.","Schema evolution is possible."],"inputs":["old schema record","new schema record","partially migrated record","unknown field","missing newly required field"],"expected_behavior":["Readers handle supported historical versions according to compatibility policy.","Migration is atomic or recoverable where required.","Unknown or deprecated fields do not cause accidental data loss."],"implementation_guidance":["Version schemas when compatibility requires it.","Separate migration logic from ordinary business logic where practical.","Define read-old/write-new and other migration strategies explicitly."],"failure_modes":["startup failure after upgrade","data loss on rewrite","incompatible reader","partial migration corruption"],"edge_cases":["migration interrupted","downgrade after upgrade","mixed-version cluster","new field with unsafe default"],"security_considerations":["Schema migration must preserve authorization, tenant, and sensitivity metadata.","Unsafe defaults for newly introduced security fields must fail closed where required."],"performance_considerations":["Large migrations can create I/O and lock pressure; use bounded and observable migration strategies where needed."],"test_considerations":["Test every supported historical schema version.","Test interruption and restart during migration."],"verification_requirements":["Verify migration invariants and backward/forward compatibility claims against actual supported versions."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable database/schema migration and serialization documentation","evidence_role":"schema evolution semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B03-R005","D44-B07-R005"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B07-R005","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B07","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Partial Record Visibility","problem_statement":"Readers may observe a subset of logically related writes when the persistence model does not guarantee the required atomic visibility.","context":"Concurrent database reads, replicated stores, caches, files, and multi-record updates expose intermediate states.","preconditions":["Multiple records represent one logical operation.","Readers can observe data concurrently with writes."],"inputs":["read before any write","read during partial update","read after complete update","read from lagging replica"],"expected_behavior":["Readers receive a state consistent with the declared isolation and consistency model.","Partial logical state is either impossible, explicitly represented, or safely handled."],"implementation_guidance":["Define logical consistency invariants independently of physical record layout.","Use transactions or versioned snapshots when required.","Do not infer consistency from sequential application code alone."],"failure_modes":["inconsistent read","orphaned relation","mixed-version object","incorrect derived calculation"],"edge_cases":["replica lag","transaction visibility boundary","cache contains old component","reader retries between records"],"security_considerations":["Partial visibility can expose information across authorization boundaries if related records are protected inconsistently."],"performance_considerations":["Stronger consistency may increase contention or latency; choose according to explicit requirements and measurements."],"test_considerations":["Read during controlled partial-write scenarios.","Test primary and replica visibility separately."],"verification_requirements":["Verify logical invariants under the declared consistency model."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable database isolation/consistency specification","evidence_role":"visibility semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B07-R001","D44-B06-R005"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B08-R001","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B08","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Malformed but Nearly Valid Serialization","problem_statement":"Serialized input can be structurally close to valid while containing one invalid delimiter, escape, type, length, or encoding condition.","context":"JSON, XML, CSV, protocol frames, configuration files, binary formats, and custom parsers process external representations.","preconditions":["The system parses externally supplied serialized data.","Malformed input can reach the parser."],"inputs":["valid document","truncated document","invalid escape","wrong type","unexpected delimiter","extra trailing data"],"expected_behavior":["Malformed input is rejected deterministically according to parser semantics.","Partial parsing does not produce a falsely successful business object.","Error reporting does not expose unnecessary sensitive content."],"implementation_guidance":["Validate framing and syntax before semantic processing.","Enforce maximum size and nesting limits where the format permits resource amplification.","Do not continue after a parser reports incomplete or invalid input unless the parser contract explicitly supports recovery."],"failure_modes":["parser confusion","partial object creation","silent data truncation","resource exhaustion"],"edge_cases":["empty document","whitespace-only document","duplicate object fields","deep nesting","truncated multibyte sequence"],"security_considerations":["Parser differentials and resource amplification can become security vulnerabilities.","Use well-defined parser behavior and strict limits for untrusted data."],"performance_considerations":["Parsing complexity and memory usage can become significant with large or deeply nested input; enforce bounded resource usage."],"test_considerations":["Test one corruption point at a time and combinations of malformed features.","Include truncated input at every framing boundary."],"verification_requirements":["Verify parser acceptance and rejection behavior against the format specification and implementation documentation."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable serialization format specification and parser documentation","evidence_role":"syntax/framing semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B03-R003","D44-B09-R002"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B08-R002","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B08","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Round-Trip Information Loss","problem_statement":"A value can be parsed successfully and serialized successfully while still losing information during the parse-transform-serialize cycle.","context":"Configuration, APIs, persistence, import/export, and schema conversion perform repeated representation changes.","preconditions":["The representation contains information that the target model may not preserve.","Round-trip behavior matters."],"inputs":["fully representable value","unknown field","ordering-sensitive representation","precision-sensitive value","explicit null/missing distinction"],"expected_behavior":["Required information survives supported round trips.","Unsupported information is rejected, preserved, or explicitly documented as lossy.","Round-trip equality uses the correct semantic equivalence relation."],"implementation_guidance":["Define semantic versus byte-for-byte round-trip requirements.","Preserve unknown fields where forward compatibility requires it.","Use canonicalization only when the contract permits it."],"failure_modes":["silent field loss","precision loss","ordering loss where order is semantic","null/missing collapse"],"edge_cases":["unknown future field","duplicate keys","numeric formatting differences","Unicode normalization differences"],"security_considerations":["Security metadata must not disappear during transformations.","Canonicalization should be defined before signing or security-sensitive comparison."],"performance_considerations":["Preservation of unknown fields may increase memory usage; optimize only if measurement identifies a real constraint."],"test_considerations":["Perform parse→serialize→parse comparisons using semantic invariants.","Test unsupported fields and high-precision values."],"verification_requirements":["Verify round-trip invariants explicitly; successful parsing alone is insufficient."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable serialization/schema specification","evidence_role":"round-trip semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B02-R001","D44-B03-R004"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B08-R003","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B08","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Unicode Normalization and Visually Equivalent Text","problem_statement":"Different Unicode sequences can represent canonically equivalent or visually similar text, affecting equality, lookup, identifiers, and security decisions.","context":"Applications process international text, identifiers, filenames, search terms, and user-generated content.","preconditions":["Unicode text is accepted.","Equality or identity depends on text comparison."],"inputs":["precomposed character","decomposed sequence","case variants","combining marks","visually confusable characters"],"expected_behavior":["The application applies the normalization and comparison rules required by its domain.","Text is not silently normalized when preservation of original representation is required.","Security-sensitive identifiers use a defined canonicalization policy."],"implementation_guidance":["Distinguish Unicode normalization from case folding and visual similarity.","Choose a normalization form based on the protocol/domain requirement.","Retain original text separately when display fidelity matters."],"failure_modes":["duplicate logical identifiers","failed lookup","security policy mismatch","display corruption"],"edge_cases":["combining marks","zero-width characters","non-Latin scripts","confusable characters","mixed normalization forms"],"security_considerations":["Identifier and authentication systems should follow established Unicode security guidance rather than ad hoc normalization.","Visual similarity must not be treated as identity."],"performance_considerations":["Normalization adds processing cost proportional to text size; perform it at controlled boundaries when appropriate."],"test_considerations":["Test canonically equivalent sequences and non-equivalent confusables separately.","Include multilingual and combining-mark cases."],"verification_requirements":["Verify normalization policy against Unicode specifications and application requirements."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Unicode Standard and Unicode Standard Annex #15","evidence_role":"normalization semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B02-R002","D44-B09-R003"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B08-R004","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B08","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Temporal Boundary and Ambiguous Local Time","problem_statement":"Local date-time values can become ambiguous or nonexistent around timezone transitions, while duration and ordering calculations may require a monotonic or absolute time model.","context":"Scheduling, expiration, reporting, logging, distributed systems, and calendar applications process dates and times.","preconditions":["The application accepts local times or timezone-aware timestamps.","Timezone rules can change or contain discontinuities."],"inputs":["ordinary local time","nonexistent local time","ambiguous local time","UTC timestamp","date-only value"],"expected_behavior":["The system distinguishes absolute instants from local calendar representations.","Ambiguous or nonexistent local times are resolved according to explicit policy.","Duration calculations use an appropriate time basis."],"implementation_guidance":["Store absolute instants when the domain requires an instant.","Retain timezone or calendar context when reconstructing local representations.","Use monotonic clocks for elapsed-duration measurement where supported."],"failure_modes":["one-hour offset error","duplicate scheduled execution","missed execution","incorrect duration","expiration at wrong instant"],"edge_cases":["daylight-saving transition","timezone rule update","leap-day date","month-end rollover","year boundary"],"security_considerations":["Time-based authorization, token expiry, and audit timestamps require explicit clock and timezone semantics."],"performance_considerations":["Timezone conversion may require rule data lookup; optimize only after measuring real workload impact."],"test_considerations":["Test transitions and ordinary dates separately.","Test both ambiguous and nonexistent local times."],"verification_requirements":["Verify temporal behavior against the relevant timezone/calendar specification and library version."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"IANA Time Zone Database and applicable language datetime documentation","evidence_role":"timezone and temporal semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B06-R004","D44-B08-R005"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B08-R005","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B08","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Boundary Between Calendar Units","problem_statement":"Operations that add, subtract, truncate, or compare calendar units can behave differently at month ends, leap days, week boundaries, and timezone transitions.","context":"Date arithmetic is used for billing periods, retention, reporting, scheduling, and business rules.","preconditions":["The operation uses calendar units rather than pure elapsed duration.","Calendar boundaries affect the result."],"inputs":["January 31 plus one month","leap-day arithmetic","month-end truncation","week boundary","year boundary"],"expected_behavior":["The operation follows the documented calendar arithmetic semantics.","Invalid calendar dates are normalized or rejected according to explicit rules.","Elapsed-duration semantics are not substituted for calendar semantics."],"implementation_guidance":["Use domain-specific date/time APIs rather than hand-coded calendar arithmetic where possible.","Specify whether operations mean elapsed duration or calendar adjustment.","Document end-of-month behavior."],"failure_modes":["unexpected rollover","incorrect billing period","off-by-one date","incorrect recurrence schedule"],"edge_cases":["February in leap and non-leap years","month with 28/29/30/31 days","year transition","negative calendar adjustment"],"security_considerations":["Retention and expiration policies can be affected by date arithmetic errors.","Audit and compliance calculations should use explicitly defined calendar semantics."],"performance_considerations":["Calendar calculations are generally inexpensive relative to I/O; avoid premature optimization."],"test_considerations":["Cover all month lengths and leap-year behavior.","Test positive and negative adjustments."],"verification_requirements":["Verify calendar semantics against the selected date/time library and domain requirement."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable language datetime documentation and Gregorian/calendar specification","evidence_role":"calendar arithmetic semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B08-R004","D44-B01-R001"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B09-R001","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B09","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","title":"Boundary Validation Bypass","problem_statement":"Security validation can be bypassed when equivalent or near-equivalent representations are validated inconsistently across layers.","context":"Input passes through parsing, normalization, authorization, storage, and execution boundaries.","preconditions":["Security decisions depend on input validation or canonicalization.","Multiple layers interpret the same value."],"inputs":["canonical valid value","alternate representation","encoded representation","normalized representation","boundary value"],"expected_behavior":["Security-sensitive validation occurs on the representation actually used for the protected operation.","Canonicalization rules are consistent across relevant layers.","Rejected representations cannot become valid after a later transformation."],"implementation_guidance":["Define canonicalization before security comparison where required.","Avoid duplicate parsers with materially different semantics.","Validate after transformations that can change security meaning."],"failure_modes":["filter bypass","authorization mismatch","path traversal bypass","duplicate identity"],"edge_cases":["double encoding","mixed case","Unicode normalization difference","alternate numeric notation","trailing separators"],"security_considerations":["Treat parser differentials and canonicalization mismatches as explicit security cases.","Use established security guidance for the affected protocol or data type."],"performance_considerations":["Security canonicalization adds processing work; correctness takes priority over unmeasured micro-optimization."],"test_considerations":["Test equivalent representations through every transformation layer.","Include malformed and ambiguous representations."],"verification_requirements":["Verify that the security decision is based on the canonical representation used by the protected operation."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"OWASP guidance and applicable protocol/security specifications","evidence_role":"canonicalization and validation security"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B08-R001","D44-B08-R003"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B09-R002","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B09","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","title":"Resource Amplification Through Small Input","problem_statement":"A small attacker-controlled input can cause disproportionately large CPU, memory, recursion, I/O, or downstream work.","context":"Parsers, decompression, regular expressions, graph processing, recursive algorithms, and database queries process externally supplied data.","preconditions":["Input size is externally controlled.","Processing cost can grow faster than input size or trigger expensive operations."],"inputs":["small normal input","small adversarial input","large bounded input","deeply nested input"],"expected_behavior":["Resource consumption is bounded according to system requirements.","Expensive processing is rejected or limited before resource exhaustion.","Limits are enforced at the relevant trust boundary."],"implementation_guidance":["Set explicit size, depth, time, and concurrency limits where applicable.","Prefer algorithms with predictable resource behavior for untrusted inputs.","Propagate deadlines and cancellation through expensive operations."],"failure_modes":["memory exhaustion","CPU exhaustion","stack exhaustion","I/O amplification","database overload"],"edge_cases":["highly nested payload","pathological regex input","compressed input with high expansion","graph with dense connectivity"],"security_considerations":["Resource exhaustion is a security concern even when no data confidentiality issue exists.","Limits should be based on explicit capacity and threat assumptions."],"performance_considerations":["Limits must be tuned against legitimate workloads; use measured capacity evidence rather than arbitrary values."],"test_considerations":["Measure resource behavior for adversarial small inputs and boundary-sized legitimate inputs.","Test cumulative amplification across layers."],"verification_requirements":["Verify resource limits are actually enforced and cannot be bypassed through alternate code paths."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"OWASP guidance and CWE resource-exhaustion classifications","evidence_role":"resource amplification security"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B08-R001","D44-B06-R003"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B09-R003","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B09","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","title":"Ambiguous Identity Representation","problem_statement":"Two inputs can appear different at the byte or syntax level while resolving to the same logical identity, or appear similar while actually being different identities.","context":"Authentication, authorization, account lookup, filenames, identifiers, usernames, URLs, and resource names use canonical identity rules.","preconditions":["Identity comparison is security-sensitive.","Multiple representations or normalization rules exist."],"inputs":["canonical identifier","case variant","Unicode equivalent","encoded identifier","visually confusable identifier"],"expected_behavior":["Identity comparison follows an explicit canonicalization policy.","Distinct identities remain distinct when required.","Equivalent identities cannot bypass ownership or uniqueness controls."],"implementation_guidance":["Define identifier normalization and comparison rules at the domain level.","Store a stable canonical identity where appropriate while retaining display representation separately.","Do not rely on visual similarity."],"failure_modes":["account confusion","duplicate identity","authorization bypass","resource misbinding"],"edge_cases":["Unicode confusable","case-insensitive filesystem versus case-sensitive application","trailing whitespace","percent-encoded identifier"],"security_considerations":["Follow established Unicode and protocol security guidance for identity systems.","Do not create new identity equivalence rules without analyzing collision consequences."],"performance_considerations":["Canonical identity calculation should normally occur at ingress rather than repeatedly during internal operations."],"test_considerations":["Test canonical equivalents, near-equivalents, and intentionally distinct values.","Test identity behavior across storage and API boundaries."],"verification_requirements":["Verify identity semantics against the authoritative domain/protocol definition."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Unicode security guidance and applicable authentication/identifier specification","evidence_role":"identity semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B08-R003","D44-B09-R001"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B09-R004","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B09","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","title":"Fail-Open Versus Fail-Closed Edge State","problem_statement":"When a required security dependency, policy source, credential verifier, or authorization decision is unavailable, the fallback behavior can materially change system security.","context":"Authorization gateways, policy engines, secret stores, identity providers, and security controls can become unavailable or return incomplete information.","preconditions":["Security decisions depend on an external or fallible component.","The component can return unavailable, unknown, or partial results."],"inputs":["explicit allow","explicit deny","unknown","timeout","dependency unavailable"],"expected_behavior":["Fallback behavior follows an explicit security policy.","Unknown security state is not silently treated as permission unless the domain explicitly requires it.","Operational degradation is observable."],"implementation_guidance":["Represent UNKNOWN separately from ALLOW and DENY.","Define emergency access paths explicitly and audit them.","Do not use generic exception handling as an authorization policy."],"failure_modes":["fail-open authorization","accidental denial of legitimate work","hidden policy outage","stale permission acceptance"],"edge_cases":["policy cache expired","identity provider timeout","partial policy response","network partition"],"security_considerations":["Security-critical unknown states generally require conservative handling, but the correct behavior must be determined by the explicit threat and operational contract."],"performance_considerations":["Failover or policy caching can reduce latency but may increase staleness; quantify and document the trade-off."],"test_considerations":["Simulate every dependency failure mode.","Verify both security outcome and operational audit evidence."],"verification_requirements":["Verify fallback behavior against the security architecture and authorization contract."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"OWASP authorization guidance and applicable authorization architecture","evidence_role":"security failure semantics"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B06-R005","D44-B09-R005"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B09-R005","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B09","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","title":"Expired Authority During Long Operation","problem_statement":"An operation can begin while authorization is valid but continue after the authority, session, lease, token, approval, or policy has expired or been revoked.","context":"Long-running jobs, workflows, deployments, administrative actions, and asynchronous tasks execute across time boundaries.","preconditions":["Authority has a lifetime or can be revoked.","The operation can outlive the initial authorization decision."],"inputs":["authority valid throughout operation","authority expires during operation","authority revoked during operation","authority unavailable during recheck"],"expected_behavior":["The system follows explicit authorization lifetime semantics.","Long-running operations do not silently gain indefinite authority.","Revocation and expiration behavior is consistent with the risk model."],"implementation_guidance":["Define authorization checkpoints for long-running high-impact operations.","Bind sensitive actions to an explicit approval or lease where appropriate.","Do not assume initial authentication alone proves continuing authorization."],"failure_modes":["expired authority continues indefinitely","revocation ignored","partial privileged action","inconsistent audit record"],"edge_cases":["expiry during final side effect","clock skew around expiry","revocation during network partition","queued action executes after approval expiry"],"security_considerations":["Authority duration should reflect the sensitivity and lifecycle of the operation.","High-impact operations may require fresh authorization or approval before execution."],"performance_considerations":["Frequent authorization rechecks add latency and dependency load; choose checkpoints based on risk and measured operational constraints."],"test_considerations":["Force expiration and revocation at multiple operation stages.","Test queued work after authority expiry."],"verification_requirements":["Verify the complete authority lifecycle, not merely initial permission."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"OWASP authorization guidance and applicable identity/authorization specification","evidence_role":"authorization lifecycle"}],"relationships":{"related_to":["D44-B04-R004","D44-B09-R004"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B10-R001","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B10","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Compound Boundary Failure","problem_statement":"Several individually valid edge conditions can occur simultaneously and invalidate assumptions that hold when each condition is considered separately.","context":"Production systems combine empty input, concurrency, network delay, schema evolution, resource limits, and lifecycle transitions.","preconditions":["Multiple independent or interacting edge conditions can occur in the same execution.","The implementation contains assumptions that may interact."],"inputs":["empty input during network timeout","maximum-size request during service restart","stale event during schema migration","expired authority during retry"],"expected_behavior":["The system maintains explicitly required invariants under combinations of edge conditions.","Failure handling does not create a new unsafe state while recovering from another edge condition.","Diagnostic evidence identifies the interacting conditions."],"implementation_guidance":["Model edge conditions as composable dimensions rather than isolated examples.","Prioritize combinations that cross trust, state, persistence, and external-system boundaries.","Avoid assuming that individually tested conditions remain safe when combined."],"failure_modes":["cascading failure","incorrect recovery","state corruption","hidden authorization bypass","resource exhaustion"],"edge_cases":["timeout plus retry plus duplicate delivery","restart plus stale event","migration plus replica lag","resource exhaustion plus cancellation"],"security_considerations":["Compound failures can bypass controls that each layer assumes another layer enforces.","High-impact paths require defense-in-depth and explicit degraded-state behavior."],"performance_considerations":["Combinatorial testing can become expensive; prioritize combinations using architectural risk and interaction dependencies rather than arbitrary enumeration."],"test_considerations":["Generate pairwise or risk-prioritized combinations of edge conditions.","Preserve evidence identifying which conditions were active."],"verification_requirements":["Verify cross-condition invariants and recovery behavior.","Do not infer compound-case correctness solely from independent test success."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"INFERENCE","provenance":[{"reference_source":"General fault-model and software reliability engineering principles","evidence_role":"compound edge-case reasoning"}],"relationships":{"composes_with":["D44-B05-R004","D44-B06-R002","D44-B07-R004","D44-B09-R005"],"requires_implementation":true}}

{"record_id":"D44-B10-R002","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B10","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Cross-Layer Semantic Drift","problem_statement":"An edge value can be interpreted differently by successive layers, producing behavior that is locally valid but globally incorrect.","context":"API gateway, application, database, cache, message broker, serializer, and client layers may apply different defaults or normalization rules.","preconditions":["A value crosses multiple implementation boundaries.","Each boundary may transform or validate it independently."],"inputs":["missing field","empty value","boundary number","normalized identifier","unknown enum"],"expected_behavior":["Semantic meaning remains consistent across layers or explicit transformations are documented.","No layer silently changes a value into a materially different security or business meaning.","Round-trip behavior preserves required invariants."],"implementation_guidance":["Define canonical semantics at the domain boundary.","Track transformations explicitly when values change representation.","Use contract tests or schema verification for cross-layer assumptions."],"failure_modes":["gateway accepts but application rejects","database truncates accepted value","cache key differs from authorization identity","serializer changes null semantics"],"edge_cases":["default applied twice","different trimming rules","case sensitivity mismatch","precision mismatch across storage"],"security_considerations":["Cross-layer semantic drift is a common source of validation and authorization mismatches.","Security decisions should use canonical values and consistent policy semantics."],"performance_considerations":["Repeated transformation and validation can increase cost; centralize invariant-preserving normalization where appropriate."],"test_considerations":["Trace representative edge values through the entire processing pipeline.","Test every transformation boundary, not just the endp