{"record_id":"D44-B11-R004","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B11","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Circular Dependency","problem_statement":"Two or more components can depend directly or transitively on each other, preventing ordinary acyclic initialization or resolution.","context":"Module systems, dependency injection, package resolution, build graphs, and plugin architectures can contain cycles.","preconditions":["Dependency relationships form a directed graph.","The resolution mechanism assumes or prefers acyclic dependencies."],"inputs":["acyclic graph","direct cycle","indirect cycle","self-dependency"],"expected_behavior":["Cycles are detected deterministically when unsupported.","If cycles are supported, their initialization semantics are explicit.","Failure identifies the cycle rather than reporting an unrelated missing dependency."],"implementation_guidance":["Detect cycles using the dependency graph rather than recursive failure alone.","Separate dependency discovery from initialization ordering.","Document supported cycle semantics if the runtime permits them."],"failure_modes":["infinite recursion","deadlock during initialization","partially initialized object","opaque resolver error"],"edge_cases":["self-cycle","two-node cycle","large cycle","cycle introduced only through optional dependency"],"security_considerations":["Dependency cycles can obscure initialization of security controls or policy components.","Do not bypass validation simply to break a security-relevant cycle."],"performance_considerations":["Cycle detection should be bounded by graph size; avoid repeated traversal when graph analysis can be reused."],"test_considerations":["Test direct and indirect cycles.","Test cycle detection with large graphs and optional dependencies."],"verification_requirements":["Verify cycle detection and resulting state against the resolver's documented contract."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","ver