{"record_id":"D44-B32-R002","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B32","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Commit Boundary Followed by Failure","problem_statement":"The durable state change succeeds, but an error occurs before the caller receives confirmation, creating uncertainty about whether a retry is safe.","context":"Network failures, process crashes, or response serialization failures can occur after commit.","preconditions":["A durable operation and acknowledgement are separate steps.","The caller can lose the response after the operation commits."],"inputs":["Committed operation","Lost acknowledgement"],"expected_behavior":["Treat completion as potentially successful when commit may have occurred.","Use idempotency keys, operation status queries, or equivalent mechanisms to resolve uncertainty."],"implementation_guidance":["Design commit and acknowledgement semantics together.","Expose durable operation identifiers when clients may need reconciliation.","Avoid blindly retrying non-idempotent operations."],"failure_modes":["Duplicate side effects.","Inconsistent client state.","Repeated provisioning or charging."],"edge_cases":["Server commits then crashes.","Commit succeeds but response serialization fails.","Client timeout occurs after server completion."],"security_considerations":["Retries of privileged operations must not create duplicate security-sensitive side effects."],"performance_considerations":["Status reconciliation adds a lookup path but can prevent expensive duplicate work."],"test_considerations":["Simulate failure immediately after commit.","Test retry and reconciliation behavior.","Verify idempotency under lost responses."],"verification_requirements":["Verify unknown-completion handling.","Verify retry behavior is safe for every externally visible side effect."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"re