{"record_id":"D44-B33-R001","dataset":"D44","dataset_title":"Edge Cases","batch":"B33","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","title":"Empty Network Response Body","problem_statement":"A network request succeeds at the transport or protocol level but the response body is empty when the application normally expects structured content.","context":"Successful responses may legitimately have no body, while other operations require a payload.","preconditions":["The client receives a response without a body.","The application expects or permits structured content depending on the endpoint contract."],"inputs":["Successful response with empty body"],"expected_behavior":["Interpret the response according to the endpoint contract.","Do not blindly deserialize an absent body as valid structured data."],"implementation_guidance":["Use status code and content semantics together.","Define whether empty body means success, no result, malformed response, or protocol violation."],"failure_modes":["Parser exception.","False success.","Null dereference.","Incorrect retry after legitimate no-body success."],"edge_cases":["Zero-byte body with content-type header.","Chunked response ending without payload.","204-like no-content semantics."],"security_considerations":["Do not bypass response validation merely because transport succeeded."],"performance_considerations":["Avoid unnecessary parser invocation when the protocol explicitly indicates no body."],"test_considerations":["Test empty body for every supported response status.","Test missing versus empty body semantics."],"verification_requirements":["Verify body requirements against the endpoint protocol contract.","Verify no-body responses are handled deterministically."],"code_applicability":"REQUIRED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","verification_status":"SPECIFIED_NOT_EXECUTED","truth_class":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"reference_source":"Applicable network protocol/API specification","evidence_role":"behavioral_basis","provenance_sta