{"record_id":"D45-B31-R001","dataset":"D45","dataset_title":"Negative Cases","batch":"B31","knowledge_type":"NEGATIVE_CASE","title":"Concurrent Update Conflict","problem_statement":"A write operation is rejected because another actor modified the same logical state after the caller read it.","context":"The operation relies on a stale version, revision, timestamp, or optimistic concurrency token.","preconditions":["A mutable resource has a concurrency control mechanism.","A caller reads an earlier resource version.","Another actor commits a conflicting update before the caller writes."],"invalid_condition":["The caller submits an update using a stale concurrency version."],"inputs_or_trigger":["resource_version=41","current_resource_version=42","update_request based on version 41"],"expected_failure_behavior":["Reject the stale write rather than silently overwriting the newer state.","Return a machine-detectable conflict condition.","Preserve the successfully committed newer state.","Allow the caller to refresh and explicitly reconcile if supported."],"failure_interpretation":["The failure indicates stale write authority rather than malformed data.","A successful response must not be returned merely because the transport request completed."],"implementation_guidance":["Compare the caller's expected version with the authoritative version atomically with the write.","Use a conflict-specific result instead of converting the conflict into a generic server error.","Do not silently retry a semantically conflicting write without a reconciliation policy."],"failure_modes":["Lost update","Silent overwrite","Incorrect automatic retry","Conflict converted to success"],"security_considerations":["Do not allow clients to bypass concurrency checks by supplying arbitrary versions.","Treat authorization and concurrency validation as separate controls."],"performance_considerations":["Conflict detection should occur close to the authoritative write boundary.","High conflict rates may