{"record_id":"D45-B31-R004","dataset":"D45","dataset_title":"Negative Cases","batch":"B31","knowledge_type":"NEGATIVE_CASE","title":"Duplicate Event Rejection","problem_statement":"An event consumer receives an event that it has already processed and must not apply the same non-idempotent effect again.","context":"At-least-once delivery or consumer restart can legitimately produce duplicate deliveries.","preconditions":["Events have stable identifiers.","The consumer maintains sufficient processing state to detect duplicates."],"invalid_condition":["An already-processed event identifier is delivered again."],"inputs_or_trigger":["event_id=evt-9001","event_id already marked processed"],"expected_failure_behavior":["Do not repeat the protected side effect.","Record or expose duplicate detection according to observability policy.","Allow the consumer to acknowledge the duplicate when safe."],"failure_interpretation":["Duplicate delivery is a delivery characteristic rather than necessarily a producer defect."],"implementation_guidance":["Use durable deduplication state where required by the effect's durability.","Ensure duplicate detection and side-effect coordination cannot introduce a new race.","Prefer inherently idempotent handlers where practical."],"failure_modes":["Repeated side effect","Duplicate billing/action","Deduplication race","Lost acknowledgment causing repeated processing"],"security_considerations":["Do not permit attacker-controlled event identifiers to bypass authorization or deduplication scope.","Validate event provenance before processing."],"performance_considerations":["Deduplication state requires storage and lookup overhead.","Retention must balance replay requirements against storage growth."],"test_considerations":["Deliver the same event multiple times.","Deliver duplicates concurrently.","Restart the consumer between processing and acknowledgment where relevant."],"verification_requirements":["Verify duplicate events do not produce duplica